window.I5ASK={"v":"2026-11-21","n":2280,"e":{"ca0-san-ysidro-port-of-entry":{"n":"San Ysidro Port of Entry","k":"p","q":[["Why is the San Ysidro Port of Entry so famous?","Federal officials call it the busiest land port in the Western Hemisphere. Interstate 5 begins right here at the Mexican border.","Why is it so famous?|Why do people say this border is such a big deal?|What's special about this place?","famous"],["How old is the San Ysidro Port of Entry?","There has been a border inspection station in San Ysidro since the early 1900s. The main port was built in 1973 and a big rebuild was finished in 2019.","How old is it?|Has there been a border here for a long time?|When did this thing start?","age"],["Is any of the old San Ysidro Port of Entry still standing?","Yes. A Mission Revival customs house from the early 1930s still stands, and it holds offices today.","Is any of it still the old part?|Is there an old building here, like really old?|Anything old left?","history"],["How big is the San Ysidro Port of Entry?","Cars line up across 34 lanes to enter the United States here.","How big is it?|How many lanes does it have?|Is it huge?","size"]]},"ca1-kumeyaay-homeland":{"n":"Kumeyaay homeland","k":"p","q":[["Who are the Kumeyaay people?","This is the homeland of the Kumeyaay Nation. Kumeyaay people live on both sides of the border today, with 13 bands in San Diego County and more communities in Baja California.","Who are they, the Kumeyaay?|Who lives here, mom?|Kumeyaay who?","people"],["How big is the Kumeyaay homeland?","Kumeyaay country runs from the Pacific to the Colorado River. Along the coast it stretches from about Oceanside to south of Ensenada.","How big is it?|How far does their land go?|Is it a big area?","size"],["How long have the Kumeyaay lived around San Diego?","Kumeyaay tradition holds that the people have lived around San Diego since 10,000 BC.","How long have they been here?|How many years ago did they start living here?|Are they old?","age"],["Why did the Spanish call them Diegueños?","The Spanish named them Diegueños after Mission San Diego. In Mexico, the Kumeyaay are known as Kumiai.","Why was it called that?|Why did they get another name, mom?|What does Diegueño mean?","name"],["How does the border affect the Kumeyaay?","Since 1848 the international border has run right through Kumeyaay country. Families and ceremonies still cross the line, and about 1,900 Kumiai came to hold special visas to attend events on the US side.","What does the border do to them?|Did a wall cut through their land?|Can they cross?","history"],["What happened here with the border wall in 2020?","In 2020, as border wall construction sped up, Kumeyaay women led protests. The La Posta Band sued to stop building through a sacred burial ground.","Did people protest it?|Did anyone fight the wall, dad?|What was the wall lawsuit?","today"]]},"ca3-tijuana-river":{"n":"Tijuana River","k":"p","q":[["Where does the Tijuana River start and where does it end?","It rises in the mountains of Baja California, runs through Tijuana in a concrete channel, then crosses into the United States for its last few miles to the sea.","Where does it start and end?|Where does the river come from, and where does it go?|Where's it start?","nature"],["What was the Little Landers colony near the Tijuana River?","William Smythe started it in 1908. Each member farmed about 2 acres and all the buying and selling was shared. It lasted until a great flood wiped out the farms in 1916.","What happened to that colony?|Who used to farm here a long time ago?|Was there a town here once?","history"],["What is there to do in the Tijuana River Valley today?","A regional park of more than 1,800 acres fills much of the valley, with trails for walking and horseback riding. Surfers have also chased big winter waves off the river mouth since at least 1937.","Can we walk or hike there?|What can kids do there?|Is there a park?","visit"],["Is the Tijuana River polluted?","Yes. The river carries Tijuana's sewage overflows, and in 2013 beaches near its mouth were closed for 204 days. In 2020, a trade agreement set aside $300 million to fight the pollution.","Why were the beaches closed because of it?|Is it dirty?|What's wrong with the water?","today"]]},"ca4-montgomery-s-silver-wing":{"n":"Montgomery's Silver Wing","k":"p","q":[["What is Montgomery's Silver Wing?","It's a 93-foot stainless steel test wing for a World War II bomber, standing on end. Locals sometimes call it the Silver Wing.","What is that giant silver thing?|Why is there a huge airplane wing standing up?|What's the big wing?","famous"],["Who was John J. Montgomery and why does the Silver Wing honor him?","In the spring of 1884 he made glider flights of up to 600 feet from the rim of Otay Mesa. Some historians count them as the first controlled flights of a heavier-than-air craft in America.","Who is it honoring?|Who was the guy the wing is for?|Who's Montgomery?","people"],["How did Montgomery design his glider wings?","He shaped his first glider's wing on the curve of a seagull's. Then he studied turkey vultures and gave his next glider hinged wing flaps, an early form of today's ailerons.","How did he make it work?|How did the old glider fly, kid question?|Where did he get his ideas?","history"],["When was the Montgomery memorial built?","The memorial was dedicated in 1950. It honors flights Montgomery made back in 1884.","How old is it?|Is the wing really old?|When did they put it up?","age"],["What happened to John Montgomery later in life?","He later taught at Santa Clara College. He died in a glider crash in San Jose in 1911.","What happened to him?|Did the glider guy get hurt, kid question?|How did he die?","people"],["Is there a freeway named after Montgomery?","Yes. In 1949 the road from the border to downtown San Diego was named the John J. Montgomery Freeway. His family's Fruitland Ranch once stood where the freeway now runs.","Is the road named for him?|Did they name this highway after him?|Freeway name?","name"]]},"ca5-the-rainmaker-s-flood":{"n":"The rainmaker's flood","k":"p","q":[["Who was Charles Hatfield, the rainmaker?","Charles Hatfield was a self-styled rainmaker who called himself a moisture accelerator. In 1915 San Diego's city council voted to pay him $10,000 to fill a reservoir.","Who was that guy?|Was he a real rainmaker, like with magic?|Who's Hatfield?","people"],["What happened in the rainmaker's flood?","In January 1916 the rain came and kept coming. On January 27 the Lower Otay Dam burst and sent a wall of water 40 feet high down the Otay River valley.","What happened with it?|Did the rain really just keep coming?|What was the flood?","history"],["Did anyone die in the rainmaker's flood?","Yes. The flood reportedly killed about 20 people, though accounts vary, among them 11 Japanese American farmers.","Did people get hurt in it?|Did anybody die?|How many died?","people"],["What else did the storms damage besides the Otay Dam?","The storms overflowed the Sweetwater Dam too, washed away bridges, stranded trains and cut phone lines.","Was that the only damage?|Did the storm wreck other stuff?|What else broke?","history"],["Did San Diego ever pay Hatfield?","No. The council refused to pay unless he accepted the blame for damages. He sued, and in 1938 the courts ruled the rain an act of God, so he was cleared but never paid.","Did he get paid for it?|Did the rainmaker get his money?|Was he in trouble?","history"]]},"ca6-south-bay-salt-works":{"n":"South Bay Salt Works","k":"p","q":[["How old is the South Bay Salt Works?","People have harvested salt here since at least 1871. That makes it the second-longest-running business in San Diego, after the San Diego Union-Tribune newspaper.","How old is it?|How long have they been making salt here, Dad?|How old?","age"],["How big are the South Bay Salt Works ponds?","The ponds cover more than a thousand acres. They yield about 75,000 tons of salt a year.","How big is it?|Are those ponds really big, Dad?|How much salt do they make?","size"],["What birds can you see at the South Bay Salt Works?","Ninety-four kinds of birds use the area, including endangered California least terns. The salty ponds breed brine shrimp and brine flies, which are a feast for birds.","Are there animals at that place?|Are there lots of birds there, Mom?|Any wildlife?","nature"],["What happened to the South Bay Salt Works in 1916?","The 1916 flood wiped out the ponds. Rebuilding took until the 1950s.","What happened to it in the flood?|Did the salt ponds get wrecked, Dad?|The 1916 flood?","history"],["Why is the South Bay Salt Works special on the West Coast?","On the West Coast, only San Francisco Bay and San Diego Bay have the natural conditions for making sea salt.","Why is it so special?|Why can they make salt here, Mom?|Is it the only one?","famous"]]},"ca7-chula-vista-the-old-lemon-capital":{"n":"Chula Vista, the old Lemon Capital","k":"p","q":[["What does Chula Vista mean?","Chula Vista is Spanish for beautiful view. The name was suggested by the designer of the Sweetwater Dam.","What does the name of that town mean?|Why's it called Chula Vista?|Chula Vista meaning?","name"],["Why was Chula Vista called the Lemon Capital?","Water from the Sweetwater Dam irrigated the farmland, and for a time Chula Vista was the largest lemon-growing center in the world. By 1911 about 4,000 acres were lemon groves.","Why was it called that?|Why did they grow so many lemons here, Dad?|Lemon Capital, why?","famous"],["Who brought lemons to Chula Vista?","Lemons were introduced by a retired professor from the University of Wisconsin.","Who started it?|Who planted the first lemons here?|Who got the lemons going?","people"],["What happened to the lemon groves in Chula Vista?","In 1941 Rohr Aircraft moved to town, and with workers off at war or at the factory, the farmland was sold. The groves never came back.","What happened to them?|Where did all the lemon trees go?|Why no lemons now?","history"],["What else did farmers grow in Chula Vista besides lemons?","Japanese American farmers made celery a big crop too. In 1931 lemons brought in $1 million and celery $600,000.","Did they grow anything else?|Was it only lemons?|What other crops were there?","nature"],["Does Chula Vista still celebrate its lemon history?","Yes, the city's Lemon Festival still pays tribute to the old groves.","Do they still honor it?|Is there a lemon party there?|Lemon festival still going?","today"]]},"ca8-sweetwater-marsh":{"n":"Sweetwater Marsh","k":"p","q":[["Why does Sweetwater Marsh matter so much?","More than 90 percent of San Diego Bay's old wetlands have been filled, drained or diked, which is why this surviving piece matters. It's part of a national wildlife refuge.","Why is it such a big deal?|Why do they protect this swampy place?|Why's it important?","famous"],["What kind of animals and plants live at Sweetwater Marsh?","The refuge breeds endangered light-footed Ridgway's rails, and over 200 kinds of birds have been recorded there. It's also the only place in the U.S. where yerba reuma grows wild.","What lives in it?|What animals can I see there, Mom?|What's in there?","nature"],["What happened at Gunpowder Point near Sweetwater Marsh?","From 1916 to 1920, the Hercules Powder Company ran a 30-acre plant there, turning kelp into acetone and potash, a key ingredient in smokeless gunpowder during World War I.","What happened there long ago?|Why is it called Gunpowder, was there really gunpowder?|Gunpowder Point?","history"],["What can we do at Sweetwater Marsh?","You can walk trails 1.5 miles out to the bay, or visit the Living Coast Discovery Center, a zoo and aquarium with green sea turtles, sharks, rays and eagles.","What is there to do at that place?|Can we see cool animals there?|Anything to see?","see"],["Who used the Sweetwater Marsh area before it became a refuge?","The Kumeyaay fished, hunted and gathered here. Later, from 1916 to 1920, a powder company ran a plant on the shore.","Did anyone live there long ago?|Who was there first, like way back?|Who lived there?","people"],["What is the Living Coast Discovery Center at Sweetwater Marsh?","It's a zoo and aquarium inside the refuge. It opened in 1987 as the Chula Vista Nature Interpretive Center and has green sea turtles, sharks, rays and bald and golden eagles.","What's that zoo and aquarium thing there?|Is there a zoo with sharks there?|Discovery center?","kids"]]},"ca11-national-city-s-royal-ranch":{"n":"National City's royal ranch","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called National City?","It's named after a ranch. Spanish soldiers grazed horses on El Rancho del Rey, which became Rancho de la Nación after Mexico won independence, and the Kimball brothers kept the name National.","Why did they name it that?|How did National City get its name, mom?|Why 'National'?","name"],["How big was the ranch National City started on?","It was huge: 26,000 acres. Spanish soldiers grazed their horses there, on land called El Rancho del Rey, the Ranch of the King.","How big was it?|Was that ranch really huge, dad?|How many acres?","size"],["Who founded National City?","Three Kimball brothers from San Francisco. In 1868 they bought the whole rancho and founded the town, keeping the name National.","Who started it?|Who built this town, dad?|Who bought it?","people"],["What did the Kimball brothers build in National City?","They built the area's first roads and railroad, a post office and a wharf. Frank Kimball's house even had a bathtub and hot running water.","What did they build there?|Did the brothers make anything cool?|What's in it from back then?","history"],["What happened when the trains came to National City?","When transcontinental trains arrived in November 1885, one of the biggest land booms in California history began.","Why were trains such a big deal there?|Did the trains do something big, mom?|What was the land boom?","history"],["Why was National Avenue called the Mile of Bars?","Sailors' bars there earned National Avenue the nickname Mile of Bars. The city later remade it as the Mile of Cars, one of the first auto malls in the world.","What is the Mile of Bars?|Why was that street nicknamed that, dad?|Mile of Cars?","famous"]]},"ca13-naval-base-san-diego":{"n":"Naval Base San Diego","k":"p","q":[["What is Naval Base San Diego?","It's the main home port of the US Pacific Fleet. More than 50 ships tie up at its 13 piers along the bay.","What is that base with all the ships?|Whose ships are those, Mom?|What's that place?","famous"],["How many people work at Naval Base San Diego?","Over 24,000 military personnel and 10,000 civilians work on the base.","How many people work there?|Do a lot of people work on that base?|How many sailors are there?","people"],["How big is Naval Base San Diego compared to other bases?","It's the second-largest surface ship base in the world, after Naval Station Norfolk.","Is it a big base?|Is that the biggest Navy base ever?|How big is it?","size"],["How did Naval Base San Diego get started?","A company building concrete ships for World War I used the shore in 1918. The Navy took over, and in February 1922 it opened as the Destroyer Base, San Diego.","How did it begin?|Who made that base and when?|How old is it?","history"],["What did Naval Base San Diego do in World War II?","It was renamed the Repair Base. From 1943 to 1945, its crews repaired, overhauled or converted more than 5,000 ships.","What did it do in the war?|Did that base fix ships in World War II?|What happened there in the war?","history"]]},"ca14-coronado-bridge-and-chicano-park":{"n":"Coronado Bridge and Chicano Park","k":"p","q":[["When did the Coronado Bridge open?","The San Diego-Coronado Bridge opened in 1969. It's the long, curving bridge you can see arching over the bay.","How old is it?|When did they build that big bridge, anyone know?|Bridge age?","age"],["Why does the Coronado Bridge curve so much?","The Navy wanted 200 feet of clearance for its ships. The bridge curves 80 degrees to gain enough length to climb that high at a reasonable grade.","Why does it curve like that?|Why isn't the bridge just straight?|Why the curve?","history"],["What is Chicano Park famous for?","Chicano Park sits under the bridge ramps and has the largest collection of outdoor murals in the United States.","What is it famous for?|Why do people go to that park under the bridge?|What's special there?","famous"],["How did Chicano Park get started?","The city promised a park but started preparing a parking lot instead. Neighbors and students occupied the land for twelve days, planting trees, flowers and cactus, until the city agreed to build the park.","How did it start?|Why did people fight for the park?|What happened there?","history"],["What happened to the Barrio Logan neighborhood?","The freeway split the mostly Mexican American neighborhood in 1963, and the bridge ramps divided it again in 1969. More than 5,000 homes and businesses were removed.","What happened to the neighborhood there?|Did people lose their homes?|Why was it split up?","people"],["Why are the pillars at Chicano Park painted?","Artist Salvador Torres proposed turning the gray concrete pillars into a giant canvas, and painting began in 1973.","Why are the concrete pillars covered in paintings?|Who painted all that?|Who started the murals?","people"]]},"ca18-el-indio":{"n":"El Indio","k":"p","q":[["How old is El Indio?","It began in 1940 as a family tortilla shop. Since 1947 it has served food from its spot on India Street.","How long has it been around?|Is El Indio really old, Dad?|How old?","age"],["Who started El Indio?","Ralph Pesqueira Sr. is the founder. He adapted his Sonoran grandmother's recipe for flautas when aircraft factory workers asked him for a portable lunch.","Who founded it?|Who made El Indio, Mom?|Who's the founder?","people"],["How did El Indio get its taquitos?","During World War II, nearby aircraft factory workers wanted a portable lunch. Ralph Pesqueira Sr. adapted his Sonoran grandmother's flauta recipe for them.","How did it start making them?|Where did the taquito come from, Mom?|Taquito story?","history"],["Did El Indio really invent the taquito?","Not for sure. Los Angeles has a rival claim: Cielito Lindo, opened on Olvera Street in 1934.","Is that for sure?|Did they make the first taquito, Dad?|Who invented it?","history"],["Is El Indio still run by the same family?","Yes, the Pesqueira family still runs El Indio. It started as a family tortilla shop in 1940.","Is it still family owned?|Is the family still there, Mom?|Still the Pesqueiras?","today"]]},"ca18-lindbergh-field":{"n":"Lindbergh Field","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Lindbergh Field?","It's named for Charles Lindbergh. His plane, the Spirit of St. Louis, was built near here in 1927, and San Diego was so proud that Lindbergh lent the airport his name.","Why is it named that?|Who is Lindbergh, and why is the airport named after him?|Why Lindbergh?","name"],["When did Lindbergh Field open?","San Diego voted bonds for an airport in 1928, and it was dedicated that August as Lindbergh Field. 140 Navy and 82 Army planes flew over for the celebration.","When was it dedicated?|How old is the airport, anyone know?|When did it start?","age"],["Why does the San Diego skyline look so even?","Because the airport is so close, federal rules since the 1970s have kept downtown buildings to 500 feet or less. That's why the skyline looks so even.","Why are all the downtown buildings the same height?|Why are there no really tall skyscrapers?|Why no big buildings?","other"],["What's Liberator Way named after at Lindbergh Field?","During World War II, Consolidated Aircraft built warplanes next door. The street is still named Liberator Way, after its B-24 Liberator.","Why is there a street called Liberator Way?|What did they build here in the war?|Were there warplanes made here?","history"],["Is Lindbergh Field still called that today?","Not officially. The name changed to San Diego International Airport in 2003, and it's still the airport where jets land today.","Is it still an airport?|Did the name ever change?|What's it called now?","today"]]},"ca19-old-town-and-presidio-hill":{"n":"Old Town and Presidio Hill","k":"p","q":[["What happened on Presidio Hill?","Spain founded its first fort and mission in California there in 1769. It sat above Kosa'aay, a Kumeyaay village of 30 to 40 families.","What happened there?|What went on up on that hill, mom?|What's the story here?","history"],["What does Kosa'aay mean?","Kosa'aay means drying out place. It was the name of the Kumeyaay village below Presidio Hill.","What does that name mean?|Why's the village called a funny name?|Kosa'aay meaning?","name"],["What can we see at Old Town San Diego State Historic Park?","The preserved town has five original adobes, plus museums. Up on Presidio Hill there's the Spanish Revival Serra Museum, but nothing of the old fort survives above ground.","What's there to see in it?|Are there old houses to look at in that park?|What's in Old Town?","see"],["Why did people leave Old Town?","Old Town was the heart of San Diego until the 1860s. Then Alonzo Horton's New Town by the harbor, today's downtown, drew people away.","Why did it stop being the main town?|Where did everybody go from there?|Why'd the town move?","history"],["Is Mission San Diego de Alcalá still around?","Yes. In 1774 the mission moved a few miles up the valley from Presidio Hill, and Mission San Diego de Alcalá still stands there today.","Is it still standing?|Did the mission always stay on the hill?|Where'd the mission go?","today"]]},"ca20-mission-san-diego-de-alcal":{"n":"Mission San Diego de Alcalá","k":"p","q":[["Is Mission San Diego de Alcalá still used today?","Yes, it's still a working parish.","Do people still use it?|Do people still go to church there like for real?|Still open as a church?","today"],["Did the mission always stand where it is now?","No. It began on a hill near the harbor and moved to its current site in 1774.","Was it always in that spot?|Did they move the mission or something?|Was it built somewhere else first?","history"],["What happened at Mission San Diego de Alcalá in 1775?","People from several Kumeyaay villages burned the mission in 1775. Father Luis Jayme was killed in the revolt, and thirteen alleged leaders were jailed afterward.","What happened there in 1775?|Did something bad happen at that mission?|What was the revolt?","history"],["Who is buried at Mission San Diego de Alcalá?","Father Luis Jayme, who was killed in the 1775 revolt, is buried by the altar.","Is somebody buried there?|Is there a grave at that mission?|Who's buried inside?","people"],["Is the church at Mission San Diego de Alcalá the original building?","Not quite. The church you see today is a 1931 rebuild of the church that was dedicated in 1813.","Is that the original church?|Is the church there really old, or new?|Original or rebuilt?","see"]],"l":"mission-san-diego-de-alcala"},"ca21-mission-bay-and-san-diego-river":{"n":"Mission Bay and San Diego River","k":"p","q":[["Was Mission Bay always a park?","No. It started as a shifting maze of lagoons and tidal marsh. From the late 1940s, dredges moved 25 million cubic yards of sand and silt to build the islands and coves of Mission Bay Park.","Was it always like this?|Did people make this whole bay?|Is the bay real or built?","history"],["Why is it called Mission Bay?","The name comes from an 1888 poem by Rose Hartwick Thorpe. Before that, the explorer Cabrillo named it False Bay in 1542.","Where did that name come from?|Who named it that?|Why Mission?","name"],["What did Mission Bay used to be called?","The explorer Cabrillo named this water False Bay in 1542, when it was a shifting maze of lagoons and tidal marsh.","Did it have another name?|What was its old name?|Who named it first?","name"],["Did the San Diego River really change its path?","Yes. Before 1821 the river usually emptied into San Diego Bay, until a flood that fall swung it into False Bay in a single night, though no rain fell on the coast.","Did it move overnight?|How did the river move?|Where did the river used to go?","history"],["What happened at Mission Bay in 1974?","On September 25, 1974, Mission Bay hosted the first modern race to be called a triathlon, with 46 athletes.","What happened here in 1974?|Is anything famous from there?|What was the first race there?","famous"]]},"ca26-mount-soledad-and-rose-canyon":{"n":"Mount Soledad and Rose Canyon","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Mount Soledad?","Mount Soledad is 823 feet high. On top there's a 29-foot cross and some TV towers.","How tall is it?|How high is that hill, is it a big mountain?|How high up?","size"],["What is the big cross on Mount Soledad?","It's a 29-foot cross on the summit, dedicated in 1954. It was fought over in court for about 25 years as a religious symbol on public land, until a private memorial association bought the ground in 2015.","What's that cross on top of it?|Why is there a giant cross on the hill?|What's the cross for?","history"],["Is there an earthquake fault under the I-5 here?","Yes, the Rose Canyon Fault runs north from downtown San Diego beneath this freeway. Scientists estimate it produces a magnitude 6.9 quake about every 700 years on average.","Is that fault dangerous?|Can the ground really shake here, like a big earthquake?|Where's the fault?","nature"],["Who was Rose Canyon named after?","Rose Canyon honors Louis Rose, a German Jewish immigrant who arrived in 1850 and was San Diego's first known Jewish resident. In 1853 he bought 650 acres there for a ranch and a tannery.","Who was Rose, the guy it's named for?|Who was the Rose guy?|Why's it called Rose Canyon?","people"],["Who lived in Rose Canyon before the Spanish came?","The Kumeyaay village of Jamo stood on Rose Creek at the mouth of the canyon before the Spanish came.","Did anyone live there before that?|Did Native Americans live in the canyon?|Who lived here first?","history"],["Did anyone famous live on Mount Soledad?","Yes, Dr. Seuss spent his last years in a house on the mountain.","Did a famous person live up there?|Did somebody famous live on that hill?|Who lived there?","famous"]]},"ca28-san-diego-california-temple":{"n":"San Diego California Temple","k":"p","q":[["How old is the San Diego California Temple?","It was dedicated in 1993. It was the church's third temple in California.","How old is it?|Mom, when did that big white building get built?|How old?","age"],["How tall are the spires on the San Diego California Temple?","The spires rise about 200 feet. A gold-leaf statue of the angel Moroni stands on top of the eastern one.","How tall are they?|Are those towers really tall?|How high?","size"],["Who designed the San Diego California Temple?","Architect William S. Lewis designed it with Dennis and Shelly Hyndman, a Roman Catholic couple who had never been inside a Latter-day Saint temple.","Who designed it?|Who made that building?|Who drew it?","people"],["How many spires does the San Diego California Temple have?","Ten in all, counting the four small spires at the base of each tower, plus the two tall ones.","How many spires does it have?|Why does that building have so many pointy towers?|How many towers?","see"],["What is the San Diego California Temple made of?","The white exterior is plaster mixed with marble chips. The designers avoided Southern California staples like stucco, tile and arches.","What is it made of?|Why is that building so white?|What's the white stuff?","other"],["What happened at the San Diego California Temple in 1993?","About 720,000 people toured it during its open house, well over the 600,000 expected.","What happened here in 1993?|Did lots of people visit it?|Did people go inside?","history"]]},"ca33-torrey-pines-and-pe-asquitos-lagoon":{"n":"Torrey Pines and Peñasquitos Lagoon","k":"p","q":[["What is special about the Torrey pines?","Torrey pines are the rarest pine in North America. They grow wild only here and on a small strip just north in Del Mar.","Why is it so special?|Why are those trees such a big deal?|What's rare here?","famous"],["What does Peñasquitos Lagoon mean in Spanish?","Peñasquitos is Spanish for the little cliffs. The lagoon spreads about 500 acres of marsh beside the bluffs where the Torrey pines grow.","What does it mean?|What does the name mean, Mom?|Why is it called that?","name"],["Why do the Torrey pines look so twisted?","Sea winds twist the exposed trees into shapes like giant bonsai.","Why do they look like that?|Why are the trees all bent and funny?|Why so twisty?","nature"],["How did Torrey Pines become a protected reserve?","It began in 1899, when George Marston persuaded the city to set aside 364 acres. Later, newspaperwoman Ellen Browning Scripps bought more land and gave it to the city.","How did it get protected?|Who saved the trees?|Who started it?","history"],["Can you see whales at Torrey Pines?","Yes, from the cliffs you can see gray and humpback whales during their migrations.","Are there whales there?|Can we see whales from it?|Any whales?","see"],["How big is Torrey Pines State Natural Reserve?","The reserve covers 2,000 acres of cliffs and trails, and a mature tree's roots can spread 246 feet.","How big is it?|Is that place huge?|How much land is it?","size"]]},"ca36-del-mar-racetrack":{"n":"Del Mar racetrack","k":"p","q":[["Who started the Del Mar racetrack?","Singer Bing Crosby and businessman William Quigley founded the track. Crosby even stood at the main gate on opening day to welcome the crowd.","Who started it?|Who made this place, Mom?|Who built it?","people"],["When did the Del Mar racetrack open?","It opened on July 3, 1937. About 15,000 fans came that first day, and Bing Crosby greeted them at the main gate.","How old is it?|How long has this place been around?|When'd it open?","age"],["What is the Del Mar racetrack famous for?","It hosted the 1938 match race between Seabiscuit and Ligaroti. It was the first thoroughbred race NBC radio broadcast nationwide, and Seabiscuit won by a nose.","What happened there that was famous?|Did anything cool happen at the horse place?|Why's it famous?","famous"],["Are there still horse races at the Del Mar racetrack?","Yes. Live racing runs from July into early September, with a second meet in November. The grounds also host the San Diego County Fair.","Do they still race there?|Can we watch horses run there?|Is it still open?","today"],["How big is the Del Mar racetrack?","The grounds cover 370 acres, and they also hold the San Diego County Fair.","How big is it?|Is that place huge?|Is it big?","size"],["What was here before this part of the freeway?","A Navy airfield was built here in the 1920s, and during World War II Navy blimps flew patrols from it. The state closed the airport in 1959 and used its land for Interstate 5.","What used to be here?|Was there something else here, Dad?|What was it before?","history"]]},"ca39-san-elijo-lagoon":{"n":"San Elijo Lagoon","k":"p","q":[["How big is San Elijo Lagoon?","San Elijo Lagoon is a 915-acre reserve, which is where Escondido Creek meets the sea.","How big is it?|Is that lagoon really huge, like a giant lake?|How much land is it?","size"],["Who lived at San Elijo Lagoon long ago?","It is Kumeyaay land, and people gathered food along this shore for at least 8,500 years. Their shell heaps hold scallops, clams, shark and abalone.","Who lived here before?|Did Native people live there way back?|Whose land was it?","people"],["What lives at San Elijo Lagoon?","The reserve counts about 300 kinds of birds and more than 300 kinds of plants.","What animals and plants are in it?|Are there lots of birds there?|What can you see there, nature-wise?","nature"]]},"ca41-encinitas-the-little-oaks":{"n":"Encinitas, the little oaks","k":"p","q":[["Who lived in Encinitas before it was a city?","People from the Kumeyaay village of Jeyal, near San Elijo Lagoon. They met the Portolá expedition in 1769, which then named this valley Los Encinos.","Who was here first?|Were there people here before the oaks got a name?|Who met the explorers?","people"],["When did Encinitas become a city?","Today's city formed in 1986 from five communities, including Cardiff-by-the-Sea, Leucadia and Olivenhain. A Mexican land grant, Rancho Las Encinitas, came way earlier, in 1842.","How old is it?|How long has this been a city, mom?|When was it founded?","age"],["What is Encinitas famous for?","Its surf break, Swami's, named for Paramahansa Yogananda, whose temple has been a landmark since the 1930s. The break even gets a mention in the Beach Boys song Surfin' U.S.A.","What's it famous for?|Is there anything cool here for surfers?|Why do people know it?","famous"],["What's the Cardiff Kook in Encinitas?","A 16-foot bronze surfer statue in Cardiff. Local surfers decided it had terrible form, so they named it the Cardiff Kook, surfer slang for a wannabe. Pranksters keep dressing it up.","What is that statue, the surfer one?|Is there a funny statue here?|Who's the Kook?","kids"]]},"ca43-ecke-poinsettia-ranch":{"n":"Ecke poinsettia ranch","k":"p","q":[["How did the Ecke family start growing poinsettias?","Albert Ecke, a German immigrant, began growing poinsettias in Los Angeles in 1909 and sold them from street stands. The family moved the business to Encinitas in 1923.","How did they start growing it?|Who started the poinsettia thing, mom?|Who began the ranch?","history"],["What made the Ecke poinsettias so bushy and full?","Grafting two varieties together gave fuller, bushier plants. Albert's son Paul worked out the grafting technique.","How did they make it so bushy?|How come the flowers looked so big, dad?|What's the grafting trick?","famous"],["How did poinsettias become a Christmas flower?","Paul Ecke Jr. sent poinsettias to TV stations to display from Thanksgiving to Christmas. He also promoted them on The Tonight Show and Bob Hope's Christmas specials.","How did it get tied to Christmas?|Why do we see poinsettias at Christmas, mom?|Who made it a Christmas thing?","people"],["How big was the Ecke poinsettia business?","Until the 1990s the Eckes had a virtual monopoly on the Christmas flower. As late as 2008 the family business still served about half the world market and 70 percent of the American one.","How much of the market did it have?|Was it a really big deal, dad?|How many poinsettias did they sell?","size"],["Where do poinsettias grow in the wild?","Wild poinsettias grow from Mexico to Guatemala. The Aztecs called the plant cuetlaxochitl.","Where is it from originally?|Where do poinsettias come from, mom?|What did the Aztecs call it?","nature"]]},"ca44-batiquitos-lagoon":{"n":"Batiquitos Lagoon","k":"p","q":[["How big is Batiquitos Lagoon?","Batiquitos Lagoon covers 610 acres of tidal wetland, right where San Marcos Creek meets the sea.","How big is it?|Is that lagoon huge, Mom?|How much space is it?","size"],["Why is it called Batiquitos Lagoon?","The name comes from batequi, a northwestern Mexican word for a watering hole dug in a streambed. It was borrowed from the Yaqui language.","Why is it called that?|What does Batiquitos even mean?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["How long have people lived around Batiquitos Lagoon?","People have lived around this lagoon for more than 9,000 years.","How long have people lived there?|Did people live here a really long time ago?|Who lived there first?","people"],["What happened to Batiquitos Lagoon over the years?","A century of roads and building blocked the tides, and the lagoon silted up with only five kinds of fish left. A restoration that began in 1997 reopened it to the sea.","What happened to it?|Why did the lagoon get sick?|Did it get fixed?","history"],["Can we walk at Batiquitos Lagoon?","Yes, a 1.75-mile nature trail follows the north shore. It is friendly to strollers and wheelchairs.","Can we walk there?|Is there a trail we can do with the stroller?|Can we hike it?","visit"]]},"ca47-the-flower-fields":{"n":"The Flower Fields","k":"p","q":[["What are The Flower Fields?","They are 55 acres of Giant Tecolote ranunculus on a hillside. The flowers bloom for six to eight weeks, from early March into early May.","What is it with all those flowers on the hill?|Why is the whole hill covered in flowers?|What's that flower hill?","famous"],["Who owns The Flower Fields and who grows the flowers?","The Ecke family of poinsettia fame owns the land, and Mellano and Company grows the flowers. The main crop is ranunculus bulbs.","Who owns it?|Who plants all the flowers, mom?|Who runs this place?","people"],["How did ranunculus start growing at The Flower Fields?","Horticulturist Luther Gage brought ranunculus to the area in the 1920s. Decades later, grower Edwin Frazee moved his ranunculus business to these coastal hillsides.","How did it all start?|Who first planted flowers here?|Who started the flowers?","history"]]},"ca48-carlsbad-strawberry-company":{"n":"Carlsbad Strawberry Company","k":"p","q":[["Who runs the Carlsbad Strawberry Company?","It's run by third-generation farmer Jimmy Ukegawa. It's a pick-your-own farm, so visitors can pick their own berries.","Who runs it?|Who owns the strawberry farm, Mom?|Who's the farmer?","people"],["When is strawberry picking season at the Carlsbad Strawberry Company?","Picking season usually runs from March through July. Seedlings go in each October, and the first berries come around the start of the year.","When can you pick them?|When can we pick strawberries, Dad?|What months?","visit"],["How did the Carlsbad Strawberry Company get started with pick-your-own?","In the late 1990s the family's farm was struggling with rising costs. In the early 2000s Jimmy began opening the fields for pick-your-own days.","How did it start letting people pick?|Why did the farm let people pick berries?|Why pick-your-own?","history"],["What kind of strawberries grow at the Carlsbad Strawberry Company?","The farm grows a single variety, the Albion strawberry. It's one of San Diego's most popular pick-your-own farms.","What kind of berries are they?|What berries do they grow, Mom?|What variety?","nature"],["Does the Carlsbad Strawberry Company have anything besides strawberries?","Yes, in fall it has a pumpkin patch that can draw up to 1,500 people a night.","Is there more than berries there?|Do they have pumpkins, Dad?|Anything fun in fall?","kids"]]},"ca49-agua-hedionda-and-the-desal-plant":{"n":"Agua Hedionda and the desal plant","k":"p","q":[["What does the Carlsbad desalination plant do?","It draws in about 100 million gallons of seawater a day and pushes it through reverse osmosis filters, turning half into drinking water.","What does it do?|How do they make drinking water from the ocean?|What's that big plant?","famous"],["How much of San Diego's water comes from the desal plant?","About 7 percent of what the San Diego region uses. It opened in 2015 as the largest desalination plant in the Western Hemisphere.","How much of our water comes from it?|Is that plant a big deal for drinking water?|How much water is that?","size"],["How long did it take to build the Carlsbad desal plant?","Nearly 14 years to permit, design and build. It cost about $1 billion with its pipelines and survived five lawsuits.","How long did it take to build it?|Was it hard to build, Mom?|Was it expensive?","history"],["Who is the Carlsbad desalination plant named after?","It's named for Bud Lewis, Carlsbad's mayor for almost 25 years.","Who is it named after?|Who's Claude Bud Lewis?|Who's the plant guy?","people"],["What happened to the big smokestack at Agua Hedionda?","The Encina Power Station, with its 400-foot smokestack, shut down in 2018 and was torn down between 2020 and 2022.","What happened to it?|Where did the giant chimney go?|Is the power plant still there?","history"]]},"ca50-carlsbad-s-mineral-water":{"n":"Carlsbad's mineral water","k":"p","q":[["Why is Carlsbad called Carlsbad?","It's named for a spa town in Bohemia. Tests on a well here found the water chemically similar to that of some of the world's most famous spas, so the town took the name of the Bohemian spa of Karlsbad.","Why is it called that?|Why's this town named Carlsbad, anyway?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["Who dug the first well in Carlsbad?","A former sailor named John Frazier dug a well here in the 1880s. He offered its water at the train stop, which became known as Frazier's Station.","Who started it?|Who found the water, a pirate?|Who dug it?","people"],["What happened to Carlsbad when land prices fell?","Land prices fell across the county in 1887. Farms of citrus, avocados and olives kept the town going.","Did the town survive that?|Did the town go broke?|What saved it?","history"],["Can you still see Carlsbad's first well?","You can still see the site of Frazier's first well at Alt Karlsbad, a copy of a German Hanseatic house on Carlsbad Boulevard.","Can we visit it?|Can we see the old well?|Is the first well still there?","see"],["When did Carlsbad become a city?","Carlsbad became a city in 1952, to avoid being annexed by Oceanside.","How old is it as a city?|When did it become official?|What year was that?","age"],["Is there a volcano near Carlsbad?","Yes, inland there's a 513-foot extinct volcano called Mount Calavera. It stands in a nature preserve.","Is there one around here?|Is there a real volcano nearby?|Any volcanoes?","nature"]]},"ca51-buena-vista-lagoon":{"n":"Buena Vista Lagoon","k":"p","q":[["How big is Buena Vista Lagoon?","Buena Vista Lagoon covers 223 acres. It sits on the border between Carlsbad and Oceanside.","How big is it?|Is that lagoon huge?|How large?","size"],["What is Buena Vista Lagoon famous for?","It's California's first ecological reserve. It's also a freshwater lagoon, unlike its neighbors, because a weir holds back the sea.","What's it famous for?|Why is that lagoon special, like, what's the big deal?|Why's it special?","famous"],["Did pink flamingos really show up at Buena Vista Lagoon?","Yes, in the 1970s a flock of pink flamingos turned up. Locals thought they escaped from the San Diego Zoo, but the zoo insisted it was missing none.","Were there really flamingos at that lagoon?|Were there pink birds there?|Flamingos?","history"],["Besides birds, what animals live at Buena Vista Lagoon?","The lagoon is home to 18 kinds of mammals and 14 kinds of reptiles and amphibians, besides all the birds.","What else lives in it?|Are there other animals there, not just birds?|Any other animals?","nature"],["Is there a nature center at Buena Vista Lagoon?","Yes, the Buena Vista Audubon Society runs a nature center on the lagoon's shore.","Can we visit a nature center at it?|Is there something to see at the lagoon besides water?|Nature center there?","visit"]]},"ca54-oceanside-and-its-pier":{"n":"Oceanside and its pier","k":"p","q":[["How long is the Oceanside Pier?","The Oceanside pier is 1,942 feet long. That makes it the second-longest wooden pier on the West Coast.","How long is it?|How big is that pier, like how many football fields?|How long's the pier?","size"],["How old is the Oceanside Pier?","The first pier went up in 1888, the year the city incorporated. Today's pier is the sixth one, and it opened in 1987.","How old is it?|Has that pier been there forever?|How old's the pier?","age"],["Why has the Oceanside Pier been rebuilt so many times?","Storms have wrecked the pier again and again. The one standing today is the sixth, and it opened in 1987.","Why did it keep getting rebuilt?|Why does the pier keep breaking?|Pier keeps getting wrecked?","history"],["What happened to the diner at the end of the Oceanside Pier?","A fire destroyed the old diner at the far end of the pier in April 2024. Rebuilding was expected to begin in 2027.","What happened to it?|Where did the diner go? I'm hungry!|Pier diner burned?","history"],["Who lived in the Oceanside area before the town was founded?","The city stands on Payómkawichum land, where the village of Wiyóoya stood at the mouth of the San Luis Rey River. Andrew Jackson Myers settled the original townsite in 1882.","Who lived there first?|Were there people there before the town?|Who was here first?","people"],["What is Cafe 101 in Oceanside famous for?","Cafe 101 has been a fixture since 1928 and is often called the oldest cafe on the historic Highway 101 route. Greyhound buses once stopped out front.","What's that cafe known for?|Is there an old diner we can see?|Why is Cafe 101 special?","famous"]]},"ca53-mission-san-luis-rey":{"n":"Mission San Luis Rey","k":"p","q":[["When was Mission San Luis Rey founded?","Mission San Luis Rey was founded in 1798. It is California mission number 18 in founding order.","How old is it?|How long ago did they start this place, like way back?|When'd it start?","age"],["What is special about the church at Mission San Luis Rey?","The church from 1815 is the only surviving mission church built in the shape of a cross.","What's special about that church?|Is the church shaped like something cool?|What's the church like?","famous"],["Who was Pablo Tac from Mission San Luis Rey?","Pablo Tac was born at the mission around 1822. A priest took him to Rome as a boy, and he wrote down the Luiseño language and the story of his people.","Who was he, the guy born at the mission?|Who's Pablo Tac, did he do something cool?|Pablo who?","people"],["Whose homeland is Mission San Luis Rey in?","It lies in the homeland of the Payómkawichum, the people the Spanish named Luiseño. Six federally recognized Luiseño tribes have reservations in Southern California today.","Who lived here before the mission?|Who were the first people here?|Whose land is it?","history"]],"l":"mission-san-luis-rey"},"ca55-camp-pendleton":{"n":"Camp Pendleton","k":"p","q":[["How big is Camp Pendleton?","It covers more than 125,000 acres and has 17 miles of coastline.","How big is it?|Is that base really huge?|How much land is that?","size"],["Who was Camp Pendleton named after?","It is named for Major General Joseph Pendleton, who long pushed for a West Coast Marine base.","Who is it named for?|Who's Pendleton, anyway?|Whose name is that?","name"],["What was Camp Pendleton before it was a Marine base?","It was Rancho Santa Margarita y Las Flores until 1942, when the Marines turned it into a World War II training base. Before that it was a cattle ranch.","What was it before that?|Was it always a base, Mom?|What was here before?","history"],["What wildlife lives on Camp Pendleton?","Its wild lands still shelter rare species, among them western snowy plovers and the Pacific pocket mouse.","What animals live there?|Are there any rare animals on it?|Any animals out there?","nature"],["What happened at Camp Pendleton in 1975?","In 1975 it became the first US military base to take in Vietnamese evacuees, more than 50,000 of them.","What did it do for refugees?|Did something big happen there?|Why is the base famous?","history"]]},"ca57-santa-margarita-river":{"n":"Santa Margarita River","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the Santa Margarita River?","The Portolá expedition camped beside it on July 20, 1769, and named it for Saint Margaret of Antioch.","Why did they name it that?|Why's the river got a girl's name?|Who named this river?","name"],["How long is the Santa Margarita River?","It runs about 31 miles from near Temecula to the sea. Its lower 10 miles spread out into a wide floodplain across Camp Pendleton.","How long is it?|How far does this river go?|Where does the river start and end?","size"],["Was there ever a plan to dam the Santa Margarita River?","Yes. The Navy and the Fallbrook water district fought over its water for decades. In 2018 the district sold its riverside land, ending a dam plan more than 60 years old.","Did they ever try to dam it?|Why isn't there a dam on it?|Did people fight over the water?","history"],["What animals live along the Santa Margarita River?","Endangered least Bell's vireos and southwestern willow flycatchers breed in its streamside habitat. In 2019, Pacific lampreys returned for the first time since 1940.","Are there animals there?|What kind of animals does it have?|Are there any fish or birds in it?","nature"],["What did the railroad have to do with the Santa Margarita River?","In 1881 the California Southern Railroad followed the river up Temecula Canyon, crossing it on 241 bridges. Chinese Americans worked on the line.","Was there a train here once?|Did trains go along it?|Who built things along the river?","history"]]},"ca59-the-surf-line-railroad":{"n":"The Surf Line railroad","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the Surf Line railroad?","It's named the Surf Line because in places the tracks run within 100 feet of the Pacific Ocean.","Why do they call it that?|How come it's named the Surf Line, is it by the ocean?|Why Surf Line?","name"],["When was the Surf Line railroad finished?","The Surf Line was finished from Oceanside to Santa Ana in 1888.","How old is it?|How old is that train track, like super old?|When'd they build it?","age"],["What happened to the first railroad route into San Diego?","Floods wrecked the first route inland. In February 1884, more than 40 inches of rain fell in four weeks and washed out two-thirds of the canyon line.","What happened to the first one, did it break?|Did the old train track get wrecked by water?|What went wrong with the first track?","history"],["What trains run on the Surf Line railroad today?","Amtrak's Pacific Surfliner and the Coaster commuter trains share the tracks, and freight trains use them too, often at night.","Is it still used today?|Are there real trains on it now?|What trains use it?","today"],["What is the Surf Line railroad famous for?","Today it is the second-busiest passenger rail corridor in the United States. It also became the only line into San Diego from the north.","Is it a big deal?|Why is that train line important?|What's special about it?","famous"],["What kind of freight does the Surf Line railroad carry?","Freight trains, often running at night, haul loads that include military vehicles for Camp Pendleton.","What do the freight trains haul on it?|What's on the cargo trains, anything cool?|What's in the freight trains?","other"]]},"ca62-las-flores-and-las-pulgas":{"n":"Las Flores and Las Pulgas","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Las Pulgas?","Las Pulgas means the fleas. A mission survey party camped in the canyon and got badly bitten, and the name stuck.","Why is that called the fleas?|Why do they call it fleas, Mom?|Las Pulgas, what's that mean?","name"],["How old is the Las Flores Adobe?","The big U-shaped ranch house was built in 1867 and 1868 by Marco Forster. It's now a National Historic Landmark.","How old is it?|Is that house really old?|How long ago was it built?","age"],["What happened at Las Flores in 1838?","In April 1838, rival Californio forces fought here over who would govern Alta California.","Was there a battle there?|Did people fight there?|What happened there in the old days?","history"],["Who built the original Las Flores station?","Luiseño and Acjachemen people, moved here by the mission, built the station's tile-roofed chapel and hostel around three sides of a square.","Who built it?|Who made that old chapel?|Who did the building, kids?","people"]]},"ca67-san-clemente-border-patrol-checkpoint":{"n":"San Clemente Border Patrol checkpoint","k":"p","q":[["What is the San Clemente Border Patrol checkpoint?","It's a permanent Border Patrol checkpoint, one of 33 fixed checkpoints near the Mexican border. Agents here may briefly stop and question drivers.","What is that checkpoint ahead?|Why is there a police stop on the freeway?|What's that stop for?","other"],["Why is there a checkpoint so far from the border?","These checkpoints sit 25 to 75 miles from the border on major highways. They're a third layer of enforcement, after agents at the line and roving patrols.","Why is it so far from Mexico?|Why are they checking us way up here?|Why here?","history"],["What famous court case started at the San Clemente checkpoint?","United States v. Martinez-Fuerte, decided in 1976. The Supreme Court upheld the power to briefly stop and question drivers here, and the case began with a stop at this checkpoint.","Did something important happen at that checkpoint?|Did a court case start there, Mom?|Was it in a case?","famous"],["Who was Martinez-Fuerte and what happened to him here?","Amado Martinez-Fuerte was stopped here and charged with illegally transporting two passengers who had come in through San Ysidro. His case went to the Supreme Court.","Who got stopped at it?|Who was the guy in the court case?|What did he do?","people"],["Do US citizens need papers at the San Clemente checkpoint?","US citizens need no papers here. But drivers aren't allowed to go on until agents are satisfied.","Do we need papers to go through it?|Do we have to show ID there?|Papers needed?","other"],["How did the Supreme Court rule on the checkpoint stops?","The court ruled 7 to 2 that such stops do not violate the Fourth Amendment. Critics still argue that the checkpoints are unconstitutional.","Is it legal for them to stop us at that checkpoint?|Did the judges say the stops were okay?|Is it legal?","history"]]},"ca71-san-onofre-nuclear-plant":{"n":"San Onofre nuclear plant","k":"p","q":[["Why did the San Onofre nuclear plant shut down?","Its last two reactors were shut down in January 2012, after early wear turned up on more than 3,000 tubes in new steam generators. Southern California Edison retired the plant for good in 2013.","Why did they close it?|Why'd they turn the power plant off, Dad?|Why is it closed?","history"],["What were the two giant domes at San Onofre?","They were the containment buildings around the plant's reactors. The plant was long known for its two giant round domes by the beach.","What are those domes for?|What are those big round things, Mom?|What's with the domes?","see"],["Are the San Onofre domes still standing?","Crews are taking the plant apart, and removal of the domes was set to begin in 2027. So they may be partly or entirely gone.","Is it still standing?|Can we still see the big domes?|Are they gone yet?","today"],["How much power did San Onofre supply?","Until 2012 the plant supplied about a fifth of the power for large parts of Southern California.","How much electricity did it make?|Did it power a lot of houses?|How big a deal was it?","famous"],["What happens to the used nuclear fuel at San Onofre?","It stays on site, sealed in 123 steel canisters inside concrete, until the federal government takes it away. Edison calls that duty decades overdue.","Where did the nuclear fuel go?|Is the fuel still there, Dad?|What about the leftover fuel?","today"]]},"ca71-san-onofre-and-trestles":{"n":"San Onofre and Trestles","k":"p","q":[["Why is San Onofre called San Onofre?","San Onofre is Spanish for Saint Onuphrius, a hermit from fourth-century Egypt.","Why is it called that?|Who was Onofre, anyway?|What's the name mean?","name"],["Why are the surf breaks called Trestles?","They're named for a wooden railroad trestle that surfers walked under to reach the beach. A concrete bridge replaced the wooden one in 2012.","Why is it called that?|What's a trestle, is that a bridge?|Why Trestles?","name"],["What is Trestles famous for?","Lower Trestles hosted the pro surfing world tour for many years, and Trestles is set to host the 2028 Olympic surfing contests.","Why is it famous?|Do real surfers surf there?|What's so special about it?","famous"],["How big is San Onofre State Beach?","It's a 3,000-acre state beach with more than 2.5 million visitors a year, on Marine Corps land leased to California.","Is it a big beach?|How many people go there?|Is it popular?","size"]]},"ca72-nixon-s-western-white-house":{"n":"Nixon's Western White House","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Nixon's Western White House?","The press nicknamed it the Western White House after President Richard Nixon bought the house in 1969. Its real name is La Casa Pacifica, the house of peace.","Why is that called the Western White House?|Why did people call it the Western White House?|Western White House name?","name"],["Who built Nixon's Western White House?","It was built in 1926 for Hamilton Cotton, one of San Clemente's founding financiers. It's a Spanish-style mansion, and Nixon bought it in 1969.","Who built it?|Who made this big house, and when?|Who built the house?","people"],["Who visited Nixon's Western White House?","Soviet leader Leonid Brezhnev and the president of Mexico visited. Earlier, Hamilton Cotton entertained President Franklin Roosevelt there.","Who came to visit it?|Did any famous people come to the house?|Who visited?","famous"],["What did Nixon change at the Western White House?","Some changes were made for the Secret Service, including a 1,500-foot wall around much of the nearly six-acre estate. Pat Nixon also brought a magnolia seedling from Andrew Jackson's tree at the White House.","What did he change about it?|Did Nixon build anything at the house?|What's that big wall for?","history"],["What happened to Nixon's Western White House after he resigned?","After Nixon resigned, he retired here to write his memoirs. The Nixons sold the estate in 1980.","What happened to it after he quit?|Where did Nixon go after he resigned?|Did Nixon live there later?","history"]]},"ca72-panhe-and-los-cristianitos":{"n":"Panhe and Los Cristianitos","k":"p","q":[["What does Panhe mean?","Panhe means \"the place at the water.\" It's the name of one of the largest Acjachemen villages, inland of the freeway in the San Mateo campground area of San Onofre State Beach.","What does it mean?|Why is it called Panhe, Mom?|What's Panhe mean?","name"],["How old is Panhe?","Panhe is more than 8,000 years old. When the Spanish arrived, perhaps 300 people lived there.","How old is it?|Is that place really that old, Dad?|How old is Panhe, like?","age"],["Why is Panhe still important today?","It remains a sacred, ceremonial and burial site for the Acjachemen people. They hold a festival there each year to honor their ancestors.","Is it still important today?|Do people still go there, Dad?|Why does Panhe matter?","today"],["What happened at Los Cristianitos in 1769?","The Portolá expedition camped near San Mateo Creek on July 22, 1769. Missionaries were allowed to baptize two dying girls, and a state plaque marks the first Christian baptism in Alta California.","What happened there?|What did the explorers do there, Mom?|What happened in 1769?","history"],["Why is it called Cristianitos?","Padre Crespí called the place Los Cristianos, and the name later became Cristianitos, which means \"the little Christians.\" It's now the name of the creek, the canyon and the Cristianitos Road exit.","Why is it called that?|What does Cristianitos mean, Dad?|Cristianitos, what's it mean?","name"]]},"ca76-spanish-village-by-the-sea":{"n":"Spanish Village by the Sea","k":"p","q":[["Who founded Spanish Village by the Sea?","Ole Hanson, a former mayor of Seattle, laid out the town in 1925 on old rancho land by the ocean.","Who started it?|Who built this town, anyway?|Who made it?","people"],["What happened to Spanish Village by the Sea in the Great Depression?","Hanson lost his house and all his other holdings, and the town's population fell 70 percent, to about 250 people.","What happened to it back then?|Did the town get in trouble, like, a long time ago?|Bad times for it?","history"],["How big was Spanish Village by the Sea supposed to be?","Hanson laid out a 2,000-acre town. He pictured it as a clean canvas five miles long and a mile and a half wide.","How big is it?|How large is the town, like, really big?|Is it big?","size"],["What is Casa Romantica in Spanish Village by the Sea?","It was Hanson's mansion on the bluff. Today it's a city-owned cultural center above the pier.","What's that mansion on the bluff?|Who lives in the big house on the cliff?|What is Casa Romantica?","see"],["What is special about Clarence Lobo Elementary School?","Opened in 1994, it was the first school in California named for an Indigenous leader. Clarence Lobo was chief of the Acjachemen people from 1946 to 1985.","Why is that school special?|Who was Clarence Lobo?|What's the famous school here?","famous"]]},"ca79-dana-point-and-the-hides":{"n":"Dana Point and the hides","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Dana Point?","It's named for Richard Henry Dana Jr., who sailed here in 1835 on the brig Pilgrim and wrote about it in Two Years Before the Mast.","Why is it named that?|Who is Dana, and why does the point have his name?|Whose name is that?","name"],["What were the hides at Dana Point?","Cowhides from Mission San Juan Capistrano were brought to the cliffs, and sailors flung them out to the beach below. Dana compared the job to flying a kite without a string.","What was that hide thing about?|Why were they throwing cow skins off a cliff?|What are the hides?","history"],["What did Richard Henry Dana think of Dana Point?","Dana called this stretch the only romantic spot on the coast.","What did he say about it?|Did the guy it's named after like it here?|Was it nice back then?","people"],["What is Dana Point famous for?","Hobie Alter opened what is called the world's first retail surf shop there in 1954. It also holds the trademarked title Dolphin and Whale Watching Capital of the World.","What's it known for?|Why do people go there?|What's special about it?","famous"],["What is the Festival of Whales in Dana Point?","Dana Point has held a Festival of Whales since the early 1970s, over two weekends each March.","When is that festival?|Is there a whale thing there?|Do they have a whale party?","see"]]},"ca82-mission-san-juan-capistrano":{"n":"Mission San Juan Capistrano","k":"p","q":[["How old is Mission San Juan Capistrano?","It was founded in 1776. It is California mission number 7 in founding order.","How old is it?|How long ago did they build this mission, like, a really long time?|When was it founded?","age"],["What is Mission San Juan Capistrano famous for?","It is famous for its swallows and for the ruins of the Great Stone Church, which fell in an earthquake in 1812.","What is it famous for?|Why do people come to this place, is it the birds?|What's it known for?","famous"],["What happened to the Great Stone Church at the mission?","It collapsed on December 8, 1812, during Mass, and about 40 people died. It needed the labor of the whole Native community to build.","What happened to that church?|Why is the big church broken, did it fall down?|Church ruins story?","history"],["Whose land is Mission San Juan Capistrano on?","It stands on Acjachemen homeland. Acjachemen families still live in this region and keep their language and traditions alive.","Who lived here before it?|Who were the first people at this place?|Who lived here first?","people"]],"l":"mission-san-juan-capistrano"},"ca83-putuidem-village":{"n":"Putuidem village","k":"p","q":[["What was Putuidem village?","Putuidem was a mother village of the Acjachemen people, one that gave rise to other villages. It stood just off the freeway, about a mile north of the mission.","What was it?|What was that village, Mom?|Who lived there?","history"],["How did the Acjachemen people get their name?","A village story says the chief's daughter Coronne died suddenly and her body became a mound of earth. The grieving people slept in a pyramid shape called acjachema, which gave the Acjachemen their name.","Where did it come from?|Why are they called that?|Where's Acjachemen from?","people"],["What happened to Putuidem village when the mission was built?","The mission was built right beside it in 1776, and the village was drained of people to serve the mission.","What happened to it?|Where did everybody go?|Did people leave?","history"],["What is at the Putuidem village site today?","Much of the site lies under the sports fields and performing arts center of JSerra Catholic High School. In late 2021 a park north of the school opened to commemorate the village.","What's there now?|Is anything there now?|Can you visit it?","today"]]},"ca83-trabuco-creek-s-lost-gun":{"n":"Trabuco Creek's lost gun","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Trabuco Creek?","Trabuco is Spanish for blunderbuss, a kind of old shotgun. The story goes a soldier lost his blunderbuss here, and the creek was named for it.","Why is that creek named that?|Why's the creek called Trabuco, what's it mean?|What does Trabuco mean?","name"],["What's the story of the lost gun at Trabuco Creek?","In July 1769 a soldier with the Portolá expedition, camped on a bluff above the creek, found his blunderbuss missing. It was never found, and the creek was named for it.","What happened to the gun there?|Did someone really lose a gun by the creek?|Who lost the gun?","history"],["How long is Trabuco Creek and where does it go?","Arroyo Trabuco is a 22-mile creek. It runs out of the Santa Ana Mountains to meet San Juan Creek beside the mission.","How long is that creek?|How big is the creek, where does it start?|Where does it end up?","size"],["Who lived along Trabuco Creek before the Spanish?","Acjachemen and Payómkawichum people lived along the creek for some 8,000 years before the Spanish came.","Who lived by it long ago?|Were there Native Americans at the creek?|Who was here first?","people"],["What wildlife lived in Trabuco Creek?","In its natural state it had one of the most significant steelhead trout runs in Orange County, and steelhead were sighted again in 2003 and 2005. The last California grizzly in the Santa Ana Mountains was shot near Trabuco Canyon.","Were there fish in it?|Any animals in the creek, like bears?|What lives there?","nature"],["What does Trabuco Creek look like under the freeway?","Under the freeway the creek squeezes through twin culverts, then drops into a small canyon toward San Juan Capistrano.","What happens to it under the road?|Does the creek go under the freeway?|Where does the water go here?","see"]]},"ca86-rancho-mission-viejo":{"n":"Rancho Mission Viejo","k":"p","q":[["Who owned Rancho Mission Viejo originally?","Don Juan Forster put the ranch together in the 1840s. He was the brother-in-law of Governor Pío Pico, and at one point he held about 200,000 acres.","Who started it?|Who first put that ranch together?|Who had it first?","people"],["Who was here before the ranch at Rancho Mission Viejo?","Before the ranch, the land held the Acjachemen village of Piwiva.","Did anyone live on that land before the ranch?|Did Native Americans live there?|Who was here first?","history"],["How big is Rancho Mission Viejo?","It is still about 23,000 acres. It was once much bigger: Don Juan Forster held about 200,000 acres at one point.","How much land is it?|Is that ranch huge?|How many acres?","size"],["Is Rancho Mission Viejo still a working ranch today?","Yes. The O'Neill family descendants still run it as a working ranch, with cattle, orchards and a nature reserve.","Do they still farm it?|Are there real cows there?|Is it still a ranch?","today"],["What grows at Rancho Mission Viejo?","The old orange groves gave way to lemons and avocados. More than 9,000 avocado trees and 63,000 lemon trees are harvested year-round.","What do they grow there?|Are there orange trees?|What fruit comes from that ranch?","nature"],["What happened to the ranch land over time at Rancho Mission Viejo?","In the early 1940s the San Diego County part went to the military to expand Camp Pendleton. In 1963 the family and its partners built the planned community of Mission Viejo on ranch land.","Did that land get sold off?|Where did all the ranch land go?|Is the town of Mission Viejo from it?","history"]]},"ca89-saddleback":{"n":"Saddleback","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Saddleback's highest peak?","Its higher summit, Santiago Peak, rises 5,689 feet. That makes it the highest point in Orange County.","How high is it?|How big is the mountain, is it huge?|How tall?","size"],["Who is Modjeska Peak named after?","Helena Modjeska, a Polish stage star who lived in a canyon at its foot in her later years. She is regarded as the greatest actress in the history of Polish theater.","Who was she?|Who's that peak named for?|Who was Modjeska?","people"],["What are all those antennas on Saddleback?","The crown of antennas on Santiago Peak sends radio signals across much of five counties.","What's it used for today?|Why does the mountain have antennas on top?|What are the antennas for?","today"],["Does it ever snow on Saddleback?","Yes. During most winters, snow falls on Santiago Peak at least once.","Does it get snow?|Can you see snow on the mountain?|Snow there?","nature"],["What happened to Saddleback in the 2024 fire?","In September 2024 the Airport Fire burned about 24,000 acres and crossed the ridge of Santiago Peak, damaging some of its radio equipment.","Did it burn?|Did the mountain catch on fire?|What was the Airport Fire?","history"]]},"ca94-el-toro-y":{"n":"El Toro Y","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the El Toro Y?","It's named for the old town of El Toro, which is Spanish for the bull, and for the Marine air station next door. The town later renamed itself Lake Forest in 1991.","Why is it named that?|Why's it called El Toro, kid-style, like what does the name mean?|El Toro name?","name"],["What is the El Toro Y?","It's where Interstate 5 and Interstate 405 meet. The interchange is at exit 94.","What is it exactly?|What's that big junction, kids want to know?|El Toro Y what?","famous"],["How busy did the El Toro Y get?","From 1975 to 2002, traffic here more than tripled, to 356,000 vehicles a day. By the early 1990s it was one of the most congested interchanges in the world.","How much traffic does it get?|How many cars go through there, a kid might ask?|Super busy?","size"],["How wide is the El Toro Y?","After a widening finished in 1997, the road here was 26 lanes wide.","How many lanes is it?|How big is that road, a kid would ask?|Lots of lanes?","size"]]},"ca95-el-toro-marine-air-base":{"n":"El Toro Marine air base","k":"p","q":[["How long did the Marines fly from El Toro?","The Marines flew from El Toro air station from 1942 until it closed in 1999.","How long was that base open?|How many years did the Marines use it?|When did it close?","age"],["What was El Toro before it was a Marine base?","It was part of James Irvine's ranch, and the land held the largest lima bean field in North America. The Marine Corps paid $100,000 for 4,000 acres of it.","What was on that land before the base?|What did they grow there, before the planes?|What was the land used for?","history"],["Who designed the El Toro Marine base patch?","The base's patch, a flying bull, was designed by Walt Disney Studios in 1944.","Who made the flying bull patch?|Did Disney make that bull?|Who drew the bull?","people"],["What happened at El Toro when Nixon resigned?","On the day he resigned in 1974, Richard Nixon flew from Andrews Air Force Base to El Toro.","What did Nixon do at that base?|Did Nixon land there?|Why did Nixon fly to El Toro?","history"],["What is El Toro Marine air base today?","Airport opponents won in 2002. About 1,300 acres became the Orange County Great Park, where the Great Park Balloon rises 400 feet above the surrounding land.","What happened to it after it closed?|Is there anything to see there now?|Did they turn it into an airport?","today"]]},"ca97-the-irvine-ranch":{"n":"The Irvine Ranch","k":"p","q":[["How big was the Irvine Ranch?","It was huge. The Irvine Ranch once stretched 23 miles from the ocean to the Santa Ana River, and by 1918 it grew 60,000 acres of lima beans.","How big was it?|Was that ranch really huge, like how many miles?|How wide was the Irvine Ranch?","size"],["How did the Irvine Ranch get started?","James Irvine and his partners began it in 1864 from old Mexican land grants. It happened after a drought killed a rancher's livestock and forced him to sell.","How did it get started?|Who started the farm, and when?|Who began the Irvine Ranch?","history"],["Why did Irvine's men meet the railroad crews with shotguns?","Southern Pacific crews laid track across the ranch without permission, so Irvine's ranch hands met them with shotguns. He let the rival Santa Fe railway build through instead.","Why did they use shotguns on the railroad workers?|Did cowboys really point guns at train builders?|What happened with the railroad and the ranch?","history"],["How did the Irvine Ranch become the city of Irvine?","In 1961 the Irvine Company sold 990 acres to the University of California for one dollar, and architect William Pereira planned a city around the campus. The new city took the family's name.","How did it turn into a city?|Where did the farm fields go?|Why is the city called Irvine?","history"],["How many people live in Irvine today?","More than 300,000 people live there now. Irvine became a city in 1971.","How many live there now?|Is it a big city now?|When did Irvine become a city?","today"]]},"ca100-jamboree-road":{"n":"Jamboree Road","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Jamboree Road?","It's named for a Boy Scout campout. In 1953 the National Scout Jamboree was held on ranch land nearby, and the muddy trail to it was later paved and renamed Jamboree Road.","Why is it named that?|How did Jamboree Road get its name, mom?|Why Jamboree?","name"],["What happened at the 1953 Jamboree?","Thousands of tents went up on ranch land, and between 45,000 and 50,000 Scouts came, by different counts. It was the first national jamboree held west of the Mississippi.","What happened there in 1953?|Were there really a lot of kids camping here?|What was the Jamboree?","history"],["Where was the Jamboree held, exactly?","On Irvine Company ranch land where Newport Beach's Fashion Island now stands. Back then it was reached only by a muddy two-lane trail.","Where did the Scouts camp?|Where did all those tents go?|What's there now?","history"],["Did anyone famous come to the Jamboree?","Yes, Bob Hope was the master of ceremonies. He joked that with Scouts from so many other countries, they were like the United Nations in short pants.","Was Bob Hope really there?|Who was at it?|Did a celebrity show up?","famous"],["How did the Scouts get to the Jamboree?","Some came by rail. The Irvine Company helped build a road from a stop on the Santa Fe's San Diegan train, so Scouts could ride in.","How did they get there back then?|Did they come by train?|How'd all those kids arrive?","history"],["How long is Jamboree Road today?","Jamboree Road runs 15.8 miles, from the edge of Balboa Island to the hills of Orange.","How long does it go?|Is it a really long road?|Where does it run?","size"]]},"ca101-tustin-s-giant-blimp-hangar":{"n":"Tustin's giant blimp hangar","k":"p","q":[["Why did the Navy build the Tustin blimp hangar?","The Navy built two giant wooden hangars during World War II for blimps that patrolled the coast. The south hangar is the one still standing today.","Why did they build it?|Why are there giant barns for blimps, Dad?|What was it for?","history"],["How big is the Tustin blimp hangar?","The surviving south hangar is more than 1,000 feet long and 17 stories tall. It's one of the largest wooden structures ever built.","How big is it?|Is it really as tall as 17 floors?|How huge is that thing?","size"],["What is the Tustin blimp hangar made of?","It's built of Douglas fir from Oregon. In 1993 the American Society of Civil Engineers named the hangars a historic civil engineering landmark.","What is it made out of?|Is it really made of wood?|What kind of wood is it?","history"],["What happened to the other Tustin blimp hangar?","The north hangar burned in a fire on November 7, 2023. Firefighters let it burn because its roof could collapse, and crews began tearing down what was left that December.","What happened to its twin?|Did one of the hangars burn down?|Where did the other one go?","history"],["Is the Tustin blimp hangar still used today?","Yes. The south hangar is still used to store and service Goodyear blimps, which come back every year for maintenance and inspections.","Do they still use it?|Are there real blimps in there now?|Is anything inside it?","today"],["Has the Tustin blimp hangar been in any movies?","Yes. The hangars appeared in movies and shows including The Hindenburg, The X-Files, Pearl Harbor and Star Trek.","Was it in any movies?|Have I seen it in a show?|Is it famous from TV?","famous"]]},"ca103-orange-county-s-orange-groves":{"n":"Orange County's orange groves","k":"p","q":[["How many orange trees did Orange County's groves once have?","By 1948 Orange County had 67,263 acres of Valencia oranges, with more than five million trees.","How many trees were in those groves?|Were there like a million orange trees here?|How many oranges trees were there?","size"],["Why did Orange County's orange groves disappear?","After the war, a housing boom took over. In 1949 alone, nearly 7,000 acres of orange trees disappeared, and more fell each year as housing tracts spread.","What happened to them?|Where did all the orange trees go, mom?|Why are the groves gone?","history"],["Who planted the first commercial Valencia orange grove in Orange County?","R. H. Gilman planted the first commercial grove of spring-ripening Valencias in 1875, on land that is now the Cal State Fullerton campus.","Who started the groves?|Who planted the very first orange trees?|First grove?","people"],["How important were oranges to Orange County's economy?","Very important. By 1936 citrus brought in two-thirds of the county's farm income, and in 1919 perhaps a third of the workers in the city of Orange had citrus-related jobs.","Was it a big business?|Did lots of people work with oranges?|Was citrus a big deal back then?","famous"],["What happened to the packing houses at Orange County's orange groves?","At their peak in the early 1940s, 45 packing houses were running. The last one, Villa Park Orchards, moved to Ventura County in 2006.","Where did the oranges get packed?|Were there any orange factories?|Last packing house?","history"]]},"ca104-santa-ana-zoo-s-50-monkeys":{"n":"Santa Ana Zoo's 50 monkeys","k":"p","q":[["Why does the Santa Ana Zoo have 50 monkeys?","Joseph Prentice gave the city the land in 1949 on the condition that the zoo keep at least 50 monkeys at all times.","Why does it have to keep that many monkeys?|Why are there so many monkeys there?|Why 50 monkeys?","history"],["When did the Santa Ana Zoo open?","The Santa Ana Zoo opened on March 8, 1952, in Prentice Park, right beside the freeway.","How old is it?|How long has the zoo been there, mom?|When did it start?","age"],["What animals can we see at the Santa Ana Zoo?","It keeps more than a dozen kinds of primates and focuses on Central and South American animals and plants. Giant anteaters, rheas and guanacos live in Tierra de las Pampas.","What can we see there?|Are there cool animals at the zoo?|What's inside?","see"],["What's the story behind the Santa Ana Zoo's train?","Its first engine, built in 1954, ran 44 years at Santa's Village in Sky Forest. When that park closed in 1998, the zoo's friends group bought the train and volunteers restored it.","Where did that train come from?|Is the zoo train old?|Tell me about the train","history"],["What's new at the Santa Ana Zoo for monkeys?","River's Edge opened in 2025 as a reworked home for howler monkeys, spider monkeys and tamarins.","Did it get new monkey homes?|What's River's Edge?|Is there a new monkey exhibit?","today"]]},"ca105-santa-ana-the-county-seat":{"n":"Santa Ana, the county seat","k":"p","q":[["How old is Santa Ana?","The modern town of Santa Ana was founded in 1869. The land itself was granted way back in 1810 as a rancho to José Antonio Yorba.","How old is it?|How long has this town been around, Dad?|When was it founded?","age"],["Who was José Antonio Yorba?","He was a sergeant in the Spanish army who was granted Rancho Santiago de Santa Ana in 1810. His rancho covered today's Santa Ana, Orange, Tustin, Irvine and Costa Mesa.","Who got the land that became it?|Who was that guy who owned all the land?|Who's Yorba?","people"],["What's the story behind the Santa Ana winds?","The hot, dry autumn winds are named for Santa Ana Canyon, which they seem to blow out of. City boosters disliked the name and spread a false story that it came from a phrase for devil wind.","Is that where the Santa Ana winds got their name?|Why are the hot winds called that, Mom?|Santa Ana winds?","name"]]},"ca107-santa-ana-river":{"n":"Santa Ana River","k":"p","q":[["How long is the Santa Ana River?","It runs 96 miles from the San Bernardino Mountains to the sea. It's the largest river entirely within Southern California.","How long is it?|How far does that river run, like how many miles?|Is it a big river?","size"],["Why is it called the Santa Ana River?","Soldiers with the Portolá expedition named it Río de Santa Ana in July 1769, probably because they had just celebrated Saint Anne's Day.","Why do they call it that?|Who gave the river its name, and why Santa Ana?|Who named it?","name"],["What did Native people call the Santa Ana River?","The Tongva and Acjachemen name for the river is Wanaawna.","Did it have another name before that?|What's the old name for the river, before the soldiers came?|Native name?","name"],["Did the Santa Ana River ever flood?","Yes. In 1862 it burst its banks and killed 20 to 40 people. In 1938 it flooded Anaheim and Orange under up to 4 feet of water, killing almost 60 people.","Was that river ever dangerous?|Did the river ever get really wild and flood stuff?|Bad floods here?","history"],["What does the Santa Ana River do for people today?","Its water soaks down through sand and gravel in the spreading grounds to refill the aquifer that supplies about half of the county's tap water.","Is that river still useful?|Do people get drinking water from the river?|What's it good for now?","today"]]},"ca107-the-crystal-cathedral":{"n":"The Crystal Cathedral","k":"p","q":[["When was the Crystal Cathedral built?","It was finished in 1980. It was built as the Crystal Cathedral, and today it is called Christ Cathedral.","How old is it?|When did they finish that giant glass church, Mom?|How old?","age"],["Is the Crystal Cathedral really made of crystal?","No. Despite the name, no crystal glass was used. It has 10,000 rectangular panes that are mirrored outside and see-through inside.","Is it actually made of crystal?|Is that glass building real crystal, Dad?|Real crystal?","name"],["What is the Crystal Cathedral famous for?","When it opened, it was described as the largest glass building in the world. It's also known for the Hour of Power broadcasts, which reached about 20 million viewers.","Why is it famous?|Why is that glass church so special, Dad?|What's so big about it?","famous"],["Who started the Crystal Cathedral church?","Robert and Arvella Schuller started the congregation in 1955 at the Orange Drive-In Theatre. Robert preached from the roof of the concession stand while people listened in their cars.","Who built it?|Who made that church, Mom?|Who started it?","people"],["How does the Crystal Cathedral survive earthquakes?","The glass is held on with silicone glue to ride out earthquakes. The building was designed to withstand a magnitude 8.0 quake.","Could it survive an earthquake?|Won't that glass building break, Dad?|Is it earthquake proof?","other"],["Who owns the Crystal Cathedral today?","Since 2019 it has been the cathedral of the Catholic Diocese of Orange. The diocese bought the campus in 2012 and renamed the church Christ Cathedral.","Is it still a church?|Does anyone use that glass church now, Mom?|Who has it now?","today"]]},"ca109-angel-stadium-and-the-big-a":{"n":"Angel Stadium and the Big A","k":"p","q":[["How old is Angel Stadium?","The Los Angeles Angels have played at Angel Stadium since 1966. Only Fenway Park, Wrigley Field and Dodger Stadium are older among major league ballparks still in use.","How old is it?|Did the Angels always play here, Mom?|How long has the stadium been there?","age"],["Why did they move the Big A?","When the stands were enlarged and enclosed for the Rams, the Big A was moved 1,300 feet to the parking lot.","Why isn't it in left field anymore?|Why did they move that sign?|Who moved it, and why?","history"],["Did football teams play at Angel Stadium too?","Yes, the Los Angeles Rams played football at the stadium from 1980 to 1994.","Did anyone else play there?|Did the Rams play in that stadium?|Was there football there?","history"],["What happened to the Angels before they came to Anaheim?","Founded in 1961, the Angels spent four seasons at Dodger Stadium before coming to Anaheim. They were the first major league team to start out in California rather than move there.","Where did the Angels play first?|Where did they play before Anaheim?|Were the Angels always in Anaheim?","history"],["Does the Big A's halo light up?","Yes. For years it blinked after dark when the Angels won. Since at least 2023 it stays lit, shining brighter after a win.","Does it light up at night?|Does the halo ever blink?|Does it glow when they win?","see"]]},"ca110-disneyland":{"n":"Disneyland","k":"p","q":[["What happened on Disneyland's opening day?","About 28,000 people came, many with counterfeit tickets or by climbing the fence. It hit 100 degrees, Main Street's fresh asphalt went soft, and it became known as Black Sunday.","What went wrong on opening day for it?|Was opening day a disaster, Dad?|Opening day story?","history"],["How many people watched Disneyland open on TV?","An estimated 70 million people watched the opening on live television, in a country of 165 million.","Did people see it open on television?|Did lots of people watch it on TV, Dad?|How many watched?","famous"]]},"ca111-anaheim-s-german-vineyards":{"n":"Anaheim's German vineyards","k":"p","q":[["Who founded Anaheim's German vineyards?","Anaheim was founded in 1857 by 50 German families from San Francisco. Most of them were carpenters and craftsmen who had never made wine.","Who started it?|Who built this town, anyway?|Who made it?","people"],["Why is it called Anaheim?","The German settlers named it Annaheim, which means home by the Santa Ana River.","Where did that name come from?|Why does the name sound so weird?|What's the name mean?","name"],["What happened to the Anaheim vineyards?","In 1884 a disease struck the vines, and within a year the wine industry was gone. It is now called Pierce's disease, and walnuts, lemons and oranges replaced the vineyards.","Why aren't there vineyards there anymore?|Where did all the grapes go?|What killed the vines?","history"],["Who planted the vines in Anaheim's German vineyards?","Chinese workers planted hundreds of thousands of vines and dug a 16-mile canal. The colonists had recruited 30 Chinese laborers in San Francisco.","Who did the work there?|Who dug that canal?|Who planted all those grapes?","people"],["Did Anaheim have another name?","Yes. Spanish-speaking neighbors called the settlement Campo Alemán, which means the German Field.","Did anyone call it something else?|What did the Spanish speakers call it?|Any nickname for it?","name"]]},"ca116-knott-s-chicken-and-boysenberries":{"n":"Knott's chicken and boysenberries","k":"p","q":[["How did Knott's Berry Farm get started?","Knott's Berry Farm began in 1923 as a roadside berry stand. It grew from there, and today the park draws more than 4 million visitors a year.","How did it get started?|How did Knott's start, like, way back?|Where'd Knott's come from?","history"],["Why are they called boysenberries?","Walter Knott named them after Rudolph Boysen, who originally grew the big berry. Knott found a few frail vines on Boysen's old farm, nursed them back to health, and sold the berries from 1932.","Why is it called that?|Why do they call them boysenberries, is that a person?|Who's Boysen?","name"],["Who was Rudolph Boysen?","Rudolph Boysen grew the big berry that became the boysenberry. He was also Anaheim's first park superintendent.","Who was he?|Who's the boysenberry guy?|Was Boysen a real person?","people"],["How popular is the Knott's chicken today?","Very popular. The restaurant serves more than 1.5 million chicken dinners each year.","Is it still popular?|Do lots of people eat the chicken, or what?|Still serving chicken?","today"],["What is the Ghost Town at Knott's?","In 1940 Walter Knott began a replica Ghost Town honoring pioneers like his own grandparents, who came to California by covered wagon in 1868.","What is that?|Is there a real ghost town there?|What's the Ghost Town about?","see"]]},"ca118-la-mirada-s-olive-ranch":{"n":"La Mirada's olive ranch","k":"p","q":[["What did they grow on the La Mirada ranch?","McNally surrounded his home, Windermere Ranch, with olive, orange and lemon groves. Later guides also mention walnut groves around town.","What was grown there?|What kind of trees did they have, Buddy?|What grew there?","nature"],["What did McNally make at the La Mirada ranch?","He built a plant to press fine olive oil. He also built a railroad station to ship his oil and fruit across the country.","What did they do with the olives?|Did they make something there, Buddy?|What was made there?","history"],["How did La Mirada grow from a ranch into a city?","The land went to developers, and in the early 1950s La Mirada was promoted as a completely planned city. By 1960 it went from about 100 homes to more than 8,000.","What happened to the ranch land?|Where did all the houses come from, Buddy?|How'd it get so big?","history"],["What is at McNally's old home site in La Mirada today?","McNally's home site is now Neff Park.","What's there now?|Is anything left of the ranch, Buddy?|Where was his house?","today"]]},"ca121-norwalk-and-rancho-los-nietos":{"n":"Norwalk and Rancho Los Nietos","k":"p","q":[["What was Rancho Los Nietos?","It was a huge Spanish land grant given in 1784 to Manuel Nieto, a former soldier. It covered the land between the Santa Ana and Los Angeles rivers, from Mission San Gabriel down to the sea.","What was that ranch?|What's the big old ranch thing here?|Rancho Los Nietos?","history"],["How big was Rancho Los Nietos?","It started at about 300,000 acres, one of the first and largest Spanish land concessions in California. After the mission padres protested, Nieto still kept about 167,000 acres.","How big was it?|Was that ranch really huge?|How many acres was it?","size"],["What cities are on Rancho Los Nietos today?","Norwalk, Downey, Buena Park, Anaheim, Whittier and Long Beach all sit on parts of it today.","What's built on it now?|Which towns are on that old ranch?|Is Anaheim on it?","today"],["How did Norwalk get its name?","The railroad crossed a spot first called the North-walk. Gilbert Sproul surveyed a town site there, and in 1874 the name was recorded as Norwalk.","Why is it called that?|Why's it called Norwalk?|Who named Norwalk?","name"],["How did the railroad help start Norwalk?","The Sproul brothers from Oregon bought 463 acres here in 1869. When the railroad came in 1873, they deeded it 23 acres on the condition that it build a passenger stop.","What did the railroad do here?|Did trains build that town?|Who were the Sproul brothers?","people"],["What was Norwalk known for in 1900?","By 1900 Norwalk was a dairy center with some of Southern California's largest sugar beet farms. Many of the dairy farmers who came in the early 1900s were Dutch.","What did people do here back then?|Were there farms here once?|Was it all cows and farms?","famous"]]},"ca123-santa-fe-springs-gusher":{"n":"Santa Fe Springs gusher","k":"p","q":[["How did Santa Fe Springs get its name?","It's named for mineral springs. Dr. James Fulton turned them into a sulfur spa in the 1870s, and the Santa Fe Railway bought them in 1886.","Why is it called Santa Fe Springs?|Why's the town called springs, mom?|Name origin?","name"],["What happened at the Santa Fe Springs gusher in 1921?","A well called Union-Bell blew in as a 2,500-barrel gusher in 1921. It set off an oil rush.","What happened here in 1921?|Did oil really shoot out of the ground, dad?|What was the gusher?","history"],["How rich was the Santa Fe Springs oil field?","Within a year it was considered one of the richest fields in petroleum history. In the 1920s it produced as much as 345,000 barrels a day.","Was it a big oil field?|Was there lots of oil, mom?|How much oil?","famous"],["How did promoters sell people on Santa Fe Springs oil?","They bused would-be investors to the field, served lunch in circus tents, and told stories of fortunes made in oil. In 1923 the state limited how much stock could be sold in a well.","How did they get investors there?|Did they trick people, dad?|Oil rush sales pitch?","people"],["What famous car was born in Santa Fe Springs?","The Shelby Cobra. In 1962 Carroll Shelby set up shop in Dean Moon's speed shop here and fitted lightweight roadsters with a 260-cubic-inch V8.","Did a famous car start there?|Was something cool built there, mom?|Shelby Cobra?","famous"]]},"ca124-san-gabriel-river-and-rio-hondo":{"n":"San Gabriel River and Rio Hondo","k":"p","q":[["How did the Rio Hondo get started?","The two rivers were once one restless system. After a flood in the late 1860s the San Gabriel cut a new course, and its old bed became the Rio Hondo.","How did it come to be a separate river?|Did the river just split in two, kid style?|Why are there two rivers?","history"],["What is the San Gabriel River named after?","It's named for Mission San Gabriel, which was founded beside the river in 1771.","Who is it named for?|Why is it called San Gabriel?|What's the name from?","name"],["What happened here in the Mexican-American War?","On January 8, 1847, American troops won the Battle of Río San Gabriel on the west bank of what is now the Rio Hondo, near Washington Boulevard.","Was there a battle near it?|Did soldiers fight here, like for real?|Any war history?","history"],["Who lived along the San Gabriel River long ago?","At least 26 Tongva villages stood along the river.","Did people live along it back then?|Were there Native Americans here, kid asking?|Who lived there?","people"],["Is the San Gabriel River still used today?","Yes. Spreading grounds now soak storm water into the ground, and about a third of the water used in southeast Los Angeles County comes from the San Gabriel.","Do people still use it?|Is it a real river or just a ditch, kid asking?|What's it used for?","today"]]},"ca125-downey-apollo-and-mcdonald-s":{"n":"Downey, Apollo and McDonald's","k":"p","q":[["Why is Downey called Downey?","Downey is named for John Gately Downey, the youngest governor California ever had.","Who was it named after?|Who was Downey anyway?|Why's it called that?","name"],["What did Downey build for the Apollo moon missions?","Its big aircraft plant, run by North American Aviation, built all of the Apollo command and service modules. Nine of them carried astronauts to the Moon.","What did they make there for Apollo?|Did they build stuff for the Moon there?|Apollo parts?","famous"],["How old is the Downey McDonald's?","It's the oldest McDonald's, a walk-up stand from 1953. It still has its original golden arches, two tall arches of sheet metal.","How old is that McDonald's?|Is that the oldest McDonald's ever?|Oldest one?","age"],["Who was Speedee at the Downey McDonald's?","Speedee was a hamburger-headed chef who was McDonald's mascot before Ronald McDonald. The Downey stand got a custom sign of him in 1959.","Who's that guy on the sign?|Was Speedee before Ronald McDonald?|Who's Speedee?","people"],["What can we see at the old Downey aircraft plant site?","Part of the site is now the Columbia Memorial Space Center. Out front is BP-12, the first Apollo capsule to fly.","Is there anything to see there now?|Is there a real space capsule?|Anything cool to see?","see"],["Did the Downey McDonald's almost get torn down?","Yes. After the 1994 Northridge earthquake the company closed it and planned to tear it down, but the Downey City Council stopped the demolition. It reopened in 1996.","Was it almost demolished?|Did they almost lose the old McDonald's?|Why is it still there?","history"]]},"ca129-the-citadel":{"n":"The Citadel","k":"p","q":[["How old is The Citadel?","It was built in 1929, so it's not ancient at all. It only looks like an ancient palace. It started out as a tire factory.","How old is it?|Is that wall really ancient, like a real palace?|When was this thing built?","age"],["Why does The Citadel look like an ancient palace?","Its architects modeled it on the castle of the Assyrian king Sargon II, in a style called Exotic Revival.","Why does it look like that?|Why is there a giant castle wall by the freeway?|Who copied what here?","history"],["What was The Citadel before it was an outlet center?","It was the Samson Tire and Rubber Company factory. Tires were made here until 1978.","What was it used for before?|Did they make something in that castle?|What used to be there?","history"],["What is The Citadel used for today?","The old factory front is now the face of the Citadel Outlets, an outlet center, with a hotel beside it.","Is it still a factory?|Can we go shopping in the castle?|What's inside it now?","today"],["What was the land around The Citadel before the factory?","It was ranch land. Its turn toward industry began in 1887, when the Santa Fe Railway built its main line through.","What was here before it?|Was it always city here?|What was the area like way back?","history"]]},"ca133-downtown-los-angeles-skyline":{"n":"Downtown Los Angeles skyline","k":"p","q":[["Which building has the thin spire on the Downtown LA skyline?","That's the Wilshire Grand Center, finished in 2017. Look for the tall downtown tower topped by a thin spire.","What's that tower with the spike on top?|Which one is the pointy building?|Skyline spire?","see"],["How tall is the Wilshire Grand Center?","Counting the spire it stands 1,100 feet. That makes it the tallest building in the United States west of Chicago.","How tall is it, counting the spire?|Is that the biggest building ever?|How high is it?","size"],["Why do so many Los Angeles towers have flat tops?","A 1974 city fire rule required rooftop helipads on tall buildings, so many towers around the Wilshire Grand have flat tops.","Why are they flat on top?|Why don't the buildings have pointy tops?|Why flat roofs?","history"],["What is El Pueblo near downtown Los Angeles?","It's near where the city was founded in 1781. Its Avila Adobe, built in 1818, is the oldest house still standing in the city.","What happened at that old part of the city?|What's the oldest place in LA?|When was LA started?","history"],["What is Olvera Street and how did it start?","A campaign to save the Avila Adobe led to Olvera Street. It opened on Easter Sunday 1930 as a Mexican marketplace.","How did it get started?|What's that old market street?|Olvera Street?","people"],["What is the old County General Hospital building?","It's an Art Deco giant from 1933 nicknamed the Great Stone Mother. It once held 3,000 beds, and its replacement nearby was renamed LA General in 2023.","What's that big old hospital?|Why is it called the Great Stone Mother?|Big stone hospital?","name"]]},"ca134-the-sears-tower":{"n":"The Sears tower","k":"p","q":[["How big is the Sears tower building?","The building has nine stories holding about 11 acres of floor space, and the tower on top rises 226 feet.","How big is it inside?|Is that building really huge?|How much space does it have?","size"],["What was the Sears building used for?","It was a mail-order building that shipped catalog orders to the Rocky Mountain and Pacific Coast states.","What did they do in that building?|What was in there, kid-style: what did people do inside?|What was it for?","history"],["Did workers really roller skate at the Sears building?","Yes! Workers filled orders on roller skates, gliding through the aisles and dropping items onto corkscrew slides that carried them down to waiting trucks and trains.","Did they really skate in it?|Wait, did people skate inside there?|Skating workers?","kids"],["Is the Sears tower still in use today?","The mail-order center closed in 1992 and the ground-floor store closed in 2021 after nearly 94 years. Plans for its future have come and gone since the 1990s.","Is it still open?|Does it still work as a Sears?|Is anyone in it now?","today"],["Who tried to buy the Sears building?","Boxer Oscar De La Hoya, a Boyle Heights native who shopped there as a child with his mother, tried twice to buy and revive the building.","Did someone try to save it?|Who wanted to buy it?|Was there a famous person who wanted it?","people"]]},"ca134-east-los-angeles-interchange":{"n":"East Los Angeles Interchange","k":"p","q":[["Which freeways meet at the East Los Angeles Interchange?","Interstate 5, Interstate 10, U.S. 101 and State Route 60 all meet here, on 135 acres of Boyle Heights.","Which roads come together at it?|What highways are all tangled up here?|What freeways meet here?","other"],["Is the East Los Angeles Interchange really the busiest in the world?","It's often called the busiest freeway interchange in the world. A 2008 count put more than 550,000 vehicles a day through its southern part.","Is it really that busy?|Are there a ton of cars here?|Busiest one ever?","famous"],["What is the East Los Angeles Interchange's official name?","It's officially the Eugene A. Obregon Memorial Interchange, honoring a Marine who received the Medal of Honor.","Does it have another name?|Who is it named after?|What's its real name?","people"],["Did people in Boyle Heights fight the freeway that led to this interchange?","Yes. Eastside residents fought the freeway because it would wipe out large parts of Boyle Heights. Councilman Edward Roybal chaired a committee to block or reroute it, but it was built anyway.","Did locals fight building it?|Did people hate this freeway?|Did anyone try to stop it?","history"]]},"ca135-mission-san-gabriel":{"n":"Mission San Gabriel","k":"p","q":[["How old is Mission San Gabriel?","It was founded in 1771. It's California mission number 4 in founding order.","How old is it?|Is that place really old, like ancient?|When did it start?","age"],["Who is Toypurina and what did she do at Mission San Gabriel?","Toypurina was a medicine woman who helped organize a revolt against the mission in 1785. It failed, and she was baptized under pressure and sent to a mission far to the north.","What did she do there?|Who was the lady who fought the mission?|Who's Toypurina?","people"],["Why did Toypurina's revolt at Mission San Gabriel start?","It began after the governor banned baptized Tongva people from holding their dances. At her trial she said she was angry that the missionaries had settled on her people's land.","Why did it start?|Why were they fighting the mission?|What made her mad?","history"],["Whose land is Mission San Gabriel on?","It stands in Tongva homeland. About 6,000 Native people are buried on its grounds.","Whose land is it on?|Who lived there first?|Were people already living there?","people"],["What happened to Mission San Gabriel in 2020?","A fire destroyed the church roof in 2020. The grounds reopened in 2023 with an exhibit on Native history.","What happened to it?|Did it burn down?|Is it open again?","today"],["How many Native people were baptized at Mission San Gabriel?","The mission museum names 7,054 Native people baptized there from 1771 to 1848.","How many were baptized there?|How many people did they baptize?|What does the museum say about it?","other"]],"l":"mission-san-gabriel"},"ca135-yaanga-and-the-tongva":{"n":"Yaanga and the Tongva","k":"p","q":[["What was Yaanga?","Yaanga was the largest and most influential Tongva village in the region. It stood where downtown Los Angeles is today, across the river from here.","What was it?|Mom, what was Yaanga, like a town?|Yaanga?","history"],["What was El Aliso at Yaanga?","El Aliso was a great sycamore growing at the center of Yaanga. It became a landmark known to traders from as far away as present-day Yuma.","What was that big tree?|Was there a giant tree in the village?|El Aliso?","famous"],["When did the Spanish arrive at Yaanga?","The Portolá expedition reached Yaanga in 1769. Then in 1781, Spanish settlers founded the pueblo of Los Angeles right next to it.","When did they show up there?|When did the Spanish people come, Dad?|When was Los Angeles started?","history"],["How big was the Tongva homeland?","The Tongva homeland, called Tovaangar, covers about 4,000 square miles. It spans the Los Angeles Basin and the southern Channel Islands.","How big was it?|How much land did the Tongva have?|What was Tovaangar?","size"],["Is anything left of Yaanga today?","Traces of Yaanga have turned up around Union Station and the old Plaza. In 1994 California recognized the Tongva as the aboriginal tribe of the Los Angeles Basin.","Is any of it still there?|Can we find the village now?|Do the Tongva still exist?","today"]]},"ca137-arroyo-seco-parkway":{"n":"Arroyo Seco Parkway","k":"p","q":[["How old is the Arroyo Seco Parkway?","It was dedicated on December 30, 1940, and opened the next day. That makes it one of the oldest freeways in the United States.","How old is it?|When did that road open, mom?|How old is this thing?","age"],["What's special about driving the Arroyo Seco Parkway?","It has tight curves and short ramps with no room to speed up, and they've barely changed. One side runs through four Art Deco tunnels in Elysian Park.","What's different about it?|Why's that road so curvy, mom?|Is it a weird road?","famous"]]},"ca138-elysian-park-and-chavez-ravine":{"n":"Elysian Park and Chavez Ravine","k":"p","q":[["How old is Elysian Park?","Elysian Park was created in 1886. At 600 acres, it's one of the largest parks in Los Angeles.","How long has it been around?|How old is this park, like really old?|When did it start?","age"],["What is Chavez Ravine?","Chavez Ravine is tucked into the Elysian Park hills, and it is the home of Dodger Stadium.","What's that ravine tucked in the hills?|What is that place, is it a stadium?|Chavez Ravine, what's there?","famous"],["Who used to live in Chavez Ravine?","By the 1940s it held three close-knit, mostly Mexican American neighborhoods: La Loma, Palo Verde and Bishop. Many families lived there because of housing discrimination elsewhere in the city.","Did people live in it before the stadium?|Were there houses where the stadium is, mister?|Who lived there?","people"],["What was the Battle of Chavez Ravine?","The city bought or condemned the homes for public housing that was later dropped. In 1958 voters approved trading 352 acres to the Dodgers, and the last holdouts lost a ten-year struggle.","What happened to the homes in it?|Why did the families have to leave, and who fought?|Battle of Chavez Ravine?","history"],["When did Dodger Stadium open?","Dodger Stadium opened in 1962.","How old is it, the stadium?|Is the baseball stadium really old?|When was the stadium built?","age"],["What is the Zanja Madre?","It's an earthen ditch, the first water system of Los Angeles, dug by the city's founders in 1781. A brick piece survives beside the light-rail tracks at Los Angeles State Historic Park.","What was that old water ditch?|What's the Mother Trench, kids ask?|Zanja Madre, what is it?","history"]]},"ca141-los-angeles-river":{"n":"Los Angeles River","k":"p","q":[["Why is the Los Angeles River lined with concrete?","Most of it was lined with concrete after the deadly flood of 1938.","Why is it all concrete?|Why did they pave the river, Buddy?|Why concrete?","history"],["Why are there plants growing in the Los Angeles River?","In this 11-mile stretch, called the Glendale Narrows, the groundwater sits so high that the bottom was left unpaved. That's why willows, reeds and sandbars grow right in the river.","Why does it have plants and sandbars?|Why isn't the bottom all concrete like the rest?|Why plants here?","nature"],["What birds can we see at the Los Angeles River?","Birdwatchers here count great blue herons, snowy and great egrets, black-crowned night herons, cormorants and even American white pelicans. Look for white egrets or gray herons standing in the shallow water.","What birds live there?|Are there any birds in the river, Buddy?|Birds?","nature"],["Can people walk or bike along the Los Angeles River?","Yes, a 7.4-mile bike and walking path follows the river, much of it right beside the freeway.","Is there a path along it?|Can we ride bikes by the river, Buddy?|Any trails?","visit"],["Can you kayak or fish in the Los Angeles River?","Fishing is still legal all year, and by 2019 kayaking on the river had become common. Steelhead trout swam here before the concrete came.","Can people kayak on it?|Can you really fish in that river, Buddy?|Kayaking or fishing there?","today"]]},"ca141-griffith-park":{"n":"Griffith Park","k":"p","q":[["Who gave Griffith Park to Los Angeles?","Griffith J. Griffith gave the first 3,015 acres to the people of Los Angeles as a Christmas gift on December 16, 1896. He asked that the park be open to all.","Who gave it to the city?|Who was the guy that gave the park away?|Who donated this place?","history"],["How big is Griffith Park?","It's more than 4,000 acres of chaparral and parkland. The city calls it the largest urban-wilderness municipal park in the United States.","How big is it?|Is that park really huge?|How many acres is it?","size"],["What can we see and do at Griffith Park?","The observatory, the Greek Theatre and trails to the Hollywood Sign are all inside the park.","What's there to do in it?|What's in the park, anything cool?|What's inside?","see"],["Was there really an ostrich farm at Griffith Park?","Yes. Griffith leased part of his land for an ostrich farm, since ostrich feathers decorated women's hats. The city's zoo later opened on the old farm in 1912.","Was it once an ostrich farm?|Were there really ostriches there?|Why were there ostriches?","history"],["Why do people picnic in old cages at Griffith Park?","The old zoo closed in 1966, and some empty enclosures were left standing with picnic tables inside. So people now picnic in the old cages.","Why are there picnic tables in cages?|Can we eat lunch in a zoo cage?|Is there a old zoo there?","kids"],["Did a mountain lion really live in Griffith Park?","Yes. A mountain lion known as P-22 made these hills his home from 2012 to 2022.","Did it have a mountain lion?|Are there real mountain lions in there?|Who was P-22?","nature"]]},"ca144-the-1933-griffith-park-fire":{"n":"The 1933 Griffith Park fire","k":"p","q":[["What was the 1933 Griffith Park fire?","It was a brush fire on October 3, 1933, that killed at least 29 men who had volunteered or been ordered to fight it.","What was that fire about?|What happened at Griffith Park, like, a long time ago?|What was the fire?","history"],["How big was the 1933 Griffith Park fire?","It burned only about 47 acres, yet for 85 years it was the deadliest wildfire in California history.","How big was it?|Was it a huge fire, or a little one?|How many acres burned?","size"],["How did the 1933 Griffith Park fire start?","It started a little after 2 in the afternoon in a pile of debris in Mineral Wells Canyon, in the hills west of the freeway.","How did it start?|What made the fire begin, anyway?|Where did it start?","history"],["Why did so many people die in the Griffith Park fire?","With no piped water nearby, men beat at flames with shovels. Untrained foremen set ill-judged backfires, and around 3 o'clock the wind shifted and the fire overtook the workers.","Why did it turn so deadly?|Why couldn't they just put it out?|What went wrong?","history"],["Was there a memorial for the 1933 Griffith Park fire victims?","A memorial plaque was placed at the park entrance, but it can no longer be found. The courts also ruled the victims could not be compensated, because they had been hired informally.","Is there a memorial for it?|Did anyone get a monument, or what?|Any plaque?","other"]]},"ca144-zoo-autry-and-travel-town":{"n":"Zoo, Autry and Travel Town","k":"p","q":[["What is at the Zoo, Autry and Travel Town stop?","It's three family stops on the north side of Griffith Park: the Los Angeles Zoo, the Autry Museum of the American West, and Travel Town, a railroad museum.","What's there to do at that stop?|What are the three places there?|What's at this exit?","see"],["Who started the Autry Museum of the American West?","Actor Gene Autry founded his museum of the West here in 1988.","Who started it?|Who made the cowboy museum?|Who is Autry?","people"],["What was the land under the zoo parking lot used for before?","It was the old Griffith Park Aerodrome, an airfield used from 1912 until 1939 by aviation pioneers such as Glenn Martin, Bill Boeing and Donald Douglas.","What was it before the zoo?|What was here a long time ago?|Old airport?","history"]]},"ca145-grand-central-air-terminal":{"n":"Grand Central Air Terminal","k":"p","q":[["How old is the Grand Central Air Terminal?","The terminal building dates to 1928. It opened with fanfare on February 22, 1929.","How old is it?|Is that building really old, like way back?|When was it built?","age"],["What was the Lindbergh Line at Grand Central Air Terminal?","It was a 48-hour coast-to-coast trip with planes by day and trains by night. Grand Central became its western end in July 1929.","What was that Lindbergh Line thing?|Did people fly planes and ride trains at the same time?|What was the 48-hour trip?","history"],["Who flew into the Grand Central Air Terminal in 1933?","Albert Forsythe and Charles Anderson landed here in 1933. It was the first round-trip flight across the country by Black pilots.","Who landed there in 1933?|Did somebody famous land at that airport?|Who made the first round trip flight?","people"],["What did Howard Hughes build near Grand Central Air Terminal?","Howard Hughes built his record-setting H-1 racer in a small building on Air Way in 1935.","What did he build at that airport?|Did Howard Hughes make a plane there?|What was the H-1?","famous"],["What happened at Grand Central Air Terminal during World War II?","The government camouflaged the field and trained pilots on it during World War II.","What did it do in the war?|Did they hide the airport in the war?|Did soldiers use that field?","history"],["Where did the old Grand Central runway go?","The old runway is now a city street, Grand Central Avenue.","What happened to the runway there?|Where did the planes used to take off from?|Is there still a runway?","today"]]},"ca146-verdugo-mountains":{"n":"Verdugo Mountains","k":"p","q":[["How tall are the Verdugo Mountains?","The Verdugo Mountains top out at 3,126 feet, a rocky range rising east of the freeway.","How high does it get?|How big are those mountains, Dad?|How tall?","size"],["Why are they called the Verdugo Mountains?","They are named for José María Verdugo, a Spanish soldier who came north with the 1769 expedition and in 1784 received Rancho San Rafael.","Why is it called that?|Who is Verdugo, and why are the mountains named after him?|Who's Verdugo?","name"],["Who was José María Verdugo?","He was a Spanish soldier who retired from the army in 1798 to ranch full time. He soon had nearly 2,000 head of cattle.","What did that guy do here?|Was Verdugo a real person, like a soldier?|What's his story?","people"],["How much land did Verdugo's rancho cover?","Rancho San Rafael was about 36,000 acres. That land now holds Glendale and Burbank.","How big was it?|Was his ranch really huge?|How many acres?","history"]]},"ca147-burbank-the-media-capital":{"n":"Burbank, the Media Capital","k":"p","q":[["Why is Burbank called the Media Capital of the World?","Burbank is commonly known as the Media Capital of the World. Warner Bros. and the Walt Disney Company both have their headquarters here, in the Media District.","Why do they call it that?|Why is this town so famous, Buddy?|Media capital?","name"],["When did Warner Bros. and Disney come to Burbank?","Warner Bros. took over the First National studio lot in 1928. Disney finished its Burbank studio on Buena Vista Street in 1939, after the success of Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.","When did they get there?|When did the cartoon people move in?|How old are the studios?","history"],["What famous movie was filmed in Burbank?","Most of Casablanca's major scenes were shot on Stage 1 at Warner Bros. in 1942. Even its foggy airport scene was made on the stage.","What was filmed at that studio?|Were any big movies made there, Buddy?|Famous movie?","famous"],["Why isn't Disneyland in Burbank?","Walt Disney first hoped to build a Mickey Mouse Park beside his studio, but his aides said the land was too small. A city council member also said they didn't want the carny atmosphere. He built Disneyland in Anaheim instead.","Why didn't they build it there?|Why is Disneyland somewhere else, Buddy?|No Disneyland there?","history"],["Can you tour a movie studio in Burbank?","Warner Bros. runs public backlot tours, while Disney is the only major studio that does not. Check before going for what's currently offered.","Can we go see it?|Can we go on a studio tour, Buddy?|Any tours?","visit"]]},"ca149-lockheed-s-hidden-factory":{"n":"Lockheed's hidden factory","k":"p","q":[["How did Lockheed hide its factory during World War II?","They covered the factory with a huge painted tarp showing a quiet country neighborhood, with rubber cars and hundreds of fake trees made of chicken wire and feathers.","How did they hide it from the air?|How do you hide a whole factory, Dad?|What was the disguise?","history"],["How many planes did Lockheed build in World War II?","Lockheed and its Vega company built 19,278 planes during the war.","How many did they make in the war?|Did they make a lot of airplanes, Mom?|How many planes total?","size"],["What is the Skunk Works and where did the name come from?","It was Lockheed's secret design shop, which took its nickname from the Li'l Abner comic strip. Work began there in 1943 on a secret jet fighter.","Where did that name come from?|Why is it called Skunk Works, are there skunks?|Skunk Works, what's that?","name"],["What famous planes came out of the Skunk Works?","That team went on to create the U-2 spy plane, the SR-71 Blackbird and the F-117 stealth fighter.","What did that team make?|Did they make any cool planes?|Famous planes from there?","famous"],["What is at Lockheed's old Burbank site today?","Aerospace work in Burbank ended in 1992. A shopping center on Empire Avenue now covers part of the old Skunk Works site.","Is it still a factory?|Can we still see the old factory?|What's there now?","today"],["What is the airport next to Lockheed's factory called?","Lockheed bought the airport in 1940 and ran it as Lockheed Air Terminal. The field has gone by at least six names, and today it is Hollywood Burbank Airport.","What happened to that airport?|Did the airport have a lot of names?|What's the airport called now?","name"]]},"ca150-sun-valley-s-thorny-name":{"n":"Sun Valley's thorny name","k":"p","q":[["What was Sun Valley called before it was Sun Valley?","It was first Wixánga, a Fernandeño Tongva village. Later it was called Roscoe, until residents chose Sun Valley in 1950.","What was it called before that?|What was its old name, Dad?|Old names?","history"],["What does Wixánga mean?","It means something like place of the thorns, named for the prickly pear cactus that grew here.","What does that name mean?|What's that weird word mean?|Wixánga meaning?","name"],["Why was Sun Valley once called Roscoe?","The name Roscoe reportedly came from a railroad crew member. A general store here served as post office and water stop for the Southern Pacific line.","Where did the name Roscoe come from?|Who was Roscoe, Dad?|Roscoe name?","name"],["Does Sun Valley flood?","Yes, it sits at the foot of the Verdugo Mountains and is prone to flash floods. Nearby Tujunga Wash is usually dry and carries much water only during and after storms.","Is it prone to floods?|Does the wash fill with water, Mom?|Any floods here?","nature"]]},"ca152-when-the-valley-grew-wheat":{"n":"When the valley grew wheat","k":"p","q":[["Who started growing wheat in the San Fernando Valley?","J. B. Lankershim and Isaac Van Nuys began dry-farming wheat across the southern half of the San Fernando Valley in 1874.","Who started growing wheat here?|Who were the wheat farmers, Dad?|Who farmed it first?","people"],["How big was wheat farming in the valley?","By 1876 the valley was the biggest wheat-growing area in Los Angeles County.","Was it a big deal back then?|Was there really that much wheat, Mom?|How much wheat?","size"],["Where did the valley's wheat get shipped to?","In 1876 they sent the first wheat shipment from San Pedro Harbor to Europe.","Where did it all go?|Did the wheat go far away, Dad?|Who bought the wheat?","history"],["Why did valley farmers stop growing wheat?","Droughts showed they needed steady water. After Los Angeles Aqueduct water arrived after 1913, farms switched from wheat to irrigated crops.","Why did it stop?|Why no more wheat, Mom?|What changed?","history"],["What did the valley grow after the wheat?","Farms grew corn, beans, squash and cotton, plus orchards of apricots, persimmons and walnuts, and big groves of oranges and lemons.","What did they plant after that?|Did they grow oranges, Dad?|What crops were there?","nature"],["What happened to the valley's farms?","After World War II the valley's population grew fivefold in 15 years, and suburbs replaced nearly all the farms, leaving only a few groves.","Is it still farmland?|Where did all the farms go, Mom?|Any farms left?","today"]]},"ca155-pacoima-ritchie-valens-hometown":{"n":"Pacoima, Ritchie Valens' hometown","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Pacoima?","The name comes from Pakoinga, a Native village here whose name meant entrance. Spanish ears heard the ng sound as an m, and that's how it became Pacoima.","Why is it called that?|Why's this place got such a weird name?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["Who is Ritchie Valens and why is Pacoima his hometown?","Ritchie Valens was born Richard Valenzuela right here in Pacoima in 1941. He turned the Mexican folk song La Bamba into a rock and roll hit in 1958.","Who was he, and did he really come from here?|Who's the famous guy from here?|Ritchie who?","people"],["What happened to Ritchie Valens?","He died at just 17 in a February 1959 plane crash in Iowa. Buddy Holly died in the same crash.","How did he die?|Did something bad happen to that singer?|What happened to the La Bamba guy?","history"],["Did Ritchie Valens go to school in Pacoima?","Yes, he went to Pacoima Junior High. He brought his guitar to school and sang for friends on the bleachers.","Did he go to school around here?|Did he sing at his school when he was a kid?|Where'd he go to school?","kids"],["Is anything named after Ritchie Valens near Pacoima?","Yes, the stretch of I-5 between the 170 and 118 freeways is named the Ritchie Valens Memorial Highway.","Is there something named after him?|Is there a road for that singer?|Anything here named for him?","famous"],["What is Pacoima's history in the San Fernando Valley?","After World War II, Pacoima became the center of African American life in the San Fernando Valley, because racial covenants kept Black families out of many other neighborhoods.","What's important about it?|Why did people move there way back?|What's the story here?","history"]]},"ca157-mission-san-fernando":{"n":"Mission San Fernando","k":"p","q":[["When was Mission San Fernando founded?","Mission San Fernando was founded in 1797. It is California mission number 17 in founding order.","How old is it?|How long ago did they start this place, like in olden times?|What year was it built?","age"],["Who lived and worked at Mission San Fernando?","Tataviam, Tongva and Chumash families lived and worked at the mission. It was built on a farming settlement called Achooykomenga.","Who were the people there?|Who stayed at that place back then?|Who lived there?","people"],["What is the old long building at Mission San Fernando?","It's the convento, finished in 1822, and it is original. It was a guesthouse for travelers on El Camino Real.","What can we see at it that is still original?|Is any of it really old, like for real?|What's the long building?","see"],["Did anything bad happen to Mission San Fernando's church?","Yes, the 1971 earthquake destroyed the church. It was rebuilt by 1974.","Is the church still the old one?|Did the church fall down or something?|Was it ruined?","history"]],"l":"mission-san-fernando"},"ca158-the-dam-that-almost-failed":{"n":"The dam that almost failed","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the dam that almost failed?","During the San Fernando earthquake on February 9, 1971, part of the Lower Van Norman Dam slid into its reservoir. Only about 5 feet of dam was left above the water.","What happened there?|Why did the dam almost fail, Mom?|What's the story here?","history"],["How many people had to leave when the dam almost failed?","Eighty thousand people living below the dam were evacuated, and they stayed away for days.","How many people did it affect?|Did people have to run away from the dam?|Who got evacuated?","people"],["How did they keep the dam from failing completely?","The reservoir was only about half full, which was lucky. Crews drained it as fast as the damaged outlets allowed, lowering it 13.5 feet in three and a half days.","How did they stop it from collapsing?|What did the workers do about the water?|How'd they save it?","history"],["What happened to the old dams afterward?","The old dams were retired, and a new Los Angeles Dam was built between them in a more stable spot. The near miss also changed how engineers judge earthquake safety at dams.","Is it still used today?|Did they build a new dam, Mom?|What changed after that?","today"]]},"ca159-sylmar-s-sea-of-olives":{"n":"Sylmar's sea of olives","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Sylmar?","Sylmar joins Latin words for forest and sea. It was named for the sea of olive trees that once covered these slopes. The name was adopted in 1893.","Why is it named that?|Why's this town called Sylmar, Mom?|What does Sylmar mean?","name"],["How big were Sylmar's olive groves?","In 1894 up to 1,700 acres were planted. By 1906 the groves were said to be the largest olive grove in the world.","How big was it?|Were there really a ton of olive trees?|How many olives?","size"],["Where did Sylmar's olives come from?","They trace back to Mission San Fernando, which tended about 70 acres of olive trees and crushed the fruit for oil. In the 1890s the Los Angeles Olive Growers Association planted the foothills.","Who started it?|Who planted all those olive trees?|Who started the olives?","history"],["Who picked the olives in Sylmar?","In the early 1900s, about 300 Japanese workers picked the olives each season. They lived in a village of tents.","Who worked there?|Who did all the picking, Dad?|Who worked the groves?","people"],["What happened to the olive company's land?","In 1938 the company put its land up for sale. It was still planted in olives, lemons, oranges and figs.","Did they sell it?|Did they sell the farm, Mom?|What happened to the land?","history"]]},"ca159-power-from-the-columbia-river":{"n":"Power from the Columbia River","k":"p","q":[["What is the Sylmar Converter Station?","It's the south end of the Pacific DC Intertie, a power line running 846 miles from the Columbia River near The Dalles, Oregon. The station turns direct current into the alternating current Los Angeles uses.","What does it do?|What's that big power place for?|What's that station?","other"],["How long is the Pacific DC Intertie power line?","The line runs 846 miles, all the way from the Columbia River near The Dalles, Oregon, down to this station near Los Angeles.","How long is that power line?|How far does the electricity travel to get here?|How long is the wire?","size"],["When was the Pacific DC Intertie built?","The intertie was completed in 1970. It was built to bring cheaper hydroelectric power from dams on the Columbia River.","How old is it?|When did they finish that thing?|Is it old?","age"],["Why does Los Angeles get power from the Columbia River?","It brings cheaper hydroelectric power from dams on the Columbia River. At the time, it was estimated to save Los Angeles customers about 600,000 dollars a day.","Why does it come from so far away?|Why not make power here?|Why bring electricity all the way from Oregon?","history"],["How much power does the Sylmar Converter Station handle?","It converts up to 3,100 megawatts. That's enough power for two to three million Los Angeles households.","How many homes does it power?|How much electricity is that?|Is it a lot of power?","famous"]]},"ca161-los-angeles-aqueduct-cascades":{"n":"Los Angeles Aqueduct Cascades","k":"p","q":[["What are the Los Angeles Aqueduct Cascades?","It's the end of the Los Angeles Aqueduct, where water from the eastern Sierra Nevada tumbles down a long concrete water staircase on the hillside.","What is that water staircase on the hill?|Why is there a waterfall by the freeway?|What's that concrete thing?","famous"],["When did the first water reach the Cascades?","The first water arrived on November 5, 1913. Chief engineer William Mulholland said, \"There it is. Take it.\"","How old is it?|When did the water first come down, like, how long ago?|How old is this thing?","age"],["How does the water get to the Los Angeles Aqueduct Cascades?","It moves by gravity alone. The original aqueduct runs 233 miles from the eastern Sierra Nevada.","How does it get here without pumps?|How does the water get all the way down here, Dad?|How's it work?","other"],["How hard was it to build the Los Angeles Aqueduct?","It took thousands of workers and 164 tunnels, a job compared to the Panama Canal.","Who built it and how long did it take?|Did lots of people have to build it?|Was it a big job?","history"],["Where does the Cascades water come from?","It comes from the Owens Valley, homeland of the Paiute people. The diversions ended most farming there and devastated Owens Lake.","Where does it come from originally?|Where's all this water from, anyway?|Where's the water start?","history"],["How far can the water travel to reach the Cascades?","After an extension finished in 1940, water can travel 338 miles from near Tioga Pass to reach the Cascades.","Is it farther than 233 miles?|How far does it go, like, total?|How long is the whole thing?","size"]]},"ca162-newhall-pass-interchange":{"n":"Newhall Pass Interchange","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the Clarence Wayne Dean Memorial Interchange?","It is named for Clarence Wayne Dean, a Los Angeles police motorcycle officer killed in the 1994 collapse. He was riding to work when he could not stop in time and fell from a broken ramp.","Who is it named after?|Who was that guy it's named for?|Who was Clarence Dean?","name"],["How wide is I-5 at the Newhall Pass Interchange?","Parts of I-5 here are up to 21 lanes wide. It's a tangle of ramps where I-5 meets the Antelope Valley Freeway.","How many lanes does it have?|How many lanes are here, like a lot?|How big is this thing?","size"],["What are the separate truck lanes at the Newhall Pass Interchange?","The separate truck lanes that loop around the main lanes are the original four-lane freeway, built as US 99.","What are those lanes that loop around it?|Why are there extra lanes looping around?|What are the side lanes for?","history"],["Where do the main lanes of I-5 go at Newhall Pass?","The main lanes follow a bypass through Weldon and Gavin canyons, built by 1930 to avoid Newhall Pass itself.","How does the main road get through here?|Why does the freeway go around Newhall Pass?|Where's the real pass?","other"],["What exit is the Newhall Pass Interchange?","It's Exit 162. Look for flyover ramps curving high above the lanes on tall columns.","What exit is it?|Which exit number is that?|What exit are we looking for?","exit"]]},"ca162-oak-of-the-golden-dream":{"n":"Oak of the Golden Dream","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the Oak of the Golden Dream?","Francisco López found gold flakes clinging to wild onion roots near an oak tree on March 9, 1842. The state's landmark plaque calls it California's first authenticated gold discovery.","What happened here at that tree?|Why is there a gold tree thing here?|What's the story?","history"],["How does the Oak of the Golden Dream compare to the Sutter's Mill gold strike?","It came first. López's find happened six years before the famous strike at Sutter's Mill.","Was it before the famous gold rush find?|Did they find gold here before Sutter's Mill?|Which came first?","famous"],["Did Francisco López just stumble onto the gold by accident?","Not really. The dream story is just legend. López had studied mineralogy at the University of Mexico and was actually searching for gold.","Was it really luck that he found it?|Did he know what he was looking for?|Was he a smart guy?","people"],["How many people came to mine gold near the Oak of the Golden Dream?","About 2,000 people came, mostly from the Mexican state of Sonora. They mined by panning, sluicing and dry washing.","Did lots of people come after that?|Did a bunch of miners show up?|Who came to dig?","people"],["What happened to the mine at the Oak of the Golden Dream?","During the Mexican-American War, the rancho's owner destroyed the mine to keep it from the United States.","What happened to the gold mine there?|Why did the mining stop?|Did somebody wreck the mine?","history"]]},"ca163-beale-s-cut":{"n":"Beale's Cut","k":"p","q":[["What is Beale's Cut?","It's a slot dug through the mountain with picks and shovels, so stagecoaches could cross the pass. It was eventually deepened to 90 feet.","What is that thing?|What's this cut in the mountain, Mom?|Beale's Cut, what's that?","history"],["Who built Beale's Cut?","Edward Beale won the right to collect tolls here in 1863. He used Chinese immigrant workers to do most of the digging.","Who dug it?|Who made this giant hole, Dad?|Who built that?","people"],["What is Beale's Cut famous for in movies?","In a 1923 John Ford film, cowboy star Tom Mix is filmed leaping across it. Historians still debate whether it was Mix, a stuntman or a camera trick.","Was it ever in a movie?|Did a cowboy really jump over it?|Movie star jump?","famous"],["Did stagecoaches really go through Beale's Cut?","Yes. Butterfield Overland Mail coaches running to San Francisco used the cut, back before tunnels and freeways.","Did they use it back then?|Did real cowboy wagons go through there?|Stagecoaches?","history"],["What happened to Beale's Cut in the earthquake?","The 1994 earthquake collapsed part of the cut. That left it about 30 feet deep.","Did it get damaged?|Did the mountain fall down, Dad?|Earthquake damage?","history"]]},"ca167-william-s-hart-park":{"n":"William S. Hart Park","k":"p","q":[["Who was William S. Hart?","William S. Hart was a silent film cowboy and one of the most popular movie stars of the late 1910s. He was known for gritty westerns and a pinto horse named Fritz.","Who was he, the guy this park is named after?|Was Hart somebody famous?|Who's Hart?","people"],["What can we see at William S. Hart Park?","You'll find his Spanish-style mansion, trails and a barnyard of farm animals, all on a 265-acre ranch. The mansion holds his collection, including Native American artifacts and works by Charles Russell.","What is there to do at it?|Are there animals there, or just old stuff?|What's in the park?","see"],["How did William S. Hart's ranch become a park?","Hart left the ranch to Los Angeles County when he died in 1946. He said the people gave him their nickels, dimes and quarters, and he wanted them to have his home.","Why is that ranch a public park now?|Why can we go to his house?|How'd it become a park?","history"],["What was William S. Hart's ranch called?","Hart called his ranch La Loma de los Vientos. He retired there after his 1925 film Tumbleweeds and made no more feature films.","Did it have a name before it was a park?|What did he call his house?|Ranch's name?","name"],["Did William S. Hart always act in cowboy movies?","No. He came to the movies from the Shakespearean stage in 1914, when he was nearly 50.","Was he an actor his whole life?|How did he get into the movies?|When did he start acting in films?","history"],["Who runs William S. Hart Park today?","The City of Santa Clarita took over the park and museum from the county in 2025. It offers guided house tours.","Is it still run by the county?|Can we tour that house now?|Who's in charge of it now?","today"]]},"ca167-pico-number-4-oil-well":{"n":"Pico Number 4 oil well","k":"p","q":[["What is special about the Pico Number 4 oil well?","It became the first commercially successful oil well in the western United States after striking oil on September 26, 1876. It is often called the longest continuously operating oil well in the world.","What was it famous for?|Why do people say that well matters?|What's the deal with the old oil well?","famous"],["Who built the Pico Number 4 oil well?","Driller Charles Mentry built the drill stem from old railroad car axles. In 1877, oil spurted to the top of the 65-foot derrick.","Who drilled it?|Who was the guy that made that thing?|Who built it?","people"],["What happened to the oil from Pico Number 4?","Mentry laid California's first oil pipeline to a refinery in Newhall. That refinery is called the oldest existing refinery in the world.","Where did the oil go after it came out?|Where did they send it?|Was there a pipeline?","history"],["What was Mentryville near the Pico Number 4 oil well?","Mentryville was a boom town down the canyon, with a schoolhouse and a gas-lit tennis court. It had no bar, because Mentry reportedly banned drinking and swearing.","What was the town near it?|Was there a town by the oil well?|Was there a bar in that old oil town?","history"],["Did people use oil here before the Pico Number 4 well?","Yes. Long before the well, the Tataviam and Tongva people used oil from seeps here as medicine.","Did anyone know about oil there before it?|Who found the oil first?|Did Native people use the oil?","people"]]},"ca169-tataviam-homeland":{"n":"Tataviam homeland","k":"p","q":[["What does Tataviam mean?","Tataviam means people on the south slope. The name comes from the neighboring Kitanemuk language.","What does that name mean?|Why are they called the Tataviam?|What's Tataviam mean?","name"],["Who are the Tataviam people?","The Tataviam are the native people whose homeland centers on the Santa Clarita Valley. They lived in about 20 villages in and around the valley.","Who were they and where did they live?|Who lived here before us?|Who were the Tataviam?","people"],["What did the Portolá expedition see at the Tataviam village?","When the Portolá expedition camped by the river in August 1769, they found a large village. They even watched a wedding celebration.","What happened when they arrived here in 1769?|What did the explorers find here?|Did the explorers meet them?","history"],["Are the Tataviam still around today?","Yes, their tribe today is the Fernandeño Tataviam Band of Mission Indians, governed from San Fernando. Their land conservancy, founded in 2018, has had more than 550 acres returned.","Is that tribe still here?|Do they still exist?|Do they have land now?","today"]]},"ca170-santa-clara-river":{"n":"Santa Clara River","k":"p","q":[["How long is the Santa Clara River?","The Santa Clara River runs 83 miles to the Pacific Ocean. It's also one of the least altered rivers in Southern California.","How long is it?|How far does that river go, like how many miles?|How long's the river?","size"],["Why is it called the Santa Clara River?","Spanish explorers named it on August 9, 1769, for Saint Clare of Assisi.","Why is it called that?|Who named this river and why, and what's it named after?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["What animals live along the Santa Clara River?","The river still shelters threatened wildlife, including the unarmored threespine stickleback, steelhead trout, southwestern pond turtles and least Bell's vireos.","What lives there?|Are there any fish or turtles in that river, kids want to know?|What wildlife is there?","nature"],["Why is the area called Santa Clarita?","The valley became known as little Santa Clara, Santa Clarita, to tell it apart from Santa Clara in Northern California.","Why is it called Santa Clarita?|Why's it a little Santa Clara, what does that mean?|Santa Clarita, why that name?","name"]]},"ca170-six-flags-magic-mountain":{"n":"Six Flags Magic Mountain","k":"p","q":[["How old is Six Flags Magic Mountain?","Six Flags Magic Mountain opened on May 29, 1971.","How old is it?|When did that park open, like how long ago?|When'd it open?","age"],["What is Six Flags Magic Mountain famous for?","Roller coasters. In 2022 it became the first amusement park to offer 20 coasters, and it still has one of the biggest collections of coasters anywhere.","What's it famous for?|Why is that park such a big deal?|What's it known for?","famous"],["Who built Six Flags Magic Mountain?","The Newhall Land and Farming Company built it, in partnership with Sea World's founder. Six Flags bought the park in 1979.","Who built it?|Who made that park, like who started it?|Who built the park?","people"],["Why is it called Six Flags Magic Mountain?","It took its name from Magic Mountain, a peak east of the Santa Clarita Valley.","Why is it called that?|Is there a real magic mountain there?|Why Magic Mountain?","name"],["What was special about the Colossus coaster at Magic Mountain?","Colossus, from 1978, was then the fastest and largest dual-track wooden coaster in the world.","What was special about it?|Was there a really big wooden coaster there?|What's Colossus?","famous"]]},"ca172-the-death-valley-rescue":{"n":"The Death Valley rescue","k":"p","q":[["What was the Death Valley rescue?","In January 1850, William Lewis Manly and John Rogers got supplies and animals here to rescue their gold-seeking emigrant party, stranded and starving in Death Valley.","What happened with that rescue?|Who got saved in Death Valley?|What's the rescue story?","history"],["Who went to rescue the stranded party?","William Lewis Manly and John Rogers reached the rancho headquarters in January 1850 and got supplies and animals to help their stranded emigrant party.","Who were the rescuers at that place?|Who saved the people, who were the guys?|Who went?","people"],["How big was Rancho San Francisco?","The rancho covered 48,612 acres, granted in 1839 to Antonio del Valle. Much of today's Santa Clarita lies inside its old boundaries.","How big was it?|Was that rancho really huge?|How much land?","size"],["How did Newhall and Saugus get their names?","Henry Newhall gave the railroad a right of way, and it named its new town Newhall. He named the first station Saugus, after his hometown in Massachusetts.","Why is it called Newhall?|Where did the names come from, like Saugus?|Who was Newhall?","name"],["What happened to the rancho later?","After floods and droughts, the family sold most of it in 1862, and it eventually passed to Henry Newhall. In 1936 his grandson-in-law, Atholl McBean, found oil on the property.","Who owns that land now, or did it get sold?|Did somebody find something there?|What came after?","history"]]},"ca172-st-francis-dam-disaster":{"n":"St. Francis Dam disaster","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the St. Francis Dam?","The St. Francis Dam failed just before midnight on March 12, 1928, in San Francisquito Canyon. The flood killed at least 431 people as it raced down to the sea.","What happened there?|Mom, what's the story with that dam?|What went wrong?","history"],["How many people died in the St. Francis Dam disaster?","At least 431 people were killed. It is considered the second-deadliest dam failure in American history.","How many people did it kill?|Did a lot of people die there?|How bad was it?","history"],["Who built the St. Francis Dam?","It was part of the Los Angeles Aqueduct system, designed and built under William Mulholland. Its failure ended his career.","Who made it?|Who was in charge of building that dam?|Who built it?","people"],["How far did the St. Francis Dam flood travel?","The flood tore down the canyon and along the Santa Clara River to the sea. It reached the ocean south of Ventura around 5:30 in the morning, nearly two miles wide.","How far did it go?|Did the water really reach the ocean?|How big was the flood?","size"],["What did the St. Francis Dam flood destroy?","It flooded at least four miles of the state's main north-south highway and washed away the settlement of Castaic Junction. Downstream, 84 workers died at a power company construction camp.","What did it wash away?|Did it wipe out a town?|What got wrecked?","history"],["Is the St. Francis Dam disaster site a national monument?","Yes. In 2019, Congress made the site the Saint Francis Dam Disaster National Monument.","Is it protected now?|Is that a national monument?|Is it a park?","today"]]},"ca173-val-verde-the-black-palm-springs":{"n":"Val Verde, the Black Palm Springs","k":"p","q":[["Why is Val Verde called the Black Palm Springs?","Black investors founded it as a resort community in 1924, when African Americans were often barred from public beaches and pools. Ella Fitzgerald and Lena Horne appeared there.","Why do they call it that?|Why's it called the Black Palm Springs, Mom?|Black Palm Springs?","name"],["Who founded Val Verde?","Black investors led by Sidney Dones founded it in 1924. It began as Eureka Villa, on ranch land whose owner, Laura Janes, had opened it to African Americans in the 1910s.","Who started it?|Who made this place, Dad?|Who built it?","people"],["What does Val Verde mean?","Val Verde is Spanish for green valley. Today it is a community by that name, in the hills west of the freeway.","What does that name mean?|What kind of name is Val Verde?|Val Verde means?","name"],["What happened to Val Verde after the 1960s?","After civil rights reforms in the 1960s opened other neighborhoods, many families moved away.","Why did families leave it?|Why did people move away, Mom?|What changed there?","history"]]},"ca176-five-mile-grade-crossover":{"n":"Five Mile Grade crossover","k":"p","q":[["Why are the downhill lanes on a gentler grade at the Five Mile Grade?","The downhill lanes got a gentler grade to cut down on runaway trucks.","Why is it built so the downhill part is gentler?|Why did they make the hill easier for trucks?|What's the deal with the runaway trucks?","history"],["What is the old road next to the Five Mile Grade crossover?","One set of lanes is the old four-lane Ridge Route Alternate, widened between 1948 and 1952. The other was added to the east, next to the original 1915 Ridge Route.","What's the older road next to it?|What was here before the freeway, kid?|Is there an old road beside it?","age"],["How big did the Five Mile Grade freeway end up being?","The plan began as a six-lane freeway and grew to eight lanes before the work was finished in 1970.","How many lanes did it end up with?|How wide did it get?|When was it finished?","size"]]},"ca176-castaic-lake-and-its-name":{"n":"Castaic Lake and its name","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Castaic?","Castaic comes from the Chumash word Kaštiq, which means the eye.","Where does the name of it come from?|What does Castaic mean, anyone know?|Castaic name meaning?","name"],["How old is Castaic Dam and how big is it?","Castaic Dam was finished in 1973. It rises 340 feet and stretches nearly a mile.","How tall is that dam?|Is the big dam really huge, like how tall?|How big is the dam?","size"],["What is Castaic Lake used for?","It sits at the end of the West Branch of the California Aqueduct. Water from Northern California rests here before flowing on to millions of people around Los Angeles.","What does it do, is it just a lake?|Why did they make this lake, where does the water go?|What's the lake for?","today"],["How did the town of Castaic begin?","Modern Castaic began in 1887 as a Southern Pacific siding called Castaic Junction.","When did it start as a town?|How did this place get started, long ago?|When was Castaic founded?","history"],["Was anything famous filmed at Castaic Lake?","Yes. Many of the Mighty Morphin Power Rangers show's action scenes were filmed at the lake.","Was that lake in a movie or TV show?|Was it on TV, like Power Rangers?|Anything filmed there?","famous"],["What happened here in the Hughes Fire?","In January 2025 the Hughes Fire burned 10,425 acres east of the lake. At its height, more than 30,000 people were under evacuation orders.","Did it burn near that lake?|Was there a big fire by the lake?|Hughes Fire, what happened?","history"]]},"ca183-the-old-ridge-route":{"n":"The old Ridge Route","k":"p","q":[["What was the old Ridge Route?","It was the first paved highway linking Los Angeles directly with the San Joaquin Valley. The original 1915 road winds along the ridgetops from Castaic toward Gorman.","What was it used for?|What's that old windy road?|Old road?","history"],["How twisty was the old Ridge Route?","Very twisty. Between Castaic and Gorman it had 697 turns, which added up to about 97 complete circles in 36 miles.","How many turns did it have?|Why was that road so curvy, mom?|How many curves?","size"],["How fast could cars go on the old Ridge Route?","Not fast at all. Between Castaic and Quail Lake the speed limit was 15 miles an hour.","What was the speed limit on it?|Could cars go fast on that old road?|Speed limit?","history"],["Where did travelers stay on the old Ridge Route?","Roadhouses served travelers along the way, including the National Forest Inn, the Tumble Inn and Sandberg's Summit Hotel, a log lodge from 1914.","Were there hotels along it?|Where did people eat and sleep on that road?|Any places to stop?","history"],["Is the old Ridge Route still open today?","Much of it is now closed to cars. About 17.6 miles of it in the Angeles National Forest went on the National Register of Historic Places in 1997.","Can we still drive it?|Can we drive on that old road?|Is it closed?","today"],["Who helped get the old Ridge Route protected?","Retired engineer Harrison Scott deserves much of the credit. About 17.6 miles of the road went on the National Register of Historic Places in 1997, thanks largely to him.","Who saved it?|Who got that road on the historic register?|Who saved the road?","people"]]},"ca191-highway-99-under-the-lake":{"n":"Highway 99 under the lake","k":"p","q":[["What is Highway 99 under the lake?","It's an old highway now covered by Pyramid Lake. It opened in 1933 as the three-lane Ridge Route Alternate, later US 99, through the canyon of Piru Creek.","What is that road under the water?|Is there really a road under the lake?|What's the drowned highway?","history"],["How did Highway 99 end up under Pyramid Lake?","After Pyramid Dam was finished, the rising water covered the old road. By 1972 it was fully under the lake.","How did it end up underwater?|Kid: how does a road get under a lake?|Why is the road underwater?","history"],["What was the suicide lane on old Highway 99?","It was the middle lane of the three-lane road. Drivers in both directions used it for passing, which is how it got its nickname.","What was that middle lane for?|Kid: why was the middle lane scary?|What's the suicide lane?","history"],["Why is it called Pyramid Lake?","It's named for Pyramid Rock, a pointed chunk of mountain left behind in 1932 when road builders cut through a ridge for the highway. The rock still stands in front of the dam.","Why is that lake named that?|Kid: is there a pyramid in the lake?|Where did the name come from?","name"],["Can you still see any of the old Highway 99 today?","Yes. A piece of the old road at the lake's north end now serves as a boat ramp. The dam there stands 386 feet high.","Is any of it still above water?|Kid: can we see the old road?|Anything left to see?","today"]]},"ca191-vista-del-lago":{"n":"Vista del Lago","k":"p","q":[["How much water is in the lake at Vista del Lago?","Pyramid Lake holds about 180,000 acre-feet of State Water Project water, pumped up from the San Joaquin Valley and over the Tehachapi Mountains.","How much water does it hold?|Is the lake really big, how much water is in it?|How big is the lake?","size"],["Where does the water go after Pyramid Lake?","It drops 1,060 feet through the 7.2-mile Angeles Tunnel, 30 feet across, to the Castaic Power Plant at the upper end of Castaic Lake.","Where does it go from there?|Does the water just stay in the lake?|Where's the water go next?","today"],["What does the Castaic Power Plant do?","It makes electricity at times of peak demand, and it can also pump water back uphill to store energy.","What does it do with the water?|Does the water make electricity?|What's the power plant for?","today"],["Can you boat or fish at Pyramid Lake?","Yes, Pyramid Lake offers boating, fishing and picnic sites, including five that can be reached only by boat.","Can we do stuff on it?|Can we go on the lake in a boat?|Can you fish there?","visit"]]},"ca198-quail-lake-on-the-fault":{"n":"Quail Lake on the fault","k":"p","q":[["What is Quail Lake used for?","Quail Lake stores water for the West Branch of the California Aqueduct. Water pumped up to the lake flows by gravity through a pipeline to a power plant at Pyramid Lake.","What's that lake for?|Why is there a lake here, is it for swimming?|What does it do?","today"],["Is Quail Lake really on the San Andreas Fault?","Yes. Quail Lake lies in the rift zone of the San Andreas Fault, and the fault itself slices right through nearby Gorman.","Is it actually sitting on the fault?|Wait, is this lake on top of an earthquake crack?|Is that on the fault?","nature"],["When did the San Andreas Fault last break here?","This part of the San Andreas last broke on January 9, 1857. It is often called the last Big One in Southern California, and this section has not had a large quake since.","When was the last earthquake near it?|When did the ground last shake here, how long ago?|Last big quake?","history"],["How big was the 1857 earthquake near Quail Lake?","It was about magnitude 7.9, one of the largest earthquakes recorded in the United States. The rupture ran about 225 miles, from Parkfield to Wrightwood.","How strong was that earthquake?|Was the quake here a really big one?|How big was the quake?","size"],["How far did the ground move in the 1857 earthquake?","The ground slipped about 15 feet on average along a rupture about 225 miles long, running from Parkfield to Wrightwood.","How much did the ground slip there?|How far did the earth move in that quake?|How much did it move?","nature"],["Did anyone die in the 1857 earthquake near Quail Lake?","Only two deaths were reported. One was a woman in nearby Gorman, killed when an adobe house collapsed.","Was anybody hurt in that quake?|Did people die when the ground shook here?|Anyone killed?","people"]]},"ca202-gorman-and-its-wildflowers":{"n":"Gorman and its wildflowers","k":"p","q":[["Why is the town called Gorman?","It's named for James Gorman, who reportedly worked at Fort Tejon as a teamster and herder. In 1867 or 1868 he bought the log roadhouse here, which became known as Gorman's Station.","Why is it called that?|Who is Gorman, anyway?|Gorman's name?","name"],["How old is Gorman as a place people stop?","Very old. People have stopped here for centuries. It was the Tataviam village of Kulshra'jek, a trading crossroads, and a local historian calls it one of California's oldest continuously used roadside rest stops.","How long has it been around?|Did people stop here a long time ago?|Is Gorman really old?","age"],["What happened here with the stagecoaches?","Stagecoach drivers changed horses here. They used six horses to pull a coach up the pass from Bakersfield.","What did stagecoaches do at Gorman's Station?|Did cowboys and horses come through here?|Stagecoach stop?","history"],["Why are the hills around Gorman covered in flowers?","After a wet winter, spring poppies and lupine can blanket the hills around Gorman. Look for the orange California poppies, best in April and May.","When does it get all orange and purple?|Why are the hills so colorful?|Flowers here?","nature"],["What was the yellow umbrella art at Gorman?","In 1991 the artists Christo and Jeanne-Claude placed 1,760 large yellow umbrellas along the roadsides and up the mountainsides around Gorman and Tejon Pass. The show closed early after a woman was crushed by a windblown umbrella.","Did someone put umbrellas on the mountains here?|Why were there giant yellow umbrellas?|Umbrella art?","famous"],["Can we do a wildflower tour near Gorman?","Next door, Hungry Valley State Vehicular Recreation Area offers guided wildflower tours from mid-April through May. Its guide notes poppies close on cloudy, windy days and at night.","Is there a place to see flowers up close?|Can kids see the flowers on a tour?|Any flower tours?","visit"]]},"ca204-tejon-pass-summit":{"n":"Tejon Pass summit","k":"p","q":[["How high is Tejon Pass summit?","Near the top of Tejon Pass, I-5 reaches 4,144 feet. That's the highest point of the freeway in California.","How high up is it here?|Are we really high up right now?|What's the elevation?","size"],["Why is it called Tejon Pass?","Tejón is Spanish for badger. The dead badger behind the name was actually found in 1806 at a different canyon, about 15 miles northeast.","Why is it called that?|What does Tejon mean?|Why's it named after a badger?","name"],["What was Tejon Pass called before?","Lieutenant Pedro Fages crossed in 1772 and called it Portezuelo de Cortes. Later it was the Castac Pass, then the Fort Tejon Pass, and finally just Tejon Pass.","Did it always have that name?|Did it used to be called something else?|Old names?","history"],["What makes Tejon Pass special geologically?","The pass sits where California's two largest faults, the San Andreas and the Garlock, meet.","What's special about the ground here?|Are there earthquake faults here?|Is it on a fault?","nature"],["Does snow close I-5 at Tejon Pass?","Yes. Snow and ice close I-5 here an average of three to five times a winter, between October and April. The Highway Patrol usually closes it outright because of the steep grade and heavy traffic.","Does it get closed by snow there?|Does it snow up here?|Why does the freeway close?","today"],["What exit is Tejon Pass summit?","Tejon Pass summit is at exit 204.","What exit is it?|Which exit do we take for it?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca205-the-brink-s-jewelry-heist":{"n":"The Brink's jewelry heist","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the Brink's jewelry heist?","Thieves cut the padlock on a Brink's trailer at a truck stop and stole about two dozen of its 73 bags of jewelry. It all took place within 27 minutes.","What happened there with the jewelry?|Mom, what did the thieves do?|What was the heist?","history"],["When did the Brink's jewelry heist happen?","It happened at 2 in the morning on July 11, 2022, when the Brink's truck pulled into a truck stop at the Frazier Mountain Park Road exit.","When did it happen?|Was this a long time ago, Dad?|What year was the heist?","age"],["How much was stolen in the Brink's jewelry heist?","Brink's put the loss at 8.7 million dollars, but some estimates reach 100 million, since some jewelers admitted listing less than the true value to keep insurance costs down.","How much was it worth?|How many millions did they get?|How much money was that?","other"],["How did the thieves pull off the Brink's heist so fast?","Each bag weighed 70 to 100 pounds, so moving that many so fast suggests several thieves. Most missing bags came from the front, so they seemed to know which ones they wanted.","How did they do it so quickly?|How did they carry all those heavy bags?|Were there lots of robbers?","people"],["Where were the guards during the Brink's jewelry heist?","One guard went inside the truck stop to eat while the other slept in the cab. The trailer was secured with just a padlock and a plastic seal.","Where were they when it happened?|Why didn't the guards stop them?|Were the guards asleep?","people"],["Did they ever catch the Brink's jewelry thieves?","In 2025, federal prosecutors indicted seven Los Angeles area men accused of following delivery trucks and stealing from them at truck stops. Some of the jewelry was recovered.","Did they catch them?|Did the robbers get caught, Mom?|Was the jewelry found?","today"]]},"ca205-frazier-mountain":{"n":"Frazier Mountain","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Frazier Mountain?","Frazier Mountain is 8,017 feet high. It's a broad, pine-covered peak west of the pass.","How high is it?|How big is that mountain, mom?|Height?","size"],["What did the Chumash call Frazier Mountain?","The Chumash call it Toshololo, the mountain of the east. It's named for its place east of Mount Pinos and tied to the morning star and the spring equinox.","What was it called before Frazier?|Did Native Americans have a name for the mountain?|Old name?","name"],["Why is it called Frazier Mountain?","Its English name honors William Frazer, a miner who worked here in the 1850s.","Who was it named after?|Who's Frazier, and why's the mountain his?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["What was Frazier Mountain's wood used for?","In 1854, lumber was cut from its trees for the new Army post at Fort Tejon.","Did people cut the trees on it?|Did they build something with the trees, dad?|Trees used for what?","history"],["Is there a town named after Frazier Mountain?","Yes, Frazier Park. It was founded in 1925 on the mountain's north side and took the mountain's name in 1926.","Is there a town on it?|Does anybody live on the mountain?|Any town there?","people"]]},"ca207-tejon-ranch-cattle":{"n":"Tejon Ranch cattle","k":"p","q":[["How big is Tejon Ranch?","Tejon Ranch is more than 270,000 acres, and it's the largest single piece of private land in California.","How big is it?|Is that ranch really huge, like how many acres?|How much land is that?","size"],["How many cattle are on Tejon Ranch?","Cattle graze about 250,000 acres of the ranch, with up to 12,000 head depending on the season.","How many cows are there on it?|How many cows can you see, like a million?|How many cattle?","nature"],["How did Tejon Ranch get started?","It grew from four Mexican land grants of the 1840s, which Edward Fitzgerald Beale bought between 1855 and 1866.","How did it begin?|Who owned that ranch first, way back?|Where did the ranch come from?","history"],["What else grows on Tejon Ranch besides cattle?","A little less than 6,000 acres grow pistachios, almonds, wine grapes, alfalfa and now olives.","Does it grow other stuff too?|Do they grow nuts or fruit there, like for snacks?|What crops?","nature"],["How can you tell winter is over at Tejon Ranch?","The ranch says you know winter is over when orange poppies spill across the hills and young calves trail their mothers.","How do they know spring is here on it?|What do baby cows do in spring, and what flowers pop up?|When is spring there?","kids"]]},"ca207-castac-lake-the-eye":{"n":"Castac Lake, the eye","k":"p","q":[["How old is Castac Lake?","Castac Lake formed about 10,000 years ago. Sediment washed down by Cuddy Creek dammed a valley along the Garlock Fault.","How old is it?|How long ago did that lake get here, Dad?|How old?","age"],["Why is it called Castac Lake, the eye?","The Chumash, Kitanemuk and Yokuts peoples all named it for an eye.","Why do they call it that?|Why is the lake called an eye?|Why the eye?","name"],["Who lived near Castac Lake long ago?","Several villages stood near its shores. One was Sasau, an Emigdiano Chumash village on the north side of the lake.","Did people live there?|Were there any villages by the lake, Mom?|Who lived there?","people"],["What happened at Castac Lake in 1772?","In 1772 Pedro Fages named it Salinas de Cortes.","Did anything happen to it in 1772?|Who named that lake first?|Did someone rename it?","history"]]},"ca210-fort-tejon":{"n":"Fort Tejon","k":"p","q":[["What was Fort Tejon used for?","Fort Tejon was an Army post from 1854 to 1864, sitting on the main route between the Central Valley and Southern California.","What was it used for?|What did the soldiers do there, mom?|What's Fort Tejon?","history"],["Did Fort Tejon really have camels?","Yes. From 1858 it was the western end of the Army's experiment with camels as pack animals. The soldiers found them hardy but temperamental, and they spooked the cavalry's horses.","Did it really have camels?|Wait, there were camels here?|Camels at the fort?","famous"],["What happened at Fort Tejon during the 1857 earthquake?","The great 1857 earthquake on the San Andreas Fault badly damaged most of the fort's adobe buildings, and the quake became known by the fort's name.","What did the earthquake do to it?|Did the fort fall down in an earthquake?|Earthquake at the fort?","history"],["Is Fort Tejon a park you can visit today?","Yes. Since 1940 it has been a state historic park, with restored barracks, 300-year-old valley oaks and living history demonstrations.","Can we still visit it?|Can we go inside the fort?|Is it a park now?","visit"],["Who was stationed at Fort Tejon?","The First U.S. Dragoons called it home until the Civil War. It was also a stop on the Butterfield Overland Mail.","Which soldiers lived at it?|Who lived in the fort, dad?|Who was there?","people"],["What hard history happened at Fort Tejon?","In July 1863, after soldiers killed hundreds of Paiute people in the Owens Valley, about 850 survivors were marched to Fort Tejon.","What else happened at it?|Did anything sad happen at the fort?|Any dark history there?","history"]]},"ca210-peter-lebeck-s-oak":{"n":"Peter Lebeck's oak","k":"p","q":[["Who was Peter Lebeck?","Almost nothing is known about him. The Catholic symbol on his carving suggests he may have been a French-Canadian trapper.","Who was he?|Who's the guy buried under that tree?|Who is Lebeck?","people"],["What happened to Peter Lebeck?","He was killed by a grizzly in October 1837 and buried under a valley oak. He is the earliest known victim of a bear attack in California.","How did he die?|Did a bear really get him?|What killed him?","history"],["What is carved into Peter Lebeck's oak?","Someone carved his epitaph into the tree: Peter Lebeck, killed by a X bear, October 17, 1837. Early settlers called grizzlies X bears.","What does it say on that tree?|What's written on the tree?|Is there writing on it?","see"],["Where is the original bark from Peter Lebeck's oak?","That piece of bark is now in the museum at Fort Tejon.","Can we see the carved bark?|Where did they put the bark?|Is it in a museum?","visit"],["Is the town of Lebec named after Peter Lebeck?","Yes, the nearby town of Lebec is named for him.","Is that why the town is called Lebec?|Why is the town named Lebec?|Lebec, like Lebeck?","name"]]},"ca210-the-grapevine-grade":{"n":"The Grapevine grade","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the Grapevine?","The name comes from wild California grapevines. In 1772 Pedro Fages called this canyon Cañada de las Uvas, the canyon of the grapes.","Why is it called that?|Why's it named after grapes, is that real?|Grapevine name?","name"],["How steep is the Grapevine grade?","It's a 6 percent grade that runs 5 miles and drops about 1,600 feet through Grapevine Canyon.","How steep is it?|Is it a really big hill, how high do we go down?|How big a drop?","size"],["What are those escape ramps on the Grapevine?","Escape ramps branch off the downhill lanes for big rigs whose brakes fail. You can spot them as steep ramps built just for trucks.","What are those ramps for?|What are the ramps for big trucks that fall off the road?|Escape ramps?","see"],["Do cars have trouble on the Grapevine?","In hot weather, car engines often overheat on the long uphill lanes of the Grapevine.","Do cars have problems on it?|Do car engines get too hot on the big hill?|Is it hard on cars?","nature"],["What happened at Dead Man's Curve on the Grapevine?","On the old road, the bend called Dead Man's Curve saw many wrecks. In July 1932, truck driver Jack Klieby steered his gasoline truck off the road to avoid oncoming cars and died when it burst into flames.","What happened there?|Why is it called Dead Man's Curve, what happened?|Dead Man's Curve?","history"]]},"ca215-california-s-first-reservation":{"n":"California's first reservation","k":"p","q":[["When did California's first reservation start?","It began in 1853, when these mountains became the site of California's first Indian reservation, the Sebastian, or Tejon, Reservation.","When did it start?|How old is that reservation, like for real?|What year was it made?","age"],["Who set up California's first reservation?","Edward Beale, the federal superintendent of Indian affairs, set it up on a private Mexican land grant.","Who set it up?|Who made that reservation, and why?|Who started it?","people"],["Who lived on California's first reservation?","These mountains are the ancestral homeland of the Tejon Indian Tribe, made up of Kitanemuk, Yokuts, Paiute and Chumash people. The state marker says 500 to 2,000 people lived there.","Who lived there?|Which people were on that land?|Whose land was it first?","people"],["What did people grow on California's first reservation?","Residents planted wheat, barley and corn, and Tejon Creek watered gardens, vineyards and orchards.","What did they grow there?|What kind of food did they farm?|Did they have crops and stuff?","nature"],["Why did California's first reservation close?","Drought, crop disease and settlers' cattle eating the crops wore it down. It closed in 1864, and many people were forced at gunpoint to the new Tule River Reservation near Porterville.","Why did it close?|What went wrong there?|How come it ended?","history"]]},"ca215-water-lifted-over-the-mountains":{"n":"Water lifted over the mountains","k":"p","q":[["How high does the Edmonston Pumping Plant lift the water?","It lifts California Aqueduct water 1,926 feet up and over the Tehachapi Mountains, sending it toward Southern California.","How high does it lift the water?|How far up do they push the water, anyway?|How high is the lift?","size"],["How powerful is the Edmonston Pumping Plant?","Apart from pumped-storage power plants, it is the most powerful water-lifting system in the world. At full capacity its pumps can send nearly 2 million gallons a minute uphill.","How powerful is it?|Is this thing really strong, like the strongest ever?|Biggest pump in the world?","famous"],["How big are the pumps at Edmonston?","There are 14 pumps. Each motor and pump unit stands 65 feet tall and weighs 420 tons, and the pumps reach six floors down.","How big are they?|Are the pumps taller than a house?|How many pumps are there?","size"],["How does the water get over the mountain at Edmonston?","Two giant pipes, 12.5 to 14 feet across, climb the mountain inside a tunnel 8,400 feet long. Each one always holds 8.5 million gallons of water.","How does it get the water over the mountain?|Do the pipes go through the mountain?|Where does the water go when it goes uphill?","see"],["How much electricity does the Edmonston Pumping Plant use?","Running every pump takes up to 787 megawatts of electricity, and it is delivered on its own power line.","How much power does it use?|Does it need its own power line?|Lots of electricity?","other"],["What happens to the water after it goes over the top at Edmonston?","Over the top, the aqueduct splits into the West and East Branches. Before reaching Edmonston, the water climbs in steps at three earlier plants: Buena Vista, Teerink and Chrisman.","What happens to it after it goes over the top?|Where does the water go next?|Does the water split up?","other"]]},"ca215-where-the-valley-begins":{"n":"Where the valley begins","k":"p","q":[["How big is the Central Valley that begins here?","It runs about 450 miles long and is 40 to 60 miles wide. It covers about 18,000 square miles, roughly 11 percent of California.","How big is it?|Mom, how huge is this valley?|How long is the valley?","size"],["How high up are we where the valley begins?","The freeway reaches the southern tip of the San Joaquin Valley at about 1,500 feet above sea level, at the foot of the Grapevine.","How high is it here?|Are we up in the mountains still, Dad?|What's the elevation?","size"],["How much does the road drop coming down from Tejon Pass?","Between Tejon Pass and here, the road changes elevation by more than 2,600 feet in about 12 miles.","How much does it drop to get here?|Why did my ears pop coming down?|How steep is the hill?","nature"],["What did the southern end of the valley look like before farming?","It was desert grassland with marshes and big lakes like Tulare, Buena Vista and Kern. Herds of tule elk and pronghorn lived here.","What was it like before the farms?|Did animals used to live here, Mom?|What was here before?","history"],["How much of the valley's natural grassland is left?","Only about 1 percent of the valley's original grassland is intact today, and more than 7 million acres are irrigated.","Is any of it still wild?|Is it all farms now?|Is any grass left?","today"],["Does the Central Valley have its own earthquake faults?","No. The valley has no earthquake faults of its own, though faults surround it on both sides.","Does it have earthquakes?|Can the ground shake here, Dad?|Any faults here?","nature"]]},"ca219-wheeler-ridge-a-growing-fold":{"n":"Wheeler Ridge, a growing fold","k":"p","q":[["What exactly is Wheeler Ridge?","Wheeler Ridge is a fold in the earth's crust that is still growing. It sits where the mountains meet the valley floor.","What is that long ridge?|Is it a mountain or a hill, Dad?|What's that ridge?","nature"],["Is Wheeler Ridge really still growing?","Yes. It is being pushed up over a thrust fault, and geologists have found that the fold is getting longer at its eastern end, toward the east-southeast.","Is it still getting bigger?|Wait, does the ridge really grow?|It's growing? Really?","nature"],["How do scientists know Wheeler Ridge is growing?","The evidence is written in the land. Streams crossing the eastern end bend east-southeast, deflected by the rising rock growing in front of them.","How can they tell it's growing?|How do they know it's moving, Mom?|What's the proof?","nature"],["What is the wind gap on Wheeler Ridge?","It is an old stream channel left high and dry on the crest. It sits at the same height as the canyon a nearby stream uses today, a sign the stream was pushed aside as the ridge rose.","What is that gap on top of it?|What's a wind gap, Dad?|Wind gap?","see"],["What fault system is Wheeler Ridge part of?","It belongs to the Pleito-Wheeler Ridge thrust fault system, which has been active since the late Pleistocene.","What fault is it on?|Is it on an earthquake fault, Mom?|Any faults there?","history"],["Is Wheeler Ridge connected to the 1952 earthquake?","Possibly. The White Wolf Fault caused the 1952 earthquake, and it may merge with the Pleito thrust fault at its southwestern end.","Did it cause an earthquake?|Was there ever a big earthquake there, Dad?|Earthquake link?","history"]]},"ca221-the-1952-earthquake":{"n":"The 1952 earthquake","k":"p","q":[["How strong was the 1952 earthquake?","It was a magnitude 7.3 quake, the strongest in California since 1906. It broke the White Wolf Fault near Wheeler Ridge at 4:52 in the morning on July 21, 1952.","How strong was it?|How big was that earthquake, Mom?|How bad was it?","history"],["Did anyone die in the 1952 earthquake?","Yes, it killed 12 people, and the mountain town of Tehachapi was hit hardest. A month later, a big aftershock killed two more people in Bakersfield.","Did it kill anybody?|Did people die in that earthquake?|Anyone hurt?","people"],["What damage did the 1952 earthquake do?","Rows of cotton in the valley fields were shifted sideways, one of them by 3 feet. Railroad tunnels with thick concrete walls also cracked.","What did it break?|What happened to the ground and stuff?|What got wrecked?","history"],["What happened to the railroad tunnels in the 1952 earthquake?","Tunnels southeast of Bealville cracked, and the gap between two entrances shrank by up to 8 feet. A crew of a thousand workers reopened the line in 25 days.","What happened to the tunnels in it?|Did the train tunnels get squished?|How long till the trains ran again?","history"],["How long is the White Wolf Fault?","The White Wolf Fault is about 56 miles long. The shaking in 1952 started at its southwestern end, near here.","How long is that fault?|How big is the crack in the ground?|Where did the shaking start?","size"],["Did the White Wolf Fault shake again after 1952?","Yes. A magnitude 5.8 aftershock hit a month later on August 22, and the same fault shook Wheeler Ridge again in 2005 with a magnitude 5.2 quake.","Did it ever shake again?|Did the ground shake again after that?|Any more quakes here?","history"]]},"ca221-start-of-the-west-side-freeway":{"n":"Start of the West Side Freeway","k":"p","q":[["Why did they build I-5 here instead of using Highway 99?","I-5 was chosen because it skips the valley's big cities, like Bakersfield and Fresno, for a faster, more direct trip.","Why didn't they just make that the Interstate?|Why didn't they use the other road, Dad?|Why not 99?","history"],["When did the West Side Freeway open?","The section from here to Los Banos opened in 1972. Construction began in the early 1960s and the whole freeway was finished in three phases, with the Sacramento link done by 1979.","When was it built?|How old is this road, Dad?|When'd it open?","age"],["What was the West Side Freeway like when it first opened?","It was a long, lonely road with no businesses beside it. The nearest towns were miles away and usually out of sight, and running out of gas was common.","What was it like back then?|Was it scary out here when it was new?|Was anything out here?","history"],["Where did all the travel stops along I-5 come from?","Travel stops grew up at the exits over the years. The road started out with no businesses beside it, so they were added gradually.","How did the stops get here?|Why are there stops at the exits, Mom?|Who built the stops?","today"],["Will the West Side Freeway get bigger someday?","Maybe. The wide median between Wheeler Ridge and Tracy leaves room to widen the freeway to six or eight lanes someday.","Will they add more lanes to it?|Can they make the road wider, Dad?|More lanes?","size"]]},"ca225-james-dean-s-speeding-ticket":{"n":"James Dean's speeding ticket","k":"p","q":[["What happened with James Dean's speeding ticket?","On September 30, 1955, a Highway Patrol officer pulled Dean over near here and wrote him a speeding ticket. He was driving north on Highway 99, headed to a sports car race in Salinas.","What happened with it?|Why did James Dean get pulled over?|What's the ticket story?","history"],["Where was James Dean going when he got the ticket?","He was on his way to a sports car race in Salinas. The Salinas Road Race was set for October 2.","Where was he headed?|Where was that guy going in his race car?|Where to?","history"],["What happened to James Dean after his speeding ticket?","Later that same day, Dean was killed in a crash farther along his trip. He was only 24 years old.","What happened to him after that?|Was he okay after the ticket?|Then what?","history"],["Who was James Dean and what was he famous for?","Dean was an actor who had made only three major films: East of Eden, Rebel Without a Cause, and Giant. Giant came out after his death.","Who was he?|Who was that guy, was he famous?|What movies was he in?","famous"],["Who wrote James Dean's speeding ticket?","The officer was O. V. Hunter of the California Highway Patrol. He wrote the ticket at about 3:30 in the afternoon.","Who was the cop?|Who pulled him over?|Which officer?","people"]]},"ca225-wind-wolves-preserve":{"n":"Wind Wolves Preserve","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Wind Wolves Preserve?","The name describes tall grass bending and parting in gusts of wind, like unseen animals running across the slopes.","Why is it called that?|Why is it named after wolves, are there wolves there?|Wind Wolves name?","name"],["How big is Wind Wolves Preserve?","It covers 93,000 acres of grassland, oak and canyon. Its owner, The Wildlands Conservancy, calls it the largest nonprofit nature preserve on the West Coast.","How big is it?|Is it a really huge park?|How many acres?","size"],["What animals live at Wind Wolves Preserve?","Tule elk live there. 19 were released in 1998 and the herd has grown to about 500. California condors also forage over it.","What animals can you see there?|Are there any cool animals there?|Any elk?","nature"],["What kinds of land are in Wind Wolves Preserve?","The land climbs from about 640 feet to more than 6,000 feet, through grassland, oak savanna, pinyon and juniper, and conifer forest.","What is it like inside it?|What does it look like there, is it all grass?|How high does it go?","nature"],["Who lived on the land of Wind Wolves Preserve before?","It is part of the homeland of Chumash, Yokuts and Kitanemuk peoples. Later it was part of an 1842 Mexican land grant, Rancho San Emidio, and a cattle ranch until the 1990s.","Who lived there first?|Did Native Americans live there?|Was it a ranch?","people"],["What can you do at Wind Wolves Preserve?","A spring nature festival brings guided hikes and wildflower viewing. Since 1996, more than 157,000 Kern County students have come for outdoor classes.","Is there anything to do there?|Can kids go there?|Any hikes?","kids"]]},"ca225-the-lakeview-gusher":{"n":"The Lakeview Gusher","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the Lakeview Gusher?","In March 1910 an oil well called Lakeview Number One blew out near Maricopa and would not stop. It gushed for 18 months and destroyed the derrick.","What happened there?|Mom, what was the oil thing that blew up?|What's the story?","history"],["How much oil came out of the Lakeview Gusher?","About 9 million barrels in all. It is often called the largest accidental oil spill in history.","How much oil came out of it?|Was it a lot of oil, like a whole lake?|How much oil?","size"],["How did they pick the spot for the Lakeview Gusher?","Legend says the spot was chosen because clumps of red grass were thought to mean oil. Drilling began on New Year's Day 1909.","How did they pick that spot?|How did they know where to dig for oil?|Why drill there?","history"],["Did they stop the Lakeview Gusher or save any oil?","Workers threw up sandbag dams and dikes and piped some oil to tanks 2.5 miles away, saving about 40 percent. It never caught fire.","Did they save any of it?|Did anyone try to stop the oil?|Did it catch on fire?","history"],["Is there anything to see at the Lakeview Gusher site?","A state landmark plaque marks the site off Highway 33.","Is there anything to see there?|Is there a sign or something?|Can we see it?","see"]]},"ca228-mountains-named-for-a-saint":{"n":"Mountains named for a saint","k":"p","q":[["Who is the saint the San Emigdio Mountains are named for?","They are named for Saint Emygdius, an early Christian martyr. He was a bishop in Ascoli Piceno, Italy.","Who is that saint it's named after?|Who was the guy these mountains are named after?|Which saint is this?","name"],["Why is a saint connected to earthquakes?","In 1703 a violent earthquake shook the region around Ascoli Piceno but spared the city. People gave Emygdius the credit, and from then on he was invoked against earthquakes.","Why do people pray to him about earthquakes?|Why is he the earthquake saint, mom?|What's the earthquake thing?","history"],["How high do the San Emigdio Mountains get?","The range tops out at 7,492 feet. Its highest peaks, including San Emigdio Mountain, Tecuya Mountain and Brush Mountain, are all above 7,000 feet.","How tall are they?|How big are those mountains, Dad?|How high up?","size"],["What mountain ranges do the San Emigdio Mountains connect?","The range links the Tehachapi Mountains with the Temblor Range. Temblor is Spanish for tremor.","What is it connected to?|Do these mountains go on forever?|What's next to them?","nature"],["Is there a creek in the San Emigdio Mountains?","Yes, San Emigdio Creek runs year-round. It carries the same name down San Emigdio Canyon.","Does it have any water?|Is there a river up there?|Any creeks?","nature"]]},"ca230-kern-lake-s-empty-bed":{"n":"Kern Lake's empty bed","k":"p","q":[["What was Kern Lake?","The flat fields here were once Kern Lake, the smallest of three big lakes at the south end of the valley. It was the first lake the Kern River fed.","What was it before it dried up?|Was there really a lake here, like a real one?|What's the deal with Kern Lake?","history"],["Why is Kern Lake empty now?","People diverted the Kern River for farms in the 1800s, and that left the lake a dry bed.","Why did it dry up?|Where did all the water go, kid-style?|What happened to the lake?","history"],["Did Kern Lake connect to other lakes?","Yes. Water ran west through a channel called Connecting Slough into Buena Vista Lake, and in wet years the two joined into one large lake.","Did it join up with anything else?|Did the lake ever run into another lake?|Was it connected to other water?","nature"],["How big was Kern Lake compared to the other lakes?","It was the smallest of three big lakes at the south end of the valley. Tulare Lake, to the north, was the largest of all.","Was it a big lake?|Was it the biggest lake in the valley?|How big was it, anyway?","size"],["Where did the water go when the lakes overflowed?","Overflow from Kern and Buena Vista lakes ran north into Tulare Lake. Tulare Lake spilled into the San Joaquin River in 19 of the 29 years from 1850 to 1878, but never again after that.","Did the lakes ever spill over?|Where did all that extra water end up?|Did it ever run out to the ocean?","nature"],["How did the Central Valley lakes get started?","All three lakes were remnants of a far bigger one, Lake Corcoran, which covered the Central Valley until about 600,000 years ago, when a new outlet broke through at San Francisco Bay and drained it.","Where did these lakes come from?|Was there a giant lake here once?|Was the whole valley underwater, like way back?","history"]]},"ca239-kern-county-nut-country":{"n":"Kern County nut country","k":"p","q":[["What kind of trees are in the Kern County nut country?","Many of the neat rows of trees here are almonds and pistachios. Look for orchards planted in long, straight rows.","What are those trees in rows?|What are those trees, Dad?|What trees are these?","nature"],["Are nuts Kern County's biggest crop?","Not quite. In 2022 Kern's most valuable farm product was grapes, mostly table grapes, followed by citrus, almonds and milk.","Is that the main thing grown there?|What's the top crop, Mom?|Biggest crop?","famous"],["How long has California been growing pistachios commercially?","Not very long. California's first commercial pistachio harvest came only in 1976.","When did it start?|When did they start the pistachio farms?|Pistachios new?","history"]]},"ca244-taft-once-called-moron":{"n":"Taft, once called Moron","k":"p","q":[["Why was Taft once called Moron?","Locals asked the railroad to name the station Moro, but the railroad feared confusion with Morro Bay and named it Moron instead, back when the word did not yet mean anything unkind.","Why was it called that?|Why did they name a town Moron?|What was Taft's old name?","name"],["Who is the town of Taft named after?","Taft is named for President William Howard Taft. The town took the new name in 1909.","Who was it named for?|Is Taft named after a president?|When did it get its new name?","people"],["How did Taft get started?","It began as Siding Number Two on the Sunset Railroad, built to carry crude oil out and bring drinking water in. It exists only because of nearby oil.","Why is there a town out here?|How did it begin?|What was Taft at first?","history"],["What can we see in Taft?","There's a bronze monument 37 feet tall of oil workers on a derrick in the middle of town. The West Kern Oil Museum keeps a historic wooden derrick.","Is it worth stopping there?|What's there to do in that town?|Is there anything cool to see?","see"],["Which exit do I take for Taft?","Take exit 244 and follow Highway 119 to Taft.","What exit is it?|How do we get to Taft?|Which road goes there?","exit"]]},"ca244-buena-vista-lake":{"n":"Buena Vista Lake","k":"p","q":[["What was Buena Vista Lake?","Much of the farmland here was once Buena Vista Lake. The Kern River fed it, and it was the second largest of the lakes in the Tulare Basin.","What was that lake out there?|Was there really a big lake here, Mom?|What lake was here?","nature"],["Who lived at Buena Vista Lake?","A Yokuts village called Tulamniu stood on the south shore, lived in for several centuries. Its people spoke Buena Vista Yokuts.","Did people live there long ago?|Did kids live by the lake way back?|Who lived there?","people"],["What happened at Buena Vista Lake in 1933?","The Smithsonian Institution excavated the village site in 1933 and 1934. It is now California Historical Landmark 374.","What did they do to that old village?|Did scientists dig up the village, Dad?|Why is it a landmark?","history"],["Is there still water at Buena Vista Lake?","Two small lakes, Lake Webb and Lake Evans, now sit on part of the old bed. They were created in 1973 for recreation.","Is it still a lake today?|Is the lake all gone now?|Any water left there?","today"],["Did Buena Vista Lake ever refill?","Yes, a little. In June 2023, after a snowpack three times normal in the southern Sierra, the Kern River began refilling the top of the old lake, a few feet deep.","Did it ever come back?|Did the lake fill up again, Dad?|Did the water return?","nature"]]},"ca246-crossing-the-kern-river":{"n":"Crossing the Kern River","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the Kern River?","John C. Frémont named it in 1845 for Edward Kern, the mapmaker of his expedition. As the story goes, Kern nearly drowned in its rushing water.","Why is it called that?|Who is the Kern River named after, anyway?|Why Kern?","name"],["Did the Kern River have other names before this one?","Yes. In 1776 the Spanish missionary Francisco Garcés called it the Río de San Felipe. Before Frémont, people locally called it Po-sun-co-la.","Did it always have that name?|What did people call the river before Kern?|Any old names?","history"],["Where does the Kern River start and where does it go?","It starts high in the Sierra Nevada northwest of Mount Whitney and drops through a deep canyon to Bakersfield. In flood years, extra water spills into the California Aqueduct.","Where does it come from?|Where does the river begin, Dad?|Where's it start?","nature"],["What happened to the Kern River in the 1857 earthquake?","Accounts say the great earthquake of 1857 briefly made the river flow backward.","What did the earthquake do to it?|Did the river really flow backward?|Earthquake river?","history"],["Is the Kern River in trouble today?","In 2022 American Rivers listed the lower Kern among America's most endangered rivers because its last 25 miles had dried up.","Is it endangered?|Is the river okay, Mom?|Is it in danger?","today"]]},"ca246-kern-water-bank":{"n":"Kern Water Bank","k":"p","q":[["What is the Kern Water Bank?","It's a huge area where water is spread into basins in wet years and soaks into the ground. It's saved underground for dry years.","What is that?|What's a water bank, anyway?|Water bank?","famous"],["How big is the Kern Water Bank?","It covers about 32 square miles of the southern San Joaquin Valley. It can store 1.5 million acre-feet underground.","How big is it?|Is it a really big place, like huge?|How much land is that?","size"],["How does the Kern Water Bank get the water back out?","About 8,800 acres are recharge basins, and 91 wells pump the water back up when it is needed.","How does it get the water out again?|How do they get the water back up?|Do they pump it?","today"],["What animals live at the Kern Water Bank?","When the basins fill in wet years they look like lakes, and wildlife moves in. The general manager has counted 240 kinds of birds and as many as 35,000 water birds.","What kind of wildlife is at it?|Are there lots of birds there?|Any animals there?","nature"],["Where does the Kern Water Bank's water come from?","It takes in water from the Kern River and from the state and federal water projects.","Where does it get its water?|Where does all the water come from?|Is it from the river?","other"],["Who owns the Kern Water Bank?","It's a public-private partnership. The largest share, 57 percent, is controlled by Stewart and Lynda Resnick, owners of brands including Pom Wonderful and Halos.","Who runs it?|Who's in charge of that place?|Is it public or private?","people"]]},"ca253-tule-elk-state-natural-reserve":{"n":"Tule Elk State Natural Reserve","k":"p","q":[["How many elk live at Tule Elk State Natural Reserve?","The reserve keeps a herd of about 30 to 35 tule elk on nearly 1,000 acres, and you watch them from a viewing platform.","How many are in it?|How many elk can we actually see there, mom?|How big is the herd?","nature"],["Why are tule elk special?","These elk are found only in California. About half a million once roamed the state, but by the 1870s they were thought to be extinct.","What makes them so special?|Are those elk different from other elk?|Why tule elk?","famous"],["How old is Tule Elk State Natural Reserve?","The reserve was established in 1932 as the Tupman Zoological Reserve.","When did it open?|How long has it been here?|When was it started?","age"],["Is it worth stopping at Tule Elk State Natural Reserve?","Yes, if you like wildlife. You can watch the herd from a viewing platform, and the park suggests bringing binoculars. The elk are most active from late summer through early autumn.","Should we stop at it?|Will we see real elk there?|Worth a stop?","visit"],["Did elk from the reserve start other herds?","Yes. Elk from here have helped start other herds, including those in the Owens Valley and near San Luis Obispo.","Did they spread to other places?|Are there elk anywhere else because of it?|Do other places have these elk?","history"]]},"ca253-elk-hills-and-teapot-dome":{"n":"Elk Hills and Teapot Dome","k":"p","q":[["What is the Teapot Dome scandal about the Elk Hills?","In 1922 Interior Secretary Albert Fall leased the Elk Hills to oilman Edward Doheny without competitive bidding. Doheny had quietly lent Fall 100,000 dollars, and the deal became part of the Teapot Dome scandal.","What was that scandal about?|Why is that a scandal, what happened?|What's the scandal?","history"],["Who went to prison over the Elk Hills oil deal?","Albert Fall was convicted in 1929 of taking bribes from Doheny. He became the first Cabinet member to go to prison, while Doheny was acquitted of paying them.","Did anybody go to jail for it?|Did somebody get in trouble, like go to jail?|Who got punished?","people"],["Why did the Navy own the Elk Hills?","In 1912 President Taft made the Elk Hills the nation's first Naval Petroleum Reserve. That meant the oil was saved for the Navy.","Why did the Navy have it?|Why does the Navy get hills, what for?|Navy oil?","history"],["How much oil has come out of the Elk Hills?","The field has produced close to 1.5 billion barrels of oil. Production peaked in 1981, when one oil pool alone yielded 42 million barrels.","How much oil did it make?|How many barrels came out of those hills?|Is that a lot of oil?","size"],["What do the Elk Hills look like from the freeway?","Look for long, low, bare hills along the west side of the valley. They sit over one of the most productive oil fields in the country.","How do we spot it?|What are those bare hills out there?|Which hills are they?","see"]]},"ca257-henry-miller-the-cattle-king":{"n":"Henry Miller, the Cattle King","k":"p","q":[["How did Henry Miller become the Cattle King?","He came west in 1850 as a butcher and teamed up with Charles Lux in 1858. Their company bought up Spanish and Mexican land grants and became California's largest cattle producer.","How did he get so rich?|How'd he get all those cows?|How'd he start?","history"],["How much land did Henry Miller own?","At its height his company owned about 1.4 million acres outright and controlled nearly 22,000 square miles across California, Nevada and Oregon.","How big was his land?|How much ground did he have?|How big was the ranch?","size"],["Was Henry Miller really his name?","No. He was born Heinrich Kreiser in Germany. He used the name Henry Miller from a ticket he bought in New York and kept it for life.","Was it his real name?|Did he have a fake name?|Real name?","name"],["How rich was Henry Miller when he died?","When Miller died in 1916, his estate was appraised at about 40 million dollars.","How much money did he leave?|Was he a millionaire?|How much was he worth?","famous"],["Do Henry Miller's descendants still live around here?","Some of his descendants still farm around Los Banos and in Kern County.","Is his family still around?|Do his grandkids still farm?|Still family there?","today"]]},"ca262-midway-substation":{"n":"Midway Substation","k":"p","q":[["What is Midway Substation?","It's a big electrical yard near Buttonwillow where Southern California Edison's power lines meet PG&E's grid.","What is that big electrical yard?|What's all that wire stuff for?|What is it?","other"],["Which way does the power flow at Midway Substation?","It depends on the season. In winter, power often flows north toward the Pacific Northwest, and in summer it flows south for air conditioners.","Which way does it send the power?|Does the electricity go up or down?|Where's the power going?","today"],["What is Path 15 at Midway Substation?","Midway is the southern end of Path 15, part of the Pacific AC Intertie. Path 26's three 500,000-volt lines also end here, crossing the Tehachapi Mountains toward the Antelope Valley.","What is Path 15 for that place?|Why do they call it Path 15?|What's Path 15?","name"],["When was the Pacific AC Intertie built, and why?","It was built in the 1970s and 1980s so California and the Southwest could use extra hydropower from the Northwest without building new power plants.","When did they build it, and why?|Why did they build all those lines way back?|How old is it?","history"],["What is Path 26 at Midway Substation?","Midway is the northern end of Path 26, three Edison lines of 500,000 volts that cross the Tehachapi Mountains toward the Antelope Valley.","What is Path 26 for that place?|Where do those big lines go over the mountains?|What's Path 26?","other"],["What lines connect to Midway Substation from the coast?","Two big lines from the Diablo Canyon Power Plant on the coast cross the coastal mountains to reach Midway.","Where do the power lines from the coast come in at it?|Does the coast plug into that place?|Which power plant feeds it?","see"]]},"ca262-the-buttonwillow-tree":{"n":"The Buttonwillow tree","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Buttonwillow?","The town is named for a single tree. Oddly, it is not a willow at all. It is a buttonbush.","Why is that town named that?|How did Buttonwillow get its name, huh?|Why Buttonwillow?","name"],["What is special about the Buttonwillow tree?","It was a lone landmark on an old trail across the valley. It is now California Historical Landmark 492.","What's so special about that tree?|Why is one tree such a big deal, is it magic?|Why is the tree famous?","famous"],["Who used the Buttonwillow tree as a meeting place?","The Yokuts used it as a meeting place long before settlers held stock rodeos beside it.","Who met at that tree long ago?|Did people hang out at the tree way back, like kids or what?|Who met there?","people"],["When was the town of Buttonwillow founded?","It was founded in 1895. It was first called Buena Vista, but the name quickly became Buttonwillow.","When did that town start?|How old is the town, is it really old?|How old is it?","age"],["What do people do in Buttonwillow today?","Cotton farming is its main industry, and locals call this cotton country. Nearby, Buttonwillow Raceway Park hosts sports car club races.","What is that town known for now?|What do they grow or do there, anything fun?|What's the main business there?","today"],["Is Buttonwillow a good stop for drivers?","Yes, it is a major stop for drivers, with gas stations, restaurants and travel centers at the Highway 58 exit.","Can we stop there on the way?|Is there stuff to stop at there, are we taking a break?|Worth a stop?","visit"]]},"ca262-california-s-balance-point":{"n":"California's balance point","k":"p","q":[["What is California's balance point?","It's the center of population of California. The Census Bureau finds it by imagining a flat, weightless map of the state with every resident given the same weight, then finding where it would balance.","What is that?|What's the balance point thing, Mom?|Balance point?","other"],["How does the Census Bureau find California's balance point?","They imagine a flat, weightless map of the state where every resident weighs the same. Then they find the spot where that map would balance.","How do they figure it out?|How can they find the middle of everybody?|How's it work?","history"],["Where is California's balance point today?","In 2020 it landed between Buttonwillow and Shafter. Since 1970 it has stayed within about 10 miles of Buttonwillow.","Where did it land in 2020?|Where's the dot now?|Current spot?","today"]]},"ca263-mckittrick-tar-pits":{"n":"McKittrick Tar Pits","k":"p","q":[["What are the McKittrick Tar Pits?","They're places where natural asphalt oozes out of the ground from the oil field below. The pits trapped animals during the ice ages.","What is it exactly?|What's a tar pit, anyway?|What's that black goo?","nature"],["What animals were found in the McKittrick Tar Pits?","Fossils found here include saber-toothed cats, short-faced bears, American lions, ground sloths, bison and mammoths.","What did they find in it?|What kind of dinosaurs were in there?|What fossils are there?","nature"],["Who used the asphalt at McKittrick Tar Pits?","Yokuts people mined the asphalt and used it to waterproof things, for decoration and for trade. Settlers began mining it in the 1860s.","Did people ever use it?|Did people live here, like Native Americans?|Who mined it?","people"],["What makes the McKittrick Tar Pits special?","They're counted among only five natural asphalt lake areas in the world, and three of those are in California: here, La Brea and Carpinteria.","Why is it famous?|Is it like the La Brea Tar Pits?|What's rare about it?","famous"],["Why is it called McKittrick?","The town is named for Captain William McKittrick, a local landowner and rancher. About 100 people live there.","Where does the name come from?|Who was McKittrick?|Who's the town named after?","name"]]},"ca268-cotton-country":{"n":"Cotton country","k":"p","q":[["Why does cotton grow so well in the San Joaquin Valley?","The valley has sandy loam soils, a long growing season without rain, big irrigation systems, and few pests like the boll weevil. That made it ideal cotton land.","Why is it good cotton land here?|Why do they grow so much cotton here, Dad?|Why cotton here?","nature"],["What is Acala cotton?","Acala is a strain developed at the cotton research station in nearby Shafter. It became so important that state laws in 1925 made it the only cotton allowed in key areas.","What was that special cotton called?|What's that Acala stuff, Mom?|Acala?","history"],["What happened in the 1933 cotton pickers strike?","More than 18,000 cotton pickers, mostly Mexican migrant workers, went on strike, and 3,500 lived in a four-acre camp outside Corcoran. The federal government stepped in to force both sides to negotiate.","What happened with that strike?|Why did the cotton pickers strike, Mom?|Tell me about the strike","people"],["How did machines change cotton farming?","After World War II, mechanical pickers sharply cut the need for hand labor.","What did they do about hand picking?|Do robots pick the cotton now, Dad?|Mechanical pickers?","history"],["Is cotton still grown in California today?","Yes, though it declined after 2000 as new insect pests arrived and other crops paid better. Kings County, to the north, is still California's top cotton county, growing mostly Pima cotton.","Do they still grow it around here?|Is there still cotton here, Mom?|Still any cotton?","today"]]},"ca268-the-temblor-range":{"n":"The Temblor Range","k":"p","q":[["How tall are the Temblor Range mountains?","The peaks average about 3,500 feet. The highest, McKittrick Summit, reaches 4,331 feet.","How tall are they?|Are those mountains really big, Dad?|Highest peak?","size"],["How old is the Temblor Range?","It's actually young. It rose mostly within the last few million years, pushed up by the San Andreas and other faults.","How old is it?|Are those mountains really old, Mom?|Old or young?","age"],["Why are there sea fossils in the Temblor Range?","Its rocks hold marine fossils because seawater once covered this end of the valley.","Why does it have fossils?|Were there really sea animals in those mountains?|Fossils up there?","nature"],["What happened to the McKittrick oil field?","Sometime in the last 1.8 million years, a huge block of shale slid down the range's northeastern side, traveled about 3 miles and buried the McKittrick oil field.","What happened there?|Did a mountain fall down, Dad?|Huge landslide?","history"],["Can you drive across the Temblor Range?","Yes. Highway 58 crosses the range at 3,750 feet, and Highway 46 uses its lowest pass, Polonio Pass, at 1,750 feet.","Can we drive over it?|Are there roads through those mountains, Mom?|Any passes?","see"]]},"ca273-san-joaquin-kit-fox":{"n":"San Joaquin kit fox","k":"p","q":[["What is the San Joaquin kit fox?","It's an endangered fox with huge ears that lives on the dry west side of the valley. Kit foxes are the smallest wild members of the dog family in North America.","What is it, that fox with the big ears?|Is that a real fox, or like a tiny dog?|What's a kit fox?","nature"],["What does the San Joaquin kit fox eat?","It hunts mostly at night, and mainly goes after kangaroo rats.","What does it eat, and when does it hunt?|Does it come out at night, and what does it catch?|What's its food?","nature"],["Where do San Joaquin kit foxes live today?","Its largest populations live in western Kern County, in and around the Elk Hills and the Buena Vista Valley. Some even live in Bakersfield.","Where can it still be found now?|Where do the foxes live, are there any near here?|Any left around?","nature"]]},"ca275-downhill-all-the-way":{"n":"Downhill all the way","k":"p","q":[["How does the California Aqueduct move water without pumps?","Gravity does the work. The canal is built on a slight grade, so the water flows downhill on its own along this stretch.","How does it move the water without pumps?|How does the water in the canal keep moving, anyway?|Why doesn't it need pumps?","nature"],["How much of the aqueduct runs downhill by gravity here?","From the Coastal Branch split south of Kettleman City, the water runs about 66 miles to the Buena Vista Pumping Plant, the first of four plants.","How far does that downhill stretch go?|How long is the part with no pumps, mom?|How far till the pumps?","size"],["What happens to the water after the Buena Vista Pumping Plant?","Buena Vista is the first of four plants that lift the water toward and over the Tehachapi Mountains.","Where does it go after that plant?|Does the water ever go uphill, dad?|What do the pumps do?","other"],["Who uses the water in the California Aqueduct?","The aqueduct carries water for about 35 million people and 5.7 million acres of farmland.","Who is all that water for?|Who gets the water from the big canal, mom?|What's it used for?","today"],["Why is the land sinking along the California Aqueduct?","Pumping groundwater has made land along parts of the canal sink. NASA's GRACE satellites measure the Tulare Basin sinking about a foot a year.","Why is the ground sinking near it?|Is the ground really sinking, dad?|What's sinking?","nature"],["Why is a sinking aqueduct a problem?","A sagging canal carries less water and costs more to run.","Why does it matter if that sags?|What's wrong if the canal sags, mom?|Why is sinking bad?","other"]]},"ca278-blackwells-corner":{"n":"Blackwells Corner","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Blackwells Corner?","It is named for George Blackwell, who started a rest stop there in 1921.","Why did they name it that?|Who was Blackwell, and why is the place named after him?|Why Blackwell?","name"],["What is Blackwells Corner famous for?","James Dean stopped there in 1955, and it is often described as the last place he was seen alive before his fatal crash near Cholame.","What is it known for?|Why do people talk about this place?|What's the big deal?","famous"],["How old is Blackwells Corner?","It goes back to 1921, when George Blackwell started a rest stop there.","How long has it been there?|How old is that place?|When did it start?","age"],["What happened to James Dean after Blackwells Corner?","Driving west near Cholame around 5:45 that evening, a driver coming the other way turned across his path, and Dean could not stop in time.","What happened to him after he left there?|Did something bad happen to James Dean?|How did James Dean die?","history"]]},"ca278-lost-hills":{"n":"Lost Hills","k":"p","q":[["How did Lost Hills get its name?","Lost Hills is named for the low Lost Hills range that sits right beside the town.","Why is it called that?|Why's this town called Lost Hills, Mom?|Name origin?","name"],["How many people live in Lost Hills?","Lost Hills is a small town of about 2,400 people.","How big is it?|Is Lost Hills a big town?|Population?","size"],["What do people in Lost Hills do for work?","Many residents work for the Wonderful Company, which has a pistachio and almond processing plant in town.","What do they do there?|What do the people in that town do all day?|Main jobs?","today"],["What is the lizard bridge in Lost Hills?","It's a green footbridge over Highway 46, shaped like a lizard. The Wonderful Company paid for it after a pedestrian was killed crossing the highway.","Why is the bridge shaped like that?|Why is there a lizard bridge?|Lizard bridge?","famous"],["What happens to the pistachios around Lost Hills?","Each fall the pistachio trees are shaken and the nuts are hauled to processing plants. At the Lost Hills plant, workers sort them by hand.","What do they do with the nuts?|How do they pick pistachios, Dad?|Pistachio harvest?","nature"],["Is Lost Hills near the freeway exit?","Lost Hills is at exit 278, and the town sits about 2 miles off the freeway.","Which exit is it?|What exit do we take for that?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca280-lost-hills-oil-field":{"n":"Lost Hills oil field","k":"p","q":[["How did the Lost Hills oil field get started?","In July 1910, two drillers named Martin and Dudley were digging a water well for livestock. At 530 feet they struck oil instead, and the Lost Hills oil field was born.","How did it get started?|How did they find the oil, anyway?|Who found it?","history"],["Who discovered the Lost Hills oil field?","Two drillers named Martin and Dudley found it in July 1910. They were drilling a water well for livestock in the low hills when they hit oil.","Who found it?|Who was the guy that found the oil?|Who dug it first?","people"],["What was the Bellevue blowout at Lost Hills?","In November 1998, a wildcat well hit gas under enormous pressure and burst into flame. The pillar of fire was about 340 feet tall and burned for 14 days.","What happened with that fire?|Was there a giant fire here?|What blew up?","history"],["Are the Lost Hills really sinking?","Yes. The hills rise barely 200 feet above the valley, and so much oil has been pumped out that parts of them have sunk as much as 8 feet.","Is the ground sinking there?|Wait, the hills are sinking?|Why are the hills getting lower?","nature"],["What trouble does the sinking ground cause at Lost Hills?","In 1976, a Getty Oil well collapsed into a crater more than 15 feet deep. It took its concrete pad and pump down with it.","What happened to the well that collapsed?|Did anything fall in a hole there?|What breaks when the ground sinks?","other"]]},"ca284-tule-fog-season":{"n":"Tule fog season","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called tule fog?","It's named for the tule marshes of the Central Valley. It's a thick ground fog that fills the valley's southern end from November through March.","Why do they call it that?|Why's the fog got a weird name?|Tule fog name?","name"],["When is tule fog season?","Tule fog can fill this southern end of the valley from November through March.","When does it happen?|When can we get the fog, mom?|What months?","other"],["How thick does tule fog get?","Inside it, visibility is usually less than 600 feet. It can drop to almost nothing within a few feet.","How bad can it get in it?|Can we see anything in the fog?|How far can you see?","nature"],["How does tule fog form?","It forms on long, calm nights after a good rain, when the damp ground cools. It's a low cloud trapped by the surrounding mountains and pressed down by warmer air above.","What makes it happen?|How does the fog get made?|Why's it foggy?","nature"],["How big does tule fog get?","It can stretch more than 400 miles, from Bakersfield all the way to Red Bluff. It can also linger for days or even weeks.","How far does it go?|Is it just here or everywhere?|How long does it last?","size"],["Is tule fog good for anything?","Yes. Almonds, cherries, pistachios and peaches need a long winter chill, and tule fog helps supply it.","Does it help farmers?|Is it good for anything?|Why do farmers like it?","nature"]]},"ca288-kern-national-wildlife-refuge":{"n":"Kern National Wildlife Refuge","k":"p","q":[["How old is Kern National Wildlife Refuge?","It was set aside on November 18, 1960.","How old is it?|How long has this place been around, mom?|When did it start?","age"],["How big is Kern National Wildlife Refuge?","It protects 11,249 acres of desert uplands, an old river corridor and marsh.","How big is it?|Is it a huge place, dad?|How much land is it?","size"],["What can we do at Kern National Wildlife Refuge?","You can drive its auto tour routes or walk its nature trails. It also has programs from waterfowl hunting to wildlife watching.","What can you do there?|Is there anything fun for kids to do there?|Anything to see?","visit"],["What animals and habitat does Kern National Wildlife Refuge protect?","Besides its marshes, it protects a remnant of the valley's original desert uplands and habitat for several endangered species.","What lives there?|Are there cool animals there, mom?|What's it protecting?","nature"],["What exit is Kern National Wildlife Refuge?","It's Exit 288.","Which exit do we take for it?|What exit do we get off at, dad?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca293-kern-and-kings-county-line":{"n":"Kern and Kings county line","k":"p","q":[["How did Kings County get its name?","It's named for the Kings River. Spanish soldiers called that river the River of the Holy Kings, which later became the Kings River.","How did it get that name?|Why is it called Kings, are there kings here?|Why Kings County?","name"],["Why is it called the River of the Holy Kings?","Moraga's Spanish expedition camped beside it on January 6, 1806, the feast of the Epiphany, when Christians honor the three wise men. So they named it El Río de los Santos Reyes.","Why did they name that river that?|What kings were they talking about, mom?|Holy Kings?","history"],["How did Kern County get its name?","Kern County takes its name from the Kern River. The county was formed in 1866.","Why is it called that?|Why is it named Kern?|Where does Kern come from?","name"]]},"ca296-valley-fever-in-the-dust":{"n":"Valley fever in the dust","k":"p","q":[["What is valley fever?","Valley fever is an illness caused by a fungus called Coccidioides that lives in the soil. People catch it by breathing in its spores. It is not contagious.","What is it, and how do people catch it?|What's that fungus in the dust, mom?|Valley fever, what's that?","nature"],["Can I catch valley fever from another person?","No. It is not contagious. People catch it by breathing in the fungus spores that rise from dusty ground.","Is it contagious?|Can I get sick from you if you have it?|Does it spread person to person?","other"],["What does grow and blow mean for valley fever?","The fungus grows in the soil after rain. Then dry, windy weather lifts its spores into the air, where people can breathe them in.","What does that grow and blow thing mean?|How does the dirt make people sick?|Grow and blow, what's that?","nature"],["Are valley fever cases getting more common?","Yes. Kern County had about 1,000 cases in 2014 and 3,000 in 2021, and California reported nearly 12,500 cases in 2024, the most the state has documented.","Is it getting worse?|Are more people catching it now?|How many people get it?","history"],["How can we avoid valley fever while driving through?","The CDC advises avoiding dusty places. During dust storms, stay inside with the windows closed.","What should we do about it in a dust storm?|How do we keep from getting it?|Should we close the windows?","other"]]},"ca300-the-coastal-branch":{"n":"The Coastal Branch","k":"p","q":[["How long is the Coastal Branch?","The Coastal Branch runs 116 miles. It splits off the California Aqueduct and heads for the coast.","How long is it?|How far does that water pipe go, Dad?|How long's that thing?","size"],["What is the Coastal Branch for?","It delivers water to San Luis Obispo, Santa Maria and Santa Barbara. It splits off the California Aqueduct and heads for the coast.","What does it do?|Why do they need that pipe, Mom?|What's it for?","today"],["When was the Coastal Branch built?","The first 15 miles, an open canal, were finished in 1968. The buried pipeline started in 1994, and the extension was finished in 1997.","How old is it?|Is that water pipe really old?|When'd they finish it?","age"],["How does the Coastal Branch get water up the hills?","It climbs through five pumping plants. They have names like Las Perillas, Badger Hill, Devil's Den, Bluestone and Polonio Pass.","How does it get uphill?|How does the water climb, Dad?|What are the pumps?","nature"],["How big is the pipe in the Coastal Branch?","Most of it is a buried pipeline 42 to 57 inches across. The first 15 miles are an open canal instead.","How wide is it?|Could I fit inside that pipe?|How big's the pipe?","size"],["Where does the Coastal Branch end?","A 42-mile extension, finished in 1997, carries water through Lompoc, Buellton and Solvang to Lake Cachuma in the Los Padres National Forest.","Where does it finally stop?|Where does that water go in the end?|Where's the end of it?","other"]]},"ca305-alpaugh-and-atwell-s-island":{"n":"Alpaugh and Atwell's Island","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Alpaugh?","The town is named for John Alpaugh, an officer of the land colony that developed it.","Who is that town named after?|Who was Alpaugh, anyway?|Alpaugh name?","name"],["What was Atwell's Island?","It was the largest island in Tulare Lake, and the town of Alpaugh sits on what was once that island.","What was it before it was a town?|Was Alpaugh really an island?|Island in a lake?","history"],["Who lived on Atwell's Island before the settlers?","The Wowol Yokuts village of Chawlowin stood on the island. After 1852, Yokuts families forced from their homes also hid there among the tules.","Did Native people live on it?|Who lived there first?|Any villages there?","people"],["What happened to Alpaugh when the lake came back?","When the lake came back in 2023, parts of Alpaugh were ordered to evacuate.","Did it flood when the lake returned?|Did the lake come back to it?|Lake return in 2023?","today"],["What's the water situation in Alpaugh today?","The town's tap water carries too much arsenic, so residents rely on bottled water.","Can people drink the tap water there?|Why do they drink bottled water?|Is the water safe?","today"]]},"ca305-colonel-allensworth-s-town":{"n":"Colonel Allensworth's town","k":"p","q":[["What is Colonel Allensworth's town?","It's the first California town founded, financed and governed by African Americans. Today it's preserved as Colonel Allensworth State Historic Park.","What is that place about?|What was that town, like, a real town?|What's Allensworth?","famous"],["Who started Colonel Allensworth's town and when?","Army veteran Allen Allensworth and four partners started it in 1908. They hoped to build a Tuskegee of the West, where Black families could own land, farm and run their own affairs.","Who started it and when?|Who made that town, kid-style, who built it?|Who founded it?","people"],["What did people do in Colonel Allensworth's town?","Farmers grew alfalfa, wheat, sugar beets and cotton. The town had a bakery, drugstore, hotel, library and a school that became California's first African American school district.","What did they do there for a living?|What was in that town, did it have stores?|What did they grow?","history"],["Why did people leave Colonel Allensworth's town?","Troubles piled up. The water table dropped, alkali salts spread, and the railroad moved its stop to Alpaugh in 1914. Many residents left after World War I.","Why did it end?|Why did everybody leave that town?|What went wrong there?","history"],["What can we see at Colonel Allensworth State Historic Park?","The park's restored buildings include the schoolhouse, hotel and general store. Every year it hosts a Juneteenth celebration and a rededication event in October.","What can you see there?|Is there stuff to see at that park, like old buildings?|What's in the park?","see"],["How did Colonel Allensworth's town get saved as a park?","A former resident, Ed Pope, led the push to save the site. The park was established in 1976.","Who saved it?|How did that old town become a park, kid asking, who fixed it up?|When did it become a park?","people"]]},"ca309-crossing-the-california-aqueduct":{"n":"Crossing the California Aqueduct","k":"p","q":[["What is the California Aqueduct?","It's a concrete river that carries water from the Sacramento-San Joaquin Delta toward Southern California.","What is that canal under the freeway?|Why is there a big concrete river here?|What's that ditch?","famous"],["How big is the California Aqueduct?","A typical stretch is 40 feet wide at the bottom and holds water about 30 feet deep. The whole aqueduct runs more than 400 miles.","How long is it?|How wide and deep is that water?|Is it really long?","size"],["Who is the California Aqueduct named after?","It is named for Governor Edmund G. Pat Brown, and it's the main feature of the State Water Project.","Why is it called that?|Who named it, kid?|Named for who?","name"],["Where does the California Aqueduct begin?","It begins at Clifton Court Forebay in the Delta, where the first pumps lift the water 240 feet.","Where does it start?|Where does all that water come from?|Start point?","history"],["How many times does I-5 cross the California Aqueduct?","I-5 crosses the aqueduct five times. Counting from the south, this is the second crossing.","Do we cross it again?|Are we going over it more than once?|Which crossing is this?","other"],["Could solar panels cover the California Aqueduct?","A 2021 study estimated that covering canals like this with solar panels could save 11 to 22 million gallons of water per mile each year from evaporation.","What could they do with it?|Why would anyone put solar panels on a canal?|Panels on the water?","today"]]},"ca309-ghost-of-tulare-lake":{"n":"Ghost of Tulare Lake","k":"p","q":[["What was Tulare Lake?","Tulare Lake was once the largest freshwater lake west of the Mississippi River. It lay to the east of here, but the rivers that fed it were diverted for farming, and by 1899 it was dry except for marshes.","What was it?|Was there really a giant lake here, Dad?|What lake?","history"],["What does Pa'ashi mean?","Pa'ashi means big water. It is what the Tachi Yokut Tribe calls Tulare Lake.","What does that name mean?|What did the Native people call the lake?|Who named it Pa'ashi?","name"],["Who lived around Tulare Lake?","For thousands of years the lake supported perhaps the largest Native population north of Mexico. The Tachi, Wowol and Chunut Yokuts lived around its shores.","Who lived there?|Were there Native kids living by the lake?|Who were the people here?","people"],["Did Tulare Lake ever come back?","Yes. In 1983 it took two years to dry out, and in 2023 storms brought back more than 114,000 acres of it. Yoimut, a woman who spent much of her life on the lake, had warned that it would return.","Can it come back?|Does the water ever return, Mom?|Did it flood again?","nature"],["What happened here during World War II?","During World War II, Navy flying boats used the lake as a backup landing place.","What did the Navy do with it?|Did planes land on the lake?|Navy planes landed there?","history"]]},"ca309-a-landfill-and-a-town-s-fight":{"n":"A landfill and a town's fight","k":"p","q":[["What is the Kettleman Hills landfill?","It's a landfill of about 1,500 acres for hazardous and household waste, run by a subsidiary of Waste Management. It sits off Highway 41, southwest of Kettleman City.","What is that landfill?|What's that big dump out there?|What's the landfill?","other"],["Why did Kettleman City fight the landfill?","When the company asked to expand, many of the town's roughly 1,500 residents fought it, fearing more PCB waste.","Why did the town fight it?|Why were people mad at the dump?|What was the fight about?","history"],["What did Greenaction report about Kettleman City?","In 2007 and 2008, Greenaction reported a cluster of birth defects and infant deaths in town and linked them to pollution sources, including the landfill.","What did the group say happened there?|What happened to the babies there?|What was the birth defects thing?","history"],["Did the landfill expansion get approved?","In 2014 state regulators approved the expansion and then denied two appeals. Opponents vowed to keep challenging it. The company says no link to the birth defects has been established.","Did they get to expand it?|Did the town win?|What happened with the expansion?","today"]]},"ca309-kettleman-city-halfway":{"n":"Kettleman City, halfway","k":"p","q":[["Why is Kettleman City such a popular stop?","It sits near the halfway point between Los Angeles and San Francisco or Sacramento, which makes this exit a major stop for food and lodging.","Why does everybody stop at it?|Why do we always stop here, mom?|Why a big stop?","visit"],["Who founded Kettleman City and when?","A real estate developer named A. Manford Brown founded the town in 1929, right after oil was found in the hills.","Who started it?|Who built this town, dad?|Who made it?","history"],["How many people live in Kettleman City?","About 1,200 people live in the town, which sits about a mile north of the travel stops, past the aqueduct.","Is it a big town?|Do lots of kids live in that town?|How big?","people"],["What did people do before Highway 41 existed near Kettleman City?","In the early 1900s, before Tulare Lake shrank, people traveled between Lemoore and Kettleman by ferry. As the lake receded in the late 1920s, that route became Highway 41.","How did people get across before the road?|Did they really use a boat here, dad?|Was there a ferry?","history"],["What kind of work do people do in Kettleman City?","Many residents work in agriculture, and most speak Spanish at home.","What do people there do for a job?|What do the grown-ups there do, mom?|Is it farm country?","today"]]},"ca314-kettleman-hills":{"n":"Kettleman Hills","k":"p","q":[["How big are the Kettleman Hills?","The Kettleman Hills are about 30 miles long and rise up to about 1,200 feet high.","How big is that range of hills?|Are those hills really long, or tall?|How tall are they?","size"],["Why are they called the Kettleman Hills?","They're named, with a spelling change, for Dave Kettelman, who grazed sheep and cattle here in the 1860s.","Why is it called that?|Who is Kettleman, and why are the hills named after him?|What's with the name?","name"],["How were the Kettleman Hills formed?","They're a long fold in the rock, squeezed up by tectonic forces, and they run parallel to the San Andreas Fault, farther west.","How did it get made?|How did those hills get here, anyway?|What are the hills made of?","history"],["Is there oil in the Kettleman Hills?","Yes. The range is two long domes, the North Dome and the Middle Dome, and each sits on an oil field. The North Dome's field alone is about 14 miles long.","Do they still pump oil out of it?|Is there oil under those hills?|Why do people drill there?","famous"],["Did an earthquake ever happen at the Kettleman Hills?","On August 4, 1985, an earthquake of about magnitude 6 caused slight damage in nearby towns. Some scientists suggest decades of oil pumping helped set it off.","Was there an earthquake near it?|Did the hills ever shake?|Did pumping oil cause a quake?","history"]]},"ca319-the-1928-kettleman-gusher":{"n":"The 1928 Kettleman gusher","k":"p","q":[["What was so strange about the oil at Kettleman?","It was so light that it was white. It reportedly could even run a car without being refined.","What was weird about that oil?|Why was the oil white?|Special oil?","nature"],["How long did it take to stop the Kettleman gusher?","The blowout took three years to bring under control. Thousands of spectators reportedly came to watch it.","How long did it take to stop it?|Could they not turn it off?|How long did it blow?","history"],["Why was Kettleman oil such a big deal for California?","The Kettleman North Dome became one of the most productive oil fields in the country. Its flood of oil pushed prices down and caused bitter feelings in the oil industry.","Why did that oil matter so much?|Why was it famous?|Why all the fuss?","famous"],["Is there still oil at Kettleman today?","Very little. Today less than half of one percent of the field's original oil is left.","Is it still pumping oil?|Did the oil run out?|Any oil left there?","today"]]},"ca319-avenal-the-oat-field":{"n":"Avenal, the oat field","k":"p","q":[["Why is the town called Avenal?","Avenal is Spanish for oat field. Spanish soldiers and explorers named it for the wild oats, waist high, that covered the plain like golden silk.","Why is it called that?|Why's it named oat field, mom?|What does Avenal mean?","name"],["Who built the town of Avenal?","Standard Oil laid out the town in 1929, after oil was found in the hills.","Who started it?|Who made this town, dad?|Who founded it?","history"],["What was Avenal's first post office?","Its first post office was a cigar box on the counter of the general store.","What was it like at the start?|Was the first post office a real building?|What was in the old general store?","kids"],["Why does Avenal have a prison?","After Avenal became a city, it asked the state for a prison, the first California prison a community actively sought. Avenal State Prison opened in 1987.","Why is there a prison there?|Did the town want a prison?|Prison in Avenal?","history"],["What is special about the gliderport in Avenal?","Avenal has a gliderport, one of the few airports in the western U.S. just for gliders.","What's a gliderport?|Are there airplanes there, dad?|Gliders in Avenal?","famous"]]},"ca322-garlic-country":{"n":"Garlic country","k":"p","q":[["Where does most of America's garlic come from?","Most of the garlic in the United States is grown in Fresno County. West side towns such as Huron picked up the slack as Gilroy ran out of room.","Where does it all get grown?|Where's the garlic come from, Mom?|Which county grows the garlic?","famous"],["How much garlic does Fresno County grow?","In 2022 the county harvested about 23,000 acres and 185,000 tons of garlic. That was nearly two-thirds of California's crop, worth about 350 million dollars.","How much of it do they harvest?|Is that a lot of garlic?|How many tons?","size"],["Why did garlic leave Gilroy?","Gilroy ran out of room. Urban growth spreading from San Jose pushed most of the garlic out, and Santa Clara County was down to about 300 acres in one count.","Why did it move away from there?|Why did the garlic go away from Gilroy?|Gilroy ran out of room?","history"],["How is garlic dried after it's harvested?","After the garlic is cut, it's left in the field to dry under its own tops. Those tops come to look like straw.","What do they do with it after cutting?|Why does the field look like straw?|How's the garlic dry out?","nature"],["Does the whole world get its garlic from here?","Not even close. Worldwide, China grows nearly three-quarters of all garlic.","Is that where all garlic comes from?|Does everybody's garlic come from here?|What about the rest of the world?","other"],["Is the garlic harvest in Fresno County still big?","Yes, it's stayed huge for years. The harvest was about 178,000 tons in 2020 and 187,000 tons in 2021.","Is it still that big now?|Did the harvest shrink over the years?|Still lots of garlic?","today"]]},"ca325-gates-substation-bottleneck":{"n":"Gates Substation bottleneck","k":"p","q":[["What is the Gates Substation?","It's a key junction on the power lines that link Northern and Southern California. You can spot it as a sprawling electrical yard east of the freeway.","What is that huge yard of electrical stuff?|What are all those towers for?|What's that power thing?","famous"],["What was the Gates Substation bottleneck?","Between here and Los Banos, the corridor once had only two of its biggest lines. That limited how much power could flow.","Why was that a bottleneck?|Why did the power lines have a problem here, mister?|What went wrong with it?","history"],["How did the Gates bottleneck relate to the 2000 electricity crisis?","It was one of the leading causes of the California electricity crisis of 2000 and 2001.","Did that cause the electricity crisis?|Was that why the lights went out back then?|Was it a big deal?","history"],["How much power could the lines carry at Gates?","Between Los Banos and Gates only 3,900 megawatts could flow. Elsewhere the lines could carry about 5,400 megawatts from south to north.","How much less could it carry than elsewhere?|How many megawatts could it hold?|What was the limit there?","size"],["Did anyone ever fix the Gates bottleneck?","Yes. A third 500,000-volt line was finished on time and under budget on December 21, 2004. Governor Arnold Schwarzenegger attended the commissioning ceremony.","Did they fix it?|Did they ever build more power lines, mister?|Is it fixed now?","today"]]},"ca325-huron-harvest-town":{"n":"Huron, harvest town","k":"p","q":[["How did Huron get started?","Huron began in 1888 as a water stop on the Southern Pacific Railroad. Joseph Mouren and his family later did much to grow the town in the late 1800s.","How did it get started?|Why is there even a town out here?|Who started Huron?","history"],["How many people live in Huron?","About 6,200 people live in Huron. At harvest time, when migrant farmworkers arrive, the town swells to more than 15,000.","How many people live there?|Is it a big town?|How many folks are in Huron?","people"],["What is Huron known for?","Huron is a farm town. In the 2000 census it had the highest share of Hispanic or Latino residents of any city in the United States.","What is it known for?|What's special about this town?|Why is Huron famous?","famous"],["What happened in Huron during World War II?","Three training airstrips around town served Lemoore Army Air Field. They were called Huron Field, West Field and Indian Field.","What happened there in the war?|Were there airplanes in Huron?|Was Huron in World War II?","history"],["Why is there a Mouren Drive in Huron?","Mouren Drive is named for Joseph Mouren. He and his family did much to grow the town in the late 1800s.","Who was Mouren?|Who is that street named after?|Who's Mouren?","name"]]},"ca327-arroyo-pasajero-bridges":{"n":"Arroyo Pasajero bridges","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the Arroyo Pasajero bridges?","On March 10, 1995, extreme rain sent fast floodwater down the creek. Both I-5 bridges failed, and four cars and a truck went into the creek. Seven people died.","What happened here at that creek bridge?|Why did the bridges fall down?|What's the story with these bridges?","history"],["Why did the Arroyo Pasajero bridges fail in 1995?","The flood scoured the creek bed down around the bridge supports. Water and debris then struck the columns at an angle and broke them.","How did the flood break it?|How does water knock down a bridge?|Why'd the bridges collapse?","history"],["Are the Arroyo Pasajero bridges the original ones?","No. Both of the bridges standing here today were built in 1995, the same year the old bridges failed in the flood.","Is it the same bridge that failed?|Did they build new bridges after the flood?|Are these bridges old?","age"],["Where does the creek at Arroyo Pasajero come from?","The creek starts in the Diablo Range near Benito Pass. It flows past Coalinga into the valley.","Where does that creek start?|Where does the water come from?|Where's the creek begin?","nature"],["What was Poso de Chane near Arroyo Pasajero?","Poso de Chane was an old waterhole and the site of a Native village the Spanish called the Chane. It was later a stop on El Camino Viejo, until the Great Flood of 1862 destroyed it.","Was there a village near that creek?|Did people live here long ago?|Who lived by the creek?","history"]]},"ca334-harris-ranch-inn-and-restaurant":{"n":"Harris Ranch Inn and restaurant","k":"p","q":[["When did the Harris Ranch restaurant open?","The Harris Ranch restaurant opened beside the freeway in 1977. It was aimed at local farmers, then became a popular stop for travelers.","How old is it?|When did this place start, mom?|How long has it been here?","age"],["How big is the Harris Ranch Inn?","The hacienda-style inn has 153 rooms. It was added in 1987, ten years after the restaurant opened.","How many rooms does it have?|Is that a big hotel?|How many rooms?","size"],["What does the Harris Ranch restaurant serve?","It serves the ranch's own beef, plus wine and other products the ranch makes. In the late 2000s it turned to a farm to fork menu.","What kind of food is it known for?|What do they cook there, Dad?|What's on the menu?","famous"],["Is Harris Ranch a popular stop?","Yes, it soon became a popular stop for travelers between Los Angeles and San Francisco. By 2008 the restaurant ranked sixth in California by sales.","Is it worth stopping at?|Do lots of people go there?|Is it a big deal?","visit"],["Is there an airport at Harris Ranch?","Yes, Harris Ranch Airport sits next to the inn. It has a single runway 2,820 feet long and has been open since 1981.","Can planes land there?|Why are there airplanes next to the inn?|Is there a runway?","see"],["What exit is Harris Ranch Inn?","Take exit 334. Look for the big hacienda-style inn right beside the freeway.","Which exit do we take for it?|How do I find it from the freeway?|What exit, quick?","exit"]]},"ca334-coalinga-oil-field":{"n":"Coalinga Oil Field","k":"p","q":[["What is the Coalinga Oil Field famous for?","It's one of only six California fields to have produced more than a billion barrels of oil. By 1910 it was the most productive oil field in the state.","What was it famous for?|Why is this oil field a big deal, mom?|What's special about it?","famous"],["What was the Silver Tip gusher at Coalinga?","In September 1909 the Silver Tip well blew in at 20,000 barrels a day, then the biggest gusher California had seen.","What happened with that gusher?|Was there a huge oil explosion here?|What's Silver Tip?","history"],["What did Native people use the oil seeps for here?","Long before drillers came, Native people here used tar from natural seeps to line baskets and to trade.","What did they do with the tar before drillers came?|Did people use the oil long ago, like cavemen?|Who used it first?","people"],["How did the local drillers fight back against Standard Oil?","They built their own pipeline to San Francisco Bay, working on a dummy pipeline in plain sight while the real one went in quietly.","What did they do about that?|Did the little guys beat the big oil company?|What's the dummy pipeline story?","history"],["What is the long ridge near the Coalinga Oil Field?","It's Anticline Ridge, a long, low ridge between the freeway and Coalinga that's part of the Coalinga Oil Field.","What's that ridge by it?|What's that long low hill, dad?|Name of the ridge?","see"]]},"ca337-coalinga-and-the-iron-zoo":{"n":"Coalinga and the Iron Zoo","k":"p","q":[["How did Coalinga get its name?","It began as a railroad coaling stop called Coaling Station A. Tradition says an official added an a to make it more musical, or a station sign may have squeezed Coaling A into Coalinga.","How did it get that name?|Why's this town called Coalinga, Mom?|Coalinga name?","name"],["When was Coalinga founded and why?","Coalinga began in 1888 as a Southern Pacific coaling stop for steam locomotives.","When did it start and why?|How old is this town, Dad?|Why'd they build it?","age"],["What is the Iron Zoo in Coalinga?","It's a collection of oil pumps painted as animals and characters. It grew to include a zebra, a giraffe, a bald eagle and a bobbing goat.","What is that Iron Zoo thing?|Are there real animals at the Iron Zoo?|Iron Zoo?","famous"],["Who painted the Iron Zoo oil pumps?","Jean Dakessian Jones began it in the early 1970s. By mid-1973 she had painted all 23 of Shell's pumps, then held a contest for local artists to paint 34 more.","Who started it?|Who made the pumps look like animals?|Who's the artist?","people"],["Can we still see the painted pumps in Coalinga?","Not all of them. Many of the painted pumps have since been shut down and taken apart.","Are they still there?|Do the animal pumps still work?|Are they all still around?","today"]]},"ca337-the-1983-coalinga-earthquake":{"n":"The 1983 Coalinga earthquake","k":"p","q":[["How strong was the 1983 Coalinga earthquake?","It was a magnitude 6.2 earthquake. It struck Coalinga at 4:42 on the afternoon of May 2, 1983.","How strong was it?|How big was that earthquake, Dad?|What magnitude?","history"],["What damage did the Coalinga earthquake cause to the town?","The eight-block downtown was almost destroyed, and 94 people were injured. The worst damage hit buildings with unreinforced brick walls.","What did it destroy?|Did the earthquake wreck the whole town?|Was anyone hurt?","history"],["How far away was the Coalinga earthquake felt?","The shaking was felt from the Los Angeles area all the way to Susanville, in Lassen County.","How far did it reach?|Did people far away feel it?|Who felt it?","history"],["Did the Coalinga earthquake break open the ground?","Not at the surface. The main shock broke no fault there; instead a ridge northeast of town rose about half a meter, roughly a foot and a half.","Did it crack the ground?|Did the ground split open, Mom?|Any fault break?","nature"],["Were there aftershocks after the Coalinga earthquake?","Yes, a lot. More than 5,000 aftershocks were recorded by the end of July.","Did it keep shaking after that?|Were there more quakes after it?|How many aftershocks?","history"],["Did the 1983 Coalinga earthquake hurt the oil fields?","Yes. Pumping units, tanks and pipelines were damaged, and 14 of 1,725 active wells had collapsed or broken casings underground.","What happened to the oil wells in it?|Did the oil pumps get broken?|Oil fields okay?","history"]]},"ca337-harris-ranch-feedlot":{"n":"Harris Ranch feedlot","k":"p","q":[["What is the Harris Ranch feedlot famous for?","It's California's largest beef producer, and the feedlot sits right beside the freeway. Many travelers notice it by smell first.","What's it famous for?|Why do people know that place, Mom?|Why's it so famous?","famous"],["How many cows are at the Harris Ranch feedlot?","It holds more than 100,000 cattle on more than 800 acres.","How many animals does it hold?|Whoa, how many cows are in there?|How many cattle?","size"],["Who started Harris Ranch and when?","Jack Harris and his wife, Teresa, started the ranch in 1937 on 320 acres of desert land that had never been farmed.","Who built it?|Who started that place, and how long ago?|Who made it?","people"],["What else does Harris Ranch grow besides cattle?","Its fields grow onions, garlic, almonds, pistachios, lettuce, tomatoes, citrus and melons. A thoroughbred division started in 1966 also raises racehorses.","What do they grow there too?|Do they grow anything else, like fruit?|Anything besides beef?","nature"],["What exit is the Harris Ranch feedlot?","It's at exit 337.","Which exit is it?|What exit do we take for that?|Which exit again?","exit"]]},"ca341-westlands-water-district":{"n":"Westlands Water District","k":"p","q":[["What is Westlands Water District?","It's the water district serving much of the west side of Fresno and Kings counties along this part of the freeway. It was formed in 1952.","What is it?|Mom, what's that big water district thing?|What's Westlands?","history"],["How big is Westlands Water District?","By irrigable acres it's the largest farm water district in the country, about 614,000 acres. Roughly 700 farmers own land there.","How big is it?|Is that a really huge farm area?|How many acres?","size"],["Where did the farms here get their water before the canal?","Farms here first irrigated from deep wells, starting around 1915. All that heavy pumping lowered the water table and made the land sink.","How did they water the crops before that?|How did the farmers get water back then?|Where'd the water come from?","history"],["When did federal water finally reach Westlands?","Federal water arrived through the San Luis Canal, which was finished in 1968.","When did it get canal water?|How did the water get here for the farms?|What's the San Luis Canal?","history"],["What happened at Kesterson Reservoir?","Salty drainage water ended up in the ponds of Kesterson Reservoir. It was shut off in the mid-1980s after selenium in it harmed water birds there.","What went wrong with that drain?|Why did the birds get hurt?|What was the Kesterson thing?","nature"],["Why is Westlands in so many California water fights?","Because of its size, it sits at the center of many of California's water fights. It also still has no outlet for its salty drainage water.","Why is it always in the news about water?|Why do people argue about that place?|Why's it so controversial?","famous"]]},"ca344-plane-wreck-at-los-gatos":{"n":"Plane wreck at Los Gatos","k":"p","q":[["What happened in the Plane wreck at Los Gatos?","A chartered DC-3 crashed in the Diablo Range near Los Gatos Creek on January 28, 1948. All 32 people aboard died, most of them Mexican farmworkers being sent back to Mexico.","What happened with that crash?|What was the plane crash about, mom?|What's the story?","history"],["Why is there a famous song about the Los Gatos plane wreck?","Woody Guthrie wrote a poem about the nameless victims, and a decade later Martin Hoffman set it to music. It's called Deportee, and Johnny Cash, Joan Baez and Bruce Springsteen have sung it.","Why is it in a song?|Who wrote the song about that crash?|Is there a song?","famous"],["Why were the victims of the Los Gatos crash nameless?","The first news reports named only the pilot, copilot, flight attendant and immigration guard. They listed the rest simply as deportees.","Why didn't anyone know their names?|Why were they just called deportees, mom?|No names?","name"],["Where were the Los Gatos plane crash victims buried?","The Mexican victims were buried in a mass grave in Fresno marked only Mexican Nationals. On Labor Day 2013, a headstone listing all 28 names was unveiled.","Where did they bury them?|Do they have graves, dad?|Is there a headstone?","history"],["How did the Los Gatos plane wreck affect Cesar Chavez?","News of the crash helped convince a young Cesar Chavez that farmworkers should be treated as important human beings, not as farm tools.","Did it matter to Cesar Chavez?|Why does that crash matter, mom?|Chavez connection?","other"]]},"ca345-joaquin-rocks":{"n":"Joaquin Rocks","k":"p","q":[["Why are they called the Joaquin Rocks?","They're named for the Gold Rush bandit Joaquin Murrieta, whose gang is said to have used them as a lookout.","Why is it called that?|How did the rocks get their name, mom?|Who is Joaquin?","name"],["What did people used to call the Joaquin Rocks?","They were once called Las Tres Piedras, which means the Three Rocks.","What was it called before?|Did the rocks have another name, Dad?|Old name?","name"],["How high up are the Joaquin Rocks?","The three stone pillars stand on a high ridge, about 4,100 feet up, and they can be seen for miles across the valley.","How tall is it up there?|Are the rocks really high up, Dad?|How high?","size"],["What can you see from the top of the Joaquin Rocks?","From their tops you look out over much of the San Joaquin Valley, including the approaches to Arroyo de Cantua, where the gang kept its rancho.","What does it look out over?|Can you see far from the rocks, Mom?|What's the view?","see"],["Is there a town named after the Joaquin Rocks?","Yes, a man named Raymond Minnite noticed the three rocks in 1954 and named the little town he founded there Three Rocks. About 250 people lived there in 2020.","Did that inspire a town?|Is there a town by the rocks, Dad?|Anyone live there?","people"],["How do we spot the Joaquin Rocks?","Look for three rock pillars standing on top of the high ridge to the west.","What should we look for?|What do the rocks look like, Mom?|How do I find them?","see"]]},"ca349-arroyo-de-cantua":{"n":"Arroyo de Cantua","k":"p","q":[["Who was Joaquin Murrieta, the bandit at Arroyo de Cantua?","Joaquin Murrieta was a Gold Rush bandit said to have hidden out at Arroyo de Cantua. Evidence that he was a real person is thin, but the legend grew anyway.","Who was he, that bandit?|Was there a real outlaw here, like in the movies?|Who's Murrieta?","people"],["What happened at Arroyo de Cantua in 1853?","On July 25, 1853, California Rangers led by Captain Harry Love attacked a band of armed Mexican men near here. They killed three and claimed one of them was Murrieta.","What happened there back then?|Was there a real shootout here?|What went down at that place?","history"],["Was the Murrieta story at Arroyo de Cantua actually true?","Nobody knows for sure. A letter to a San Francisco newspaper claimed the rangers had killed innocent mustang catchers, and evidence for a real Murrieta is thin.","Was it true, or is it just a legend?|Did they get the right guy, or was it fake?|Is the bandit story real?","famous"],["Where is the head of Joaquin Murrieta now?","It's gone. The head was destroyed in the 1906 San Francisco earthquake and fire.","What happened to it in the end?|Is the jar still around somewhere?|Where did the head go?","history"],["Who made the legend of Murrieta so famous?","An 1854 novel by John Rollin Ridge, a Cherokee writer, helped the legend grow, even though the evidence for a real Murrieta is thin.","How did it get so famous?|Did somebody write a book about him?|Who wrote about him?","famous"]]},"ca349-mendota-cantaloupe-town":{"n":"Mendota, cantaloupe town","k":"p","q":[["What is Mendota famous for?","Mendota is known as the Cantaloupe Center of the World. It's reached by Highway 33 from the freeway.","What is it famous for?|Why do they call it the cantaloupe place?|What's Mendota known for?","famous"],["What is the Delta-Mendota Canal?","It brings Delta water through 117 miles of canal from near Tracy. Since 1951 it has ended at the pool behind Mendota Dam.","What does that canal do?|Where does the canal water come from, and where does it go?|Delta-Mendota Canal?","today"],["Why does Mendota need canal water for its river?","The San Joaquin River bed is often dry here. The Delta-Mendota Canal pours in water to refill it.","Why does it need that water?|Is the river dry here, or what?|Why is the riverbed empty?","nature"],["Did outlaws really hang out near Mendota?","Yes. After the Gold Rush, a nearby settlement called Las Juntas was a haven for fugitives, visited by bandits Joaquin Murrieta and Tiburcio Vasquez.","Were there bandits at that place?|Were there robbers here, like cowboys?|Bandits near Mendota?","history"]]},"ca353-the-nation-s-top-farm-county":{"n":"The nation's top farm county","k":"p","q":[["Why is Fresno County called the nation's top farm county?","Fresno County was the number one farm county in the nation in 2017. Its crops and livestock were worth almost 8 billion dollars that year.","Why is it the top farm county?|Why is this the best farming place, Mom?|Top farm county?","famous"],["How much of Fresno County is farmland?","About 1.9 million acres are farmed here. That is nearly half of the whole county.","How much of it is farmed?|Is the whole county just farms, Dad?|How big are the farms?","size"],["What crops grow in Fresno County?","More than 300 crops grow here. The leaders range from grapes, pistachios and almonds to tomatoes, cotton and milk.","What do they grow there?|What are all those plants out the window?|What's growing?","nature"],["Why are there so many pistachio orchards in the Central Valley?","The hardy trees can thrive with the somewhat salty water and soil found in parts of the Central Valley. California's pistachio orchards grew from about 106,000 acres in 2002 to 554,000 in 2022.","Why do pistachios grow so well here?|Why do they grow so many nuts, Dad?|Why pistachios?","nature"],["How many of the country's pistachios come from Fresno County?","Fresno County alone grew about 40 percent of the country's pistachios in 2021.","How many pistachios does it grow?|Do we get a lot of our pistachios from here?|Pistachio share?","famous"]]},"ca355-the-farm-water-signs":{"n":"The farm water signs","k":"p","q":[["Why are there big signs in the fields along I-5?","Farmers say federal rules meant to protect smelt and salmon have cut their water, and the signs are their protest. They carry slogans like No Water Equals No Jobs.","Why do they put those signs up?|Why are there so many signs in the farms, Mom?|What's with the signs?","history"],["When did the farm water signs start going up?","Starting around 2008, big signs went up in fields along I-5. By June 2011 the Associated Press reported several hundred new signs along I-5 and Highway 99.","How old are those signs?|Have those signs been there a long time, Dad?|When'd they start?","age"],["Who put up the farm water signs?","Many came from Families Protecting the Valley, a loosely organized group of San Joaquin Valley farmers and other locals. Russ Waymire, a Hanford real estate broker and former farmer, put up the first sign with a friend.","Who made those signs?|Who's the person that put the signs up, Mom?|Who's behind them?","people"],["What do the farm water signs say?","Slogans include Congress Created Dust Bowl, No Water Equals No Jobs, Man-made drought, and Food grows where water flows.","What are the slogans on that?|What do the signs say, Dad?|Any funny ones?","see"],["Are the farm water signs telling the whole story?","Not everyone agrees. Environmentalists said the signs gave a false picture of the drought and its causes, and a congressional report found most of the supply cuts came from the drought itself.","Is that really true, what the signs say?|Do the signs tell the truth, Mom?|Does everyone agree with them?","other"]]},"ca357-the-1991-dust-storm":{"n":"The 1991 dust storm","k":"p","q":[["What happened in the 1991 dust storm?","A sudden dust storm hit this stretch of I-5 and dropped visibility to near zero. In a series of chain-reaction crashes, 104 vehicles collided, 17 people died and 150 were hurt.","What happened here with that dust storm?|Why did all those cars crash, Buddy?|What was the dust storm?","history"],["When was the 1991 dust storm?","It happened on November 29, 1991, the Friday after Thanksgiving. A sudden dust storm swept this stretch of I-5 north of Coalinga.","When did it happen?|Was that dust storm a long time ago?|What day was the dust storm?","age"],["Why was the 1991 dust storm so bad?","The valley was in its sixth year of drought and the rainy season had not begun. Fields near the road had been plowed and left bare, with no crops to hold the dust.","Why did it get so dusty?|Why was there so much dust, Buddy?|What caused the dust storm?","history"],["How strong was the wind in the 1991 dust storm?","The nearest weather station tracking wind, at Naval Air Station Lemoore, recorded gusts of 48 miles an hour.","How fast was the wind in it?|Was the wind really strong, Buddy?|How windy was it?","nature"],["How did rescuers respond to the 1991 dust storm crashes?","Rescuers reached the site within half an hour, even though a fire truck and an ambulance crashed on the way in. Several vehicles had caught fire.","Did help get there fast after it happened?|Did anyone come to help, Buddy?|Was there rescue?","history"],["How much of I-5 was closed after the 1991 dust storm?","About 150 miles of I-5 were closed for a day. Most of the crashes happened in five groups along a mile and a half of freeway.","Did they shut the road because of it?|Was the freeway closed for long, Buddy?|How long was the road closed?","size"]]},"ca362-the-diablo-range":{"n":"The Diablo Range","k":"p","q":[["How big is the Diablo Range?","It stretches from the Carquinez Strait, near the Bay Area, south to Polonio Pass, near Cholame. Its highest point is San Benito Mountain at 5,241 feet.","How big is it?|How far do these mountains go, Dad?|How long is that range?","size"],["What is the highest point in the Diablo Range?","San Benito Mountain is the highest point, rising to 5,241 feet in remote country to the southwest.","What's the tallest part of it?|What's the biggest mountain there, Mom?|Highest peak?","size"],["Why are the Diablo Range mountains so dry?","Other coastal ranges to the west catch most of the ocean's rain, so these mountains stay dry. They are mostly rolling grassland, chaparral and oak woodland.","Why is it so dry there?|How come those mountains look brown and grassy?|Why no rain?","nature"],["What Native peoples lived in the Diablo Range?","The range is part of the homelands of many Native peoples, including the Ohlone, Yokuts, Miwok, Amah Mutsun and Salinan.","Who lived in those mountains first?|Did Native Americans live there, Mom?|Whose homeland is it?","people"],["What strange animal lives in the Diablo Range?","A millipede found only in the southern part of the range, Illacme plenipes, can have as many as 750 legs. Until 2021, no animal known to science had more.","What lives in it that's special?|Is there a bug with lots of legs there?|Any weird creatures?","nature"]]},"ca365-el-camino-viejo":{"n":"El Camino Viejo","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called El Camino Viejo?","It means the Old Road to Los Angeles. It was an older road that ran along this side of the valley long before the freeway was built.","Why is it called that?|What does El Camino Viejo mean, Mom?|What's the name mean?","name"],["Where did El Camino Viejo actually go?","It ran between San Pedro Bay and the shore of San Francisco Bay at Oakland, crossing Tejon Pass from Los Angeles and coming down through the San Emigdio Mountains into the valley.","Where did it go?|How far did the old road go, Dad?|Where'd it start and end?","history"],["Who used El Camino Viejo?","Spanish ox carts used it, and later Californio vaqueros drove cattle along it. Mustang catchers herded wild horses on it, and it was also favored by people avoiding Spanish officials on the coast.","Who used it?|Who traveled on that old road, like cowboys?|Who was on it?","people"],["What were the creeks along El Camino Viejo called?","The names described what travelers found, like Creek of the Herons, Waterhole of the Pronghorns, and Creek of the Dust Storms.","What were the creeks called?|Were there any funny names on that road?|What names did they use?","nature"],["Is El Camino Viejo still used today?","Not as the old trail. Through Santa Nella, its route has been replaced by Interstate 5 and Highway 33.","Is it still there?|Can we drive on the old road now?|What replaced it?","today"]]},"ca368-the-sinking-ground":{"n":"The sinking ground","k":"p","q":[["Why is the ground sinking near Mendota?","Too much groundwater was pumped from under the fields. When that happens, loose layers underground compact, and past a certain point the ground cannot spring back.","Why is it sinking?|How come the ground goes down, like, for real?|Why's the ground sinking?","history"],["How far did the ground sink at the sinking ground?","About 28 feet, between 1925 and 1977. That's from all the groundwater pumped out from under the fields.","How much did it sink?|How far down did the ground really go?|How deep?","size"],["What is the famous photo at the sinking ground?","It shows scientist Joseph Poland beside a utility pole. Signs high on the pole mark where the ground surface was in 1925 and 1955.","What's that photo they mention?|Is there a picture of the pole with the signs?|Famous photo?","famous"],["Who was Joseph Poland, the man in the sinking ground photo?","He was a U.S. Geological Survey scientist who spent 50 years studying sinking land. He became known as Mr. Land Subsidence.","Who is he?|Who was the guy by the pole?|Mr. Land Subsidence?","people"],["How much of the valley has been affected by the sinking ground?","By 1970, more than a foot of sinking had spread across about 5,200 square miles, about half the valley.","Did it spread across the valley?|How big is the sinking area, like, huge?|How widespread?","size"],["Does the sinking ground cause problems for canals?","Yes. The sinking has cut how much water the Delta-Mendota Canal can carry and has meant expensive repairs to canals, including the California Aqueduct.","Did it hurt anything?|Did the sinking break stuff?|Any damage?","today"]]},"ca370-panoche-hills":{"n":"Panoche Hills","k":"p","q":[["How did the Panoche Hills get their name?","The name is thought to come from a local Native word for a cane that once grew in the valley beyond the hills. People made panoche, a kind of sugar, from that cane.","How did it get that name?|Why's it called Panoche, Mom?|Name origin?","name"],["What animals live around the Panoche Hills?","Panoche Valley, just west of the hills, is home to endangered animals like the San Joaquin kit fox, the giant kangaroo rat and the blunt-nosed leopard lizard.","What kind of animals live there?|Are there any cool animals out there?|Any wildlife?","nature"],["What birds can you see near the Panoche Hills?","The valley's grasslands are critical habitat, especially in winter, for birds such as golden eagles, ferruginous hawks, prairie falcons, mountain plovers and long-billed curlews.","Are there birds around there?|Are there eagles out there?|Birds there?","nature"],["Are there dinosaur-age fossils in the Panoche Hills?","Yes, the hills hold fossils of ancient cold seeps from the Mesozoic era, the age of dinosaurs.","Are there fossils in it?|Are there dinosaur bones there?|Fossils there?","history"],["Can you drive vehicles in the Panoche Hills recreation area?","Not all year. The Bureau of Land Management keeps motor vehicles out of its Panoche Hills recreation area from mid-April to mid-October, the fire season.","Can we drive there?|Can we take our car out there?|Cars allowed?","visit"],["Which exit is for the Panoche Hills?","The Panoche Hills exit is exit 370.","Which exit is it?|What exit do we take, Dad?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca372-panoche-creek-and-the-mercury-mines":{"n":"Panoche Creek and the mercury mines","k":"p","q":[["What makes Panoche Creek special?","It drains a larger area than any other stream on the eastern slope of the Diablo Range.","What makes it special?|Is that creek a big deal?|Why's it famous?","famous"],["How did people use Panoche Creek long ago?","It was part of a route between Native settlements on the central coast and the valley, and a watering place on El Camino Viejo.","How did people use it long ago?|Did anybody live by that creek?|Who used it way back?","history"],["What were the New Idria mercury mines?","They dug mercury from 1854 until 1972 and were once the second most productive mercury mines in the country. Gold Rush miners needed mercury to pull gold out of ore.","What were they mining there?|Why did they dig up mercury, Mom?|What was that mine for?","history"],["What is left of the New Idria mines today?","The mines left behind a ghost town with more than 100 standing buildings. Mercury from the old workings has also polluted the creeks that feed Panoche Creek.","What is left of it now?|Is there a ghost town up there?|Is there anything still there?","today"]]},"ca379-mercey-hot-springs":{"n":"Mercey Hot Springs","k":"p","q":[["How old is Mercey Hot Springs?","It has been a resort since the early 1900s. Native people knew the springs long before settlers ever came.","How old is it?|Has that place been around a long time?|How long has it been open?","age"],["How hot is the water at Mercey Hot Springs?","Water flows from one spring at 119 degrees Fahrenheit. A hot artesian well feeds the swimming pool.","How hot is it?|Is the water super hot?|How warm is the spring?","nature"],["What can you do at Mercey Hot Springs?","There are cabins, campsites, bathtub soaking pools and a swimming pool fed by a hot artesian well.","What is there to do there?|Can kids swim there?|What's at that place?","visit"],["What happened at Mercey Hot Springs during the Gold Rush?","It was a watering place called Aguaje Panochita, where mustang catchers held captured horses. The gang known as the Five Joaquins also stopped there while driving horses south.","What was it like back then?|Were there outlaws at that place?|Did cowboys come here?","history"],["Who built the hotel at Mercey Hot Springs?","San Francisco developer Frederick Bourn bought the springs in 1912 and built cabins and a hotel. The hotel burned in the mid-1930s and a bathhouse and restaurant replaced it.","Who built it?|Was there ever a hotel there?|What happened to the old hotel?","people"]]},"ca382-the-san-luis-canal":{"n":"The San Luis Canal","k":"p","q":[["What is the San Luis Canal?","It's the federally built section of the California Aqueduct. It carries water for both the state and federal water projects.","What is that big canal we're driving next to?|Is that a river or a ditch, what is it?|What's that canal?","other"],["How big is the San Luis Canal?","It has room for 13,100 cubic feet of water a second, which makes it one of the largest irrigation canals in the country.","How much water does it hold?|Is it a really big canal, how big is it?|How much water is in it?","size"],["Where does the San Luis Canal start and end?","It carries water from O'Neill Forebay to Kettleman City.","Where does it go?|Where does all that water come from and go to?|How long does it run?","other"],["How did the San Luis Canal change the land around here?","Little Panoche Creek shows it. Before irrigation its water may have reached a slough of the San Joaquin River in very wet years. Today it simply ends in the aqueduct.","Did it change anything out here?|Did the creeks here used to run somewhere else, kid-style: where did the water go?|What happened to the creeks?","nature"],["What does Little Panoche Creek's old name mean?","Its old Spanish name, Arroyita de Panoche, meant Little Sugarloaf Creek. It was also a watering place on El Camino Viejo.","Why was it called Arroyita de Panoche?|What did the Spanish call that creek?|What's Arroyita de Panoche?","name"]]},"ca385-firebaugh-s-ferry":{"n":"Firebaugh's Ferry","k":"p","q":[["Who was Andrew Firebaugh?","Andrew Firebaugh was a Virginian who ran a ferry across the San Joaquin River during the Gold Rush. The town is named after him.","Who was that guy the town is named after?|Who was Firebaugh, anyway?|Andrew Firebaugh?","people"],["What did Andrew Firebaugh build over Pacheco Pass?","In 1857 he built a wagon road with a gentler grade over Pacheco Pass. It replaced a steep, rough horse and mule trail near today's Highway 152.","What did he build there?|Did he build a road or something?|Pacheco Pass road?","history"],["What was the Butterfield Overland stage line connection?","The settlement of Firebaugh's Ferry became a station on the Butterfield Overland stage line.","What did it have to do with stagecoaches?|Were there stagecoaches there?|Stage line stop?","history"],["What is there to see in Firebaugh?","A water tank in its small downtown is painted with the city's name. The town sits on the San Joaquin River, where the old ferry crossed.","Is there anything to see in it?|What can we look at there?|Anything cool in town?","see"]]},"ca386-john-chuck-erreca-rest-area":{"n":"John Chuck Erreca Rest Area","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the John Chuck Erreca Rest Area recently?","Caltrans rebuilt both sides of the rest area in a 30 million dollar project. One side reopened in August 2025 and the other in May 2026.","What happened at it recently?|Why does this rest area look so new?|Did they fix it up?","history"],["What makes the John Chuck Erreca Rest Area special?","Caltrans calls it one of the most sustainable rest areas it has ever built, with utilities designed to use little water and power.","What makes it special?|Why is this rest area such a big deal?|What's so cool about it?","famous"],["How does the John Chuck Erreca Rest Area save water?","A small treatment system cleans gray water so it can be used again to flush the toilets. That cuts its need for water from the state system during droughts.","How does it save water?|Do they really reuse toilet water here?|How do the toilets work here?","nature"],["Why does the John Chuck Erreca Rest Area have Basque graphics?","The plaza's design was inspired by aerial views of farmland, and graphics honor the region's Basque history.","Why does it have Basque stuff on it?|What's the farmland pattern about?|What's the design supposed to look like?","history"],["Why is the parking at the John Chuck Erreca Rest Area light-colored?","The parking areas used more than 180,000 cubic feet of light-colored concrete. It reflects sunlight, which helps keep the air cooler.","Why is the concrete so light?|Why is the pavement pale?|Is it for staying cool?","other"]]},"ca388-the-san-joaquin-river":{"n":"The San Joaquin River","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the San Joaquin River?","The Spanish name comes from explorer Gabriel Moraga, who named one of its branches for Saint Joachim between 1805 and 1808. The name was in common use by 1810.","Why is it called that?|Who named the river, and why?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["Were there really salmon in the San Joaquin River?","Yes, a lot. Before irrigation, the San Joaquin and its branches had the third largest salmon run in California, likely 200,000 to 500,000 spawning fish a year.","Did it used to have a lot of salmon?|Were there lots of fish in the river?|How many salmon?","nature"],["Why does the San Joaquin River run dry in places?","Most of its water is held behind Friant Dam and sent into canals, so long stretches of the river often run dry.","Why is it dry sometimes?|Where did all the river's water go?|Why no water?","history"],["Are they trying to restore the San Joaquin River?","Yes. In 2009 the federal government began releasing water from Friant again to restore two dry stretches, about 64 miles in all, with salmon to be brought back.","Is anyone fixing it?|Are they bringing the salmon back?|Is the river coming back?","today"],["Did everyone agree with the San Joaquin River restoration?","Not everyone. Irrigators lost some water, and a lawsuit claimed the new flows were seeping through old levees onto farmland near Los Banos.","Was it controversial?|Did anyone fight it?|Who didn't like it?","other"]]},"ca391-dos-amigos-pumping-plant":{"n":"Dos Amigos Pumping Plant","k":"p","q":[["What does the Dos Amigos Pumping Plant do?","It lifts California Aqueduct water 118 feet. That single boost lets the water flow by gravity for about 95 miles, down to where the Coastal Branch splits off south of Kettleman City.","What does it do?|What's that big building by the canal for?|What's it for?","famous"],["How old is the Dos Amigos Pumping Plant?","It was built between 1963 and 1966 as part of the State Water Project.","How old is it?|When did they build that thing, like how long ago?|When was it built?","age"],["Why is it called Dos Amigos Pumping Plant?","Dos Amigos means two friends in Spanish. That is what the plant's name means.","Why is it called that?|Why does it have a funny name?|What does Dos Amigos mean?","name"],["How much water can the Dos Amigos Pumping Plant move?","It has six pumps that can move 15,450 cubic feet of water a second.","How much water can it move?|How many pumps does it have, like a lot?|How big is it?","size"],["Where does the Dos Amigos Pumping Plant get its water?","Its water arrives through about 16 miles of canal from O'Neill Forebay.","Where does the water come from?|Where's the water come from that goes in there?|Where's its water from?","nature"],["Is there a place to stop near Dos Amigos Pumping Plant?","Yes. A vista point called Dos Amigos sits beside the freeway nearby.","Can we stop near it?|Can we get out and look at it?|Is there a vista point?","visit"]]},"ca393-the-grasslands":{"n":"The Grasslands","k":"p","q":[["What are the Grasslands?","They're about 250 square miles of wetlands and grasslands along the San Joaquin River, named a wetland of international importance under the Ramsar treaty.","What is it out there east of the freeway?|What's that big wild area?|Grasslands, what's that?","famous"],["What birds can we see at the Grasslands?","Water birds, both resident and migrating. Each autumn more than 20,000 cranes and 60,000 Arctic-nesting geese fly in from Alaska and Canada.","What kind of birds are in it?|Are there lots of birds there?|What birds live there?","nature"],["Why do so many birds come to the Grasslands?","The Grasslands sit on the Pacific Flyway, the great bird migration route, and are important habitat for resident and migrating water birds.","Why do they all stop there?|Why do the birds fly all the way here?|Why so many birds?","nature"],["Why was Merced National Wildlife Refuge created?","It was set up in 1951 to draw wintering ducks and geese away from nearby farm fields, where they were damaging crops.","Why did they set it up?|Why did they make a bird refuge?|How old is that refuge?","history"],["Are there wildflowers at the Grasslands?","Yes. In spring, wildflowers bloom in rings around drying vernal pools, the kind of color that led John Muir to call the valley floor the floweriest part of the world he had seen.","Does it have flowers in spring?|Are there pretty flowers there?|Any flowers?","nature"]]},"ca397-path-15-power-lines":{"n":"Path 15 power lines","k":"p","q":[["What is Path 15?","Path 15 is part of California's main north-south power corridor. Its 500,000-volt lines run from the Los Banos substation to Midway, near Buttonwillow.","What is that line of giant power towers?|Why are there so many big electric lines out here?|What's Path 15?","famous"],["How powerful are the Path 15 power lines?","They are 500,000-volt lines. They run from the Los Banos substation, west of Los Banos, down to Midway, near Buttonwillow.","How much electricity do those lines carry?|Are those wires super dangerous?|How many volts is it?","size"],["What does Path 15 connect?","It links hydroelectric plants in the north with fossil fuel power plants in the south.","What is it for?|Where does all that power go?|What do the lines do?","today"],["Do the Path 15 lines ever cross the freeway?","Yes. In places the lines run beside the freeway, and the two eastern lines cross it twice.","Do those lines go over us?|Do the wires cross the road, Dad?|Do they cross it?","see"],["Which Path 15 line is the newest?","The newest line was finished in 2004 between Los Banos and the Gates substation near Coalinga. It runs apart from the older two, often out of sight.","When was that line built?|Which wires are the new ones?|Is any of it new?","age"],["What is special about the Path 15 wires?","The newest line is the only one in Path 15 that strings its wires in bundles of three.","Is it different from other power lines?|Why do some wires look funny, bundled together?|What's weird about them?","other"]]},"ca399-los-banos-the-baths":{"n":"Los Banos, the baths","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Los Banos?","Los Banos is Spanish for the baths. The town is named for pools known as the baths of Padre Arroyo, and later the creek, post office and town all took the name.","Why is it named that?|Why's the town called the baths, Mom?|What does Los Banos mean?","name"],["Who was Padre Arroyo?","The source doesn't say much about him. It says the pools were known as the baths of Padre Arroyo and were a regular stop for priests from Mission San Juan Bautista traveling into the valley.","Who was he, the baths guy?|Was there a real priest there?|Who's Arroyo?","people"],["Who was Henry Miller in Los Banos?","Henry Miller was a cattle baron who ran his valley empire from Canal Farm in Los Banos, set up in 1873. In the late 1800s he was one of the largest landowners in the United States.","Who was that rich cattle guy?|Who was the cowboy boss, Dad?|Henry Miller who?","people"],["What's the story with Uriah Wood and the ferryman?","Wood raced Miller to the land office in Stockton. After crossing the San Joaquin River, he paid the ferryman five dollars to hold the boat on the east side, and won his land for 45 cents an acre.","What did he do with the boat?|How'd he beat the cattle guy, Dad?|The five dollar ferry thing?","history"],["When did Los Banos move to where it is now?","The town moved to its present site in 1889, when the railroad arrived.","When did it move?|Did the town move, Mom?|Why did they move it?","history"],["Is there anything to see in Los Banos?","Downtown there's a 10-acre plaza honoring Henry Miller, with a big bronze of him and his cattle.","What's there to see in it?|Is there a statue or anything, Dad?|Worth a look?","see"]]},"ca400-tomato-country":{"n":"Tomato country","k":"p","q":[["Who runs the big tomato factories around Tomato country?","That would be the Morning Star Company, which was the world's leading tomato processor as of 2023. It opened its first tomato factory in Los Banos in 1990 and another at Santa Nella in 2002.","Who runs it all out here?|Whose tomato factories are those, Dad?|Who owns them?","people"],["What are all these tomatoes turned into?","They become products like tomato paste and diced tomatoes. Morning Star supplied about a tenth of the world's ingredient tomato products as of 2023.","What do they make with them?|What happens to the tomatoes, Mom?|What's it all for?","famous"],["How did the Morning Star tomato company get started?","Chris Rufer started Morning Star in 1970 as an owner-operator trucker, hauling tomatoes from fields to canneries.","Who started it?|Did some kid start that company?|How'd it begin?","history"],["What's special about how Morning Star's tomato company is run?","It's known for having no bosses. Workers define their own job duties, and pay is based on reviews from coworkers.","Is it a weird company?|Do they really not have bosses?|What's different about it?","famous"]]},"ca403-tule-elk-at-san-luis-refuge":{"n":"Tule elk at San Luis refuge","k":"p","q":[["How old is San Luis National Wildlife Refuge?","The refuge was established in 1967. It keeps a herd of reintroduced tule elk.","How old is it?|When did this place start, Mom?|When was it founded?","age"],["What makes tule elk special?","Tule elk are the smallest kind of elk. They were nearly wiped out in the 1800s, and this refuge has played a vital role in their recovery.","What's special about them?|Why are the elk so small, Dad?|Tiny elk?","famous"],["What else lives at San Luis refuge besides elk?","It's a major wintering ground and stopover for waterfowl and shorebirds on the Pacific Flyway. The land has wetlands, riverside woods, native grasslands and vernal pools.","What else is there besides the elk?|Are there other animals there, Dad?|Any birds?","nature"],["How big is San Luis National Wildlife Refuge?","It covers more than 26,800 acres of wetlands, riverside woods, native grasslands and vernal pools.","How big is it?|Is it a huge place, Mom?|Big or small?","size"],["How can we see the tule elk at San Luis refuge?","Start at the visitor center, which has an exhibit hall and a place to view tule elk. A Tule Elk auto tour route winds through the refuge, and in hot months mornings or early evenings are best.","How do we see them?|Where do we look for the elk, Dad?|Best time to spot them?","see"],["What is there to do at the San Luis refuge visitor center?","The visitor center has an exhibit hall with displays and a place to view tule elk. From there, a Tule Elk auto tour route and a Waterfowl route wind through the refuge.","What's at the visitor center?|Is there stuff to look at inside, Mom?|Worth stopping?","visit"]]},"ca403-pacheco-pass":{"n":"Pacheco Pass","k":"p","q":[["How high is Pacheco Pass?","Pacheco Pass sits at 1,368 feet. It's the main route through the hills between the Central Valley and the Santa Clara Valley.","How high is it up there?|Are we going up a really tall mountain?|How tall is the pass?","size"],["Who is Pacheco Pass named after?","It's named for Francisco Perez Pacheco, a Californio rancher.","Who was it named for?|Who was Pacheco anyway?|Whose name is that?","name"],["Why was Pacheco Pass called Robber's Pass?","In the 1850s travelers called it Robber's Pass because of frequent holdups.","Why did they call it that back then?|Were there really robbers here?|Why Robber's Pass?","history"],["What was Pacheco Pass Station?","It was a stop on the Butterfield Overland Mail stagecoach route, which linked St. Louis with San Francisco from 1858 to 1861.","What was that old station for?|Were there stagecoaches here?|Was there a mail route?","history"],["Is there a train tunnel planned under Pacheco Pass?","Yes, the planned high-speed rail line would run beneath the pass in the Pacheco Pass Tunnels, about 13 miles long and expected to be the longest rail tunnels in North America.","What's the high-speed rail tunnel there?|Will trains go under it?|Is it a big tunnel?","today"]]},"ca405-san-luis-reservoir":{"n":"San Luis Reservoir","k":"p","q":[["What is San Luis Reservoir?","It's the largest offstream reservoir in the nation. Most of its water isn't from a river at all. It's pumped in from canals fed by the Delta and stored for the dry months.","What is it, exactly?|Is that a lake or a dam?|What's that big water thing?","famous"],["How big is the dam at San Luis Reservoir?","B.F. Sisk Dam is 382 feet high and more than three and a half miles long. It holds back San Luis Reservoir.","How big is it?|How tall is that dam, anyway?|Is the dam huge?","size"],["What was here before San Luis Reservoir?","The valley it flooded is Yokuts homeland. Later it was part of Rancho San Luis Gonzaga.","What was under the water before it?|Did people live where the reservoir is?|What was there before the lake?","history"],["How does San Luis Reservoir work like a water battery?","When electricity demand is low, eight reversible units pump water up into the reservoir. When demand is high, the water runs back down through the same machines, making up to 424 megawatts.","How does it make power?|Does it make electricity?|What's the giant water battery?","today"],["Is San Luis Reservoir going to change?","A plan approved in 2023 would raise the dam 10 feet by 2032.","Are they changing it?|Will the dam get bigger?|Are they building more on it?","other"]]},"ca407-santa-nella-s-name":{"n":"Santa Nella's name","k":"p","q":[["Who was Saint Nella, and why is the town called Santa Nella?","There is no Saint Nella. The name is an English mangling of the Spanish word centinela, which means sentinel.","Who was she, that Santa Nella lady?|Is there really a saint named Nella?|Why Santa Nella?","name"],["Who started the ranch that gave Santa Nella its name?","Stockmen from San Juan Bautista and Monterey started the horse ranch here in 1810.","Who started that ranch?|Who had the horse ranch, like, way back?|Who founded it?","people"],["What happened to the horse ranch at Santa Nella?","It failed. Many of the horses escaped into the valley, and there was fighting with Native people.","What happened to the ranch, did it fail?|Did the horses run away?|Why'd the ranch end?","history"],["Why was Santa Nella an important stopping place in the old days?","It sat on El Camino Viejo. In the Gold Rush, the stage road from Hill's Ferry crossed San Luis Creek here on its way to Pacheco Pass.","Why did people stop here back then?|Was it on some old road, like for stagecoaches?|What was here before the freeway?","history"],["Is Santa Nella still a place for travelers to stop today?","Yes. Santa Nella is still mostly a place for travelers to stop.","Is it still a stopping place?|Do people still stop at it today?|Is it just a rest stop town?","today"]]},"ca407-pea-soup-andersen-s":{"n":"Pea Soup Andersen's","k":"p","q":[["How old is Pea Soup Andersen's?","This Santa Nella location opened in 1976. It was the second location of a roadside restaurant whose original opened in 1924.","How old is it?|Is this place really old?|When did it open?","age"],["Is this the only Pea Soup Andersen's left?","Yes. The 1924 original in Buellton closed in January 2024, just before its 100th anniversary, so this one became the only Pea Soup Andersen's left.","Is that the last one?|Are there more of them or just this one?|Is it the only one?","history"],["Who started Pea Soup Andersen's?","Danish-born Anton Andersen and his French-born wife, Juliette, opened the first cafe in 1924. The pea soup recipe was handed down in Juliette's family.","Who made it?|Who started it?|Where did the pea soup recipe come from?","people"],["What are Hap-pea and Pea-wee?","They're the restaurant's mascots, two cartoon chefs splitting peas with a hammer and chisel. They came from a cartoon by Charles Forbell.","Who are those two cartoon chefs?|Who are the little chef guys?|What are the mascots?","kids"],["What's the windmill at Pea Soup Andersen's?","It's a 77-foot working windmill that you can see from the freeway. It still turns whenever the restaurant is open.","Does it really work?|Is that big windmill real?|How tall is the windmill?","see"]]},"ca409-san-joaquin-valley-national-cemetery":{"n":"San Joaquin Valley National Cemetery","k":"p","q":[["Who gave the land for San Joaquin Valley National Cemetery?","The Romero Ranch Company donated the land in 1989. It sits on McCabe Road near Santa Nella, west of the freeway.","Who donated the land for it?|Whose land was this cemetery built on?|Who gave the land?","history"],["How big is San Joaquin Valley National Cemetery?","It covers 322 acres. By 2008, more than 30,000 burials had taken place there.","How big is it?|Is that cemetery really huge?|How large is it?","size"],["What is the Korean War memorial at San Joaquin Valley National Cemetery?","It's a circle of 16 granite pillars, each five feet tall, engraved with the names of 2,495 Californians who died in the Korean War. It was set up in 1998.","What is that memorial made of?|What are those tall stone pillars for?|What's the Korean War memorial?","see"],["What can we see at San Joaquin Valley National Cemetery?","There's the Korean War memorial of granite pillars, an 11th Airborne memorial, and a small museum displaying uniforms and medals.","What's there to see at that cemetery?|Is there anything to look at there?|What's on the grounds?","see"],["Who is buried at San Joaquin Valley National Cemetery?","Seaman William Troy, a Medal of Honor recipient for action against Korean forts in 1871, is buried here. So is boxer Mac Foster, a Marine sergeant.","Is anyone famous buried there?|Who's buried in that cemetery?|Any famous people there?","people"]]},"ca411-two-canals-side-by-side":{"n":"Two canals side by side","k":"p","q":[["What are the two canals side by side?","They are the state's California Aqueduct and the federal Delta-Mendota Canal. Both run side by side along the western edge of the valley between here and Tracy.","What are those canals out there?|Why are there two big ditches next to each other?|Which canals are those?","other"],["When was the Delta-Mendota Canal built?","The Delta-Mendota Canal was finished in 1951. Its builders worked three shifts a day, 21 hours a day, six days a week.","How old is it?|How long ago did they finish that canal?|When'd they build it?","age"],["How long is the Delta-Mendota Canal?","It runs 117 miles, from a pumping plant near Tracy to the San Joaquin River near Mendota.","How far does it go?|How big is that canal?|How long is it?","size"],["Why was the Delta-Mendota Canal built?","It came from a water swap. Friant Dam sends San Joaquin River water to the east side of the valley, so the canal brings Sacramento River water from the Delta to farms downstream.","Why does it exist?|Why did they dig that big ditch?|What's it for?","history"],["How does the Delta-Mendota Canal get its water?","It starts at a pumping plant near Tracy that lifts water 197 feet out of the Delta.","Where does it start?|How does the water get into it?|Where's the water come from?","nature"],["What went wrong with the Delta-Mendota Canal over the years?","The ground beneath it sank more than 6 feet along its southern 30 to 40 miles. In places bridges, pipes and even cattle guards ended up underwater.","Did something happen to it?|Why did the ground sink by the canal?|Did it break or sink?","history"]]},"ca416-kesterson-and-the-selenium-birds":{"n":"Kesterson and the selenium birds","k":"p","q":[["What happened at Kesterson Reservoir?","In 1983 scientists found that 64 percent of the water bird embryos and hatchlings there were deformed or dead. Selenium in farm drainage water had poisoned them.","What happened there?|Why were the birds dying, Mom?|What's the Kesterson story?","history"],["Where did the selenium at Kesterson come from?","It washed out of black shale in the Coast Ranges. It ended up in farm drainage water, which collected at Kesterson and evaporated, concentrating the selenium.","Where did it come from?|Why were the poisons in the water, Dad?|Where's the selenium from?","nature"],["What was the San Luis Drain supposed to be?","It was planned to run 290 miles and carry away salty water from under west side fields. Only 82 miles were built before work stopped in 1975.","What was it supposed to do?|Why did they build a drain, Dad?|How long was the drain?","history"],["What did the poisoned birds look like at Kesterson?","Birds hatched with missing or deformed eyes, beaks, wings and legs.","What happened to the birds in it?|What was wrong with the baby birds?|How were the birds hurt?","nature"],["What is the Kesterson effect?","It's what scientists call selenium's path from rock to duck.","What does that term mean?|Why is it called the Kesterson effect, Mom?|Kesterson effect?","name"]]},"ca418-gustine":{"n":"Gustine","k":"p","q":[["How did Gustine get its name?","Gustine is named for Sara Miller, the daughter of cattle king Henry Miller. Her middle name was Augustine and her nickname was Gussie.","Who is it named after?|Why is this town called Gustine, Mom?|Gustine name?","name"],["What is Gustine famous for?","In March 1970 Gustine became the first place in California with a 9-1-1 emergency phone system. It also has a Portuguese festa described as the largest in the nation.","What is it known for?|What's special about this town, Dad?|Famous for what?","famous"],["How old is the town of Gustine?","Gustine grew up in the early 1900s as a Southern Pacific station. Its post office opened in 1907 and it became a city in 1915.","When did it become a town?|How long has this place been here?|Gustine age?","age"],["What is the festa in Gustine?","It's a festa steeped in Portuguese tradition, and it is described as the largest in the nation.","What happens at that festa?|Is there a big party in this town?|Portuguese festival?","see"],["What is the Gustine High School basketball tournament?","Gustine High School hosts what is called the longest-running basketball tournament in California. The local Rotary Club puts it on, and it celebrated its 75th year in 2017.","How long has that tournament been going?|Is there a basketball thing here?|Tournament since when?","today"]]},"ca421-hills-ferry":{"n":"Hills Ferry","k":"p","q":[["What was Hills Ferry?","Hills Ferry was a river crossing where the Merced River meets the San Joaquin. It began in 1850 and grew into a busy grain shipping town.","What was it?|Mom, what was that place Hills Ferry?|What's Hills Ferry?","history"],["What happened to Hills Ferry?","When the railroad came through Newman in 1888, Hills Ferry quickly faded away. By 1976, not even the shadows of a ghost town were left.","What happened to it?|Where did the town go?|Why did everybody leave?","history"],["Did anyone famous cross at Hills Ferry?","Yes, the naturalist John Muir crossed the river here in May 1868 on his long walk from Oakland to Yosemite.","Did anyone famous go there?|Did somebody important come through there?|Any famous people?","famous"],["How big was the town of Hills Ferry?","By the late 1870s it was a busy grain shipping town of several hundred people.","How big was it?|Were lots of people living there?|Was it a big town?","size"]]},"ca423-newman-and-orestimba-creek":{"n":"Newman and Orestimba Creek","k":"p","q":[["Who founded the town of Newman?","Newman was founded in 1888 by Simon Newman, a businessman who came to California from Bavaria as a teenager in 1862.","Who started it?|Who was Newman, anyway?|Who built this town?","people"],["What's special about Orestimba Creek?","Orestimba Creek comes down from the Diablo Range and has the largest watershed of the 13 streams that feed the San Joaquin from its west side.","Why does that creek matter?|Is the creek a big deal?|What's it famous for?","famous"]]},"ca426-almond-bloom":{"n":"Almond bloom","k":"p","q":[["When is the almond bloom?","February is the best month to see it. That's when the almond orchards are pollinated, the largest managed pollination event in the world.","When do the almond trees bloom?|When do the trees get all the flowers?|Best month to see it?","visit"],["Why are there so many beehives in the almond orchards?","Most almond varieties can't pollinate themselves, so growers count on bees to carry pollen between the different varieties they plant.","Why do they bring bees to it?|Why are there bees everywhere?|Why all the bees?","nature"],["How many beehives come for the almond bloom?","More than a million beehives are trucked in, nearly half of all the hives in the country.","How many hives do they bring for it?|How many bees are there?|Lots of hives?","size"],["Where do the bees for the almond orchards come from?","Pollination brokers hire migratory beekeepers from at least 49 states to bring their hives to the orchards.","Who brings the bees for that?|Whose bees are they?|Who owns the bees?","people"],["How big is California's almond industry?","In 2017 California had about a million acres of almonds, producing 2.25 billion pounds of shelled nuts.","How many almonds does it grow?|How many almonds are there?|Lots of almonds?","size"],["Do almond trees really need bees?","Mostly yes, but after honey bees ran short around the turn of this century, government scientists developed almond trees that can pollinate themselves.","Do they still need bees for it?|Can the trees make almonds without bees?|Bees or no bees?","nature"]]},"ca428-crows-landing-airfield":{"n":"Crows Landing airfield","k":"p","q":[["When was Crows Landing airfield built?","The Navy broke ground on it on December 1, 1942, during World War Two.","When did they build it?|How old is this old airfield?|When'd it start?","age"],["What happened at Crows Landing airfield during World War Two?","The Navy trained pilots there in Wildcat and Avenger warplanes, and later added big patrol bombers.","What did they do there in the war?|What did the Navy do at that place?|Who used it in the war?","history"],["How did the town of Crows Landing get its name?","It began as a landing on the San Joaquin River, started by John Crow, who came west from Missouri by covered wagon.","Why is it called Crows Landing?|Why's it called that, are there crows?|Who was Crow?","name"],["What exit do we take for Crows Landing airfield?","Take exit 428. The old airfield is about 1.7 miles off the freeway, east of the road.","Which exit is it?|What exit number is that?|Exit for the airfield?","exit"],["Do the Crow family still live around Crows Landing?","Yes, members of the Crow family still farm walnuts there. The town moved four miles west in 1887 to be on the Southern Pacific railroad.","Is the Crow family still there?|Are there still Crows around there?|Do they still farm there?","people"]]},"ca434-patterson-the-paris-plan":{"n":"Patterson, the Paris plan","k":"p","q":[["Why does Patterson look different from other farm towns?","Thomas Patterson wanted his town to stand out, so he laid it out like Washington, D.C., and Paris, with circles and streets spreading out like spokes.","Why does it look so different from other towns?|Why is the town laid out in circles, Mom?|Why is Patterson shaped weird?","history"],["What trees were planted along Patterson's main streets?","The main streets were planted with palms, eucalyptus and sycamores.","What trees are on the streets there?|What kind of trees does the town have, Mom?|Are there palm trees in Patterson?","nature"],["When did Patterson become a city?","Sales of farms and town lots began in 1909, and Patterson became a city in 1919.","When did it become a city?|How old is this town, Dad?|When was Patterson started?","age"],["What is the Apricot Fiesta in Patterson?","It's held on the first weekend of June and has run since 1971. It started when California grew 95 percent of the nation's apricots.","What is that fiesta about?|Is there a party for apricots, Dad?|When is the Apricot Fiesta?","famous"]]},"ca434-diablo-grande":{"n":"Diablo Grande","k":"p","q":[["What is Diablo Grande?","Diablo Grande is a gated hillside community in the Diablo Range, about 9 miles southwest of Patterson, at 1,535 feet.","What is that place up in the hills?|Is Diablo Grande a real town?|What kind of place is it?","other"],["Who planned Diablo Grande and what was the dream?","Developer Donald Panoz dreamed of a resort town with up to 10,000 homes, six golf courses, a resort hotel and spa, vineyards and a winery.","Who started it and what were they building?|Who built that whole place?|What was it supposed to be?","people"],["How many homes were actually built at Diablo Grande?","Only about 350 homes were built, far short of the 10,000 that were planned. The owners filed for bankruptcy in March 2008.","Did they build all those houses there?|Did it ever get big?|How many houses are there?","size"],["What went wrong with the Diablo Grande plan?","A court rejected the environmental report by 1999, partly because it hadn't shown where the water would come from. After about 120 million dollars invested, the owners filed for bankruptcy in 2008.","Why did it fail?|Why didn't it work out?|What was the trouble with that project?","history"],["Are the golf courses at Diablo Grande still open?","No. A course designed by Jack Nicklaus and Gene Sarazen closed in 2014 during the drought, and the last course closed in 2019.","Can you still golf there?|Did the golf courses close?|Is it still a golf resort?","today"],["Did a fire ever threaten Diablo Grande?","Yes. In August 2020 the community was ordered to evacuate for about a day as the SCU Lightning Complex fires burned in the hills.","Was it ever evacuated?|Did it ever burn?|Was there a wildfire up there?","history"]]},"ca436-del-puerto-canyon":{"n":"Del Puerto Canyon","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Del Puerto Canyon?","Spanish travelers called its creek Arroyo de La Puerta, the Creek of the Door, for the narrow gap where it squeezes out of the canyon into the valley.","Why is it called that?|Why's it named that, Mom?|Name origin?","name"],["Who lived in Del Puerto Canyon long ago?","It is the homeland of the Hoyumne and Miumne, Northern Valley Yokuts peoples. They left grinding rocks and a path carved into the Gateway rocks at the canyon's mouth.","Who lived there first?|Were there Native Americans there, Dad?|Who were the first people?","people"],["Did outlaws really hide in Del Puerto Canyon?","Many place names there, like Hideout Canyon and Murderers Gulch, recall Gold Rush bandits. Joaquin Murrieta is said to have hidden wild mustangs up Adobe Creek before driving them to Sonora.","Were there bandits in it?|Were there robbers there, Dad?|Bandit hideout?","history"],["What wildlife did people see in Del Puerto Canyon?","In June 1862, William Brewer camped there and wrote of meeting a herd of thirty or forty antelope, as fleet as the wind.","What animals were there?|Were there cool animals there, Mom?|Any animals?","nature"],["What is special about the water in Del Puerto Canyon?","A spring up the canyon, Adobe Springs, gives magnesium-rich water that is sold to the 7 Up bottling plant in Modesto.","What's up with the spring there?|Is the water special, Dad?|Adobe Springs?","famous"],["What exit is Del Puerto Canyon on I-5?","Del Puerto Canyon is at exit 436. From there, Del Puerto Canyon Road follows the creek west into the mountains.","What exit is that?|Which exit do we take, Mom?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca438-the-2020-lightning-fires":{"n":"The 2020 lightning fires","k":"p","q":[["How big were the 2020 lightning fires?","The SCU Lightning Complex burned nearly 400,000 acres, more than 600 square miles, across six counties. It was one of the largest wildfires in modern California history.","How big was it?|Was that fire really huge?|How much burned?","size"],["When did the 2020 lightning fires happen?","They burned in August 2020. The SCU Lightning Complex ran from August 16 to October 1, burning through the Diablo Range west of the freeway.","When was it?|When did the fire start, and when did it stop?|What month was that?","history"],["Did the 2020 lightning fires burn Lick Observatory?","The fires reached Lick Observatory on Mount Hamilton on August 20, but the telescope domes did not burn.","Did it burn the observatory?|Did the telescopes burn up?|Is the observatory okay?","history"],["Did anyone die in the 2020 lightning fires?","No one died, though six people were hurt and 222 buildings were destroyed.","Did anyone die in it?|Were people hurt in the fire?|How many houses burned?","history"],["Was the burn from the 2020 lightning fires bad for nature?","A year later, a study of 6,000 acres of East Bay parkland found the burn was mostly good for the land. It spared most trees, renewed plants and set off sprouting of rare fire poppies.","Was it bad for the land?|Did the plants grow back?|What are fire poppies?","nature"],["What exit is the 2020 lightning fires stop?","It's listed at exit 438.","What exit is it?|Which exit do we take for that?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca441-the-westley-tire-fire":{"n":"The Westley tire fire","k":"p","q":[["What caused the Westley tire fire?","Lightning. It struck a huge pile of about six million old tires near Westley on September 22, 1999.","What started it?|How did a pile of tires even catch fire?|What lit the tires?","history"],["How long did the Westley tire fire burn?","It burned for 34 days. The melting rubber turned into at least 250,000 gallons of oil.","How long did it burn?|How many days was the fire going?|How long was it on fire?","history"],["How many tires were in the Westley tire fire?","About six million old tires were in the pile that lightning struck near Westley.","How many tires were in it?|How many tires burned up?|How big was the pile?","size"],["Why were so many tires piled up at Westley?","Waste tires had been stockpiled at the site since the 1960s. They were used to fuel a waste-to-energy operation.","Why was that pile of tires there?|Why did they have a mountain of tires?|Why all the tires?","history"],["How much did the Westley tire fire cleanup cost?","Cleanup was estimated to cost more than 20 million dollars. In April 2000 the state sued the landowners, tire haulers and operators to make them pay.","How much did the cleanup cost?|How expensive was it to clean up?|Who paid for the mess?","history"],["What damage did the Westley tire fire do?","It poured tons of smoke and pollutants into the air and threatened surface water and groundwater. It also left contaminated soil behind.","Was it bad for the environment?|What did the smoke do?|Did the fire hurt anything?","other"]]},"ca444-mount-diablo":{"n":"Mount Diablo","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Mount Diablo?","Mount Diablo is 3,849 feet tall. It stands alone on the skyline, with two peaks, and you can spot it on clear days.","How tall is it?|How big is that mountain, Dad?|Mount Diablo height?","size"],["What is Mount Diablo famous for?","Surveyors use it as a starting point. In 1851 its south peak became the base for land surveys, and land in most of Northern California and all of Nevada is still described from it.","What is it famous for?|Why is that mountain a big deal?|What's special about it?","famous"],["What does Tuyshtak mean for Mount Diablo?","The Chochenyo-speaking Ohlone call it Tuyshtak, meaning at the dawn of time. In Miwok and Ohlone tradition it was the place of creation.","What did people call it before?|What did the Ohlone call that mountain?|What's Tuyshtak?","name"],["Who lived on Mount Diablo long ago?","Most of the mountain, including its peak, was the homeland of the Volvon, a Bay Miwok-speaking people.","Who lived there first?|Did people live on that mountain, Mom?|Who owned it?","people"],["Is Mount Diablo still growing?","Yes, it is still growing. A hidden thrust fault beneath the mountain keeps pushing it up.","Is it still growing?|Wait, can a mountain grow?|Does the mountain get bigger?","nature"],["What was the Eye of Diablo on Mount Diablo?","It was a 10-million-candlepower aviation beacon Standard Oil put on top in 1928. It could be seen for a hundred miles, and Pearl Harbor survivors have relit it every Pearl Harbor Day since 1964.","What was that light on top?|Was there a giant flashlight on the mountain?|Eye of Diablo?","history"]]},"ca446-the-i-580-junction":{"n":"The I-580 junction","k":"p","q":[["Where does I-580 go from the I-580 junction?","I-580 heads for the Bay Area over Altamont Pass, a major crossing of the Diablo Range. It runs about 76 miles in all, from San Rafael through Oakland to this meeting with I-5.","Where does it go from here?|Where does that road take us, Buddy?|Where's it lead?","other"],["How long is I-580?","I-580 runs about 76 miles, from San Rafael through Oakland to this meeting with I-5 south of Tracy.","How long is it in total?|How far does that whole road go?|How many miles?","size"],["Why is the I-580 junction important?","Together with I-5, I-580 is a main route between the Bay Area and Southern California.","Why does it matter?|Why do so many people use that road?|Is it a big deal?","famous"],["Who is the William Elton Brownie Brown Freeway named for?","A stretch of I-580 is named for a Tracy man who helped decide the route of I-5 through the San Joaquin Valley.","Who is it named after?|Who was Brownie Brown, Buddy?|Who's that guy?","name"],["When was this part of I-580 built?","The piece between the I-205 split and this junction was built in the late 1960s and early 1970s on new right-of-way through low hills near Patterson.","How old is it?|Was that road built a long time ago?|When'd they build it?","age"],["Is the I-580 junction on a scenic highway?","Yes. That piece of I-580 is an official state scenic highway, and so is I-5 from Highway 152 near Santa Nella to this junction, the only stretch of I-5 the state has named one.","Is it a scenic road?|Is that stretch pretty, Buddy?|Is it scenic?","see"]]},"ca449-vernalis":{"n":"Vernalis","k":"p","q":[["Why is Vernalis called Vernalis?","Vernalis is Latin for springlike. But a local historian, Earle Williams, said it was named after two ranchers' daughters, Verna Carpenter and Alice Hamilton.","Why is it called that?|Why does this town have such a weird name?|What's the name mean?","name"],["What happened in Vernalis during World War Two?","Vernalis had a Navy airfield, Naval Auxiliary Air Station Vernalis. It also had a reconditioning center and a camp for prisoners of war.","What was it used for in the war?|Was there a war thing here, like soldiers or planes?|Vernalis in WWII?","history"],["When did the Vernalis post office start?","Its post office began in 1851 at nearby San Joaquin City. It moved to Vernalis in 1888.","How old is it?|Is this an old town, like how long ago?|When did it start?","age"],["What was San Joaquin City, near Vernalis?","It was probably the largest of the boomtowns that sprang up after the Gold Rush. Founded in 1851, it kept its size until 1880, when competition from Stockton drew its trade away.","What happened to that old town nearby?|Was there a boomtown near here, kids?|San Joaquin City?","history"],["Which exit do we take for Vernalis?","Take exit 449 and follow Highway 33. Vernalis is east of the freeway.","What exit is it?|How do we get to that town, which road?|Vernalis exit?","exit"]]},"ca449-san-joaquin-river-refuge":{"n":"San Joaquin River refuge","k":"p","q":[["What is the San Joaquin River refuge for?","It protects more than 7,000 acres of river woodland, wetlands and grassland where the Tuolumne and Stanislaus rivers join the San Joaquin.","What is it for?|Why do they have this place, Mom?|What's it protect?","famous"],["When was the San Joaquin River refuge set up?","It was set up in 1987 to protect the winter home of the Aleutian cackling goose, which was endangered at the time.","How old is it?|Is this place really old?|When'd it start?","age"],["What animals can we see at the San Joaquin River refuge?","Swainson's hawks nest in tall cottonwoods, and herons and cormorants nest together in big oaks. From autumn through spring, big flocks of geese and sandhill cranes gather.","What lives there?|Are there cool animals there?|Any birds?","nature"],["When is the best time to visit the San Joaquin River refuge?","The best months are November through February. That fits the season when big flocks of geese and sandhill cranes gather there, from autumn through spring.","When should we go there?|Is there a good season for it?|Best months?","visit"],["What is special about the trees at the San Joaquin River refuge?","A nonprofit called River Partners led the planting of about 400,000 native trees across 1,700 acres of floodplain, one of California's largest projects to bring back streamside forest.","Did somebody plant them?|Are the trees real or did people put them there?|Why so many trees?","history"],["What happened to the rabbits at the San Joaquin River refuge?","Endangered riparian brush rabbits, raised in captivity, have been returned to their old habitat there.","Are there rabbits there?|Do bunnies live there?|Rabbits?","nature"]]},"ca451-estanislao-s-river":{"n":"Estanislao's river","k":"p","q":[["Who was Estanislao, the man this river is named for?","Estanislao was the baptized name of Cucunuchi, a leader of the Lakisamni, a Yokuts people. He was also a Native official at Mission San Jose.","Who was he?|Who's the guy the river is named after?|Who was Estanislao?","people"],["What did Estanislao do that made him so famous?","In the late 1820s he left the mission with about 400 followers and led raids on missions and settlers, until a Mexican army under Mariano Vallejo drove his people from the river in 1829.","What was he famous for?|What did that guy do, did he fight someone?|Why do people remember him?","famous"],["Was Estanislao connected to Zorro?","He is thought to be one of the inspirations for Zorro. Legend says he carved an S to mark his raids.","Is it true he inspired Zorro?|Was he like the real Zorro?|Zorro, really?","famous"],["How did Estanislao train his fighters?","He used tactics learned from Spanish and Mexican soldiers, digging trenches and building palisades. At one point he led as many as 4,000 men.","How did it work, did he have an army?|How big was his army, like soldiers and forts?|Did he build forts?","history"],["What happened to Estanislao in the end?","He was pardoned in 1829. He spent his last years at Mission San Jose teaching others the Yokuts language and culture until his death in 1838.","What happened to him after that?|Did he get in trouble, or get away?|How did it end?","people"]]},"ca457-banta":{"n":"Banta","k":"p","q":[["How old is Banta?","Banta is a settlement dating to the 1840s, so it goes back to stagecoach days.","How old is it?|Is that town really old, like ancient?|When did Banta start?","age"],["Why is the town called Banta?","Henry Banta bought its inn in 1863 and changed the sign to Banta's White House. Travelers soon called the whole place Bantas.","Why is it called that?|Who is Banta, anyway?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["What was Banta used for in stagecoach days?","In stagecoach days it was the last stop before Altamont Pass on the road between Stockton and the Bay Area.","What did it do back then?|Did cowboys stop there?|What was it for in the old days?","history"],["Did a railroad ever come through Banta?","Yes, in 1869 it became a stop on the new Western Pacific Railroad. In 1878 the railroad built its junction at Tracy instead, and Banta went quiet.","Did trains stop there?|Were there choo-choo trains in Banta?|Train town?","history"],["Is the Banta Inn haunted?","Some locals say so. The Banta Inn, established in 1879, is said by some locals to be haunted.","Is it haunted?|Are there ghosts in that old inn?|Spooky place?","famous"],["Did anyone famous live in Banta?","Yes, Steve Perry, later the lead singer of Journey, once called Banta home. His band Alien Project formed there in the mid-1970s.","Did a famous person come from there?|Anyone cool from that town?|Famous people?","people"]]},"ca458-tracy-the-railroad-town":{"n":"Tracy, the railroad town","k":"p","q":[["How old is Tracy?","Tracy was born on September 8, 1878, when a Southern Pacific line from Oakland was joined to the Central Pacific line there.","How old is it?|Mom, when did Tracy start?|When was Tracy born?","age"],["Why is it called Tracy?","The junction was named for Lathrop J. Tracy, a railroad executive from back east, according to the Tracy Historical Museum.","Why is it named that?|Who is Tracy named after?|Who was Tracy?","name"],["What happened to the old railroad town of Ellis near Tracy?","Once the railroad moved its facilities to the new Tracy station, railroad families and businesses followed, and Ellis faded.","What happened to that town?|Where did the people from Ellis go?|Did Ellis disappear?","history"],["What was Tracy's main business over the years?","It began as a railroad town, with a roundhouse added in 1896. By the 1950s farming had become Tracy's main business.","What did people do there?|What did Tracy used to be about?|What's the town's main thing?","history"],["How big is Tracy today?","Growth from the Bay Area spilling over the Altamont since the 1970s has turned the railroad town into a city of more than 90,000.","Is it a big place now?|Is it a small town?|How many people live there?","size"],["Whose land was Tracy built on?","This is Yokuts homeland, and Yokuts people still live in the region.","Who lived there first?|Were there people there before the railroad?|Do native people still live there?","people"]]},"ca459-san-joaquin-county-farms":{"n":"San Joaquin County farms","k":"p","q":[["What do San Joaquin County farms grow the most?","In the 2024 crop report, milk was the top commodity and almonds came second. Walnuts ranked fifth and cherries sixth.","What do they grow on that land?|What's all this farm stuff growing, mom?|What's the main crop here?","nature"],["What are San Joaquin County farms famous for?","It has more acres of wine grapes than any other county in California. It also grows about half of California's cherries.","What is it known for?|Is there anything special about these farms?|Why are they famous?","famous"],["How big is farming in San Joaquin County?","In 2024 its farms produced about 3.1 billion dollars' worth of crops and livestock. The source calls the county a major national center of farming.","Is that a lot of money?|How much stuff do these farms make?|Is it a big deal?","size"],["What happens to the cherries grown in San Joaquin County?","The county's more than a dozen cherry packing houses handle nearly all the cherries grown in the state.","Where do they take them?|Where do the cherries go, mom?|Cherries too?","today"],["What is special about walnuts in San Joaquin County?","The county is home to one of the largest walnut processing plants in the world. It handles a lot of Livermore red walnuts, a variety developed in the San Joaquin Valley.","What's that about walnuts?|Do they make walnut stuff here?|Anything about nuts?","other"]]},"ca460-mossdale-crossing":{"n":"Mossdale Crossing","k":"p","q":[["What happened at Mossdale Crossing in 1869?","The first railroad bridge opened here on September 6, 1869. That afternoon the first through train from Sacramento crossed it, and it was the last link of the first transcontinental railroad to the Pacific.","What happened at it in 1869?|Why was that railroad bridge such a big deal?|What was the first train thing here?","history"],["Why is it called Mossdale Crossing?","William Moss, a former Ohio riverboat captain, bought the ferry here in 1856. The valley beside it became Moss' Dale.","Why is it called that?|Who was Moss, and why is his name on it?|Where does the name come from?","name"],["What was Mossdale Crossing before the railroad?","A sharp bend slowed the river and made a shallow ford, a favorite fishing ground of the Yokuts. The Spanish called it El Pescadero, the fishing place.","What was it before the trains came?|Did people live here a long time ago?|What did the Spanish call it?","history"],["Who built the railroad bridge at Mossdale Crossing?","By August 1869 about 600 men were building the railroad bridge, and 500 of them were Chinese.","Who built it?|How many people built the bridge?|Who worked on the train bridge?","people"],["Is the Mossdale Crossing bridge the original one?","No. The first bridge was a wooden swing bridge, rebuilt in steel in 1895 and replaced with today's lift bridge in 1942.","Is it the original bridge?|Is that the same bridge from 1869?|Is the old bridge still there?","today"]]},"ca461-manteca-a-dairy-town-named-lard":{"n":"Manteca, a dairy town named lard","k":"p","q":[["Why is Manteca called Manteca?","Nobody agrees. One story says locals wanted a word for butter and the railroad got it wrong. Another says the Southern Pacific simply liked giving its stops appealing Spanish names.","Why is it called that?|Why's this town named after lard, Dad?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["What does Manteca mean?","Manteca is Spanish for lard. That's an odd name for a town whose railroad stop was there to ship dairy products.","What does that word mean?|What's Manteca mean, is it food?|Manteca means what?","name"],["Who founded Manteca?","Joshua Cowell founded the town and brought dairy farming to the area. When Manteca incorporated in 1918, Cowell was elected its first mayor.","Who started it?|Who built this town, who was the first guy?|Founder?","people"],["What was Manteca called before it was Manteca?","The railroad stop was first called Cowell Station. The line already had a second Cowell Station, named for his brother Henry, so a new name was needed.","Did it have another name?|Was it named something else before?|Old name?","history"],["What is Manteca like today?","Manteca lived mostly on farming into the 1970s. Rising Bay Area home prices then turned it into a commuter town, and its population has more than tripled since 1980.","Is it still a farming town?|Do people still farm there, or is it a big city now?|Is it big now?","today"],["Did the railroad really misprint the name Monteca?","That's a local legend, but a local columnist points out that the word monteca is nowhere to be found in Spanish dictionaries.","Is that misprint story true?|Did the railroad mess up the name, was it a typo?|Was it a typo?","other"]]},"ca462-lathrop-born-of-a-quarrel":{"n":"Lathrop, born of a quarrel","k":"p","q":[["Why was Lathrop started?","It began with a quarrel. Stockton dragged its feet on where the railroad should run, so Leland Stanford built the line around the city and started a new town here.","Why was it built in the first place?|Why does this town even exist?|Who started Lathrop?","history"],["Why is it called Lathrop?","It was first a depot called Wilson's Station. In 1869 it was renamed for the family of Jane Lathrop Stanford, Leland Stanford's wife, and her brother Charles, an engineer for the railroad.","Where did that name come from?|Who is Lathrop named after?|How did this town get its name?","name"],["What did Lathrop do in World War Two?","Permanente Metals made aircraft parts and magnesium bombs here. Sharpe Army Depot was also here, one of the Army's major supply depots in the West.","What was it used for in the war?|Did they make bombs here?|What was the Sharpe depot?","famous"],["What is Lathrop like today?","Lathrop did not become a city until 1989. Today large planned neighborhoods, among them River Islands, spread west of the freeway.","Is it still a small town?|Is it a real city now?|What's out west of the freeway there?","today"]]},"ca465-flying-scotsman-in-hiding":{"n":"Flying Scotsman in hiding","k":"p","q":[["Why was Flying Scotsman hidden near Roth Road?","Its owner had gone bankrupt touring it across North America, so he stored the engine at the Army depot here, out of reach of his creditors.","Why was it hidden at that Army depot?|Why was a train hiding here?|Why'd they hide the train?","history"],["What was the Flying Scotsman famous for?","It's often called the world's most famous steam locomotive. It was the first steam locomotive officially clocked at 100 miles per hour.","What makes it so famous?|What was special about that train, mister?|Why is it famous?","famous"],["How old is the Flying Scotsman?","It was built in 1923. It retired from regular service in 1963 after running more than 2 million miles.","When was it built?|How old is that train, anyway?|When was it made?","age"],["Who owned the Flying Scotsman when it came to America?","Businessman Alan Pegler bought it. With support from the British government, he toured it through the United States and Canada from 1969 to promote British exports.","Who brought it over here?|Whose train was it, and why did he bring it?|Who owned it?","people"],["Why did the Flying Scotsman tour run into trouble in America?","Some states treated the steam engine as a fire hazard. The tour ran 15,400 miles, but in 1972 Pegler, deep in debt, declared bankruptcy.","What went wrong with it on the tour?|Why did the train get in trouble, mom?|What went wrong?","other"]]},"ca467-french-camp":{"n":"French Camp","k":"p","q":[["Who camped at French Camp?","French Canadian trappers working for the Hudson's Bay Company camped here every year with their families, from about 1832 to 1845.","Who stayed at it every year?|Who lived here, like way back?|Who camped here?","people"],["How old is French Camp?","French Camp is considered the oldest settlement in San Joaquin County.","Is it really that old?|Is French Camp like super old?|How old is it?","age"],["What did the trappers do at French Camp?","They were fur trappers working for the Hudson's Bay Company. It was the southernmost regular camp of fur brigades sent all the way from Fort Vancouver.","What were they doing at it?|What were the trappers hunting for, anyway?|What was the camp for?","history"],["What is at French Camp today?","Today French Camp is home to the county hospital and an Army depot.","Is it still used today?|Is there anything there now?|What's there now?","today"]]},"ca470-first-sikh-temple-in-america":{"n":"First Sikh temple in America","k":"p","q":[["When did the first Sikh temple in America open?","Gurdwara Sahib Stockton opened on October 24, 1912, in a house. It was the first Sikh house of worship in the United States.","When did it open?|How old is that temple, like really old?|When was it built?","age"],["Who founded the first Sikh temple in America?","Its founders were Punjabi Sikh immigrants who farmed the land around Stockton. One founder was Jawala Singh, a successful potato farmer.","Who started it?|Who made that temple, kid-style?|Who built it?","people"],["Who was Jawala Singh?","Jawala Singh was a successful potato farmer who worked a 500-acre ranch next to Stockton with his partner Wasakha Singh. He later helped form the Ghadar Party.","What did he do?|Who was that farmer guy?|Jawala Singh, who?","people"],["Where did the Sikh farmers first pray before the temple?","Sikh farmers newly arrived from Punjab first gathered to pray in a room on Jawala Singh's ranch.","Where did they pray at first?|Where did they meet before it was built?|Where did they start?","history"],["Who got help from the first Sikh temple in America?","The gurdwara paid for the university studies of Dalip Singh Saund. He later became the first Sikh American and the first Asian American elected to Congress.","Did it help anyone famous?|Did a famous person come from it?|Who did it help go to college?","famous"]]},"ca472-little-manila":{"n":"Little Manila","k":"p","q":[["What was Little Manila in Stockton?","It was a downtown Stockton neighborhood south of Main Street. By 1946 it held the largest Filipino community in the United States.","What was it?|Mom, what's Little Manila?|Little Manila?","history"],["Why did Filipino men build their own community in Stockton?","Racist laws kept most of them from marrying, and they were barred from crossing Main Street into the white north side. So they built their own community with hotels, barber shops, restaurants and social clubs.","Why did they build it?|Why couldn't they just live in town?|Why their own place?","history"],["What happened to Little Manila when the freeway came?","The Crosstown Freeway was built in the early 1970s through part of it. Residents fought the freeway and lost, and it displaced many Filipino homes and businesses.","What happened to it?|Did the freeway wreck it?|Why a freeway there?","history"],["What is the Filipino Plaza near Little Manila?","The community raised money to build it, and it was completed in 1972. It became home to displaced families and to the Barrio Fiesta, a Filipino cultural festival held each August.","What is that plaza?|What's the Barrio Fiesta?|Is there a festival?","see"]]},"ca472-stockton-once-tuleburg":{"n":"Stockton, once Tuleburg","k":"p","q":[["Who was Stockton named after?","It was named for Commodore Robert Stockton, a famed naval commander of the Mexican-American War.","Who was it named after?|Is there a real guy named Stockton?|Who's Stockton?","people"],["What is Stockton famous for in California history?","It was the first town in California with a name that was neither Spanish nor Native American. It also boomed as a supply stop for gold miners.","What is it known for historically?|Why is this town special, like what's the big deal?|Anything famous about it?","famous"],["Why did Stockton grow during the Gold Rush?","As the head of navigation on the San Joaquin River, Stockton boomed as a supply stop for gold miners.","Why did it boom for gold miners?|Why did the miners go there, like what was it for?|Gold rush town?","history"],["Who lived in the Stockton area before the town?","This is the homeland of the Yatchicumne, a branch of the Northern Valley Yokuts. They built their villages on low mounds to stay above the regular floods.","Who lived here first?|Were there Native Americans there, kids want to know?|Who were the first people?","people"],["Did Stockton have any other nicknames?","Yes. In its early years Stockton also went by Weberville, Fat City and Mudville. It's also one of a handful of cities claiming to have inspired the poem Casey at the Bat.","Did it go by other names too?|Were there funny names for it?|Other names?","name"]]},"ca473-port-of-stockton":{"n":"Port of Stockton","k":"p","q":[["How can Stockton be a seaport if it's so far inland?","Ocean ships come up a dredged channel, 35 feet deep, from San Francisco Bay. Ships up to 900 feet long can make the trip to the docks.","How do ocean ships even get up to it?|How do big boats get all the way to Stockton?|Seaport inland?","other"],["When did the Port of Stockton open?","The modern port opened on February 2, 1933, when the ship Daisy Grey arrived with a load of lumber from Oregon.","How old is it?|How long has that been here?|When was it built?","age"],["What was the Port of Stockton used for in the Gold Rush?","The San Joaquin River was deep enough for ocean-going vessels, so Stockton became a natural supply point for miners. The first freight vessel, the sloop Maria, called here in 1848.","What did it do back then?|Why were boats coming here for gold?|Gold Rush boats?","history"],["What happened at the Port of Stockton in World War Two?","The War Department wanted ships built at an inland port, safe from attack on the coast. So many new ships were built here.","What did it do in the war?|Did they build ships here?|War ships?","history"],["How big is the Port of Stockton?","By land area it is the second largest port in California, covering about 4,200 acres.","Is it a big port?|How much land does that cover?|Is it huge?","size"],["What is the Navy supply depot on Rough and Ready Island?","It was a major communications post for submarines in the Pacific during the Cold War. It is now the port's West Complex.","What was that used for?|Did the Navy have a base here?|Navy base?","today"]]},"ca474-university-of-the-pacific":{"n":"University of the Pacific","k":"p","q":[["How old is the University of the Pacific?","It was chartered in 1851, which made it the first university chartered in California. It started out in Santa Clara before it ever came to Stockton.","How old is it?|Is that school really old, like how many years?|When did it start?","age"],["Where was the University of the Pacific before Stockton?","It started out in Santa Clara, moved to San Jose in 1871, and then moved to Stockton in 1924.","Did it always stay in the same place?|Did the school move around, like to other towns?|Where'd it start?","history"],["What is the University of the Pacific famous for?","It's known for firsts. Pacific opened the first medical school on the West Coast in 1858, and its music conservatory, founded in 1878, was the first of its kind west of the Mississippi.","What's it known for?|What's so special about that school?|Why is it famous?","famous"],["Did anyone famous go to the University of the Pacific?","Yes, the pianist and composer Dave Brubeck graduated from Pacific in 1942.","Did a famous person study there?|Did anybody cool go to that school?|Any famous alumni?","people"],["Has Hollywood filmed at the University of the Pacific?","Yes, Hollywood has filmed here many times, including scenes for Raiders of the Lost Ark. The brick buildings, tower and rose gardens look like an old East Coast college.","Was it in any movies?|Did they shoot a movie at that campus?|Is it in Raiders of the Lost Ark?","see"],["What can you see on the University of the Pacific campus?","You'll see brick buildings, a tower and rose gardens, and the Calaveras River flows through the campus.","What's it look like there?|Is there anything cool to see on that campus?|What's on campus?","see"]]},"ca476-calaveras-river-river-of-skulls":{"n":"Calaveras River, river of skulls","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called the Calaveras River?","Calaveras is Spanish for skulls. The river is said to have been named in 1806 by the Spanish explorer Gabriel Moraga, when he found many skulls of Native people along its banks.","Why is it called that?|Why's the river named after skulls?|Why that name?","name"],["Who named the Calaveras River?","Spanish explorer Gabriel Moraga is said to have named it in 1806. Another story says an 1836 party that included John Marsh and José Noriega gave it the same name.","Who named it?|Who got to name the skull river?|Who named that?","people"],["What happened to the people whose skulls were found there?","Moraga believed the people had died of famine or in fights over hunting and fishing grounds. That is his belief, as the source tells it, not a proven fact.","What happened to them?|Why were there skulls everywhere?|How'd they die?","history"],["How long is the Calaveras River?","It runs about 52 miles, from Calaveras County to the San Joaquin River, with just one reservoir along the way, New Hogan Lake.","How long is it?|How big is the skull river?|Is it a long river?","size"],["What does the Calaveras River have to do with Stockton?","The river and its side channel, Mormon Slough, wrap around central Stockton, so much of the city is really one of the many islands of the Delta.","What does it do around Stockton?|Does the river go around the city?|Is Stockton on an island?","nature"],["Which exit is the Calaveras River?","The freeway crosses the Calaveras River here, at exit 476.","What exit is it?|What exit do we take for the skull river?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca477-benjamin-holt-and-the-caterpillar":{"n":"Benjamin Holt and the Caterpillar","k":"p","q":[["Who was Benjamin Holt?","Benjamin Holt was a Stockton inventor. He came to Stockton in 1883 and, with his brothers, started a company making wagon wheels, then combine harvesters and tractors.","Who was he?|Who's this Holt guy the exit is named after?|Holt who?","people"],["How did the Caterpillar get its name?","A company photographer was reported to have said Holt's tracked tractor crawled like a caterpillar. Holt liked it and said, \"Caterpillar it is.\"","Why is it called that?|Why is a tractor called a caterpillar?|Why Caterpillar?","name"],["When did Holt first test the Caterpillar tractor?","Holt tested it on Thanksgiving Day, November 24, 1904. The tractor rode on tracks instead of wheels and plowed soggy Delta farmland near Stockton.","When was that test?|When did the tractor first roll, kids want to know?|What day was it tested?","history"],["Why did Holt put tracks on a tractor?","Most fields around Stockton were reclaimed from the Delta, so the ground was soft. Tracks spread a heavy machine's weight so it would not sink.","Why did it need tracks?|Why not just use wheels like a normal tractor?|Why tracks?","history"],["What did Holt's company do in World War One?","During World War One, Allied armies used about 10,000 Holt vehicles, many of them hauling heavy guns.","Was it used in the war?|Did soldiers use Holt tractors?|Army tractors?","famous"],["Can we see a Holt Caterpillar tractor today?","Yes, a restored Holt Caterpillar tractor from around 1918 is on display at the Haggin Museum in Stockton.","Is there one on display?|Can we see an old one somewhere?|Where's a real one?","see"]]},"ca481-the-last-piece-of-i-5":{"n":"The last piece of I-5","k":"p","q":[["What was the last piece of I-5 to open?","A 4.6-mile stretch of freeway north of Stockton was the very last piece of Interstate 5 to open.","What was it that finished last?|What part of the freeway was built last, mom?|Last piece of I-5?","history"],["When did the last piece of I-5 open?","It opened on October 12, 1979, after 26 years of construction, completing I-5 from the Mexican border to the Canadian border.","When did it open?|When was the freeway finally done, dad?|What year was it finished?","age"],["How long did it take to build all of I-5?","Construction took 26 years, ending in 1979 when the last piece opened and I-5 was complete from Mexico to Canada.","How long did it take to finish it?|Did it take a really long time to make this road?|How many years to build?","history"],["Who was at the last piece of I-5 opening ceremony?","The consuls of Canada and Mexico joined California's transportation secretary to cut a gold-colored chain, finishing I-5 from border to border.","Who was there when it opened?|Did anybody important come, dad?|Who cut the chain?","people"],["What exit is the last piece of I-5?","It's exit 481, on the stretch of freeway north of Stockton.","What exit is that?|Which exit do we look for, dad?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca485-lodi-wine-and-root-beer":{"n":"Lodi, wine and root beer","k":"p","q":[["What is Lodi famous for?","Lodi is a town surrounded by vineyards, and it's the birthplace of A&W Root Beer. It also once called itself the Tokay Capital of the World.","What is it famous for?|Why do people come to Lodi, mom?|What's Lodi known for?","famous"],["Why is the town called Lodi?","Nobody is sure why Lodi was chosen. Most likely some early settlers came from a place called Lodi in Illinois. It was first called Mokelumne, but that caused confusion with other towns.","How did it get that name?|Why's it called Lodi, mom?|Where's the name from?","name"],["What grapes grow around Lodi?","Lodi's growers raise more than 130 grape varieties. Many of the old vines were planted between the Gold Rush and Prohibition. It was also known for Flame Tokay grapes.","What grows there?|Are there lots of grapes, dad?|Wine grapes there?","nature"],["Is there a song about Lodi?","Yes, Creedence Clearwater Revival has a song called Lodi. John Fogerty admits he had never been here and just thought it was the coolest sounding name.","Is it in a song?|Is there a song named Lodi, mom?|Famous song?","famous"]]},"ca487-delta-asparagus":{"n":"Delta asparagus","k":"p","q":[["When did asparagus first get grown in the Delta?","The first commercial crop was grown in 1892 on Bouldin Island. Chinese farmers planted it, and by about 1900 it was becoming a signature crop of the Delta.","When did they start growing it?|When did kids' favorite veggie farming start here?|How old is it?","age"],["How did they harvest Delta asparagus?","Every spear was cut by hand. Workers slipped a long knife just below the surface of the sticky peat soil, and crews walked each field every few days during the spring harvest.","How did they pick it?|How did they cut the spears, anyway?|Cut by hand?","history"],["Who worked the Delta asparagus fields?","Chinese immigrants did the first hand labor, followed by Japanese workers, then Filipinos, and later Mexican workers. In 1930, 5,500 of about 7,000 harvest workers were Filipino.","Who did that work?|Who picked all the asparagus?|Who were the workers?","people"],["Who was the Asparagus King?","Thomas Foon Chew was known as the Asparagus King. In 1919 he moved his Bayside Cannery to Isleton, and at its peak it was the third-largest canning company in the country.","Who was that guy?|Who's the Asparagus King, really?|Asparagus King?","famous"],["Is Delta asparagus still a big crop today?","Not like it used to be. By the mid-1980s, competition from lower-wage countries such as Peru and Mexico had shrunk the crop, and fresh Delta asparagus has become hard to find.","Do they still grow it?|Is it still a thing, or did it go away?|Still farmed?","today"],["How big was the Delta asparagus business back then?","Pretty big. In 1930 the Delta had more than 350 asparagus camps holding about 7,000 harvest workers.","How big was it?|Were there lots of workers or just a few?|How many people?","size"]]},"ca490-sandhill-crane-reserve":{"n":"Sandhill crane reserve","k":"p","q":[["When do the sandhill cranes come to the Sandhill crane reserve?","They arrive in the Delta around late September and spend the fall and winter here. The best months to see them are October through February.","When do they show up here?|Kid: When do the big birds come?|What season for the cranes?","nature"],["What's the best time of day to watch the cranes at the Sandhill crane reserve?","Sunset is the best time. That's when you can watch them fly in, and they make a loud, trumpeting call you can hear from a long way off.","When should we watch them?|Kid: When do the birds fly in?|Best time to see them?","visit"],["How big are the sandhill cranes at the Sandhill crane reserve?","Their wings spread about six and a half feet, so these are big birds.","How big are they?|Kid: Are the birds really huge?|How wide are their wings?","size"],["How old are sandhill cranes as a bird?","Sandhill cranes have one of the longest fossil histories of any living bird. The oldest sure fossil is 2.5 million years old.","How long have they been around?|Kid: Were these birds around with dinosaurs?|How ancient is that bird?","age"],["Are sandhill cranes in trouble?","California listed the greater sandhill crane as threatened in 1983, after its numbers fell.","Is it a rare bird?|Kid: Are the birds in danger?|Are they protected?","nature"]]},"ca490-the-delta-california-s-holland":{"n":"The Delta, California's Holland","k":"p","q":[["Why is the Delta's soil so rich?","Over thousands of years, tules and reeds grew and decayed there into peat as much as 50 feet deep. That deep peat is why the soil is so rich.","Why is it so rich and good for growing?|Why's the dirt there so good, anyway?|Why's the soil so good?","nature"],["Is the land in the Delta really below sea level?","Yes. Much of the western and central Delta sits at least 15 feet below sea level, and the land has sunk as much as 25 feet since the late 1800s.","Is it really that low, below the sea?|Wait, is the ground there lower than the ocean?|Below sea level?","size"],["Why has the land in the Delta sunk so much?","Once the marsh was drained, the peat soil met the air and slowly broke down. The land has sunk as much as 25 feet since the late 1800s.","Why did it sink like that?|Why is the ground sinking, Dad?|What made it sink?","history"],["Who built the levees around the Delta?","Chinese laborers did much of the first levee building, from the 1850s to the 1870s. About 1,100 miles of levees now ring the islands and tracts.","Who made them, those levees?|Who built the walls that hold the water back?|Who built the levees?","history"],["Why is the Delta important for California's water?","About half of all the river water in California flows through the Delta. Water pumped from its southern end helps supply some 23 million people.","Why does it matter so much for water?|Why do people care about the Delta's water?|What's the big deal with its water?","famous"]]},"ca493-mokelumne-people-of-the-fish-net":{"n":"Mokelumne, People of the Fish Net","k":"p","q":[["What does Mokelumne mean?","Mokelumne means People of the Fish Net. It joins the Miwok words moke, a fishnet, and umne, people of.","What does it mean?|Why's it called People of the Fish Net, Mom?|Mokelumne meaning?","name"],["Where does the name Mokelumne come from?","Both Mokelumne and the nearby Cosumnes come from Miwok. Cosumnes is thought to be built the same way, from kosum, meaning salmon.","Where does that name come from?|Who named it, Dad?|Whose word is it?","name"],["Who lived along the Mokelumne River long ago?","This lower river country is thought to be mostly Plains Miwok homeland. Many villages once stood between the Sierra foothills and the edge of the Delta.","Who lived there before?|Were there Indians here, Dad?|Who was here first?","people"],["What did the Miwok call the Mokelumne River?","The Miwok called it Wakalumitch, possibly meaning big river. A Spanish missionary first wrote down the name as Muquelumnes in 1817.","What was it called before that?|What's the river's old name, Mom?|Old name?","history"],["What happens to the Mokelumne River's water?","Far upstream, Pardee Dam, finished in 1929, feeds the Mokelumne Aqueduct. It carries the river's water across the Delta to the East Bay, the only water supply for about 1.4 million people there.","Where does it all go?|Does the water go anywhere, Dad?|Who uses it?","today"],["Where does the Mokelumne River go after the freeway?","Downstream from here, the Mokelumne splits in two around Staten Island before it reaches the San Joaquin.","Where does it go from here?|Where's the river headed, Mom?|Does it end somewhere?","nature"]]},"ca498-cosumnes-river-preserve":{"n":"Cosumnes River Preserve","k":"p","q":[["How big is the Cosumnes River Preserve?","It covers nearly 50,000 acres, including some of the largest remaining wetlands and streamside habitat in the Central Valley.","How big is it?|Is that place really huge, like how many acres?|How much land is it?","size"],["What makes the Cosumnes River special?","It's one of very few rivers on the western slope of the Sierra Nevada without a major dam, so it still floods over its banks in season.","What's special about that river?|Why is the river different from other rivers, mom?|Why no dam?","famous"],["What animals can we see at the Cosumnes River Preserve?","Raptors, herons, sandhill cranes, shorebirds and waterfowl all use the preserve. The seasonal flooding makes important habitat for wintering birds.","What animals live there?|Are there any cool birds there, like big ones?|Birds there?","nature"],["Why does the Cosumnes River Preserve flood?","With no major dam, the river still floods over its banks in season. That creates important habitat for wintering birds.","Why does it flood?|Why does the water go over the banks?|Flooding there?","nature"],["Who runs the Cosumnes River Preserve?","The preserve is managed by a partnership of state, local, federal and private groups. The visitor center is on Franklin Boulevard.","Who manages it?|Who's in charge of that place?|Who owns it?","people"]]},"ca498-locke-a-chinese-river-town":{"n":"Locke, a Chinese river town","k":"p","q":[["Who built Locke and why?","Immigrants from Heungshan County, in Guangdong, built it up after a 1915 fire destroyed the Chinese community in Walnut Grove.","Who built it and why did they build it?|Who made this town, kids want to know?|Who lived there?","people"],["Why couldn't the people in Locke own their land?","California's Alien Land Law barred them from owning land. They leased about nine acres and owned only their buildings, and for decades couldn't buy the land under their homes.","Why couldn't they own it?|Why did people rent the ground, what was the rule?|Who owned the land?","history"],["How many people lived in Locke?","At times as many as 1,000 to 1,500 Chinese people lived in Locke, with the numbers swelling during the growing and harvest seasons.","How many people lived there?|Was it a big town with lots of people?|How big was it?","size"]]},"ca504-stone-lakes-national-wildlife-refuge":{"n":"Stone Lakes National Wildlife Refuge","k":"p","q":[["What can we do at Stone Lakes National Wildlife Refuge?","You can walk a mile of paved trail loops around managed wetlands. Kids can use a natural playscape, but there's no visitor center.","Can we walk around it?|Is there anything for kids to do there?|What's there to do?","visit"],["Why is Stone Lakes National Wildlife Refuge important?","It protects wild habitat and farmland in an area squeezed by urban growth, between the city of Elk Grove and farm country.","Why does it matter?|Why did they save that land?|Why is it there?","history"],["What is a heron rookery at Stone Lakes National Wildlife Refuge?","A rookery is a place where birds nest together in colonies. Stone Lakes holds a major one where great blue herons and other birds nest together.","What's a rookery?|Where do the herons nest there?|What do the birds do there?","nature"]]},"ca506-elk-grove":{"n":"Elk Grove","k":"p","q":[["How did Elk Grove get started?","Elk Grove began in 1850 as a stagecoach stop for travelers between Sacramento and the Bay Area, when James Hall opened the Elk Grove Hotel.","How did it get started?|How'd this town even begin, Dad?|Who started Elk Grove?","history"],["When did the railroad reach Elk Grove?","The Central Pacific's tracks came through in 1868, about a mile east of the original town. A second Elk Grove Hotel went up near the railroad.","When did it get the railroad?|When did the train come here?|Was there a train station?","history"],["Was Elk Grove always a big suburb?","No. It stayed a small farm town for over a century, until suburbs began spreading in the late 1980s. It became a city on July 1, 2000.","Was it always this big?|Was this always a big town?|Has it changed a lot?","today"],["How fast has Elk Grove grown?","In 2004 and 2005 it was declared the fastest growing city in the country. Its population nearly tripled between 2000 and 2020.","How fast did it grow?|Is this a fast growing town?|Did a lot of people move here?","size"],["What is Sky River Casino in Elk Grove?","Sky River Casino opened in 2022. The Wilton Rancheria, a federally recognized tribe of Miwok people based in Elk Grove, opened it on the site of a half-built shopping mall.","What is that casino?|Who owns that casino?|Who is the Wilton Rancheria?","people"]]},"ca510-freeport-a-town-born-of-taxes":{"n":"Freeport, a town born of taxes","k":"p","q":[["How did Freeport get started?","In 1862, businessmen formed the Freeport Railroad Company to build a new port and a railroad that bypassed Sacramento, so they could dodge Sacramento's port taxes.","How did it get started?|Why did people even build a town here?|Who built this place?","history"],["How many people live in Freeport?","Very few. The 2020 census counted just 58 people. At its peak, in a three-year boom, the town reached 300 to 400 people.","How many people live there?|Is this a big town or a tiny one?|Population?","people"],["What was the plan for the Freeport Railroad?","It was meant to meet the Sacramento Valley Railroad halfway between Sacramento and Folsom, so freight could skip Sacramento entirely.","What was it supposed to do?|What were the trains for?|Why did they need a railroad?","history"],["What is the bridge at Freeport?","Freeport has a drawbridge across the Sacramento River, a bascule bridge. It opened in 1929 and was rebuilt in 1955.","What's that bridge over the river?|Can we see the drawbridge?|When was the bridge built?","see"],["Where is Freeport on the river?","Freeport sits on the east bank of the Sacramento River, about 7.5 miles south of downtown Sacramento.","Is it on the river?|Which side of the river is it on?|Is it near Sacramento?","nature"]]},"ca516-sacramento-zoo-and-land-park":{"n":"Sacramento Zoo and Land Park","k":"p","q":[["When did the Sacramento Zoo open?","The zoo opened on June 2, 1927, as the William Land Park Zoo. It started with 40 animals gathered from local parks.","How old is it?|When did the zoo start, mom?|How old is this zoo?","age"],["Why is the Sacramento Zoo's elephant named Sue?","Sue stands for Sacramento Union Elephant. Two local sisters named her after the newspaper ran a fund drive in 1948, and she arrived in 1949.","Why did they name it Sue?|Why's the elephant called Sue?|Who named Sue?","name"],["Who was William Land, the man Land Park is named for?","William Land was a pioneer who built the Western Hotel in 1875. The park around the zoo is named for him.","Who is that park named after?|Who was the park guy, dad?|Who was Land?","people"],["What else is there to do at Land Park besides the zoo?","Land Park also holds Fairytale Town, with play equipment based on fairy tales and nursery rhymes. There's also Funderland, with small carnival rides for young children.","What else is in the park?|Is there anything for little kids?|Anything else there?","kids"],["How big is the Sacramento Zoo and how many animals does it have?","The zoo grew from 4.2 acres to 14.3 acres in the early 1960s. It had just over 500 animals by 2012.","How big is it?|Does the zoo have lots of animals?|Is it a big zoo?","size"],["Is the Sacramento Zoo moving somewhere bigger?","No. A 2024 plan to move it to Elk Grove was cancelled in April 2025 over cost. The city council approved an expansion where it is.","Are they moving it?|Is the zoo getting bigger, dad?|Did the zoo move?","today"]]},"ca518-sacramento-the-state-capital":{"n":"Sacramento, the state capital","k":"p","q":[["How long has Sacramento been California's capital?","Sacramento has been California's capital since 1854. Before that, lawmakers met in San Jose, then Vallejo in 1852, then Benicia in 1853, before settling here.","How long has it been the capital?|Has Sacramento always been the capital, Mom?|Since when is it the capital?","history"],["Where did California's capital used to be before Sacramento?","Lawmakers first met in San Jose, moved to Vallejo in 1852 and to Benicia in 1853, and then settled in Sacramento.","Where was the capital before it moved here?|Did the capital used to be somewhere else, Dad?|Old capitals?","history"],["What does the State Capitol in Sacramento look like?","The State Capitol was modeled on the U.S. Capitol. It was begun in 1860 and finished in 1874, and lawmakers moved into its chambers in 1869 while work went on around them.","What was it modeled on?|Does the Capitol look like the one in Washington?|Capitol building?","see"],["What is there to see around the Capitol in Sacramento?","Capitol Park spreads over 40 acres around the Capitol, with more than 200 kinds of trees and 155 memorials.","What else is there near it?|Are there any parks or trees to see, Mom?|Stuff by the Capitol?","nature"],["What was the Pony Express building in Old Sacramento?","The B. F. Hastings Building was the western end of the Pony Express in 1860 and 1861. Riders carried mail to and from St. Joseph, Missouri, more than 1,800 miles away.","What happened at that old brick building?|Did the Pony Express really end here, Dad?|Pony Express stop?","history"],["How old is the city of Sacramento?","Sacramento is the oldest incorporated city in California, dating to February 27, 1850.","How old is it?|Is Sacramento a really old city, Mom?|Oldest city?","age"]]},"ca519-tower-bridge":{"n":"Tower Bridge","k":"p","q":[["How old is the Tower Bridge?","The Tower Bridge was dedicated in 1935. It replaced an older railroad bridge from 1911.","How old is it?|How long has that bridge been here, Dad?|When was it built?","age"],["How does the Tower Bridge lift up?","Counterweights in the 160-foot towers weigh as much as the 1,150-ton middle span. That balance means just two 100-horsepower electric motors can raise it for tall boats.","How does it go up?|How does the bridge move, Mom?|What lifts it?","see"],["Why is the Tower Bridge gold?","In 1976 it was painted yellow ochre to match the gold-leafed cupola of the State Capitol. Later, in 2001, residents voted and all gold beat two other color choices.","Why is it that color?|Why is the bridge yellow, Dad?|What color was it before?","name"],["What happened at the Tower Bridge dedication?","A thousand homing pigeons were released to carry the news across California. The dedication was on December 15, 1935.","What happened when it opened?|What did they do on opening day, Mom?|Any pigeons at the bridge?","history"],["What was here before the Tower Bridge?","A 1911 railroad bridge stood here first. Trains kept crossing the Tower Bridge until 1962.","What was it before?|Was there another bridge, Dad?|Did trains use it?","history"],["What else is there to see near the Tower Bridge?","Just upriver, the Delta King, a 285-foot sternwheel steamboat, is tied up as a hotel. Across the river is the ballpark Sutter Health Park.","What's near it?|Is there a boat nearby, Mom?|What's by the bridge?","see"]]},"ca519-the-boat-section":{"n":"The Boat Section","k":"p","q":[["When was the Boat Section built?","It was built in the 1960s and 1970s. The freeway was put below street level on purpose.","How old is it?|Is the sunken part really old, Mom?|When'd they build it?","age"],["Why did they build the freeway below street level here?","So the freeway would stay out of view of downtown offices and shops. Pumps, drains and a retaining wall keep the river's seepage out.","Why is it sunk down like that?|Why is the road in a hole, Dad?|Why so low?","history"],["What happened in Sacramento's Great Flood of 1862?","The American River put much of the city under water for three months. Newly elected governor Leland Stanford had to go to his own inauguration by rowboat.","What was that big flood about?|Did a whole city really go underwater, Dad?|Tell me about the flood","history"],["What is Old Sacramento next to the Boat Section?","Between the freeway and the river lies Old Sacramento, a 28-acre historic district with 53 historic buildings.","What's between the freeway and the river?|Is there an old town by the river, Mom?|Old Sacramento?","see"]]},"ca519-california-state-railroad-museum":{"n":"California State Railroad Museum","k":"p","q":[["What is inside the California State Railroad Museum?","It holds 21 restored locomotives and railroad cars, some dating back to 1862. Inside, there's also a big mock-up of railroad construction at Donner Pass around 1867.","What's in it?|What can we see in there, Mom?|What's inside?","see"],["How old are the trains at the California State Railroad Museum?","Some of the restored locomotives and cars date back to 1862, and there's also a 1944 steam locomotive, the only surviving Southern Pacific cab-forward.","How old are they?|Are those real old trains?|How old stuff?","age"],["What is the Gov. Stanford locomotive at the railroad museum?","It's the Central Pacific's first locomotive. It came to California in pieces by ship, around Cape Horn.","What's that engine about?|Did a train come on a boat?|Who's Stanford's train?","history"],["What is a cab-forward at the California State Railroad Museum?","It's a 1944 steam locomotive with the engineer's cab at the front. The museum has the only surviving Southern Pacific cab-forward.","What's special about that locomotive?|What's a cab-forward, Dad?|The weird train?","famous"],["What is the Crocker Art Museum connected to the railroad?","The railroad's lawyer, Edwin Crocker, spent his railroad fortune on art. His family's gallery became the Crocker Art Museum, the oldest art museum in the western United States.","Why is it connected to it?|Where did the art museum come from?|Art museum?","history"]]},"ca521-where-two-rivers-meet":{"n":"Where two rivers meet","k":"p","q":[["Which two rivers meet here?","The American River flows into the Sacramento River near here. The freeway crosses the American River close to where the two meet.","Which rivers is it where they come together?|What rivers are we crossing, Dad?|Two rivers?","nature"],["Why was the American River once called the River of Wounds?","A Spanish expedition under Gabriel Moraga named it the Rio de las Llagas, the River of Wounds. It was perhaps because of hostile encounters with Native people.","Why did they call that river that name?|Why would a river be named after wounds?|River of Wounds?","name"],["Who has lived along the American River?","Maidu, Miwok, Nisenan and Wintun peoples have lived along the American River for at least 5,000 years. The Nisenan call it Kum Sayo, roundhouse river.","Who lived there first?|Were there Native people by this river?|Who lived here?","people"],["What happened where the two rivers meet in 1839?","John Sutter arrived at the meeting of the rivers on August 13, 1839. The next year his party built Sutter's Fort.","What did John Sutter do there?|Who showed up there in the old days?|What's the history here?","history"],["What is special about Discovery Park at the meeting of the rivers?","Discovery Park is designed to flood. In winter high water it can end up underwater, which takes pressure off the American River levees.","Why is it designed to flood?|Does that park really go underwater?|Park floods?","nature"],["Do people still use the American River today?","Yes. Today the American River is the main source of drinking water for Sacramento.","Is that river important now?|Do we drink the water from it?|Why does the river matter?","today"]]},"ca524-arco-arena-gone-but-loud":{"n":"ARCO Arena, gone but loud","k":"p","q":[["Who played at ARCO Arena?","The Sacramento Kings called ARCO Arena home from 1988 to 2016, before they moved to the new Golden 1 Center downtown.","Who used to play at it?|What team lived at that big arena, Dad?|Who played there?","people"],["What was ARCO Arena famous for?","In 2013 it set a Guinness World Record for the loudest sports roar, at 126 decibels.","What was it famous for?|Why do people remember that place, Mom?|What's the big deal?","famous"],["What else happened at ARCO Arena besides basketball?","It hosted concerts, ice shows, rodeos and monster truck rallies. At one point nearly two million people a year came through its doors.","Did anything else happen there?|Did they have fun stuff there, Mom?|Other events?","history"],["Did anyone famous play at ARCO Arena?","LeBron James played his first regular-season NBA game there on October 29, 2003. He scored 25 points in a loss to the Kings.","Did a star player play at it?|Did any famous basketball guy play there, Dad?|Any big names?","people"],["Is ARCO Arena still there today?","No, it was torn down in 2022. The site was donated in 2021 to California Northstate University for a medical school and hospital.","Can we still go see it?|Can we go inside it, Mom?|Is it still standing?","today"]]},"ca528-airport-and-the-big-red-rabbit":{"n":"Airport and the big red rabbit","k":"p","q":[["When did Sacramento International Airport open?","Sacramento International Airport opened on October 21, 1967, with a single 8,600-foot runway.","When did it open?|How old is the airport, Dad?|What year was it built?","age"],["What is the big red rabbit at the airport?","It's a sculpture called Leap, a 56-foot red rabbit by the artist Lawrence Argent. It's made to look as if it jumped into Terminal B from outside.","What is that rabbit about?|Why is there a giant bunny in the airport?|Big red rabbit?","famous"],["How heavy is the red rabbit sculpture?","Leap weighs about 10,000 pounds. It's made of aluminum, steel, granite and bronze.","How much does it weigh?|Is the giant bunny really heavy?|Rabbit weight?","size"],["Why is there a red rabbit at the Sacramento airport?","The airport leaves that question unanswered, so visitors can make up their own story about the red rabbit.","Why did they put that rabbit there?|Why is the bunny red?|What's the rabbit's story?","name"],["How big is Sacramento International Airport?","It covers about 6,000 acres, which is said to make it the largest commercial airport in California by land area.","How big is it?|Is the airport really huge?|Biggest airport?","size"],["What was Sacramento International Airport first called?","It opened as Sacramento Metropolitan Airport, with five airlines, and took over from the old municipal airport, now Sacramento Executive Airport.","What was it called before?|What was the airport's old name, Mom?|Original name?","history"]]},"ca531-crossing-the-sacramento-river":{"n":"Crossing the Sacramento River","k":"p","q":[["What bridge are we crossing over the Sacramento River?","This is the Elkhorn Bridge, which is also called the Vietnam Servicemen Memorial Bridge. It carries the freeway across the river.","What's the name of this bridge?|What bridge is this, Buddy?|Bridge name?","name"],["How old is the bridge over the Sacramento River?","The Elkhorn Bridge was built in 1969.","How old is it?|Is this bridge really old, Buddy?|When was it built?","age"],["How big is the Sacramento River?","It's the largest river in California. It flows about 400 miles from the Klamath Mountains to the Delta and drains some 26,500 square miles.","How long is it?|Is this a really big river?|Is it the biggest?","size"],["Why is it called the Sacramento River?","In 1808 the Spanish explorer Gabriel Moraga named it the Rio del Santisimo Sacramento, the river of the Most Holy Sacrament.","Who named it that?|Why does the river have that name, Buddy?|Where'd the name come from?","name"]]},"ca531-yolo-bypass":{"n":"Yolo Bypass","k":"p","q":[["What is the Yolo Bypass?","It's a floodway 41 miles long and about 3 miles wide. When the Sacramento River runs high, water spills into it, and it can turn into an inland sea.","What is that wide flat area?|Is that a lake or a field, what is it?|What's the Yolo Bypass?","other"],["Why does the Yolo Bypass flood on purpose?","It takes pressure off the levees and lowers the flood risk for Sacramento and Davis. It can carry about four times the river's own flow.","Why does it flood?|Why do they let it fill with water, mom?|Why the flooding?","history"],["How old are the weirs at the Yolo Bypass?","The Sacramento Weir was built in 1916, and the two-mile-long Fremont Weir was finished in 1929.","When was it built?|How old is that stuff, is it really old?|When did they build it?","age"],["What grows in the Yolo Bypass?","In late spring and summer much of it is farmed, mostly in rice, along with safflower, tomatoes, corn and sunflowers.","What do they farm in it?|What are those crops, what's growing there?|What's growing?","nature"],["What wildlife lives in the Yolo Bypass?","Young Chinook salmon grow much faster there than in the river itself. It also holds the Vic Fazio Yolo Wildlife Area, a haven for waterfowl, shorebirds and wading birds.","Are there animals in it?|Are there fish or birds out there?|Any wildlife?","nature"]]},"ca536-knights-landing":{"n":"Knights Landing","k":"p","q":[["Who founded Knights Landing?","Dr. William Knight, a physician from Baltimore, founded it in 1843. He built it on a mound that had long been a meeting place for the Native people living around Cache Creek and the river.","Who started it?|Who made this town, anyone know?|Who built Knights Landing?","people"],["Why did Knights Landing lose the name Baltimore?","When the town was laid out in 1849 it was named Baltimore, but a dispute over selling the town lots cost it that name.","Why isn't it called that anymore?|Why did they change the name, was somebody mad?|What happened to the old name?","history"],["Who lived around Knights Landing before the town?","This is Patwin homeland. A Patwin group called the Yolotoi lived near Woodland and close to present-day Knights Landing, and the town's mound was a long-time meeting place.","Did Native people live there?|Were there Indians here before, who?|Whose land was it first?","history"]]},"ca537-woodland-tomato-cannery":{"n":"Woodland tomato cannery","k":"p","q":[["How old is the Woodland tomato cannery?","Tomatoes have been processed and canned there since 1958, so it has been running for a long time.","How long has it been around?|How old is that place, like really old?|When did it start?","age"],["Who owns the Woodland tomato cannery?","Pacific Coast Producers has run it since 2001. Earlier owners included Contadina and Del Monte.","Who runs it now?|Whose factory is that, Mom?|Who's in charge there?","people"],["How many tomatoes does the Woodland tomato cannery handle?","About 495,000 tons of tomatoes a year, which is close to a billion pounds in about three months.","How much does it process in a year?|How many tomatoes is that, a million?|How big is it?","size"],["When does the Woodland tomato cannery operate?","It works in season, generally July through September.","Is it working right now?|Is it only busy part of the year?|When do they make the tomatoes?","today"],["How does the Woodland tomato cannery deal with all its water?","Recycled water from the cooling towers makes up about 30 percent of the daily wash water. Wastewater is sprinkled over about 900 acres of fields, mostly grasses like Bermuda grass.","What do they do with the dirty water from it?|Where does the tomato wash water go?|Do they recycle water there?","other"]]},"ca537-woodland-city-of-trees":{"n":"Woodland, City of Trees","k":"p","q":[["Why is Woodland called the City of Trees?","Woodland calls itself the City of Trees because of the valley oaks that were planted around town.","Why does it call itself that?|Why do they say it's full of trees, Dad?|City of Trees?","name"],["How did Woodland get its name?","Frank Freeman bought the Yolo City store in 1857 and gave land to anyone who would clear it and build a home. In 1859 he suggested the name Woodland.","Who named it that?|Who picked the name Woodland, Mom?|Name story?","name"],["How did Woodland first get started?","In 1853 Henry Wyckoff built a store here called Yolo City. Frank Freeman bought it in 1857, and the town later became Woodland.","How did it begin?|Who built the first thing there, Mom?|Where'd it start?","history"],["Why did Woodland become the county seat?","Woodland took over as county seat of Yolo County in 1862, after a flood hit the old seat at Washington, which is now part of West Sacramento.","How did it get to be the county seat?|Was there some kind of flood, Dad?|County seat why?","history"],["What was Woodland's economy built on?","For decades Woodland grew on farming, with three rice mills, a sugar beet refinery and a tomato cannery. In the 1950s it was said to have the most millionaires per capita in California.","What did people do for work there?|Did they have rice or tomatoes, Mom?|What made it rich?","history"],["What is special about the Woodland Opera House?","It was the first opera house to serve the Sacramento Valley, and John Philip Sousa and his band played there. It closed in 1913, but as a state historic park it puts on shows again.","Is it worth seeing the opera house?|Did anyone famous play there, Dad?|Opera House story?","famous"]]},"ca541-capay-valley-yocha-dehe-homeland":{"n":"Capay Valley, Yocha Dehe homeland","k":"p","q":[["Who are the Yocha Dehe Wintun Nation?","They are a federally recognized Patwin tribe, and the valley is the traditional home of Patwin, or southern Wintun, people. The tribe is headquartered in Brooks, in the valley.","Who are they, the people who live there?|Who lives in the Capay Valley, anyway?|Who are the Yocha Dehe?","people"],["What was traditional Patwin life like in the Capay Valley?","Traditional Patwin life included fishing for king salmon, harvesting acorns, hunting and gathering.","What did they do there long ago?|What did the Native kids eat back then?|How'd the Patwin live?","history"],["What does the Yocha Dehe tribe do in the Capay Valley today?","The tribe runs the Cache Creek Casino Resort in Brooks. It also farms, growing wine grapes and olives and selling olive oil, vinegar and wine under its Seka Hills brand.","What do they do there now?|Do they still grow stuff there?|What's Seka Hills?","today"],["What creek runs through the Capay Valley?","Cache Creek flows through the Capay Valley. You reach the valley by taking Highway 16 west past Esparto.","Is there a river in it?|Is there water there, like a creek?|What runs through it?","nature"]]},"ca541-cache-creek-the-trappers-creek":{"n":"Cache Creek, the trappers' creek","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Cache Creek?","Hudson's Bay Company fur trappers cached, or hid, their furs along the Sacramento River and its smaller streams. They called this one Riviere la Cache.","Why is it named that?|Why's it called Cache, like hiding stuff?|Cache Creek name?","name"],["Who named Cache Creek?","Hudson's Bay Company fur trappers. They called it Riviere la Cache, after the furs they cached, or hid, along the rivers and streams.","Who gave it that name?|Who called this creek Cache, kid-style: who named it?|Who named it?","people"],["How long is Cache Creek?","Cache Creek runs 87 miles, from the outlet of Clear Lake through a canyon and the Capay Valley, then out across the valley floor.","How far does it run?|Is that creek really long, how long?|How long is it?","size"],["Did people use Cache Creek before the trappers?","Yes. It was a trade route long before the trappers came. The main trading trail of the Patwin followed the creek.","Was it used before the trappers came?|Did Native people use that creek, like way back?|Old trade route?","history"],["Is Cache Creek still used today?","Yes. A diversion dam built near Capay in 1914 sends its water through about 175 miles of canals across Yolo County.","Is it still used for anything?|Do farms use that water, or is it just a creek?|Still useful?","today"],["Did the 1906 earthquake affect Cache Creek?","Yes. The 1906 San Francisco earthquake set off a landslide that briefly dammed the creek upstream, north of Rumsey.","What happened to it in the earthquake?|Did the creek get blocked, like by a landslide?|Earthquake and the creek?","history"]]},"ca542-yolo-place-of-rushes":{"n":"Yolo, place of rushes","k":"p","q":[["Where does the name Yolo come from?","It comes from the Patwin language, though nobody is quite sure of the exact source. One idea is Yo-loy, said to mean a place abounding in rushes.","Where did it get that name?|What does Yolo even mean?|Yolo name?","name"],["Was Yolo always called Yolo?","No. Before it settled on Yolo, the town went by Cacheville, Cochran's Crossing, Hutton's Ranch and Travelers Home.","Did it have a different name before?|Did Yolo have other names, like nicknames?|Old names?","history"],["How many people live in Yolo?","It's a little town. About 425 people lived there at the 2020 census.","How big is it, how many people live there?|Is Yolo a big town?|Population?","people"],["How old is Yolo County?","Yolo County was one of California's original counties, created at statehood in 1850.","How old is it, the county there?|Is the county really old?|County age?","age"],["What exit is Yolo?","Yolo is at exit 542.","What exit do we take for it?|Which exit number is Yolo?|Yolo exit?","exit"]]},"ca548-sunflower-seed-fields":{"n":"Sunflower seed fields","k":"p","q":[["What are the sunflower seed fields for?","These sunflowers aren't grown for snacks. Some fields in this part of the valley grow sunflowers for planting seed.","What are they growing those sunflowers for?|Are those sunflowers for eating, Dad?|Why so many sunflowers?","famous"],["How much of the sunflower seed comes from California?","California has historically produced about 95 percent of the hybrid sunflower seed planted in the United States, and a quarter of the world's supply.","Is that a lot of the country's sunflower seed?|Does the whole world get seeds from here?|How big is California's sunflower seed business?","size"],["How do bees help the sunflower seed fields?","Bees carry pollen between interplanted rows of male and female plants. That's how the hybrid seed gets made.","Do the bees do something with it?|Why are there bees in the sunflowers?|How do they make the seeds?","nature"],["What is hybrid sunflower seed?","Hybrid seed grows into plants with more vigor and other traits farmers want.","Why do farmers want that kind of seed?|What's special about hybrid seeds?|What's a hybrid sunflower?","other"],["Are the sunflower seed fields still as big as they used to be?","No, the business has been shrinking here. Certified fields topped 53,600 acres in 2018 and fell to about 27,000 acres by 2023, as seed companies moved production overseas.","Is it still a big business?|Why are there fewer sunflower fields now?|Are they growing less?","today"],["Why did sunflower seed production move overseas?","Pioneer said it had enough capacity in Europe for the European market, so it no longer needed extra capacity at Woodland. One grower blamed the exchange rate.","Why is it going to Europe?|What happened to the seed business?|Why did the companies leave?","history"]]},"ca548-zamora-cut-by-the-highways":{"n":"Zamora, cut by the highways","k":"p","q":[["Why was Zamora first called Black's?","It was named after J. J. Black, who settled here in 1865. Zamora was once called Black's in his honor.","Why did it used to be called that?|Why was the town named after some guy?|Who was Black?","name"],["How did the town of Zamora get started?","When the Northern Railway built a line north from Woodland in the 1870s, Black donated land for a depot. A little town grew up around the station.","How did it start growing up?|How did a town get built here, anyway?|Who built it?","history"],["What was in Zamora when the railway town was growing?","The little railroad town had a hotel, stores, a jail, blacksmith shops and six warehouses.","What was in it back then?|Did it have stores and stuff, like a real town?|What used to be there?","history"],["What happened to Zamora when the highways came?","Modern highways rolled through in 1949 and 1969, and a local history says they cut the heart from the town. It was left with no services.","What did the highways do to it?|Why is the town so tiny now?|Where did the town go?","history"],["Who lived in the Zamora area before the town?","This is Patwin homeland. Patwin hunters stalked deer, tule elk and antelope, and hunted ducks, geese and swans with bows and nets in the tule marshes.","Who lived there first?|Were there Native Americans there?|What did people hunt there long ago?","people"]]},"ca553-i-505-once-i-5w":{"n":"I-505, once I-5W","k":"p","q":[["Why was I-505 once called I-5W?","It was first planned as I-5W, a western loop of Interstate 5 through the Bay Area. The route through Sacramento was to be I-5E.","Why was it called I-5W?|Why did it have a W in its name, Mom?|What was I-5W?","name"],["What happened to the I-5W name?","The split numbering was dropped. The western loop became parts of I-580, I-80 and I-505.","What happened to that name?|Where did the W go?|Why'd they drop it?","history"],["When did the top end of I-505 open?","The northernmost piece, from Highway 16 at Madison to this junction, opened in August 1977.","When did it open?|How old is this road, Dad?|When was it built?","age"],["What is I-505 used for today?","It's the main route between the Bay Area and the northern Sacramento Valley. Drivers can skip Sacramento and its traffic.","What is it used for now?|Why do people drive on it, Mom?|Is it important?","today"],["Where does I-505 go from here?","It runs south to I-80 at Vacaville. It's a four-lane freeway the whole way and skirts the eastern edge of Winters.","Where does it go?|Where does that road end, Dad?|How long is it?","other"],["Are there other split Interstates like I-5W?","Most were renumbered too, but I-35E and I-35W survive in Texas and Minnesota.","Are there others like it?|Do other roads have a W or E, Mom?|Any left today?","famous"]]},"ca556-dunnigan-once-antelope":{"n":"Dunnigan, once Antelope","k":"p","q":[["How did Dunnigan get started?","In the winter of 1853 a flood washed two settlers, Copp and Wilson, off their claims near the Sacramento River. They moved up to higher ground here.","Why did people settle here in the first place?|Who lived here first, kids?|What's the story behind it?","history"],["How old is Dunnigan?","Its story goes back to 1853, when settlers moved here after a flood. A post office opened in 1856, the first store in 1866, and the railroad arrived in 1876.","How long has it been around?|Is it really old?|When was it founded?","age"],["Who lived in the Dunnigan area before the settlers?","This is Patwin homeland.","Who were the first people here?|Did Native Americans live around it?|Whose land was this?","people"],["How big is Dunnigan today?","Dunnigan is a small freeway community of about 1,400 people. It sits nearly 40 miles from both Vacaville and Sacramento.","Do many people live in it?|Is it a big town?|How many people live here?","size"]]},"ca559-yellow-billed-magpie":{"n":"Yellow-billed magpie","k":"p","q":[["How do we spot a yellow-billed magpie?","Look for a black-and-white bird with a bright yellow bill and a small patch of yellow skin around each eye. In good light its black feathers shine green, blue or purple.","How can we tell that bird is one?|What does a yellow-billed magpie look like, mister?|How do I spot one?","see"],["Where do yellow-billed magpies live?","They live only in California, in the Central Valley and the foothills and mountains beside it. Watch the fields and tall trees along here.","Where can you find them?|Are those birds only here, Buddy?|Where do they live?","nature"],["What do yellow-billed magpies eat?","They eat mostly insects, especially grasshoppers, plus acorns, fruit and carrion.","What does it eat?|What do those birds munch on?|What's their food?","nature"],["Do yellow-billed magpies really hold funerals?","Sort of. When one dies, others gather around it and call loudly for 10 to 15 minutes, a kind of funeral. They're social birds that roost together.","Is it true that they have a funeral?|Do the birds have funerals for dead ones?|Magpie funerals?","famous"],["Where do yellow-billed magpies build their nests?","Pairs build dome-shaped nests of sticks and mud high in tall trees. Sometimes they build far out on long branches to keep predators away.","Where does it make its nest?|What do the bird houses look like?|Where do they nest?","nature"],["Are yellow-billed magpies in danger?","The IUCN lists them as vulnerable, though they're still common in many places. West Nile virus hit them hard, and about half are thought to have died of it between 2004 and 2006.","Is that bird endangered?|Are they going extinct?|Are they rare?","other"]]},"ca566-college-city-no-college":{"n":"College City, no college","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called College City if there's no college?","The town began with a school, Pierce Christian College, paid for with money from the estate of Andrew Pierce. The college opened in 1875 and left town in 1896.","Why is it named that if it has no college?|Why does it say college when there's no college, Mom?|Why College City?","name"],["Who paid for the college in College City?","The school was paid for with money from the estate of Andrew Pierce. Pierce died around 1871, and the sale of his property paid for the school.","Who paid for it?|Who was the guy who gave money for the school?|Who started it?","people"],["When did the college in College City open and close?","The college opened in 1875 and left town in 1896. The College City post office had opened two years before classes began, in 1873.","When did it open and when did it leave?|How long was there a real college there, Dad?|How old is it?","history"],["What was College City like back in 1875?","A January 1875 newspaper story listed two general stores, a hotel, a livery stable, a saddle shop, two blacksmith shops, a bakery, a drug store, a milliner and Chinese wash houses.","What was in it back then?|What did the town have long ago, Dad?|What was there?","history"],["How big was the college building in College City?","Pretty small. The college building measured just 30 by 40 feet.","How big was it?|Was the college building huge, Mom?|How small was the school?","size"],["What happened to the college buildings in College City?","When the college left, the local high school district took over its buildings. Today College City is served by the Pierce Joint Unified School District.","What happened to them after it left?|Who got the school buildings when the college left, Dad?|Who uses them now?","today"]]},"ca566-tacitus-arbuckle-s-town":{"n":"Tacitus Arbuckle's town","k":"p","q":[["How did Arbuckle get its name?","The town was named for Tacitus Arbuckle, on whose ranch it grew. It was laid out in 1875.","Why is it called Arbuckle?|Who's Arbuckle, anyway?|Name origin?","name"],["How did Tacitus Arbuckle get people to move to his town?","He gave lots to anyone who would build on them. He also became the town's first postmaster.","How did he get it started?|Did he just give people land?|How'd the town start?","history"],["When did the first train come to Arbuckle?","The first train pulled in on July 4, 1876, just two months after the depot was finished.","When did the train get to it?|When did the first choo-choo come?|First train date?","history"],["Is the Arbuckle train depot still there?","Yes. According to the county, Arbuckle's depot is now the only train depot left in Colusa County.","Is it still standing today?|Can we see the old train station?|Is the depot special?","see"],["What do they grow around Arbuckle?","The farmland mixes field crops with orchards, mostly almonds. Several almond hullers work nearby.","What's growing around it?|What are those trees in the fields?|What crops are here?","nature"],["Is it true that Arbuckle is sinking?","Yes. The town dropped 2.14 feet between 2008 and 2017, more than any other area in a state study. Pumping water out of the aquifers made the soil collapse upon itself.","Is it sinking into the ground?|Why is the ground sinking?|Did the town really drop?","nature"]]},"ca567-almond-orchards":{"n":"Almond orchards","k":"p","q":[["How much are the almond orchards worth?","In 2024 almonds were Colusa County's most valuable crop, worth about 279 million dollars, from roughly 63,000 acres.","How much money do they make from it?|How much money do almonds make?|How valuable are they?","famous"],["When do the almond orchards bloom?","Pollination season usually runs from about mid-February to mid-March. The trees bloom with fragrant white to pale pink flowers before their leaves come out.","When do they flower?|When do the trees get flowers?|When is bloom time?","nature"],["Is an almond really a nut?","Not quite. An almond is the seed of a fruit related to the peach, sealed in a hard shell inside a leathery hull.","Is it actually a nut?|Are almonds real nuts?|What is an almond, then?","other"],["When do almonds ripen?","Almonds ripen in autumn, seven to eight months after the bloom.","When are almonds ready to pick?|When is harvest time?|When do they ripen?","nature"]]},"ca572-sutter-buttes-closest-view":{"n":"Sutter Buttes, closest view","k":"p","q":[["How old are the Sutter Buttes?","They are the worn remains of a volcano that was active roughly a million and a half years ago.","How old is it?|How long ago did that mountain thing form, like a million years?|How old are those mountains?","age"],["What are the Sutter Buttes?","They are the worn remains of an old volcano, and they are often called the world's smallest mountain range.","What is it, a volcano?|Are those real mountains or what?|What's that clump of peaks?","famous"],["What do the Native names for the Sutter Buttes mean?","The Maidu, Nisenan and Wintun names for them all roughly mean the Middle Mountains.","What did Native people call it?|What's the Native name for those mountains?|Did the Maidu name it?","name"],["Who was the first European to see the Sutter Buttes?","The Spanish explorer Gabriel Moraga was the first European to see them, in 1806.","Who found it first?|Who discovered those mountains?|Who saw it first?","people"],["Can we visit the Sutter Buttes?","Not yet. The state bought land there for a park in 2003, but it is still not open to the public, and almost all the land around the Buttes is privately owned.","Can we go up there?|Can we hike it?|Is there a park there?","visit"]]},"ca575-patwin-homeland":{"n":"Patwin homeland","k":"p","q":[["Who are the Patwin people?","The Patwin are the southern branch of the Wintun peoples. The western Sacramento Valley is their homeland.","Who are they, the people whose homeland this is?|Who were the Patwin, anyway?|Patwin?","people"],["What language do the Patwin speak?","Patwin has two main dialects: River Patwin, spoken along the Sacramento River, and Hill Patwin, in the hills and plains to the west.","What language is it that they speak?|Do the Patwin have their own language, like a secret one?|Patwin language?","people"],["Are there Patwin people living here today?","Yes. Today Patwin people are citizens of three federally recognized tribes, including the Kletsel Dehe Wintun Nation, headquartered in Williams.","Are they still around today?|Do real Patwin people still live here now?|Patwin today?","today"],["What happened to the Patwin during the Gold Rush?","The Gold Rush brought disaster. Settlers took over acorn groves and water sources, disease swept through the villages, and many Patwin were forced into unpaid labor.","What happened to them back then?|What went wrong for the Patwin in the Gold Rush?|Gold Rush Patwin?","history"],["How big is the Kletsel Dehe Wintun Nation's land?","The nation holds the Cortina Rancheria, a 640-acre reservation in the hills, and has about 270 citizens.","How big is their reservation?|How much land do they have, is it big?|Size of the reservation?","size"],["Is the Patwin language still spoken?","Very few speak it. As of 2021, one person was documented as a first-language speaker, and tribal members are working to bring the language back.","Is it still spoken by anyone?|Can anybody still talk Patwin, or is it gone?|Language alive?","today"]]},"ca577-granzella-s-in-williams":{"n":"Granzella's in Williams","k":"p","q":[["How old is Granzella's in Williams?","Granzella's has served the public since 1976. It's a family business in Williams.","How long has it been around?|How old is this place, mom?|When did it open?","age"],["What can we get at Granzella's?","It's a family business with a restaurant, deli, bakery and sports lounge. They also sell hand-packed jars of olives.","What do they have at that place?|Do they have anything besides a restaurant?|What's there to eat?","see"],["Can we stay overnight at Granzella's?","Yes. Across the parking lot, the family runs Granzella's Inn, a 42-room motel.","Is there a hotel there?|Can we sleep near that place?|Is there a motel?","visit"],["What exit is Granzella's in Williams?","Take exit 577 for Granzella's in Williams.","Which exit do we take for it?|What exit do we get off at, dad?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca578-colusa-and-korusi":{"n":"Colusa and Korusi","k":"p","q":[["How did Colusa get its name?","The name comes from Korusi, a Patwin village that stood on the west bank of the Sacramento River, right where the city is today.","Where did that name come from?|Why's it called Colusa, Dad?|Colusa name origin?","name"],["Who founded Colusa and when?","Charles Semple bought Rancho Colus in 1850 and founded the town. The land had first been given to John Bidwell as a Mexican land grant.","Who started it and when was that?|Who built this town, Mom?|Colusa founder?","people"],["Was Colusa always called Colusa?","No. Charles Semple first called the spot Salmon Bend, and the legislature settled on the spelling Colusa in 1854. The 1850 county law even spelled it Colusi.","Did it have a different name before?|Did Colusa have another name, Mom?|Old name of Colusa?","history"],["What is there to see in Colusa?","There's a historic Chinatown, a Carnegie library from 1905, and a courthouse built in 1861 that is a California Historical Landmark.","What can we see in that town?|Is there anything cool to look at there?|Colusa sights?","see"],["What are the Mile Houses near Colusa?","They were rest stops for travelers that grew up at set road distances from town, running from Five Mile House out to Seventeen Mile House.","What were those rest stops about?|What's a Seventeen Mile House, Mom?|Colusa Mile Houses?","history"]]},"ca582-rice-country":{"n":"Rice country","k":"p","q":[["Why are these fields covered in water in Rice country?","Many of the flat fields around here are rice paddies. Many growers also flood their fields in winter and let the leftover straw break down.","Why is it all flooded out here?|Why are the fields full of water, are they broken?|Why so much water?","nature"],["What are all those birds doing in Rice country?","The flooded rice fields feed and shelter about 7 million ducks and geese, according to the California Rice Commission. More than 200 kinds of wildlife use them, including sandhill cranes.","What are those birds doing in the fields?|Why are there so many birds in the water?|What's with the birds?","nature"],["How much rice does Colusa County grow around Rice country?","In 2024, Colusa County harvested rice from about 118,000 acres, roughly 185 square miles. That made a crop of more than half a million tons.","How much rice do they grow here?|How big are all these rice fields?|Is that a lot of rice?","size"],["Is rice a big crop in Rice country?","Yes. Rice was Colusa County's most valuable crop in 2023, and in 2024 it was second only to almonds.","Is rice important around here?|Do they make a lot of money from rice?|Is rice the main thing they grow?","famous"],["What do farmers do with the leftover rice straw in Rice country?","Growers once burned the straw, but a state law since 1992 has limited burning. Many now flood their fields in winter and let the straw break down.","What happens to the straw after harvest?|Do they burn the fields?|What do they do with the leftovers?","history"]]},"ca586-sites-reservoir-a-lake-to-come":{"n":"Sites Reservoir, a lake to come","k":"p","q":[["What is Sites Reservoir?","It's a planned new lake in a valley in the hills west of Maxwell. Instead of damming a river, it would be filled with water taken from the Sacramento River during high flows.","What is it going to be?|Is it a real lake yet, or what?|What's that lake thing?","other"],["How does Sites Reservoir get its water?","Water would be taken from the Sacramento River during high flows, carried by canals and pumped up into the valley. It would arrive through the Tehama-Colusa Canal and another canal from Hamilton City.","Where would it get its water from?|How do they fill a lake without a river?|Where's the water come from?","nature"],["How big would Sites Reservoir be?","It would stretch 13 miles north to south and 4 miles across, about 260 feet deep at its lowest point. That would make it the eighth largest reservoir in California.","How big would it get?|Is it gonna be a huge lake?|How big's the lake?","size"],["How much water would Sites Reservoir hold?","Full, it would hold up to 1.5 million acre-feet. That's enough to serve 7.5 million people for a year.","How much water can it hold?|How many people could it give water to?|Is it a lot of water?","size"],["When was Sites Reservoir first proposed, and when will it be built?","It was first proposed in the 1950s. Construction is planned to begin in 2027, with operations by 2034.","When will it be finished?|How long have they wanted to build it?|When do they start building?","history"],["What are the concerns about Sites Reservoir?","Diversions could at times take 60 percent of the river's flow, which could harm salmon and other fish.","Is there a downside to it?|Would it hurt the fish?|Is anybody against it?","nature"]]},"ca588-swift-s-stone-corral":{"n":"Swift's Stone Corral","k":"p","q":[["Why did they build Swift's Stone Corral out of stone?","There was no timber anywhere around, so Swift and his partner Frank Sears built the corral from the flat stones scattered over the land.","Why did they build it out of stone?|Why isn't the corral made of wood, mom?|Why stone?","history"],["How old is Swift's Stone Corral?","It was built in 1850 by rancher Granville Swift and his partner Frank Sears, who needed a corral for their ranch.","How old is it?|Is that corral really, really old?|When was it built?","age"],["Who was Granville Swift?","He was a Kentuckian whose great-uncle was Daniel Boone. He came to California at 19, and in 1846 he helped capture Sonoma in the Bear Flag Revolt.","Who was he, the guy who built it?|Who was that Swift guy, dad?|Who is Swift?","people"],["Is Swift's Stone Corral a historic landmark?","Yes. It has been a state historical landmark since 1936.","Is that a official landmark?|Is the old corral a big deal, dad?|Is it protected?","famous"],["What exit is Swift's Stone Corral?","Swift's Stone Corral is at exit 588.","What exit do we take for it?|Which exit is that one, dad?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca588-delevan-refuge":{"n":"Delevan refuge","k":"p","q":[["How big is Delevan refuge?","It's nearly 5,800 acres of managed marsh and upland, and more than 4,500 of those acres are intensively managed wetlands.","How large is it?|Is that place really big?|How many acres is it?","size"],["What can we see at Delevan refuge?","You can watch wildlife from a simple roadside overlook on the Maxwell-Colusa Highway.","What is there to see at it?|Can we see animals there?|What do we do there?","see"],["What animals live at Delevan refuge?","Beavers, muskrats and black-tailed deer live there year round, and peregrine falcons and bald eagles spend the winter. It also shelters the rare giant garter snake.","What lives in it?|Are there any cool animals there?|Any eagles or deer?","nature"],["Why are refuges like Delevan important?","About 95 percent of the Central Valley's wetlands have been lost over the last 100 years, so waterfowl depend more and more on refuges like this one.","Why does it matter?|Why do the birds need that place?|Why save wetlands?","other"],["What exit is Delevan refuge?","Delevan refuge is at exit 588.","Which exit do we take for it?|What exit is that?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca595-rancho-larkin-s-children":{"n":"Rancho Larkin's Children","k":"p","q":[["Why was Rancho Larkin's Children named that?","The land grant was made out to children. Thomas Larkin couldn't get land himself, so in 1844 he had three of his children naturalized, and they received the grant.","Why is it called that?|Why did kids get a ranch?|Why the weird name?","name"],["Why couldn't Thomas Larkin get the land himself?","Larkin was the United States consul in Monterey and wouldn't become a Mexican citizen, so he couldn't receive land himself. His naturalized children could.","Why couldn't he just own it?|Why did the kids need to get it?|Why use his kids?","people"],["How big was Rancho Larkin's Children?","The grant covered about 44,000 acres, and it was given to the children two days after they were naturalized in 1844.","How big was it?|How much land did the kids get?|Was it a lot of land?","size"],["Who ran Rancho Larkin's Children for Larkin?","Larkin sent a Missourian named John S. Williams to run the rancho. In 1847 Williams built an adobe near an abandoned Patwin village called Chah'de'-he.","Who looked after it?|Who ran the ranch for the kids?|Who was the boss?","people"],["How did the town of Princeton get started?","Princeton began in 1851 as a roadhouse called the Sixteen-Mile House. Later it shipped grain down the river by boat.","How did it start?|Where did that town come from?|What was there first?","history"],["Was there a ferry at Princeton?","Yes. For nearly 130 years a ferry carried people across the river at Princeton, until it shut down for good in the late 1990s.","Did a ferry cross there?|Did boats take people across?|Is the ferry still there?","history"]]},"ca595-sacramento-national-wildlife-refuge":{"n":"Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge","k":"p","q":[["Who built the Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge wetlands?","The Civilian Conservation Corps built the first wetlands here between 1937 and 1942, on dry, alkaline land.","Who made it?|Did people make this marsh, Mom?|Who built it?","people"],["Why was the Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge created?","Migrating birds kept eating rice in farm fields, so the refuges were created partly to settle that conflict between the birds of the Pacific Flyway and farmers.","Why is it here?|Why did they make this place for birds?|Why was it made?","history"],["What birds can you see at the Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge?","More than 250 kinds of birds use the refuge, from snow geese and white pelicans to bald eagles.","What birds are there?|What animals live here, Mom?|Any cool birds?","nature"],["When is the best time to visit the Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge?","Fall is the big show. Waterfowl counts average about 1.5 million in October and 1.8 million in November, when geese arrive by the thousands.","When should we come to see it?|When are the most geese here, Dad?|Best season?","visit"],["What is there to do at the Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge?","The refuge has a visitor center and a 6-mile auto tour. At dusk in November, flocks still fly off to feed in the rice.","Can we stop at it?|Is there anything to do there, Mom?|What can we see?","see"]]},"ca601-mendocino-national-forest":{"n":"Mendocino National Forest","k":"p","q":[["How big is Mendocino National Forest?","It covers about 913,000 acres across six counties. It's also the only national forest in California without a major paved road entering it.","How big is it?|Is that forest really huge, like how many acres?|How large is that?","size"],["When did Mendocino National Forest become a forest?","President Theodore Roosevelt set the land aside in 1907 as the Stony Creek Forest Reserve.","When was it set aside?|Who made that forest, and when?|How old is it?","age"],["Why is it called Mendocino National Forest?","President Hoover renamed it Mendocino in 1932 to avoid confusion with the name of the state. It had been called the California National Forest since 1908.","Why did it get that name?|Why'd they change the name of that forest?|Was it always called that?","name"],["What animals live in Mendocino National Forest?","Tule elk live there. They were once hunted nearly to extinction, and were brought back to the Lake Pillsbury basin in the late 1970s.","Are there elk in it?|Are there any big animals in that forest?|Any wildlife there?","nature"],["What happened in Mendocino National Forest in 2020?","The August Complex burned mostly through this forest. It began as 38 separate lightning fires and grew to more than a million acres, the largest wildfire in California history.","Did that forest burn?|Was there a big fire in it?|What was the huge fire there?","history"]]},"ca603-willows-the-cattle-stop":{"n":"Willows, the cattle stop","k":"p","q":[["Why is Willows called Willows?","It's named for a shady watering place ringed by willow trees, where cattle drivers traveling through the valley stopped to rest.","Why is it called that?|Why's the town named after trees, Dad?|Name origin?","name"],["When did Willows become a town?","Willows incorporated in 1886. Its post office had opened earlier, in 1862, under the singular name Willow.","When did it become a town?|How old is Willows, Mom?|How old?","age"],["Did Willows ever have big fires?","Yes. Fires swept through downtown twice in the 1880s, and each time the town rebuilt. In 1887 it replaced its volunteer bucket brigade with a formal fire department.","Did it ever burn down?|Did the town catch on fire, Dad?|Any fires?","history"],["What's the Glenn County Courthouse in Willows like?","It was finished in 1894. Its central dome was taken off for safety reasons in 1951. Willows has been the Glenn County seat since 1891.","What's special about that courthouse?|Is there a cool old building there, Mom?|Courthouse history?","see"],["What's the history of the Willows airport?","It opened in 1928 as a training ground for early pilots. In World War II it hosted practice missions linked to the Doolittle Raiders.","What did it do in the war?|Did planes fly there a long time ago, Dad?|Airport story?","history"],["Is Willows a good place to stop on I-5?","It's a handy stopover between Sacramento and Redding. That's been true since the state finished building Interstate 5 in 1979.","Is it worth stopping there?|Is it a good rest stop, Mom?|Worth a stop?","visit"]]},"ca607-hugh-glenn-the-wheat-king":{"n":"Hugh Glenn, the Wheat King","k":"p","q":[["Who was Hugh Glenn, the Wheat King?","Hugh Glenn was a doctor who came west in the Gold Rush and became California's Wheat King. Glenn County is named for him.","Who was he?|Who was that Wheat King guy?|Who's Hugh Glenn?","people"],["How big was Hugh Glenn's wheat ranch?","His land along the Sacramento River grew to 55,000 acres. At harvest he hired 150 men to bring in the crop.","How big was it?|How much land did the Wheat King have?|Was his farm huge?","size"],["How much wheat did Hugh Glenn harvest in one day?","On August 8, 1879, his crews brought in 5,779 bushels of wheat in a single day.","How much did it produce in a day?|How much wheat is that, in one day?|What was his big harvest record?","famous"],["How did Hugh Glenn get started in California?","He trained as a doctor in St. Louis and left Missouri in 1849 with an ox team and a wagon. He became a cattle rancher, then bought Rancho Jacinto land and kept adding to it.","How did he get here?|How did he travel out west?|What did he do before wheat?","history"],["What happened to Hugh Glenn in the end?","On February 17, 1883, Glenn was shot from ambush on his ranch by his bookkeeper, Huram Miller. It came several days after Glenn had struck him for his drunken insults.","How did he die?|What happened to the Wheat King?|Was he killed?","history"],["Did Hugh Glenn ever run for governor?","Yes, he ran for governor in 1879, but he lost.","Did he go into politics?|Was he ever a politician?|Did the Wheat King win an election?","other"]]},"ca610-artois-once-germantown":{"n":"Artois, once Germantown","k":"p","q":[["Why is Artois called Artois?","It was named after the battles fought in the Artois region of France during World War One. Before 1918 the town was called Germantown.","Why did it get that name?|Why's this town got a weird French name, mom?|Where's the name from?","name"],["What was Artois called before?","It was called Germantown until 1918. The Germantown post office had opened back in 1877.","What was it called before that?|What was this town's old name, dad?|Old name?","history"],["Why did Germantown change its name to Artois?","Local belief says a World War One troop train stopped here for water, and the soldiers took offense at the German name. Petitions to change the post office name then succeeded.","Why did they change it?|How come they got rid of the old name?|Why the switch?","history"],["Did anything dramatic ever happen in Artois?","Yes. On June 1, 2011, a tornado touched down east of Artois, uprooting hundreds of almond trees and damaging farm equipment.","Did anything happen there?|Was there ever a tornado there?|Anything exciting there?","nature"],["How many people live in Artois today?","About 300 people live there today, so it's a very small town.","Is it a big town?|Do lots of people live there?|How many people?","size"]]},"ca614-prune-orchards":{"n":"Prune orchards","k":"p","q":[["How much of the world's prunes come from California?","California grows more than 90 percent of the nation's prunes and about 40 percent of the world's.","Is that a lot of the country's prunes?|Does everybody get their prunes from here?|Is California big on prunes?","famous"],["How do they harvest the prune plums?","Machines grab each trunk and shake the plums onto catching frames. Harvest runs from mid-August into early September and usually takes about 30 days.","How do they get the fruit off the trees?|How do they pick all those plums, Mom?|Do people pick them?","nature"],["How does a plum turn into a prune?","The fruit's high sugar lets it dry without fermenting around the pit. In the dryers, three pounds of fresh fruit become one pound of prunes.","How do they dry the fruit?|How do plums become prunes, Dad?|Why don't prunes go bad?","nature"],["Did anyone really try using monkeys to pick prunes?","Yes. In 1905, during a labor shortage, one grower tried 500 monkeys to pick the fruit. They picked well, but they ate the crop.","Is it true they used monkeys on it?|Monkeys picked the plums, really?|Did monkeys eat the prunes?","kids"]]},"ca619-orland-queen-bee-capital":{"n":"Orland, Queen Bee Capital","k":"p","q":[["Why is Orland called the Queen Bee Capital?","Orland's city council adopted the title in 2017 because the town's locally owned commercial apiaries produce so many queen bees.","Why does it call itself that?|Why is this town about bees, Dad?|Queen bee town?","name"],["Is there a bee museum in Orland?","Yes, Orland has the Honeybee Discovery Center, a museum all about bees.","Is there anything to see about bees there?|Can we go see real bees there?|Bee museum?","see"],["How many US queen bees are raised near Orland?","The Honeybee Discovery Center says about 80 percent of the queen bees used in the United States are raised in Butte, Glenn, Shasta and Tehama counties.","How many of the country's queens come from there?|Do most queen bees come from here?|Where do queen bees come from?","nature"],["How many bees live in a hive with a queen?","A hive can hold 60,000 to 80,000 bees, and the queen is usually the mother of most of them.","How big is one of those hives?|How many bees are in a hive, Mom?|Hive size?","kids"]]},"ca619-will-green-s-water-notice":{"n":"Will Green's water notice","k":"p","q":[["What was Will Green's water notice?","On December 18, 1883, Will S. Green posted a notice on an oak tree on the river's west bank. He claimed 500,000 miner's inches of river water to irrigate the west side of the valley.","What was that notice he posted?|What did that guy put on the tree?|What's the notice about?","history"],["Why did Will Green claim the river water?","He wanted it to irrigate the west side of the valley. His notice claimed 500,000 miner's inches of river water for that purpose.","Why did he want that water?|Why did he want all the river water, anyway?|What was the water for?","history"],["Who was Will S. Green?","He packed a lot into one life. He was a ferryboat captain, mail carrier, surveyor, newspaper editor, writer and legislator, and later U.S. Surveyor General and California's State Treasurer.","Who was that guy?|Who was the man who posted it, anyway?|Was he somebody important?","people"],["Is Will Green's water notice a historical landmark?","Yes. The spot is now a state historical landmark.","Is it a landmark now?|Is that spot officially a big deal?|Is it famous?","famous"],["What is Hamilton City known for?","Hamilton City began in 1905 with a big sugar beet plant. The plant closed in 2006, but its tall silos can still be seen from all over town.","What's the town near it about?|What's in that town?|What did the town start with?","other"]]},"ca621-stony-creek":{"n":"Stony Creek","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Stony Creek?","It was named for the loads of rock and sediment it once washed down from the mountains in floods.","Why is that creek called that?|How did the creek get its name, Mom?|Why Stony?","name"],["Did Stony Creek ever have another name?","Yes, it was once called the Capay River.","Was it called something else before?|Did the creek used to have a different name?|Old name?","name"],["How long is Stony Creek?","It runs 73.5 miles, and it's the second largest stream feeding the Sacramento from the west, after Cottonwood Creek.","How long does it run?|Is that a big creek, or a little one?|How big?","size"],["What did Peter Lassen do at Stony Creek?","In 1845 his party quarried grindstones on one of its tributaries, now called Grindstone Creek. They took them down the river to sell at Sutter's Fort and San Francisco.","What happened here in 1845?|Did somebody take rocks from the creek?|Who was Lassen?","history"],["What is hungry water at Stony Creek?","Black Butte Dam, built in 1963, traps most of the sediment. Without fresh gravel from upstream, the creek below the dam eats away at its own banks, an effect called hungry water.","Why is the creek eating its own banks?|Why does the creek eat the ground, Dad?|What's hungry water?","nature"]]},"ca628-paskenta-band-of-nomlaki":{"n":"Paskenta Band of Nomlaki","k":"p","q":[["Who owns Rolling Hills Casino?","Rolling Hills Casino belongs to the Paskenta Band of Nomlaki Indians, the Hill Nomlaki people of this part of the valley.","Who owns it?|Who runs that casino, Mom?|Whose casino is it?","people"],["What happened to the Paskenta Band in 1959?","In 1959 the federal government terminated the tribe's rancheria, and its land was sold. The Paskenta Band kept its identity and culture, though.","What happened to them in 1959?|Why did the tribe lose its land?|What went wrong in 1959?","history"],["When did the Paskenta Band get its tribal status back?","The Paskenta Band won back full tribal status in 1994, after losing its rancheria in 1959.","When did they get it back?|Did the tribe ever win back its status?|When did they win?","history"],["What was the Nomi Lackee Indian Reservation?","Set up in 1854 in the foothills west of here, it was a military post that controlled 300 to 2,500 Native people. In 1866 they were moved to Round Valley.","What was that reservation?|What was that old military place?|Was there a reservation here?","history"],["When did Rolling Hills Casino open?","The tribe signed a gaming compact with the state in 1999 and opened the casino in 2002.","When did it open?|How long has the casino been here?|When did they build the casino?","age"],["How big is the Paskenta Band today?","The tribe has about 240 members and is headquartered in Corning. Its rancheria covers about 2,000 acres.","How big is it now?|How many people are in the tribe?|How much land do they have?","size"]]},"ca628-walnut-orchards":{"n":"Walnut orchards","k":"p","q":[["What kind of walnuts grow in the Walnut orchards?","Many of the orchards in Tehama County are English walnuts.","What kind of walnuts are those?|What are those trees, Mom?|Which walnuts?","nature"],["How big is the walnut business in Tehama County?","In 2024 the county had about 31,000 acres of bearing walnut trees, worth about 95 million dollars. That makes walnuts its second most valuable crop after almonds.","How much are those orchards worth?|How many walnut trees are there?|Is walnut a big crop here?","size"],["Why did walnut orchards move north to central California?","Walnuts were first grown mainly in Southern California. By 1910 oranges and lemons were crowding them out of Los Angeles and Orange counties, so orchards spread into central California.","Did walnuts always grow here?|Where did walnuts grow first?|Why aren't walnuts grown in Los Angeles anymore?","history"]]},"ca630-lassen-s-rancho-and-stanford-s-vines":{"n":"Lassen's rancho and Stanford's vines","k":"p","q":[["Who was Peter Lassen?","Peter Lassen was a Danish blacksmith whose name is on Lassen Peak. He owned Rancho Bosquejo, a land grant near Vina.","Who was he?|Who's that guy the mountain is named after?|Lassen who?","people"],["What happened to Benton City?","Lassen laid out Benton City on Deer Creek in 1845. Its settlers soon left for the gold fields.","What happened to it?|Why is there no town now?|Where did the town go?","history"],["What did Leland Stanford build at Vina?","In 1881 Stanford built the Vina Ranch, with what was then the largest vineyard and winery in the world.","What did he build there?|Why did Stanford have vines here?|Was there a big winery?","famous"],["Why did Stanford's ranch make brandy instead of wine?","The vines made poor wine, so brandy became the ranch's main product.","Why did it switch to that?|Why didn't the wine work?|Was the wine bad?","history"],["What is on the old Vina Ranch land today?","A Trappist monastery, the Abbey of New Clairvaux, occupies part of the old homestead. The rest was sold off piece by piece by Stanford University.","Is anything still there?|Can we see anything from back then?|What's there now?","today"]]},"ca631-corning-olive-town":{"n":"Corning, olive town","k":"p","q":[["When was Corning founded?","Corning began in 1882, when the railroad was extended to this point.","How old is it?|When did this town start, mom?|How old?","age"],["What is Corning famous for?","Olives. Growing and preparing table olives is its biggest local industry, and it's home to Bell-Carter Foods, maker of Lindsay Olives and the largest table olive processor in the United States.","What's it known for?|Why do they call it the olive town?|Famous for what?","famous"],["What is the Corning Olive Festival?","It's held the second Saturday in October. It began in 1946 as a fundraiser for the local Catholic church and is called the longest-running olive festival in the country.","When did it start?|Is there a party for olives?|Olive festival?","see"],["How big is the olive business around Corning?","In 2024, Tehama County's table olives were worth about 25.6 million dollars, from about 3,900 acres of bearing trees. Oil olives covered about 3,800 acres more.","How much does it make?|How many olive trees are there?|Lots of olives?","size"]]},"ca633-helmick-rest-area":{"n":"Helmick rest area","k":"p","q":[["Who is the Helmick rest area named after?","It's named for Lieutenant John C. Helmick, the California Highway Patrol's Red Bluff Area commander.","Who was it named after?|Who was Helmick, anyway?|Who's Helmick?","name"],["What happened to Lieutenant Helmick?","On February 27, 1989, he was driving to speak at a Rotary Club luncheon in Oroville when his car crashed into a Caltrans truck parked on the shoulder of Highway 99. He was 43.","What happened to him?|How did the guy it's named after die?|What's the sad story here?","history"],["How long was Helmick with the Highway Patrol?","Helmick was a 20-year veteran of the Highway Patrol and had led the Red Bluff Area for three years.","How long did he do that job?|How long was he a cop?|Was he a big boss?","people"],["How old is the Helmick rest area?","The rest areas were first built in 1965. Caltrans completely rebuilt them starting in 2015, and they reopened on May 8, 2017.","When was it built?|Is this place really old?|How long has it been here?","age"],["What's new at the rebuilt Helmick rest area?","The rebuild added LED lights, low-flow plumbing, shade structures and drought-tolerant plants. The builders also added sundials around each flagpole as a finishing touch.","What did they change about it?|What's special about it now?|Is there anything cool there?","see"],["What exit is the Helmick rest area?","It's at exit 633, right at the freeway.","Which exit do we take for it?|What exit number is that?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca633-thomes-creek":{"n":"Thomes Creek","k":"p","q":[["Who was Thomes Creek named after?","It's named for Robert Thomes, a pioneer who arrived in the 1840s.","Who is it named after?|Who was Thomes, Mom?|Who's the creek guy?","name"],["Why is Thomes Creek spelled so many different ways?","Robert Thomes's name was misspelled so often that official records list the creek as Thomas Creek, Thoms Creek and Toms Creek.","Why do they spell it wrong?|Why does the name have so many spellings, Dad?|Spelling mix-up?","name"],["Why is Thomes Creek just rocks and no water?","It's a highly seasonal stream. In summer its wide bed of gravel and boulders is often dry.","Why is it dry in summer?|Where did the water go, Mom?|Dry creek?","nature"],["How big does Thomes Creek get in a flood?","On December 22, 1964, during that year's Christmas flood, it carried 37,800 cubic feet of water per second.","How much water can it carry?|Does it ever get really huge, Dad?|Biggest flood?","size"],["Where does Thomes Creek start and where does it go?","It runs about 62 miles from the Coast Range to the Sacramento River. It begins at a small pond called Kingsley Lake in the Mendocino National Forest.","How long is it?|Where does the creek begin, Mom?|Where's it flow?","size"],["What fish live in Thomes Creek?","Steelhead trout and spring-run Chinook salmon still use the creek.","Are there fish in it?|Are there salmon in the creek, Dad?|Any fish?","nature"]]},"ca636-tehama-the-first-county-seat":{"n":"Tehama, the first county seat","k":"p","q":[["Why is Tehama called Tehama?","Tehama is most often said to come from a Wintun word for high water, though other meanings have been suggested.","Where did it get that name?|What does Tehama even mean?|Name meaning?","name"],["Why do the houses in Tehama have such tall stairs?","Because the town floods easily, its houses must sit above flood level, so most have tall stairways to the front door.","Why does it have those tall stairways?|Why are the houses up so high, Dad?|Tall stairs, why?","nature"],["Was Tehama really the county seat?","Yes, when Tehama County formed in 1856. But a county-wide election moved the seat to Red Bluff just the next year.","How long was it the county seat?|Why isn't it the county seat anymore?|Who took the county seat?","history"],["Who founded Tehama and when?","Robert Thomes laid out the town in 1850. He had received the Mexican land grant Rancho Saucos in 1844.","Who started it?|Who built the town?|Who laid it out?","people"],["Who lived where Tehama is before the town?","A Nomlaki village of the Wintun people once stood where the town is.","Did anyone live there before it was a town?|Were there Native Americans there?|Who was there first?","history"],["How many people live in Tehama?","Tehama is a little city, home to 435 people in 2020. Its population peaked around 2,000 in the 1890s.","Is it a big town?|How big is it?|Population?","size"]]},"ca642-gerber-the-railroad-stop":{"n":"Gerber, the railroad stop","k":"p","q":[["How old is Gerber, the railroad stop?","Gerber was platted in 1910, so the town goes back over a century. The railroad made it a division point starting in 1916.","How old is it?|Was this place around a long time ago, or is it new?|When did it start?","age"],["Why is the town called Gerber?","It was named for the Gerber family, who owned the town site when it was platted in 1910.","Why is it called that?|Who's Gerber? Is it a person?|How'd it get its name?","name"],["What did the railroad do in Gerber?","From 1916 to 1965 it was a Southern Pacific division point. Steam locomotives took on fuel, got repairs and changed crews there.","What happened there on the railroad?|Did trains do stuff there, like stop for fuel?|Why was it a train town?","history"],["What else was in Gerber besides the railroad?","The town had livestock yards and an icing plant for refrigerator cars. In its early years it also served ranchers and alfalfa growers.","What else did it have?|Were there animals there?|What was in town?","history"],["Do people still live in Gerber today?","Yes, about 1,000 people live there today. The railroad work wound down after diesel locomotives replaced steam.","Is it still a town?|Does anybody live there now?|Is it still used?","today"],["When did the passenger trains stop at Gerber?","Passenger trains kept stopping until 1972, when Amtrak's Coast Starlight dropped the station.","Did it stop being a train station?|Why don't trains stop there anymore?|When did Amtrak leave?","history"]]},"ca642-ishi-and-the-yahi":{"n":"Ishi and the Yahi","k":"p","q":[["What does the name Ishi mean?","Ishi means man in the Yana language. Anthropologist Alfred Kroeber gave him the name, because Yahi custom kept a person from speaking his own name until another Yahi introduced him.","Why was he called that?|Why's he named Ishi?|Ishi means what?","name"],["Who were the Yahi people?","The Yahi were the southernmost group of the Yana people. Their ancestral homeland was the foothill canyons east of here, along Mill and Deer creeks.","Who were they, the Yahi?|Who lived here before us?|Yahi who?","history"],["What happened to the Yahi after the Gold Rush?","Settlers killed nearly all of the Yahi. In 1865, about 40 were killed in the Three Knolls Massacre on Mill Creek, and a few survivors hid in the canyons for decades.","What happened to them?|What happened to his people?|Did the Yahi get hurt?","history"],["What did Ishi do at the museum in San Francisco?","University of California anthropologists took him to their museum, where he worked as a janitor and showed how he made bows and stone arrowheads. He was also recorded singing and telling stories on 148 wax cylinders.","What happened to him after he came out?|What did he do in the city?|Did he live in a museum?","people"],["What is the Ishi Wilderness?","The 41,339-acre Ishi Wilderness protects canyons believed to be his people's ancestral grounds. The 2024 Park Fire burned nearly all of it.","What is that wilderness for?|Is there a park for Ishi?|Where's the Ishi place?","see"]]},"ca647-red-bluff-diversion-dam":{"n":"Red Bluff Diversion Dam","k":"p","q":[["How big is the Red Bluff Diversion Dam?","It's a concrete dam nearly 6,000 feet long, stretching all the way across the Sacramento River.","How big is it?|Is that dam really long, Mom?|How long is the dam?","size"],["When was the Red Bluff Diversion Dam built?","It was finished in 1964. It raised the river so water could flow into the Tehama-Colusa and Corning canals.","When was it finished?|How old is that dam?|What year was the dam done?","age"],["What is the Red Bluff Diversion Dam used for?","It raised the river to feed two canals that water about 150,000 acres of farms.","What does it do?|Why did they build that dam?|What's the dam for?","history"],["What happened to the salmon at the Red Bluff Diversion Dam?","Winter-run Chinook counts fell from 118,000 in 1969 to a record low of 191 in 1991. Fish ladders and a fish screen did not solve the problem.","What happened to the fish there?|Did the dam hurt the fish?|Where did the salmon go?","nature"],["How does water reach the canals now at Red Bluff Diversion Dam?","A new pumping plant with a fish screen, just upstream, now lifts water into the canals.","How do the farms get water now?|Is it still used today?|Where does the canal water come from?","today"]]},"ca649-john-brown-s-widow":{"n":"John Brown's widow","k":"p","q":[["Who was John Brown's widow?","Mary Brown was the widow of the abolitionist John Brown. She arrived in Red Bluff in the fall of 1864 with her children, poor and homeless.","Who was she?|Who was that lady, mom?|Who's the widow?","people"],["What happened to John Brown, her husband?","He was executed in 1859 after leading a raid on Harpers Ferry, Virginia. The raid was meant to start a campaign to free enslaved people.","What happened to him?|What did her husband do, dad?|What did John Brown do?","history"],["What was her trip west like?","It was risky. Confederate sympathizers chased the family, who reached a Union army post in Idaho just three hours ahead of them. Soldiers then escorted them 200 miles.","Was it dangerous getting here?|Was somebody chasing them, dad?|How'd she travel?","history"],["What did she do in Red Bluff, and did she stay?","She worked as a nurse and midwife. But pro-slavery people harassed her, and in 1870 the family sold the house and moved to Humboldt County.","What was her job there?|Did she like it here, mom?|Did she leave?","people"]]},"ca649-red-bluff-head-of-navigation":{"n":"Red Bluff, head of navigation","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Red Bluff?","It's named for red bluffs that once stood out along the banks of the Sacramento River. It was first called Leodocia, then Covertsburg, before it became Red Bluff.","Why is it named that?|Why Red Bluff? Is there a red hill?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["What does head of navigation mean in Red Bluff?","It's the farthest point riverboats could reach on the river. The town grew up there, and miners landed here on their way to the gold fields of Trinity County.","What does that mean?|What's head of navigation, like a boat thing?|Head of what?","history"],["What is the Red Bluff Round-Up?","It's a rodeo the town hosts every April, billed as the largest three-day rodeo in the country. In 2024 it drew 675 competitors and about 35,000 fans.","What is it, the rodeo thing?|Is there a real rodeo in Red Bluff?|Cowboys? Where?","famous"],["What happened in Red Bluff's Chinatown in 1886?","In February 1886, thousands of white people in an Anti-Coolie League marched on Chinatown and ordered the Chinese residents to leave. The Chinese community refused and stayed.","What happened to the people there?|Did the Chinese people have to leave?|What's the Chinatown story?","history"],["When did the railroad come to Red Bluff?","The railroad arrived in 1872. For a few years the town was the end of the line.","When did it get a train?|Did trains come here way back?|Was it the end of the line?","history"]]},"ca650-william-b-ide-adobe":{"n":"William B. Ide Adobe","k":"p","q":[["Who was William B. Ide?","William Ide was the only president of the California Republic. That short-lived republic was born in the Bear Flag Revolt in 1846.","Who was he and why is this place named after him?|Who's the guy this park is named for?|Who was Ide?","people"],["How long did the California Republic last?","Not long at all. The California Republic lasted from June 14 to July 9, 1846, less than a month.","How long did it last?|Was the California Republic a real country for long?|How short was that republic?","history"],["Did William Ide actually live in the Ide Adobe?","Surprisingly, no. Research later showed that Ide never owned or lived in the adobe that bears his name.","Did he really live in it?|Did the president live here, for real?|Was this his house?","history"],["Who built the Ide Adobe and when?","An early Red Bluff settler named A. M. Dibble built the adobe in 1852. About 20 different owners held the property before 1942.","Who built it?|Who made this old house, kid-style?|When was it built?","age"],["What can we see and do at the William B. Ide Adobe?","You can see the restored adobe, a recreated 1850s homestead with a blacksmith shop, and a picnic area above the river. On special days you can try Gold Rush card games like faro and monte.","What is there to see at it?|What's fun to do there for kids?|What's inside the park?","see"]]},"ca651-alvin-coffey-s-freedom":{"n":"Alvin Coffey's freedom","k":"p","q":[["Who was Alvin Coffey?","Alvin Coffey was born enslaved in Kentucky in 1822. He was taken to California to mine gold, later bought freedom for himself and his family, and settled in Red Bluff.","Who was he?|Who's the guy at Alvin Coffey's freedom?|Alvin Coffey who?","people"],["How did Alvin Coffey get his freedom?","He worked mines around Shasta and Sutter until he could pay 1,000 dollars. His new owners had agreed to let him buy his own freedom for that price.","How did he get free?|How did the slave guy get out?|How'd he get free?","history"],["Why was Alvin Coffey brought to California?","In 1849 his enslaver forced him to come to California to mine gold. Later the enslaver took him back east and sold him.","Why did he come to California?|Why did the owner bring him out west?|Why California?","history"],["Did Alvin Coffey get to keep the money he earned?","Not at first. His enslaver hired out his labor as a cobbler, laundryman and miner and kept the money. Later he mined enough gold to buy freedom for himself and his family.","Did he get paid?|Did the bad guy take his money?|Who got his pay?","history"],["What did the Coffey family do in Red Bluff?","In Red Bluff the Coffeys farmed, raised turkeys and ran a laundry. In 1887 Alvin joined the Society of California Pioneers, and he is buried at Oak Hill Cemetery.","What did they do after settling there?|What jobs did his family have?|What did they do for work?","people"]]},"ca653-bend-once-horsethief-bend":{"n":"Bend, once Horsethief Bend","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Bend?","It's named for a loop in the Sacramento River nearby. It was first called Horsethief Bend, then Sander's Bend, and finally just Bend.","Why is that place named Bend?|How did Bend get its name, Mom?|Why Bend?","name"],["What was Bend called before?","It started out as Horsethief Bend, then became Sander's Bend, and eventually just Bend.","What was it called in the old days?|Did Bend have a funny old name?|Old name?","history"],["How many people live in Bend?","Bend is a little river community. About 600 people live there today.","How big is it, how many people live there?|Is Bend a big town or a tiny one?|Population?","people"],["What wildlife can you see near Bend?","The Sacramento River Bend Area protects habitat for bald eagles, osprey, songbirds, deer and salmon.","What animals live around there?|Are there eagles or fish near Bend?|Any animals?","nature"],["Is there anything to do near Bend?","Boaters launch at Bend Bridge Park, and the Jellys Ferry day-use site is a trailhead for the Yana Trail. The trail can be impassable in wet weather.","Can we stop and do something there?|Can kids go on the river or hike there?|What's there to do?","visit"],["Did Bend ever have its own post office?","Yes, Bend had its own post office from 1897 to 1935.","Did it have a post office once?|Did that little town used to get mail?|Post office?","history"]]},"ca653-herbert-s-miles-rest-area":{"n":"Herbert S. Miles rest area","k":"p","q":[["Who was Herbert S. Miles?","He was a highway engineer who spent 42 years working on California's state highways. As a district engineer, he helped lead the creation of the state's Safety Roadside Rest program.","Who was he, the guy this rest area is named after?|Who is that Miles guy?|Miles who?","people"],["Is there anything honoring Herbert S. Miles at the rest area?","Yes, a plaque honoring him stands at the rest area on the east side of the freeway.","Is there anything there about him?|Is there a statue of that guy or something?|Anything to see?","see"],["How long have California rest areas been around?","California's modern rest stop system dates to 1963, when a state law directed the state to plan, build and maintain safety roadside rests along its highways.","How old is that whole rest stop system?|Were there rest stops way back in the day, like in the olden days?|When did rest stops start?","history"],["How many rest areas does California have today?","Today California has 87 rest areas. The original 1963 plan called for 257 of them, and later plans cut that number down over the years.","How many are there now?|How many rest stops are there in the whole state, do you know?|How many total?","size"],["What was along California's roads before modern rest areas?","In the 1930s, highway crews built roadside fountains and picnic areas. They were wiped out by highway work after World War II.","What came before them?|What did they have before rest stops, like in the old days?|Were there older ones?","history"]]},"ca657-mount-shasta-from-afar":{"n":"Mount Shasta from afar","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Mount Shasta?","It towers nearly 9,800 feet above the land around it, and it rises abruptly from the surrounding landscape.","How tall is it?|How big is that mountain, Mom?|How high is it?","size"],["How far away can you see Mount Shasta from?","On a clear winter day, it can be seen from the floor of the Central Valley as far as 140 miles to the south.","How far can you see it from?|Can we really see that mountain from way far away?|How far can you see it?","see"],["How high is Mount Shasta compared to other mountains?","It's the second-highest peak in the Cascade Range and the fifth-highest in California.","Is it the tallest one in California?|Is that the biggest mountain ever?|Is it the highest?","famous"],["How long has Mount Shasta been a landmark for travelers?","From the 1820s on, it was a landmark for travelers on the Siskiyou Trail.","Did people use it to find their way?|Did old-time travelers see that mountain too?|Was it always famous?","history"],["Who considers Mount Shasta sacred?","The mountain holds religious significance for the Wintu people.","Does anyone consider it special?|Does anybody think that mountain is special?|Is it sacred?","people"]]},"ca662-cottonwood-truck-scales":{"n":"Cottonwood truck scales","k":"p","q":[["What are the Cottonwood truck scales for?","It's the Cottonwood Commercial Vehicle Enforcement Facility, run by the California Highway Patrol. Officers and inspectors check trucks and their drivers against safety standards and state law.","What does that station do?|Why do the trucks pull in there?|What's that truck place?","other"],["How old are the Cottonwood truck scales?","The first station opened in 2000. A second one, for trucks going the other way, opened in 2025.","How long has it been there?|When did they build that?|Is it new or old?","age"],["Who runs the Cottonwood truck scales?","The California Highway Patrol runs it. Officers and inspectors check the trucks and their drivers there.","Who works at that place?|Are those real police there?|Who's in charge there?","people"],["What else do the officers at the Cottonwood truck scales look for?","Staff there are trained to spot impaired drivers, stolen vehicles and human trafficking.","Do they check for more than weight at that place?|What are they hunting for there, kids ask?|What else do they check?","other"],["Why are the big rigs going slower than us?","By law, California limits trucks with three or more axles, and every vehicle towing a trailer, to 55 miles an hour, even where cars may go 70.","Why can't trucks go as fast as cars?|Why are the trucks so slow, kids say?|Truck speed limit?","other"]]},"ca662-cottonwood-creek":{"n":"Cottonwood Creek","k":"p","q":[["Why is it called Cottonwood Creek?","John C. Frémont named it in 1846. He described cottonwoods growing along its sandy, gravelly bed.","Why is it called that?|Why is it named after cotton, kid?|Cottonwood name?","name"],["What is Cottonwood Creek famous for?","It is the largest undammed tributary of the Sacramento River. That is why it still has runs of Chinook salmon and steelhead.","What is it famous for?|Why is this creek special?|Famous for what?","famous"],["What county line is Cottonwood Creek on?","The creek forms the line between Tehama and Shasta counties along its whole length. When Tehama County was created in 1856, the line was drawn along the creek.","What does it divide?|What's the creek got to do with the counties?|County line?","history"],["Does Cottonwood Creek always have lots of water?","No, it changes with the seasons. It often runs like a large river in winter and spring and shrinks to a trickle by summer.","Is it always that full?|Does the creek dry up?|Is it a river or what?","nature"],["Why is Cottonwood Creek still undammed?","Since the 1940s, the government has proposed big dams, including one 247 feet tall. Local opposition and concern for salmon stopped them.","Did anyone try to dam it?|Why no dam, kid asks?|Dam plans?","history"]]},"ca664-cottonwood-the-halfway-town":{"n":"Cottonwood, the halfway town","k":"p","q":[["How old is Cottonwood?","Cottonwood was settled in 1849 as a stagecoach town. It got its first post office in 1852.","How old is it?|When did people start living here, Mom?|How long's this town been around?","age"],["Why is it called Cottonwood?","It's named after Cottonwood Creek, which the town sits beside, on both banks of the creek.","Why is it named that?|Is there a cotton tree here, Dad?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["How many people live in Cottonwood?","About 6,300 people live there now, nearly twice as many as in 2010.","How big is it?|Is it a big town, Mom?|How many people live here?","people"],["What was Rancho Buena Ventura near Cottonwood?","It was a 26,632-acre Mexican land grant given in 1844 to pioneer Pierson B. Reading. It was the northernmost land grant in California.","What was that old land grant?|Who owned all this land, Mom?|Who was Pierson Reading?","history"]]},"ca665-coleman-fish-hatchery":{"n":"Coleman fish hatchery","k":"p","q":[["How big is Coleman fish hatchery?","It's the largest salmon hatchery in the continental United States. Its goal is to raise about 12 million fall-run Chinook salmon each year.","How big is it?|Is that hatchery a big one, Mom?|Is it huge?","size"],["Why was Coleman fish hatchery built?","It was built in 1942 to make up for spawning grounds lost when Shasta Dam blocked the river.","Why was it built?|Why did they make a fish place, Dad?|Why's it here?","history"],["How does Coleman fish hatchery make baby salmon?","Salmon swim up Battle Creek and are drawn in by the flow of water. Workers collect and fertilize their eggs, and the young fish grow in ponds until they're big enough to head to sea.","How does it work?|Where do the baby fish come from, Mom?|How do they raise them?","nature"],["What kinds of fish are raised at Coleman fish hatchery?","Besides fall-run Chinook, Coleman raises late-fall Chinook and steelhead. A sister hatchery at the base of Shasta Dam raises winter-run salmon.","What fish do they raise there?|Is it just salmon at that hatchery?|What fish live there?","nature"]]},"ca668-lassen-peak":{"n":"Lassen Peak","k":"p","q":[["What happened at Lassen Peak in 1915?","On May 22, 1915, it blasted a column of ash more than 30,000 feet into the sky. A hot avalanche of ash, pumice and rock also rushed down the mountain.","What happened on that mountain back in 1915?|Did the volcano explode, for real?|What was the big blast?","history"],["How long was Lassen Peak quiet before it erupted?","It had been quiet for about 27,000 years. Then steam explosions began on May 30, 1914, and about 400 eruptions followed through 1921.","How long was it asleep before it woke up?|How long was that volcano sleeping?|When did it start erupting?","age"],["What is the Devastated Area at Lassen Peak?","It's a 3-square-mile scar left by the 1915 blast, when a hot avalanche of ash, pumice and rock came down the mountain. It's now called the Devastated Area.","What is that scar on the mountain called?|What did the volcano wreck?|What's the Devastated Area?","history"],["Does Lassen Peak have another name?","Yes. The Mountain Maidu call the peak Kom Yamani, which means Snow Mountain.","What do the native people call that mountain?|Does it have a different name?|What's Kom Yamani?","name"]]},"ca668-anderson-a-railroad-town":{"n":"Anderson, a railroad town","k":"p","q":[["Why is the town called Anderson?","It's named for Elias Anderson, a rancher and hotel keeper. He deeded the Central Pacific Railroad a right of way through his land, and the railroad named its depot after him.","Why is it called that?|Who is Anderson named after, Mom?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["When did Anderson get started as a railroad town?","It goes back to 1872. That's when Elias Anderson gave the Central Pacific Railroad a right of way through his land, and the railroad named its depot after him.","How old is it?|How long ago did the railroad come, Dad?|How old is this place?","age"],["Who was Elias Anderson?","He was a rancher and hotel keeper. He bought the American Ranch in 1856 and built the American Ranch Hotel in what became downtown, with the post office inside.","What did he do?|Who was the guy the town is named after?|Who's that guy?","people"],["How big is Anderson, and what is it known for today?","Anderson is a city of about 11,000 people. It's the headquarters of Sierra Pacific Industries, the second-largest lumber producer in the United States.","What's it famous for?|Do lots of people live there, Dad?|What's there now?","famous"],["How much land does Sierra Pacific own?","About 1.7 million acres, which makes it the largest private landowner in California. That's twice the area of Yosemite National Park.","How big is it?|Is that company really huge?|How much land is that?","size"],["Is there anything to do in Anderson?","There's Anderson River Park, which sits on part of Elias Anderson's old land. The town is also the headquarters of Sierra Pacific Industries.","Is it worth a stop?|Is there a park there, Mom?|What can we see?","visit"]]},"ca673-reading-s-gold-on-clear-creek":{"n":"Reading's gold on Clear Creek","k":"p","q":[["Who found gold on Clear Creek?","Pierson B. Reading and his Native workers found gold at a spot called Reading's Bar, five miles up the creek, in 1848.","Who found gold at it?|Who found the gold here?|Who got the gold?","people"],["When did Reading find gold on Clear Creek?","Reading's strike came in March 1848, shortly after James Marshall's discovery at Sutter's Mill.","When did that happen?|When did they find the gold, like how long ago?|What year was the gold?","history"],["Is Reading's gold site a historical landmark?","Yes. Both Clear Creek and Reading's Bar are now state historical landmarks, and the gold discovery site was declared a California Historical Landmark in 1932.","Is it a landmark?|Is it a real famous place?|Is it official?","famous"],["Where does Clear Creek start and end?","It begins in the Trinity Mountains, between Shasta Lake and Trinity Lake, flows through Whiskeytown Lake, and joins the Sacramento River.","Where does it start?|Where does the creek go, kids?|Where's it come from?","nature"],["What is the Spring Creek Tunnel on Clear Creek?","Below Whiskeytown Dam, it bypasses the lower creek, carrying water from Whiskeytown Lake straight to Keswick Reservoir as part of the Central Valley Project.","What does that tunnel do?|Is there a tunnel for the water?|What's the water tunnel?","today"]]},"ca673-redding-rancheria":{"n":"Redding Rancheria","k":"p","q":[["Who are the people of the Redding Rancheria?","It's a federally recognized tribe made up of Wintu, Pit River and Yana people.","Who are they, the people there?|Who lives at that rancheria, anyway?|Which tribe is it?","people"],["How did the Redding Rancheria get started?","The Bureau of Indian Affairs bought the 31-acre site in 1922 as a place to camp and live for Native people who had been made landless by settlers.","How did it start?|Why did they get that land, Mom?|Where'd it come from?","history"],["When did the Redding Rancheria become one official tribe?","The three groups organized as one tribe and were recognized in 1979. They adopted a constitution in 1989.","When did it get recognized?|When did they become a real tribe, Dad?|What year was that?","history"],["How is the Redding Rancheria governed?","The tribe is governed by seven councilors elected by its members.","Who runs it?|Who's the boss of the tribe?|Who's in charge there?","people"],["What did the Redding Rancheria build near the freeway?","The tribe built the Win-River Resort and Casino along Highway 273, near the freeway.","What did they build there?|Is there a casino, Dad?|What's the big building?","see"],["What does the casino money do for the Redding Rancheria?","The casino's revenue has funded payments to each tribal member.","What's the casino money used for?|Where does the casino money go, Mom?|Does the tribe make money from it?","today"]]},"ca675-the-1846-river-massacre":{"n":"The 1846 river massacre","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the 1846 river massacre?","On April 5, 1846, an armed expedition led by Captain John C. Frémont attacked a large Wintu camp on the Sacramento River near what is now Redding. Most of the people there were women and children.","What happened there?|Mom, what was the massacre about?|What's this massacre?","history"],["Who was killed in the 1846 river massacre?","The victims were Wintu people, most of them women and children, trapped against the river. Estimates of the dead range from 125 to 900.","Who died there?|Who got hurt in the massacre?|Who were the victims?","people"],["Why did Frémont attack the Wintu camp in 1846?","Frémont's party had come up from Peter Lassen's ranch after settlers claimed that Native people were preparing to attack.","Why did he do it?|Why did those soldiers attack the camp?|Why'd they attack?","history"],["Was anyone punished for the 1846 river massacre?","No. No one was ever punished. Frémont went on to become one of California's first United States senators and, in 1856, a candidate for president.","Did anyone get in trouble for it?|Did the bad guys get caught?|Any punishment?","history"],["Could the Wintu men defend the camp in the 1846 river massacre?","They tried. The camp's men formed a line to shield the women and children, but their arrows could not reach the riflemen.","Did they fight back at that massacre?|Did the Wintu have weapons?|Did they fight back?","history"],["Do Wintu people still live near the 1846 river massacre site?","Yes. Wintu people live in this region today, including as members of the Redding Rancheria.","Are there Wintu people around there today?|Do Native people still live here?|Any Wintu left?","today"]]},"ca677-the-carr-fire":{"n":"The Carr Fire","k":"p","q":[["What caused the Carr Fire?","It started when a trailer's tire blew out and its steel rim scraped the pavement. That threw sparks into dry brush near Whiskeytown, west of Redding.","What started it?|How did the fire start, Buddy?|What started the fire?","history"],["How big was the Carr Fire?","It burned 229,651 acres and destroyed 1,604 buildings, including more than a thousand homes.","How much did it burn?|How big was that fire?|Was it huge?","size"],["How many people had to evacuate because of the Carr Fire?","About 38,000 people had to evacuate. The fire jumped the Sacramento River into the west side of Redding on July 26, 2018.","How many people left their homes for it?|Did people have to run away from the fire?|How many evacuated?","people"],["Did anyone die in the Carr Fire?","Yes, eight people died. One was Redding fire inspector Jeremy Stoke, who was killed by the fire whirl.","Was anybody killed by it?|Did people get hurt in that fire?|Did anyone die?","people"],["What did the Carr Fire whirl do to the land?","The whirl stayed on the ground for about half an hour, toppling transmission towers and stripping bark from trees.","What did it destroy?|What did that fire tornado do?|Did it knock stuff over?","history"]]},"ca678-redding-once-poverty-flats":{"n":"Redding, once Poverty Flats","k":"p","q":[["Who is Redding named after?","It's named for Benjamin Redding, the Central Pacific Railroad's land agent. He bought land here in 1868 for a northern terminus, and the railroad built a town and named it for him.","Who was it named for?|Who was Redding, did he live here?|Named after who?","people"],["Was Redding ever called a different name?","Yes. In 1874, local legislators renamed it Reading to honor the pioneer Pierson B. Reading, whose land grant it sits on. By 1880 it was officially Redding again.","Did it change names?|Did they rename it, why?|Other names?","name"],["How hot does Redding get?","Very hot. The average July high is near 100 degrees, and on July 6, 2024, Redding hit 119, its all-time record.","Is it really that hot there?|Is it super hot there?|Hottest day?","nature"],["Has it ever snowed in Redding?","Yes, it can. On December 23, 1968, 16 inches of snow fell in a single day.","Does it snow there, even though it's hot?|Can it snow there?|Snow there?","nature"]]},"ca678-sundial-bridge":{"n":"Sundial Bridge","k":"p","q":[["Who designed the Sundial Bridge?","It was designed by Santiago Calatrava. It's a 700-foot footbridge that crosses the Sacramento River.","Who made it?|Who came up with that crazy bridge, Mom?|Who's the designer?","people"],["How does the Sundial Bridge work as a sundial?","Its 217-foot mast points due north and casts a shadow on a giant dial. The shadow tip moves about a foot a minute, so you can watch the Earth turn.","How does it tell time?|Wait, is the bridge really a clock?|What's the shadow thing?","famous"],["Why doesn't the Sundial Bridge touch the water?","The bridge crosses the river without touching the water. That helps protect the salmon spawning grounds beneath it.","Why does it skip the river?|Why isn't the bridge in the water, Dad?|Is it floating?","nature"],["When did the Sundial Bridge open and what did it cost?","Calatrava's design opened in 2004, three years late, at a cost of 23.5 million dollars. The McConnell Foundation paid for it.","How old is it?|Is that bridge new or old?|What'd it cost?","history"],["What can we see and do near the Sundial Bridge?","The bridge links the two halves of Turtle Bay Exploration Park, with a museum, animal exhibits, gardens and playgrounds. It also joins the Sacramento River Trail.","Is there more to do there than the bridge?|Is there stuff for kids there?|Worth stopping?","see"],["What does the Sundial Bridge look like at night?","Its glass walkway glows aquamarine at night.","Does it light up?|Does it glow in the dark?|Is it pretty at night?","see"]]},"ca680-old-shasta":{"n":"Old Shasta","k":"p","q":[["What happened to Old Shasta?","In the 1850s it was the biggest town in the area, with about 3,500 people. Then in 1873 the railroad bypassed it for Redding, and Shasta faded into a near ghost town.","What happened to it?|Why is nobody living there, Dad?|Why did it die out?","history"],["What can we see in Old Shasta?","There's a row of Gold Rush brick buildings, now a state historic park. The courthouse is restored to its 1861 look and holds exhibits and historic California art.","What can you see there?|Are there real old buildings, Mom?|What's there to look at?","see"],["What is special about the Old Shasta courthouse?","It has been restored to the way it looked in 1861. Today it holds exhibits and a collection of historic California art.","What's so special about it?|Is the courthouse like it used to be?|What's inside the courthouse?","visit"],["Who got jailed in Old Shasta?","Poet Joaquin Miller wrote about being jailed here for horse stealing. He said he escaped with help from his Native American wife.","Did anyone famous get jailed there?|Did a bad guy escape from jail there?|Who was the poet?","people"],["Did a wildfire hit Old Shasta?","Yes, the 2018 Carr Fire destroyed the old elementary school and damaged the cemetery.","Did it ever burn?|Did a fire wreck that place?|Was there a fire there?","history"]]},"ca682-the-9-5-mile-conveyor-belt":{"n":"The 9.5-mile conveyor belt","k":"p","q":[["Why did they build the 9.5-mile conveyor belt?","Builders needed mountains of sand and gravel to make concrete for Shasta Dam. So they built a huge belt to carry the material from Redding to the dam site.","Why did they build that belt?|Why was there a giant belt, anyway?|What was it for?","history"],["How big was the 9.5-mile conveyor belt?","It ran 9.5 miles from Redding to the dam site, and it was then the largest conveyor belt system in the world.","How big was it?|Was that belt really the biggest one ever?|How long was the belt?","size"],["How much could the conveyor belt carry?","It could carry 1,100 tons of material an hour. Over the whole job, it hauled more than 12 million tons of rock.","How much stuff could it carry?|How many rocks did that belt move?|How much did it haul?","famous"],["How did the 9.5-mile conveyor belt work?","Twenty-three of its 26 sections had their own 200-horsepower motors. The other three ran downhill and needed no power. Builders rigged those to make electricity for other sections.","How did it run?|How did that belt move without stopping?|Did the belt have motors?","other"],["What happened at the end of the conveyor belt?","A mixing plant at the end of the line made concrete. Steel buckets holding 8 cubic yards then rode cables out from a main tower 465 feet tall.","Where did the rock go after that?|What did they do with all the gravel?|What was at the end of the line?","history"],["Is anything from the conveyor belt project still there?","Yes. The 465-foot main tower was never fully torn down. It still stands underwater behind the dam and shows up when the lake drops low enough.","Is any of it still standing?|Can you still see part of it?|Is there a tower under the lake?","today"]]},"ca684-shasta-lake-the-dam-towns":{"n":"Shasta Lake, the dam towns","k":"p","q":[["Why did the Shasta Lake dam towns spring up?","The city of Shasta Lake grew out of five small communities that sprang up in 1938, when work began on Shasta Dam.","Why did it start up in the first place?|Why are there towns here, Dad?|Why'd they build it?","history"],["What were the five communities that became Shasta Lake?","They were Central Valley, Project City, Summit City, Pine Grove and Toyon.","What were they called before it became one city?|What were the little towns named?|Name the five towns!","name"],["When did the Shasta Lake dam towns become one city?","The five communities did not join into one city until 1993.","When did it become one city?|When did they all join up?|What year did it become a city?","age"],["Who lived in Toyon, the old government camp?","Toyon was first called Government Camp. The Bureau of Reclamation built it, and everyone who lived there worked for the Bureau.","Who lived there?|Who got to live in that camp?|Whose camp was it?","people"],["What happened to Toyon after the Bureau left?","It became a Job Corps site until 1972. Then local Native Americans occupied it, hoping to have it recognized as tribal land, but that never happened.","What happened to it after they left?|Why is the camp gone now?|Is the camp still there?","history"],["Is anything left of Toyon today?","Not much. A metal storage building and a flagpole are all that remain, on a fenced-off site along Shasta Dam Boulevard.","Is anything left of it?|Can we still see the old camp?|Is there anything to see there?","today"]]},"ca685-shasta-dam":{"n":"Shasta Dam","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Shasta Dam?","Shasta Dam is 602 feet tall. When it was finished in 1945, only Hoover Dam was taller.","How tall is it?|Is that dam really big?|How high is the dam?","size"],["When was Shasta Dam finished?","It was finished in 1945, which was 26 months earlier than planned.","When was it finished?|How old is that dam?|When did they finish the dam?","age"],["Who built Shasta Dam during World War II?","With so many men gone to fight, many of the workers were women and high school students.","Who built it back then?|Did kids build the dam?|Who worked there?","people"],["What was Shasta Dam called before?","It was called Kennett Dam, and it was planned to stand more than 800 feet tall.","What was it called before?|Did the dam have another name?|Old name?","name"],["Why isn't Shasta Dam taller than 602 feet?","Wartime shortages of supplies and labor cut it down from the planned height of more than 800 feet to 602 feet.","Why did they make it shorter?|Why is the dam not as tall as planned?|Why not 800 feet?","history"]]},"ca687-kennett-the-drowned-town":{"n":"Kennett, the drowned town","k":"p","q":[["What happened to Kennett?","Kennett was flooded. By 1944 the whole town was underwater, and it now sits under roughly 400 feet of water, depending on the lake level.","What happened to it?|Why is there a whole town under the lake?|Where did the town go?","history"],["What was Kennett known for?","Mining. Kennett was once the largest mining town in the area outside Redding and Shasta, and its two biggest mines held unusually pure copper ore.","What was it known for?|What did people do in that town?|What was the town for?","famous"],["Why is it called Kennett?","It was named for a railroad man known as Squire Kennet. The second t may have come from a mapmaker's mistake.","Why is it called that?|Who is Kennett named after?|Why does it have two t's?","name"],["Who lived at the Kennett site before the town?","The site lies in the homeland of the Wintu people, who called the place Munuktsiraw.","Who lived there first?|Did anyone live there before the miners?|Did it have another name?","people"],["When did Kennett's mining boom happen?","Kennett boomed during World War I, when metal prices were high. Its biggest mine, the Mammoth, closed for good in 1923.","When was it busiest?|When did that town get rich?|When did the mine close?","history"],["What Kennett event happened for Shasta Dam?","The groundbreaking ceremony for Shasta Dam was held in Kennett in 1937.","What happened here for the dam?|Was it part of building the dam?|What was the dam ceremony?","other"]]},"ca688-the-drowned-highway":{"n":"The drowned highway","k":"p","q":[["What is the drowned highway?","It's old Highway 99, which ran through Mountain Gate and then plunged into the valley that Shasta Lake now fills.","What is that, the old road under the lake?|Is there really a road under the water?|Drowned highway? What's that?","history"],["Can you ever see the drowned highway?","Yes, in drought years. When the water falls, old bridges, road cuts and guardrails come back into view.","Can we see it when the water goes down?|Does the old road ever come back out of the lake?|Can you see it?","see"],["What happened to the highway and railroad when the dam went in?","Both the highway and the Southern Pacific railroad had to move east onto a brand-new, much higher bridge over the Pit River.","Where did the road and train go after the dam?|Did the trains have to move too?|What happened to the road?","history"],["When did the lake start filling over the old highway?","The lake began filling in February 1944, when the diversion tunnel was sealed.","When did it fill up with water?|When did they start filling the lake?|What year did the water come?","history"],["What exit is the drowned highway?","It's exit 688.","Which exit do we take for it?|What exit is that?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca690-bass-hill-stage-road":{"n":"Bass Hill stage road","k":"p","q":[["What was the Bass Hill stage road?","It was part of the California and Oregon stage road. The route crossed the top of Bass Hill and then dropped down to the Pit River.","What was that old road about?|Was this a road for horses and wagons?|What's the stage road?","history"],["What does the Bass Hill marker honor?","The marker honors the stage drivers. It is titled Pioneers Who Held the Ribbons.","Who is that marker for?|Who is the sign about, kids want to know?|What's on the marker?","famous"],["How far did the California and Oregon stage line run?","By 1860 the line ran all the way from Sacramento to Portland. It carried mail and passengers every day.","How far did the stagecoach go?|How far did the old stagecoaches travel, kids ask?|Where did the stage go?","history"],["What happened to the stagecoaches in winter?","In winter the line switched to sleighs to get over the Scott Mountains.","How did they cross the mountains in snow?|Did the stage use sleds, kids ask?|Winter stagecoach?","history"],["When did the Bass Hill marker go up?","The marker went up in 1920, and the site is California Historical Landmark No. 148. The stages ran until the railroad replaced them in 1887.","How old is that marker?|How long ago was the sign put there, kids ask?|When was the marker built?","age"]]},"ca689-shasta-lake":{"n":"Shasta Lake","k":"p","q":[["How big is Shasta Lake?","Shasta Lake is California's largest reservoir, with 365 miles of steep, wooded shoreline. At its deepest, it's 517 feet.","How big is it?|Is that lake really huge?|How large is the lake?","size"],["How deep is Shasta Lake?","At its deepest, Shasta Lake is 517 feet deep.","How deep is it?|Is that lake super deep?|How deep does it go?","size"],["What rivers flow into Shasta Lake?","Shasta Lake has four big arms, made by the Sacramento River, the McCloud River, the Pit River and Sulanharas Creek.","What rivers feed it?|Where does the lake's water come from?|Which rivers go in there?","nature"],["Which is the biggest river flowing into Shasta Lake?","It's the Pit River, not the Sacramento. Before the dam, the Pit carried about four times as much water as the Sacramento where the two met.","Which river is biggest for it?|Is the Sacramento the biggest river there?|What's the main river?","nature"],["Why is it called the Pit River?","It's named for the pits its people dug, both for winter homes and as traps for game that came to drink.","Why is that river called the Pit?|Why does the river have such a weird name?|Pit River name?","name"],["Can we rent a houseboat on Shasta Lake?","Yes. Bridge Bay, the lake's largest marina, has more than 700 slips and about 100 houseboats for rent.","Can you rent them there?|Can we stay on a boat on the lake?|Are there houseboats to rent?","visit"]]},"ca691-pit-river-bridge":{"n":"Pit River Bridge","k":"p","q":[["How old is the Pit River Bridge?","The Pit River Bridge was built in 1942.","How old is it?|When did they build this bridge, mom?|How old?","age"],["What makes the Pit River Bridge special?","It's a double-decker, and it's the highest double-deck bridge in the United States. I-5 rides the top deck while the railroad runs underneath.","What's so special about it?|Why is this bridge a big deal, dad?|Why famous?","famous"],["How high is the Pit River Bridge?","It stands about 500 feet above the old bed of the Pit River. When the lake is full, the water comes within about 40 feet of the bridge.","How high up is it?|Are we really high up on this bridge?|How tall?","size"],["Is there a train under the Pit River Bridge?","Yes, it's a double-decker. I-5 rides the top deck and the railroad runs underneath.","Is there really a train down there?|Can a train go under us, dad?|Train too?","see"],["What happened on the Pit River Bridge in 1953?","On May 3, 1953, a produce truck's steering failed and the cab crashed through the railing, dangling over the water with two men inside. Walter Schau and other drivers rescued both men.","What happened here in the old days?|Did something crazy happen on this bridge?|Any rescue here?","history"],["What famous photo was taken at the Pit River Bridge?","Virginia Schau took a photo of the rescue with her Kodak Brownie. It won the 1954 Pulitzer Prize for Photography, the first ever won by a woman.","Who took that rescue picture?|Did someone win a prize for a photo here?|Pulitzer photo?","people"]]},"ca693-the-giant-median":{"n":"The giant median","k":"p","q":[["What is the giant median on I-5?","Just north of the Pit River Bridge, the two directions of I-5 split apart, leaving a huge median between them.","What is that giant median?|Why is the road split in two like that?|What's that big gap in the middle?","other"],["Is the giant median the biggest median in California?","Not quite. It's said to be the second-largest in California. Only Interstate 8's In-Ko-Pah grade has a bigger one.","Is it the biggest one in the state?|Is there a bigger median than this one?|Is this the biggest gap ever?","size"],["Where does the giant median start?","It starts just north of the Pit River Bridge, where the two directions of I-5 split apart.","Where does it begin?|Where does the road split apart?|Where do the lanes separate?","other"],["What exit is the giant median near?","The giant median is listed at exit 693.","What exit is it at?|Which exit do we look for?|What's the exit number for that?","exit"],["Does this part of I-5 have a nickname?","Yes. From Red Bluff all the way to the Oregon line, the road carries the nickname Cascade Wonderland Highway.","Does the highway here have a nickname?|Does this road have a special name?|What's this highway called?","name"],["Does it snow on I-5 north of Redding?","North of Redding it regularly gets snow at higher elevations from fall to spring. In winter, trucks must carry chains whether or not chain controls are in force.","Will there be snow up ahead?|Do we need chains up here?|Do trucks need chains in winter?","nature"]]},"ca694-shasta-salamanders":{"n":"Shasta salamanders","k":"p","q":[["What are the Shasta salamanders?","They're salamanders found nowhere else on Earth, hiding in the limestone hills around this lake. Scientists once thought there was just one kind.","What are those salamanders?|Are those little lizards or something?|What's a Shasta salamander?","famous"],["How many kinds of Shasta salamanders are there?","Three. Scientists once thought there was only one kind, but in 2018 they found three, including the Wintu Shasta salamander.","How many kinds are there?|Wait, are there different salamanders?|How many types?","nature"],["How can you tell the Shasta salamanders apart?","You mostly can't just by looking. The three species look so alike that only their ranges and their genes tell them apart.","How can you tell them apart?|Do they look different from each other?|Which one is which?","nature"],["Where do the Shasta salamanders live?","They favor damp limestone cracks and caves, where females lay their eggs. The Samwel kind lives on the north-central and northwest sides of the lake.","Where do they live?|Where do they hide?|Where are the salamanders?","nature"],["Why are the Shasta salamanders in trouble?","Building the dam and filling the lake probably broke their small range into pieces. Raising the lake higher would flood more of their homes, and limestone quarrying is another threat.","Why are they in trouble?|Are they going extinct?|Is something hurting them?","history"],["Are the Shasta salamanders protected?","Not under the Endangered Species Act. In 2021, the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service found that they did not warrant that protection.","Are they protected?|Does the government protect them?|Are they endangered?","other"]]},"ca695-lake-shasta-caverns":{"n":"Lake Shasta Caverns","k":"p","q":[["How do you get to Lake Shasta Caverns?","You take a catamaran ride across the lake's McCloud arm, then a bus up a steep mountain road. The caves sit about 900 feet above the lake.","How do we get up to it?|Do we have to hike to the caves, Mom?|How's the trip in?","visit"],["Why were the caves once called Baird Cave?","They were named for U.S. Fisheries Commissioner Spencer Fullerton Baird. Richardson worked for the federal fish hatchery on the McCloud River.","Why was it called that?|Who is Baird, Mom?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["What is the Discovery Room at Lake Shasta Caverns?","Workers blasted a tunnel into the mountain and broke into a room nobody knew about. It contains every type of limestone formation found there.","What is it, that room?|Was there a secret room, Dad?|What's in there?","see"],["How did people get into Lake Shasta Caverns before 1964?","Only a few hardy cavers squeezed in, through a hole in the ceiling barely big enough for one person. Then workers blasted a tunnel to open the caves to visitors.","How did they get in back then?|Could anyone go in it before?|How'd cavers get in?","history"],["What exit is Lake Shasta Caverns and where does the name come from?","It's exit 695, at O'Brien, named for Con O'Brien, who ran a resort here.","What exit is it, and why that name?|Which exit do we take, Dad?|Exit name?","exit"]]},"ca696-winnemem-wintu-homeland":{"n":"Winnemem Wintu homeland","k":"p","q":[["What does Winnemem Wintu mean?","Winnemem Wintu means middle water people or middle river people.","What does it mean?|What's that name mean?|Name meaning?","name"],["What happened to the Winnemem Wintu villages when the lake filled?","Scores of their village sites, burial grounds and sacred places went underwater when the lake filled.","What happened to their villages when it filled up?|Did the water cover their homes?|What got flooded?","history"],["Are the Winnemem Wintu still here today?","Yes. The Winnemem are still here. They have long sought federal recognition, and they oppose plans to raise the dam.","Do they still live here?|Are those people still around?|Still here?","today"],["What happened to the Winnemem Wintu at Kabyai Creek in 1854?","In 1854, settlers killed 42 Winnemem men, women and children at Kabyai Creek on the McCloud River.","What happened to them at that creek in 1854?|What did the settlers do to them?|What was the 1854 killing?","history"],["What was the Winnemem Wintu war dance in 2004?","In 2004 the Winnemem held a war dance against raising the dam, which they said was the first since 1887.","Why did they hold a war dance in 2004?|What was that war dance about?|War dance?","famous"]]},"ca698-the-salt-fire":{"n":"The Salt Fire","k":"p","q":[["What caused the Salt Fire?","Forest officials believe it was likely sparked by hot material that fell from a vehicle on the interstate. It started beside I-5 near the Salt Creek exit.","What started it?|How'd the fire start, did somebody do it?|What sparked it?","history"],["When did the Salt Fire happen?","It started on June 30, 2021, beside I-5 near the Salt Creek exit, and it shut down the freeway.","When was that fire?|What year was it, how long ago?|When'd it burn?","age"],["How much damage did the Salt Fire do?","It destroyed 27 homes and 14 other buildings around Lakehead. By evening it covered about 1,000 acres.","How many houses did it burn?|Did people lose their houses?|How bad was it?","history"],["Did the Salt Fire close the freeway?","Yes, it shut down I-5. The freeway reopened the next morning, and firefighters built their lines near the interstate to keep it open.","Was I-5 shut down because of it?|Could cars not drive on the highway?|How long was the road closed?","history"]]},"ca700-the-first-federal-fish-hatchery":{"n":"The first federal fish hatchery","k":"p","q":[["When did the first federal fish hatchery open?","It opened in 1872 on the McCloud River, and it was the nation's first federal fish hatchery.","When did it open?|How old is that hatchery, like, really old?|What year was it built?","age"],["Who built the first federal fish hatchery?","Livingston Stone built it, at a spot where he found Wintu people catching Chinook salmon.","Who built it?|Who made the fish place, mom?|Who was the builder?","people"],["What happened to the salmon eggs from the first federal fish hatchery?","Its eggs were shipped across the country and as far as Japan and New Zealand.","Where did the eggs from it go?|Did the fish eggs go far away?|Where'd the eggs go?","history"],["How did the Wintu people react when the hatchery crew arrived?","Wintu people gathered with bows and arrows on the far bank and spent a whole day trying to drive the crew off. Stone later hired Wintu workers until 1883.","How did the Wintu feel about it?|Were the Native people mad at them?|Did the Wintu fight them?","history"],["What fish came from the second station Stone started upriver?","It raised rainbow trout, and its eggs went all over the country. Many rainbow trout in the world's waters today descend from McCloud River fish.","What did he raise at the other station?|Do we still have those fish today?|What other fish?","nature"],["What happened to the McCloud River salmon run?","The salmon run declined after a railroad was built nearby in the 1880s.","Did the salmon go away from there?|Why are there fewer fish, dad?|What hurt the salmon?","nature"]]},"ca702-lakehead-top-of-the-lake":{"n":"Lakehead, top of the lake","k":"p","q":[["How many people live in Lakehead?","About 470 people lived in Lakehead in 2020. In summer, more than 1,500 seasonal residents can join them.","How many people live there?|Is it a big town, or just a few people?|How many live here?","people"],["What exit is Lakehead?","Lakehead is at exit 702.","What exit is it?|Which exit do we take for that?|Which exit, Mom?","exit"]]},"ca707-delta-end-of-the-line":{"n":"Delta, end of the line","k":"p","q":[["Why was Delta called the end of the line?","When the railroad pushed up this canyon in the 1880s, Delta was where the tracks stopped, and it stayed the end of the line for about two years.","Why was it the end of the line?|Why did the train stop at Delta, Mom?|What does end of the line mean?","history"],["What happened to the town of Delta?","In 1887 the tracks moved on up the canyon and Delta slowly emptied out. Its post office closed in 1954.","What happened to it?|Where did everybody go, Dad?|Is anyone still there?","history"],["What was Dog Creek, and why do people mention it with Delta?","Dog Creek was a mining town founded in 1855 a few miles west. Many people kept calling the place Dog Creek even after it became Delta.","Why did people call it Dog Creek?|Dog Creek? Like a dog, Mom?|Where's Dog Creek?","name"],["Is anything from old Delta still standing?","A few buildings remain. The old one-room Delta school, since enlarged, is one of the few still standing.","Is any of it still there?|Can we see an old building, Mom?|Any old stuff left?","see"]]},"ca711-the-upper-sacramento-river":{"n":"The upper Sacramento River","k":"p","q":[["Where does the upper Sacramento River begin?","It begins near Mount Shasta, where its North, Middle and South forks meet. From there it passes through Lake Siskiyou and runs down this canyon.","Where does it start?|Where does the river come from, mom?|River start?","nature"],["How long is this stretch of the upper Sacramento River?","It runs about 60 miles through this canyon, past Dunsmuir and Castella, before reaching Shasta Lake near Lakehead.","How far does it run through this canyon?|Is the river really long, Dad?|How long is it?","size"],["Why is the upper Sacramento River famous for fishing?","It's a blue ribbon trout stream where wild rainbow trout abound. Anglers come from all over the world to fish it.","What makes it so good for fishing?|Are there fish in the river, Dad?|Fish there?","famous"],["Where does the water from the upper Sacramento River go?","It flows into Shasta Lake, supplying about 15 percent of that lake's inflow. That water later becomes part of the Central Valley Project and is sent on through the valley.","What happens to the water from it?|Where does the river end up, Dad?|Where's it go?","today"],["Are there salmon in the upper Sacramento River?","Salmon once spawned up here, but dams built in the 20th century cut them off from the whole upper river.","Did salmon ever live in it?|Why don't the salmon come up here, mom?|Any salmon?","history"]]},"ca712-portuguese-flat":{"n":"Portuguese Flat","k":"p","q":[["What was Portuguese Flat?","Portuguese Flat was a Gold Rush camp of the early 1850s, made up largely of Portuguese miners. It had rich diggings and a reputation as one of the roughest camps in northern Shasta County.","What was that place?|What was Portuguese Flat, like, back then?|What is this place?","history"],["Who ran the inn at Portuguese Flat?","Ross and Mary Campbell McCloud ran an inn at Portuguese Flat from about 1853 to 1855. After that they moved on to Upper Soda Springs.","Who lived at that inn?|Who ran the old hotel at Portuguese Flat?|Who had the inn?","people"],["What famous poet was connected to Portuguese Flat?","Joaquin Miller, who later became a well-known poet. In 1855, as a young man, he was brought to the McClouds' inn at Portuguese Flat after being hurt in the Battle of Castle Crags.","Did anyone famous stay at that inn?|Did a famous person ever come to Portuguese Flat?|Any famous guests?","famous"],["How did Joaquin Miller get better at Portuguese Flat?","Mary McCloud nursed him back to health at the inn. By his own account, an arrow had pierced his jaw and neck. Miller went on to become a well-known poet.","Who took care of him after he got hurt?|How did the kid with the arrow get saved?|Who nursed Miller?","kids"]]},"ca715-river-rails-and-road":{"n":"River, rails and road","k":"p","q":[["What is the River, rails and road canyon?","It's one canyon where the river, the railroad and the freeway all squeeze through together. The railroad follows the old Siskiyou Trail.","What is it about this canyon?|Why are there so many things squeezed in here?|What's special here?","history"],["Do trains still run along the river here?","Yes. Amtrak's Coast Starlight still runs these tracks every day, traveling between Los Angeles and Seattle.","Is that railroad still used?|Do real trains still come through, Dad?|Any trains today?","today"],["What was the Shasta Daylight train?","It was a Southern Pacific streamliner that ran this route from 1949, between Oakland and Portland, in about 15 and a half hours.","What was that old streamliner?|Was there a fancy old train here?|Tell me about the old train","history"],["Where does the Coast Starlight stop near here?","Dunsmuir is a Coast Starlight stop. It's the train's only stop in Siskiyou County and the northernmost passenger station in California.","Does the train stop near here?|Is there a train station close by?|Where's the train stop?","famous"]]},"ca718-southern-s-stage-station":{"n":"Southern's stage station","k":"p","q":[["Who was Sims Southern?","Simeon Southern, nicknamed Sims, opened a log stage station and hotel here in 1859. He was born on a Kentucky farm in 1822 and reached Shasta County in 1854.","Who was that guy who ran it?|Who was Sims, mom?|Who was Sims?","people"],["How old is Southern's stage station?","Sims Southern opened it as a log stage station and hotel in 1859. Travelers stopped there to rest and eat for 50 years.","How old is it?|How long ago did people start coming here, dad?|When did it start?","age"],["What happened at Southern's stage station?","Travelers rested and ate there for 50 years. In September 1880, President Rutherford B. Hayes stayed there with Generals Sherman and Sheridan.","What happened at that place?|Did anything cool happen here, dad?|What's the story?","history"],["Why is this exit called Sims?","The exit's name goes back to Sims Southern, whose station was the center of town. The settlement of Hazel Creek took the name Sims in 1871.","Why is it called that?|Why's it called Sims, mom?|Where's the name from?","name"],["What happened to Southern's stage station?","Sarah Southern sold the station to a timber company in 1902, and a sawmill followed. The site is California Historical Landmark No. 33.","What happened to it?|Is it still there, dad?|Did it close?","today"],["Did Black Bart have anything to do with this place?","Not here directly. In September 1880, Black Bart robbed a stage in Oregon, and three days later Hayes and Sherman rode that same stage.","Was that connected to Black Bart?|Was there a bandit, mom?|Black Bart story?","famous"]]},"ca720-the-siskiyou-trail":{"n":"The Siskiyou Trail","k":"p","q":[["What is the Siskiyou Trail?","It started as a web of Native American footpaths between California and the Northwest. Fur trappers later followed them south, and the route became known as the Siskiyou Trail. I-5 follows the same path today.","What was it before it was a freeway?|What's this old trail we're on, Mom?|Siskiyou Trail?","history"],["Who first used the Siskiyou Trail?","Native Americans did. The route began as a web of footpaths between California and the Northwest, and Hudson's Bay Company fur trappers followed those paths south from the Columbia River in the 1820s.","Who made that trail in the first place?|Who walked here before cars, Dad?|Who used it first?","people"],["What was the Siskiyou Trail called at first?","At first it was called the California Brigade Trail. That was after trapper Alexander McLeod reached the Sacramento River in 1828.","Did it always have that name?|Did the trail have another name, Dad?|Old name?","name"],["What happened on the Siskiyou Trail in 1837?","Ewing Young bought hundreds of cattle in Mexican California and drove them up the trail to Oregon, a trip of nearly three months.","What did Ewing Young do on it?|Did cowboys really drive cows up this trail?|The cattle drive?","history"],["How far could travelers go in a day on the Siskiyou Trail?","Early travelers on mules and horses managed perhaps 20 miles a day.","How far did people get on it each day?|Did it take them forever to get anywhere?|Miles per day?","other"],["When did stagecoach roads and telegraph lines reach the Siskiyou Trail?","Toll roads for stagecoaches were not carved through these mountains until the 1860s, and the first telegraph line arrived in 1864.","When did it get real roads?|When did they get the telegraph, Dad?|Stagecoach era?","history"]]},"ca723-homeland-and-the-1855-battle":{"n":"Homeland and the 1855 battle","k":"p","q":[["Whose homeland is the Castle Crags area?","The country around Castle Crags is the ancestral home of the Okwanuchu Shasta people. The Wintu, Achumawi and Modoc peoples have long revered the Crags too.","Who lived around it before the miners came?|Whose land was this, really?|Who are the original people here?","people"],["What happened in the 1855 battle at Battle Rock?","In June 1855, miners fought Modoc people below Battle Rock. The spot is now marked at the state park entrance.","What happened there in 1855?|Was there a real fight here, Dad?|What was the battle?","history"],["Is Battle Rock a historic landmark?","Yes, Battle Rock is California Historical Landmark No. 116.","Is it officially recognized?|Is that rock a big deal?|Does it have a landmark number?","famous"]]},"ca724-castle-crags-state-park":{"n":"Castle Crags State Park","k":"p","q":[["How did Castle Crags State Park get started?","In 1943 the state bought 925 acres of the old springs property, which was the start of Castle Crags State Park. Before that, the spring water had been bottled and shipped all over the world.","How did it get started?|Why is there a park here, mom?|How'd the park begin?","history"],["What was the mineral water at Castle Crags used for?","In the 1890s, mineral water from a spring here was bottled and shipped all over the world. Native people used the spring long before anyone bottled it.","What was that water used for?|Why did people want the spring water?|Who drank the spring water?","history"],["What happened to the bottling company at Castle Crags?","The bottling company went broke in the 1929 crash. The bottling works had been founded back in 1889.","What happened to it?|Why did the water company stop, dad?|Did the company fail?","history"],["What can we do at Castle Crags State Park?","The park has 28 miles of trails and a vista point facing the Crags and Mount Shasta. The Crags Trail climbs to the base of Castle Dome.","What can we do there?|Is there stuff to do for kids?|Is it worth a stop?","visit"],["Does the mineral spring at Castle Crags still bubble up?","Yes, the water still bubbles up with a sulfur smell. In the 1930s the Civilian Conservation Corps built a stone enclosure around it.","Does it still bubble?|Is the water still there, and does it stink?|Can we see the spring?","see"],["Is the Pacific Crest Trail at Castle Crags State Park?","Yes, today the Pacific Crest Trail winds through the park.","Does that trail go through it?|Can we walk a famous trail there?|Is the PCT there?","famous"]]},"ca726-castle-crags":{"n":"Castle Crags","k":"p","q":[["How old are the Castle Crags?","California State Parks puts the spires' age at more than 170 million years. The granite formed underground in the Jurassic period, the age of dinosaurs.","How old is it?|How long ago did those spires form, like in dinosaur times?|How old are the rocks?","age"],["How did the Castle Crags get their shape?","The granite formed underground, and Ice Age glaciers later scraped away the softer rock around it, leaving the spires standing.","How did it get shaped like that?|What made those pointy rocks, Ice Age stuff?|How'd they form?","history"],["How tall are the Castle Crags?","The spires rise from about 2,000 feet at the river to more than 6,500 feet at the top.","How tall is it?|How high do those big rocks go?|How big are they?","size"],["What is the round hump among the Castle Crags?","That's Castle Dome, shaped as huge curved slabs of granite peeled away.","What's that round part of it?|What's that big bald dome thing in the rocks?|Round hump?","see"],["What plants grow at Castle Crags that grow nowhere else?","A bellflower grows here and is found nowhere else in the world. A second plant, the Castle Crags ivesia, also grows only here.","Does anything grow only at it?|Are there any special flowers there?|Any rare plants?","nature"],["What is the Castle Crags Wilderness?","Much of the Crags lies in the Castle Crags Wilderness, set aside by Congress in 1984. Its northern part has bowls carved by glaciers.","Is it protected land?|Is that a park or something?|Wilderness area?","other"]]},"ca728-sleep-in-a-caboose":{"n":"Sleep in a caboose","k":"p","q":[["Can you really sleep in a caboose at this stop?","Yes. A lodge just off this exit lets guests sleep in old cabooses, and it also has vintage cabins and campsites if you want something different.","Can we sleep in it?|Mom, can we sleep in a real train?|Sleep in a caboose?","visit"],["How old is the Sleep in a caboose lodge?","The lodge was founded in 1968.","How old is it?|Is this place really old, Dad?|When did it start?","age"],["What was the Sleep in a caboose lodge called before?","It was once called Railroad Park Resort, and it now goes by Jubilee Railroad.","What was it called before?|Why did the name change, Mom?|Old name?","name"],["Where do you eat at the Sleep in a caboose lodge?","The restaurant is inside an original train car. Its dining car also has outdoor seats where diners can gaze up at the Crags.","Where do you eat there?|Do we eat in a train too?|Restaurant?","see"],["How big is the Sleep in a caboose property?","The property covers 43 acres in a forested, spring-fed creek valley.","How big is it?|Is it a huge place, Dad?|Size?","size"],["What is special about the nature around the Sleep in a caboose lodge?","The lodge calls itself the eastern gateway to the Klamath Knot, a global hot spot for plant and animal variety.","What's special about it?|Are there cool animals and plants there?|Why is it special?","nature"]]},"ca729-dunsmuir-the-railroad-town":{"n":"Dunsmuir, the railroad town","k":"p","q":[["Why is Dunsmuir called the railroad town?","Dunsmuir was a railroad town first. Extra steam engines were added here to push trains up the steep grades north of town, which earned it the nickname Pusher.","Why is it called a railroad town?|Why do they say trains here, what's the deal?|Railroad town?","history"],["How did Dunsmuir get its name?","It's named for Alexander Dunsmuir, son of a British Columbia coal baron. In 1888 he offered the town a fountain if it would take his name.","How did it get that name?|Who is Dunsmuir, was he a real person?|Who's the name from?","name"],["What can we see in Dunsmuir?","The depot houses a railroad museum, the town still has an operating turntable, and three drinking fountains line Dunsmuir Avenue. Downtown is a historic district too.","What is there to see in it?|Is there anything cool to look at, like trains?|What's to see?","see"],["Why does downtown Dunsmuir look so old?","When the railroad switched from steam to diesel in the 1950s, it needed fewer workers and the town shrank. That left downtown looking much as it did in the 1920s and 1930s.","Why does it look so old-fashioned?|Why does the town look like an old movie?|Why so old-looking?","history"],["How big was Dunsmuir in the old days?","In the early 1900s it was the largest town in Siskiyou County. For a long time it was the largest California city north of Sacramento.","How big was it back then?|Was this ever a big city, or always tiny?|Was it ever big?","size"]]},"ca730-upper-soda-springs":{"n":"Upper Soda Springs","k":"p","q":[["What is Upper Soda Springs?","It's a riverside park in north Dunsmuir, under the freeway. Long ago it was a river crossing, then home to a toll bridge, a stage hotel and a springhouse serving soda water.","What is it exactly?|Is that a park or what?|What's this place?","other"],["Who built the toll bridge at Upper Soda Springs?","Ross and Mary Campbell McCloud built a toll bridge across the Sacramento River around 1855.","Who built the bridge there?|Who made that toll bridge?|Was there a bridge here once?","history"],["What's the Dolly Varden trout story at Upper Soda Springs?","Elda McCloud, daughter of Ross and Mary McCloud, was the first to call a colorful local fish the Dolly Varden trout.","What's the Dolly Varden thing about it?|Who named the Dolly Varden fish?|What's that fish called?","name"],["Why did the Upper Soda Springs resort close?","Well-to-do Victorian travelers once came to take the waters, but the resort closed by 1920 as cars changed vacation habits.","Why did it shut down?|What happened to the old resort?|Why did people stop coming?","history"],["What kind of history happened at Upper Soda Springs?","Ewing Young's cattle drive stopped here in the 1830s, and the U.S. Exploring Expedition passed through in 1841. In the 1850s, Wintu people fleeing miners settled near the springs.","What happened at that place?|Who famous passed through here?|What's the story here?","history"]]},"ca731-babe-ruth-in-dunsmuir":{"n":"Babe Ruth in Dunsmuir","k":"p","q":[["What did Babe Ruth do in Dunsmuir?","He played an exhibition game against the locals in the city park.","What did he do there?|Did Babe Ruth really play baseball here?|What happened with Babe Ruth?","famous"],["Who came to Dunsmuir with Babe Ruth?","Fellow New York Yankees star Bob Meusel came along on the same barnstorming tour.","Was anyone else with him?|Who was the other player with him?|Who came along?","people"],["What is the Babe Ruth park in Dunsmuir like today?","The park is now home to a botanical garden along the river. It covers about 10 acres and is looked after by a nonprofit group of volunteers.","What is it now?|What's in the park these days?|Is the park still there?","today"],["Is there a big league player from Dunsmuir?","Yes, outfielder Ray Coleman was born in Dunsmuir and made it to the big leagues.","Did anyone from there play pro ball?|Any baseball players born there?|Who else is famous from Dunsmuir?","people"],["What exit is Dunsmuir, where Babe Ruth played?","Babe Ruth in Dunsmuir is at exit 731.","What exit do we take for it?|Which exit is that?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca732-hedge-creek-falls":{"n":"Hedge Creek Falls","k":"p","q":[["Can you really walk behind Hedge Creek Falls?","Yes. A short trail from the exit leads to the waterfall, and there's a small cave behind it, so you can walk behind the falling water.","Can we walk behind it?|Mom, can we go behind the waterfall?|Walk behind the falls?","see"],["Why is Hedge Creek Falls called the Million Dollar Waterfall?","I-5's original plans would have buried the falls. Locals objected so strongly that the freeway was moved farther into the hill, at a cost of about a million dollars.","Why is it called that?|Why is it a million dollars, Dad?|Why Million Dollar?","name"],["How did the cave behind Hedge Creek Falls form?","The falls pour over the tip of an old lava flow from Mount Shasta. The cave is a natural hollow in that thick lava, not something carved by splashing water.","How did that cave get there?|Did somebody dig the cave?|Cave origin?","nature"],["What happened at Hedge Creek Falls with Black Bart?","In October 1879, stagecoach robber Black Bart held up a stage near Dunsmuir, and evidence suggested he hid in this cave first. He wore a flour sack mask and was known for being polite.","What did that robber do here?|Was there a bad guy at the waterfall?|Black Bart story?","history"],["What else is there to see at Hedge Creek Falls besides the waterfall?","There's a viewing platform over the river with a view of Mount Shasta.","What else is there at it?|Is there anything else to see there?|Any views?","see"]]},"ca733-where-shasta-soda-began":{"n":"Where Shasta soda began","k":"p","q":[["What was Shasta Springs?","It was a summer resort on the railroad main line in the late 1800s and early 1900s, just north of Dunsmuir.","What was it back then?|What was this place, like a hotel?|What's Shasta Springs?","history"],["Is this really where Shasta soda began?","Yes. Natural springs here were the original source of the water and drinks that became the Shasta brand of soft drinks.","Is that really where the soda came from?|Did the soda really start here?|Soda came from here?","famous"],["What was at the Shasta Springs resort?","It had its own train station, an incline railway up the hillside, fountains, a kiosk and a bottling plant.","What did it have there?|What cool stuff was at the resort?|What was there to see?","see"],["Can we visit Shasta Springs today?","No. The property now belongs to the Saint Germain Foundation and is closed to the public.","Can we go see it?|Can we go in there?|Can we stop?","visit"],["What happened to the Shasta Springs resort?","It closed in the early 1950s. The lower resort is gone, and blackberry bushes have overgrown its ruins.","What happened to it?|Why isn't it there anymore?|Where did the resort go?","today"],["Is it safe to hike to Mossbrae Falls from here?","Not really. The usual route means trespassing along the railroad tracks, and hikers were injured by trains there in 2011 and 2019.","Can we walk to the falls from there?|Is the waterfall hike dangerous?|Mossbrae Falls okay?","other"]]},"ca734-the-cantara-loop-spill":{"n":"The Cantara Loop spill","k":"p","q":[["What happened at the Cantara Loop?","A train derailed at a horseshoe curve called the Cantara Loop on the night of July 14, 1991. A tank car spilled about 19,000 gallons of pesticide into the river.","What happened there?|Mom, what went wrong at that loop?|What was the spill?","history"],["How bad was the Cantara Loop spill?","It's still the largest hazardous chemical spill in California history. More than a million fish died.","How bad was it?|Did a lot of animals die?|Was it a big deal?","famous"],["What chemical spilled at the Cantara Loop?","It was a soil fumigant called metam sodium. It left a 41-mile trail of destruction all the way to Shasta Lake.","What was that chemical?|What was in the tank car?|What spilled out?","history"],["What else died in the Cantara Loop spill besides fish?","Tens of thousands of frogs and crayfish died, plus millions of insects and other small river creatures. Hundreds of thousands of willows, alders and cottonwoods eventually died too.","Did it hurt anything else?|Did frogs and trees die too?|What else got killed?","nature"],["Has the river recovered from the Cantara Loop spill?","Largely, yes. The river is again open to catch-and-release fishing all year, though crayfish and frogs still have not fully returned.","Is it better now?|Can people fish there again?|Did the river come back?","today"]]},"ca736-mccloud-a-company-town":{"n":"McCloud, a company town","k":"p","q":[["What is McCloud, the company town?","McCloud is a lumber company town on the southern slope of Mount Shasta. For decades the company mill supplied the homes with electricity and even steam heat.","What is it exactly?|Why is it called a company town?|What's McCloud?","history"],["Is the McCloud mill still running?","No, the mill closed for good in 2002.","Is it still open?|Does the mill still make lumber?|Mill still working?","today"],["Why was the first McCloud mill a failure?","The first mill, built in 1892, failed because hauling lumber over the hills by oxen was too hard. The McCloud River Railroad, founded in 1895, solved that problem.","Why did it fail?|Why couldn't they get the logs out?|What went wrong with the first mill?","history"],["Is there anything historic to see in McCloud?","Yes, part of downtown McCloud is on the National Register of Historic Places. There's also a statue called Joaquin's Book in Hoo Hoo Park.","Is it historic?|Is there anything cool to see there?|Worth a stop?","see"]]},"ca737-mount-shasta-trout-hatchery":{"n":"Mount Shasta trout hatchery","k":"p","q":[["How old is the Mount Shasta trout hatchery?","The Mount Shasta Hatchery has raised trout since 1888. The state calls it the oldest trout hatchery west of the Mississippi.","How old is it?|How long have they been raising fish here, mom?|When did it start?","age"],["What can we see at the Mount Shasta trout hatchery?","From mid-March through July, visitors can watch the eggs hatch and the baby fish grow. Trout here can also grow to nearly 10 pounds.","What can you see at it?|Can we see baby fish?|What do we do there?","see"],["What was the Mount Shasta hatchery site before it raised fish?","The same address was once the Strawberry Valley Stage Station. Stagecoach drivers got fresh horses there from 1857 until the railroad arrived in 1886.","What was it before?|Was something else here a long time ago?|What happened here with the horses?","history"],["Who was Justin Sisson at the Mount Shasta hatchery?","Justin Sisson was the first postmaster of the Berryvale post office across the road. He built a hotel nearby in 1865 and started his own trout hatchery in 1887.","Who was he, the postmaster guy?|Who started the first hatchery here?|Who built the hotel?","people"],["Where can we learn more about the Mount Shasta hatchery's story?","The Sisson Museum tells the story of the hatchery and the area. The old stage station site is also California Historical Landmark No. 396.","Is there a museum about it?|Is there somewhere to learn the history?|Any museum near it?","visit"]]},"ca738-mount-shasta-city":{"n":"Mount Shasta City","k":"p","q":[["Who was Justin Sisson?","Justin Sisson ran the Strawberry Valley stage inn. He also gave the land for the railroad station, and the town was named Sisson after him.","Who was he?|Who was that Sisson guy, dad?|Sisson who?","people"],["How much snow does Mount Shasta City get?","A typical winter brings nearly 103 inches of snow. The snowiest year on record was 1952, with 349.6 inches.","Does it get a lot of snow there?|Does it snow a ton there, mom?|Snowy?","nature"],["How high up is Mount Shasta City?","The town sits at about 3,600 feet, less than 9 miles from the summit of the mountain.","How high is it?|Are we way up in the mountains there, dad?|Elevation?","size"],["Who famous was born in Mount Shasta City?","Anita Loos, the writer behind Gentlemen Prefer Blondes, was born here in 1888, when the town was called Sisson.","Was anyone famous born there?|Did any famous person come from here, mom?|Famous people?","famous"],["What's Shasta Abbey near Mount Shasta City?","Shasta Abbey is a Buddhist training monastery just north of town.","What is that abbey?|Is there a monastery there, dad?|Abbey?","see"]]},"ca739-headwaters-of-the-sacramento":{"n":"Headwaters of the Sacramento","k":"p","q":[["Where does the water at Headwaters of the Sacramento come from?","It started as glacial melt and spent more than 50 years filtering through volcanic rock before pouring out of the hillside at Big Springs.","Where does it come from?|How is there water just pouring out of a hill?|Where's the water from?","nature"],["How much water flows out at Headwaters of the Sacramento?","About 12 million gallons a day flow out here, and that water helps start the Sacramento River.","How much comes out of it?|Is it a lot of water, or just a trickle?|How much water?","size"],["Can we drink the water at Headwaters of the Sacramento?","People fill jugs there, but a sign warns the water has not been tested and may not be safe to drink.","Is it safe to drink?|Can we taste the water, Mom?|Drink it?","visit"],["Can we swim or wade at Headwaters of the Sacramento?","No. Wading and swimming in the spring are not allowed.","Can we get in it?|Can we splash around in the spring?|Swimming okay?","visit"],["What happened here at Headwaters of the Sacramento in 1901?","In 1901, a water wheel at Big Springs gave the town its first electricity, for a few street lights.","What did that water wheel do?|Did people long ago use the spring for something?|What's the 1901 thing?","history"],["Where does the water go after Headwaters of the Sacramento?","It flows through the Mount Shasta Hatchery and into Lake Siskiyou before joining the Sacramento River.","Where does it flow next?|Where does the water end up, kids wonder?|What's downstream?","nature"]]},"ca740-mount-shasta":{"n":"Mount Shasta","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Mount Shasta?","Mount Shasta rises to 14,179 feet. By volume it is the biggest stratovolcano in the Cascade Range.","How tall is it?|How big is that mountain, like really big?|How high?","size"],["Is Mount Shasta an active volcano?","Its last major eruption was about 3,000 years ago, with smaller ones about 200 years ago. A 2019 report put the chance of an eruption by 2049 at 3.5 percent.","Could it erupt again?|Is that mountain going to explode?|Will it erupt?","nature"],["Does Mount Shasta have glaciers?","Yes, seven glaciers ring its slopes. The Whitney Glacier is the longest in California, and the Hotlum holds the most ice.","Is there ice on it?|Is that snow on the mountain real ice?|Any glaciers?","nature"],["Are Mount Shasta's glaciers shrinking?","A 2002 survey found that, unusually, they had grown since 1951, fed by heavier snowfall. Researchers warned that further warming would likely make them retreat.","Is the ice on it melting?|Is the snow on that mountain going away?|Do the glaciers grow?","nature"],["What is Mount Shasta's name in Native languages?","The Shasta people call it Waka-nunee-Tuki-wuki, and in the Karuk language it is Uytaahkoo.","What do Native people call it?|Does that mountain have another name?|Other names?","name"]]},"ca741-the-lemurians-legend":{"n":"The Lemurians legend","k":"p","q":[["What is the Lemurians legend of Mount Shasta?","It's a legend that a hidden city sits on Mount Shasta, home to advanced beings from a lost continent called Lemuria. No one has ever found the city.","What's that legend about?|What's the secret city on the mountain, mom?|Lemurians?","famous"],["Has anyone ever found the Lemurian city on Mount Shasta?","No. Nobody has ever found the city.","Did they ever find it?|Is the hidden city real, dad?|Anyone find it?","other"],["Where did the idea of Lemuria come from?","It began in 1864, when a zoologist proposed a sunken continent to explain why lemur fossils turned up in both Madagascar and India.","Where did that idea start?|Who made up Lemuria?|How'd Lemuria start?","history"],["Is Lemuria a real lost continent?","Scientists dropped the idea once plate tectonics explained the puzzle of the lemur fossils in Madagascar and India.","Is that continent actually real?|Did a whole continent really sink?|Lemuria real?","history"],["How did the Lemurians legend get tied to Mount Shasta?","By 1925 writers described a hidden Lemurian village on Shasta, and a 1931 book by Harvey Spencer Lewis, written under a pen name, cemented the legend.","How did it end up on Shasta?|Who put the legend on the mountain?|Who wrote the story?","people"],["Are there older legends about Mount Shasta?","Yes. The Klamath Tribes tell much older stories of Skell, the Spirit of the Above-World, living on the mountain and battling Llao of Mount Mazama with hot rocks and lava.","Is there an older story about it?|Any other mountain stories, mom?|Older legends?","history"]]},"ca742-black-butte":{"n":"Black Butte","k":"p","q":[["How old is Black Butte?","Black Butte is a cluster of lava domes that squeezed out about 9,000 to 10,000 years ago.","How old is it?|How long ago did that thing form, like, a million years?|How old is this rock?","age"],["What other names has Black Butte had?","Over the years it has also been called Wintoon Butte, Cone Mountain, Sugar Loaf and Muir's Peak.","Did it ever have a different name?|Does that mountain have any other names, like funny ones?|Old names?","name"],["Was there ever a fire lookout on Black Butte?","Yes. A Forest Service fire lookout stood on top from the early 1930s until the Columbus Day Storm of 1962 destroyed it. A new one was used from 1963 to 1973.","Did they have a lookout on it?|Was there a little house on top of that mountain?|Any lookout up there?","history"],["Can you hike up Black Butte?","Yes, a 2.5-mile trail climbs to the summit. On clear days you can see Mount McLoughlin in Oregon, 70 miles away.","Can we climb it?|Can we walk up to the top of that mountain?|Is there a trail?","see"],["What is Black Butte Summit on I-5?","I-5 crosses a 3,912-foot pass at the western base of Black Butte. It is called Black Butte Summit.","How high is the pass at it?|How high up are we at that mountain?|What's the pass called?","size"],["What railroad lines meet at Black Butte?","The railroad's Cascade Line by way of Klamath Falls, built in the 1920s, meets the older Siskiyou Line at Black Butte.","Is there a railroad near it?|Are there trains by that mountain?|Any tracks there?","other"]]},"ca743-shastina-and-diller-canyon":{"n":"Shastina and Diller Canyon","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Shastina?","Shastina is about 12,330 feet tall. That's even taller than Mount Adams in Washington.","How tall is it?|How big is that little mountain, like for real?|How high?","size"],["How old is Shastina?","Shastina was built about 9,500 years ago, after the last ice age. That's why its summit crater is still intact.","How old is that peak?|Is that mountain, like, a million years old?|How old?","age"],["Why is it called Shastina?","Shastina simply means little Shasta. It's the smaller peak on Mount Shasta's west side.","Why is that called Shastina?|Why does that mountain have such a funny name?|Shastina means what?","name"],["What is Diller Canyon?","Diller Canyon is the big gash down Shastina's side facing Weed. Hot volcanic flows cut it, and it drops more than 7,000 vertical feet.","What made that big gash in it?|What's that giant scratch on the mountain?|What's the groove?","nature"],["Is there a lake on top of Shastina?","Yes, the crater holds three small lakes, including Clarence King Lake and Sisson Lake. They're the highest open-air lakes in the whole Cascade Range.","Are there lakes up there on it?|Can you swim in a lake on top of the mountain?|Any lakes up top?","nature"],["What did Shastina's eruptions do to the land?","Its eruptions, along with Black Butte's, sent hot flows across about 43 square miles. That includes much of the ground where Mount Shasta City and Weed now stand.","What did it do when it erupted?|Did the volcano blow up near where towns are now?|What did the lava hit?","history"]]},"ca744-mount-eddy":{"n":"Mount Eddy","k":"p","q":[["How tall is Mount Eddy?","Mount Eddy is 9,037 feet tall, and it's the highest point in the Trinity Mountains.","How tall is it?|How big is that mountain, mom?|How high up is that?","size"],["Why is Mount Eddy called Mount Eddy?","It's named for Olive Paddock Eddy, who is often mistakenly credited as the first woman to climb Mount Shasta.","Why is it called that?|Who is that mountain named after?|Who's Eddy?","name"],["What is Mount Eddy made of?","It's made mostly of a rock called serpentinized peridotite.","What kind of rock is it?|What's that mountain made out of?|Is it rock?","nature"],["What did the Wintu call Mount Eddy?","The Wintu name may have been Num-mel-be-le-sas-pam, which means west blaze mountain.","Did it have another name?|What was it called before?|Old name?","name"]]},"ca747-pluto-s-cave":{"n":"Pluto's Cave","k":"p","q":[["How old is Pluto's Cave?","Pluto's Cave is a lava tube roughly 190,000 years old.","How old is it?|How many years old is that cave, like, really old?|How old?","age"],["Why is it called Pluto's Cave?","It was named for Pluto, the god of the underworld.","Why is it called that?|Is Pluto the planet or the dog? Why is it named that?|Why Pluto?","name"],["Who found Pluto's Cave?","A man named Nelson Cash stumbled onto it in 1863 while he was looking for stray cattle.","Who found it?|Did some kid find that cave?|Who discovered it?","people"],["Can we go inside Pluto's Cave?","Yes, visitors can hike about 1,200 feet inside the cave.","Can you go in it?|Can we walk in the cave, is it scary?|Can we hike inside?","visit"],["What lived or was found inside Pluto's Cave?","In 1863 the survey scientists found bats all the way to the end, and skulls of bighorn sheep near the entrance, where the roof had fallen in.","What did they find in it?|Were there animals in that cave, like bats?|What's in there?","nature"],["Why did John Muir say Pluto's Cave was hard to find?","John Muir warned that it was hard to find because its openings are level with the ground.","Why is it hard to find?|Why can't people find that cave?|Is it hidden?","other"]]},"ca747-weed-a-windy-lumber-town":{"n":"Weed, a windy lumber town","k":"p","q":[["Why is the town called Weed?","It's named for Abner Weed, who found that the strong winds here were good for drying lumber.","Why is it called that?|How did Weed get such a funny name, Mom?|Weed name?","name"],["What is the motto of Weed?","The town motto is Weed like to welcome you. Over the years its road signs have had a habit of disappearing.","What does it say on the signs?|Does Weed have a funny slogan?|Weed motto?","famous"],["What is there to see or do in Weed?","You can visit the Weed Historic Lumber Town Museum to see sawmill machinery from the early days. The town also holds a Carnevale every June or July, with bocce ball tournaments.","Is it worth stopping there?|Is there anything fun to do for kids?|Things to see?","visit"],["Where does Weed get its drinking water?","The town's water comes from Beaughan Spring and is piped straight to homes.","Where does it get water from?|Is the water from a spring or something?|Weed water?","nature"]]},"ca749-weed-s-wildfires":{"n":"Weed's wildfires","k":"p","q":[["What was the Boles Fire in Weed?","The Boles Fire was a wind-driven fire that spread through Weed on September 15, 2014. It destroyed more than 150 buildings in a single day, most of them homes.","What was that fire in Weed?|What happened to Weed in the big fire?|Boles Fire?","history"],["What was the Mill Fire in Weed?","The Mill Fire started on September 2, 2022, at the lumber mill north of town. It burned the Lincoln Heights neighborhood.","What was that fire that started at the mill?|Did a fire burn down houses here too?|Mill Fire?","history"],["What caused the Mill Fire in Weed?","Fire officials traced it to the mill's operations. The company said a machine meant to cool hot ash from its wood-burning power plant may have failed.","How did it start?|Who started the fire, Mom?|Mill Fire cause?","history"],["How much damage did the Mill Fire do?","The Mill Fire destroyed 118 structures, and two people died. It raced north into Lake Shastina.","How bad was it?|Did people get hurt in that fire?|How many buildings burned?","history"],["What was the Lava Fire near Weed?","The 2021 Lava Fire is believed to have been started by lightning. It burned more than 26,000 acres on Mount Shasta's slopes and damaged a railroad trestle.","What happened on Mount Shasta in the Lava Fire?|Did a fire burn the mountain?|Lava Fire?","nature"],["Did the Lava Fire affect the train?","Yes. The Lava Fire damaged a railroad trestle, which disrupted Amtrak's Coast Starlight for weeks.","Did that fire stop Amtrak?|Why did the train stop running, Dad?|Lava Fire and Amtrak?","history"]]},"ca748-a-two-block-highway":{"n":"A two-block highway","k":"p","q":[["How long is A two-block highway?","It's only two blocks long. That makes it the second-shortest state highway in California.","How long is it?|How many blocks is that, really?|Is it really that short?","size"],["Where does A two-block highway go?","Route 265 runs from U.S. 97 in Weed to the North Weed interchange on I-5.","Where does it start and end?|Where does that little road lead?|What does it connect?","other"],["What was A two-block highway before it was Route 265?","It used to be part of U.S. Route 99.","Was it always called that?|Was that road something else once?|What did it used to be?","history"],["Why is A two-block highway called a hidden highway?","For many years it had no signs at all. The only clue was a bridge number spray-painted on the I-5 overpass: 265 overcrossing.","Why didn't it have signs?|Why was that road a secret?|How did people know it was there?","famous"],["When did A two-block highway finally get road signs?","Signs finally went up in 2005, in preparation for a full rebuild of Weed Boulevard.","When did they put up signs for it?|When did that road get signs?|Does it have signs now?","history"]]},"ca751-gray-wolves-return":{"n":"Gray wolves return","k":"p","q":[["When did gray wolves come back to California?","In late December 2011, a collared wolf from Oregon called OR-7 crossed the state line. He was the first wild wolf confirmed in California since 1924.","When did they come back?|Wait, wolves are back in California? Since when?|When did wolves return?","history"],["Who was OR-7, the wolf that came back to California?","OR-7 was a wolf from Oregon nicknamed Journey. He wandered more than 1,000 miles from the Wallowa Mountains of northeastern Oregon.","Who was he?|Did that wolf have a nickname?|Which wolf was that?","people"],["What was the Shasta Pack?","It was the state's first resident wolf pack in over a century, with two adults and five pups. The pack later vanished, and no one has explained why.","What happened to that pack?|Was there a whole wolf family here?|Shasta Pack, what's that?","history"],["How many gray wolves live in California now?","By December 2025 the state counted 55 wolves in nine packs.","How many are there now?|Are there lots of wolves out here?|How many wolf packs?","nature"],["Are gray wolves protected in California?","Wolves are listed as endangered in California. The state has also set aside money to pay ranchers for livestock lost to them.","Is it against the law to hurt them?|Can people shoot the wolves?|Are they endangered?","nature"],["Has California ever killed wolves that attacked cattle?","Yes, in 2025, after nearly 100 tries at scaring them off, officials killed four wolves from a Plumas County pack that kept preying on cattle. It was the first time the state had done so.","Did they kill the wolves?|Why did officials kill wolves?|Do wolves eat the cows?","other"]]},"ca755-old-highway-99":{"n":"Old Highway 99","k":"p","q":[["What was U.S. 99 before I-5 replaced it?","U.S. 99 was the main West Coast highway before I-5. It ran from the Mexican border to the Canadian border, and people called it the Golden State Highway and the Main Street of California.","What was it before the freeway came along?|What was the road before this big highway, mom?|Old 99, what was that?","history"],["How old is Old Highway 99?","U.S. 99 was created in 1926. That's the road this old stretch comes from, back when it was the main West Coast highway.","How old is it?|How long ago did they build this old road?|When did it start?","age"],["What happened to U.S. 99?","A 1963 bill by State Senator Randolph Collier renumbered California's highways. By 1968 the 99 route was gone, replaced here by I-5.","Why did it go away?|Where did the old road go, dad?|Who got rid of 99?","history"],["Can you still drive on Old Highway 99?","Yes. A two-lane county road called Old Highway 99 runs between Weed and Yreka, still in the same form it had when it was U.S. 99.","Is it still a road you can use?|Can we drive on the old road, dad?|Does it still exist?","today"],["Does Old Highway 99 run next to the freeway?","Roughly, yes. It parallels the freeway, but at one point it swings several miles away from it.","Does it follow I-5?|Is the old road right beside us, mom?|Does it go the same way?","see"],["What happened to Old Highway 99 north of Yreka?","North of Yreka, old 99 became State Route 263. Much of it between Hornbrook and Oregon has been torn up or sits on private land.","Does it keep going past Yreka?|Where does the old road go after Yreka, dad?|Is old 99 still there up north?","other"]]},"ca759-gazelle-s-antelope-name":{"n":"Gazelle's antelope name","k":"p","q":[["What is Gazelle's antelope name all about?","A gazelle is a small African antelope. Gazelle's post office took the name in 1870, and a steamer called the Gazelle also ran on the Sacramento River in the 1850s.","What's that name about?|Why's it called Gazelle, like the animal?|Where's the name come from?","name"],["When did Gazelle get its name?","Gazelle's post office took the name Gazelle in 1870.","When did it get that name?|How old is Gazelle's name?|When was it named?","age"],["How many people live in Gazelle?","Gazelle is a tiny town of about 95 people.","How big is it?|Is it a big town?|How many people live there?","people"],["What kind of land is Gazelle in?","Gazelle sits in a dry farming valley in the Shasta River's watershed. Most of the river's water there comes from groundwater and irrigation runoff.","What's the land like around it?|Is it a dry place?|What do they grow there?","nature"],["Which exit do we take for Gazelle?","Gazelle is at exit 759.","What exit is it?|Which exit is that?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca759-the-hummocks-of-shasta-valley":{"n":"The hummocks of Shasta Valley","k":"p","q":[["What are the hummocks of Shasta Valley?","They're the little lumpy hills scattered across the flat valley. They are pieces of debris left behind when an older version of Mount Shasta collapsed in a huge landslide.","What are those lumpy hills?|What are those bumpy hills, Mom?|What are those hills?","other"],["How did the hummocks of Shasta Valley form?","An older Mount Shasta collapsed in a giant landslide, and the debris spread across the valley. The hummocks are the pieces of that debris.","How did those hills get there?|Why are there bumpy hills in the flat valley?|How'd they get made?","history"],["How old are the hummocks of Shasta Valley?","The landslide that made them happened hundreds of thousands of years ago, when an older version of Mount Shasta collapsed.","How old are those hills?|Did dinosaurs see those bumpy hills?|How long ago did that happen?","age"],["How big was the landslide that made the Shasta Valley hummocks?","It was one of the largest landslides known on Earth. About 6.5 cubic miles of rock flowed into the valley, spreading over more than 170 square miles.","How big was that landslide?|Was the mountain landslide really huge, Dad?|How much rock fell?","size"],["How far do the Shasta Valley hummocks stretch?","The hills reach from the base of Mount Shasta to just past Montague. The whole flow is 28 miles long.","How far do those hills go?|Where do the bumpy hills start and stop?|How long is the landslide?","size"],["What exit is the Shasta Valley hummocks view?","It's exit 759. Look for small lumpy hills scattered across the flat valley floor.","What exit is it for that?|Which exit for the lumpy hills?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca762-hay-and-cattle-country":{"n":"Hay and cattle country","k":"p","q":[["What is the biggest crop grown in Hay and cattle country?","Alfalfa hay is the biggest field crop in Siskiyou County. In 2023, farmers cut about 175,000 tons of it from some 29,000 acres.","What do they grow in those fields?|What are all those fields growing, mom?|Biggest crop here?","nature"],["How many cattle live in Hay and cattle country?","Ranchers counted around 45,000 cattle and calves at the start of 2023.","How many cows are out there?|Are those a lot of cows, dad?|How many cattle?","nature"],["Why do the farms in Hay and cattle country need irrigation?","The valley sits in the rain shadow of the Klamath Mountains and gets only about 14 inches of rain a year, so the fields depend on groundwater and irrigation.","Why does it need so much watering?|Why don't the fields just get rain, dad?|Why so dry here?","nature"],["How much are the cattle in Hay and cattle country worth?","In 2023, Siskiyou County's cows, steers, heifers and bulls together were worth about 112 million dollars.","How much money is all that cattle worth?|Are the cows worth a lot of money, dad?|Cattle worth?","famous"],["What did people do here before ranching in Hay and cattle country?","Shasta people harvested grasshoppers here. They set patches of grassland on fire and gathered the cooked insects.","Who lived here before the ranchers?|Did people live here a long time ago, mom?|Who was here first?","history"],["What else do farmers grow near Hay and cattle country?","Siskiyou County also grows wheat, barley, rye and even mint.","Do they grow anything besides hay?|Do they grow weird stuff here too, dad?|Other crops?","nature"]]},"ca764-a-billion-strawberry-plants":{"n":"A billion strawberry plants","k":"p","q":[["Do they really grow a billion strawberry plants in Siskiyou County?","Roughly, yes. In 2023 the county grew them on more than 2,800 acres, at about 360,000 plants per acre.","Is that billion number real?|Wait, a billion plants? Really?|A billion? Seriously?","size"],["Are the strawberry plants grown for berries we can eat?","No. They count as nursery crops, grown for planting rather than for berries.","Do we eat what they grow?|Are those strawberries for eating or what?|Are there berries on them?","nature"],["How much money do the strawberry plants make for the county?","About 184 million dollars. That beats hay and cattle as the county's most valuable farm product.","How much is it worth?|How many dollars do all those plants bring in?|Is it a lot of money?","famous"],["What else do farmers grow around Siskiyou County besides strawberry plants?","The county also grew raspberry plants and garlic for planting. Farmers harvested about 3,300 acres of potatoes, including organic ones, and 170 acres of horseradish.","What else grows around it?|What other stuff do kids see growing here?|Any other crops?","nature"],["What exit is the billion strawberry plants area at?","It's at exit 764.","What exit is it?|Which exit do we take for the strawberry plants?|Exit number?","exit"]]},"ca766-shasta-valley-wildlife-area":{"n":"Shasta Valley Wildlife Area","k":"p","q":[["How big is Shasta Valley Wildlife Area?","It protects some 4,700 acres of juniper woodland, riverside forest, wetlands and fields, with Mount Shasta as its backdrop.","How big is it?|How much land is that, mom?|How large is this place?","size"],["When was Shasta Valley Wildlife Area set aside?","The wildlife area was set aside in 1991.","How old is it?|Dad, when did this place start?|When did it begin?","age"],["What birds can we see at Shasta Valley Wildlife Area?","Sandhill cranes, waterfowl, birds of prey and shorebirds all use it.","What birds are there?|Are there any big birds there, dad?|Which birds live there?","nature"],["What animals live at Shasta Valley Wildlife Area?","Besides the birds, deer, porcupines and coyotes live there too.","Are there animals there?|Can we see deer or coyotes, mom?|What lives there?","nature"],["Can you fish at Shasta Valley Wildlife Area?","Yes, it has three deep-water reservoirs, including Trout Lake, a trophy fishery for rainbow trout and largemouth bass.","Is there fishing there?|Can we catch fish there, dad?|Any fish there?","see"],["What should we know before visiting Shasta Valley Wildlife Area?","During waterfowl hunting season, other visitors can come only on certain days, so it is worth checking before a visit.","Can we just go in anytime?|Is it okay to go there, mom?|Anything to check first?","visit"]]},"ca770-state-of-jefferson":{"n":"State of Jefferson","k":"p","q":[["What was the State of Jefferson?","It was a 1941 plan to break away and form a new state from Siskiyou, Del Norte and Modoc counties in California plus four counties in Oregon.","What was that about?|Was Jefferson a real state, or what?|What's this Jefferson thing?","history"],["What happened on the day the State of Jefferson started?","On November 27, 1941, young men waving hunting rifles stopped traffic on U.S. 99 south of Yreka and handed out a proclamation of independence for the State of Jefferson.","What did they do that day?|Did people really point guns at cars?|What went down on the highway?","history"],["What is the Double Cross on the State of Jefferson seal?","The seal is a gold pan with two Xs, called the Double Cross. It stands for feeling abandoned by both state capitals.","What does it stand for?|Why is the seal a gold pan with Xs, Mom?|What's the double cross?","name"],["Who led the State of Jefferson movement and what happened to him?","Port Orford mayor Gilbert Gable led the movement. He died suddenly on December 2, 1941, and Yreka had been picked as the capital.","Who was in charge of it?|Who was the boss of Jefferson?|Who was the leader?","people"],["Why did the State of Jefferson rebellion fizzle out?","Five days after the leader died, Pearl Harbor was attacked, and people turned their attention to the war.","Why did it end?|Why didn't they get their own state?|Why'd it stop?","history"],["Where can we see the original State of Jefferson gold pan?","The gold pan said to be the first model for the seal is on display at the Siskiyou County Museum in Yreka.","Can we see it somewhere?|Is the gold pan on display anywhere?|Where's the gold pan?","see"]]},"ca773-yreka-the-gold-town":{"n":"Yreka, the gold town","k":"p","q":[["How did Yreka get its gold rush start?","A mule packer named Abraham Thompson found gold here in March 1851. By April, 2,000 miners had arrived at what they called Thompson's Dry Diggings.","How did it get started?|Why did people rush to Yreka, Dad?|When was gold found there?","history"],["Where does the name Yreka come from?","It comes from waik'a, the Shasta people's word for Mount Shasta. In the Shasta language, waik'a means north mountain or white mountain.","Where does that name come from?|Why is it called Yreka, such a weird name?|What does Yreka mean?","name"],["What is Mark Twain's story about the name Yreka?","Mark Twain told a funnier story: a bakery sign seen backwards, BAKERY without the B, read YREKA.","What was that funny story about it?|Did a bakery sign really name Yreka?|Twain story?","name"],["What is the old courthouse in Yreka?","Yreka is the county seat, and its brick courthouse was finished in 1857. It stayed in use until 2021.","What is that brick building about?|Is the courthouse still used, Mom?|Courthouse history?","history"],["What is there to see in downtown Yreka?","The heart of downtown, along West Miner Street, is a historic district and a California Historical Landmark. The town also holds Gold Rush Days every June.","What can we see in it?|Is there anything cool to look at there?|Is downtown worth a stop?","see"],["What is the Karuk name for Yreka?","In the Karuk language, Yreka is kahtishraam. The Karuk Tribe has housing, offices and the Rain Rock Casino on its land here.","What do the Karuk call it?|Is there another name for Yreka?|Karuk word?","name"]]},"ca776-yreka-builds-its-own-railroad":{"n":"Yreka builds its own railroad","k":"p","q":[["Why did Yreka build its own railroad?","The Southern Pacific skipped Yreka for a shorter, more level route through Montague. So the people of Yreka built their own railroad.","Why did it have to build its own railroad?|Why didn't the train just come to Yreka?|Why'd Yreka make its own train?","history"],["When did the Yreka Railroad open?","The Yreka Railroad opened in January 1889. It connected the town to the main line at Montague.","When did it open?|How old is that railroad?|When did the little train start?","age"],["Who bought the Yreka Western Railroad in 1953?","Willis Kyle bought it in 1953. He went on to build a group of short-line railroads.","Who owned it after that?|Who bought the little train?|Who got the train in 1953?","people"],["Is the Yreka railroad still running today?","Yes, it still hauls freight such as wood chips, forest products and propane. Its tourist excursions ran for years, until 2016.","Is it still used?|Do trains still run on it?|Does the little train still go?","today"]]},"ca779-the-shasta-river":{"n":"The Shasta River","k":"p","q":[["Where does the Shasta River start and where does it end up?","The Shasta River starts on Mount Eddy and runs 58 miles through Shasta Valley to the Klamath.","Where does it start and finish?|Where does the river begin, Dad?|Where's it go?","nature"],["Where does the Shasta River get its water?","Most of it comes from groundwater and irrigation runoff. Some is Mount Shasta snowmelt that flows out of lava tubes as springs.","Where does it get its water from?|Is the river water from the mountain?|Where's the water come from?","nature"],["Do salmon still return to the Shasta River today?","Yes, but far fewer. Today fewer than one in ten of those salmon still return, and recent droughts have made it hard for young fish to survive.","Are there still salmon in it?|Are there still lots of fish there?|Any fish left?","today"],["What kinds of fish live in the Shasta River?","Chinook salmon spawn there, and the river also supports coho salmon and steelhead.","What fish does it have?|What animals live in the river?|What's swimming in there?","nature"]]},"ca781-the-shasta-people":{"n":"The Shasta people","k":"p","q":[["Where is the homeland of the Shasta people?","Shasta Valley and the nearby Klamath River are part of the Shasta homeland. The people of the Shasta River were known as the Ahotireitsu.","Is that where they lived?|Where did the Shasta people live, mom?|Shasta homeland?","history"],["What did Shasta children do along the Klamath River?","Women and children dove in the Klamath River for mussels.","Did they do that as kids too?|What did Shasta kids do for fun or food?|Kids on the Klamath?","kids"],["Do Shasta people still live here today?","Yes. Today Shasta people live in Yreka and on the Quartz Valley Indian Reservation, and on the Grand Ronde and Siletz reservations in Oregon.","Are they still around?|Are there still Shasta people, or did they all go away?|Shasta people today?","today"],["What happened to the Shasta people during the Gold Rush?","Gold miners poured onto Shasta land in 1850 and 1851. In July 1852, miners killed 14 Shasta people in Shasta Valley, and in the late 1850s families were forcibly removed to reservations in Oregon.","What did that do to them?|Why did the miners hurt the Shasta people?|Gold miners and Shasta?","history"],["What was the Shasta treaty of 1851?","Shasta leaders signed a treaty in November 1851 promising a reservation in Scott Valley. It was one of 18 California treaties the state opposed and the U.S. Senate rejected.","Did that treaty work?|Did the Shasta get promised land and then not get it?|Shasta treaty?","history"]]},"ca783-the-klamath-mountains":{"n":"The Klamath Mountains","k":"p","q":[["How did the Klamath Mountains form?","Their rocks began as volcanic islands and pieces of other continents in the Pacific. They later crashed into North America.","How did that happen?|How'd these mountains get here, anyway?|How did they form?","history"],["What trees grow in the Klamath Mountains?","They hold one of the richest mixes of cone-bearing trees on Earth, about 30 kinds. Brewer's spruce and Port Orford cedar grow almost nowhere else.","What kind of trees are in there?|What are all those trees on the mountains?|What's growing there?","nature"],["What is the Miracle Mile in the Klamath Mountains?","It's a single square mile of the Russian Wilderness where botanists counted 17 kinds of conifers in 1969. An 18th was found there in 2013.","What was that Miracle Mile thing?|What's the Miracle Mile?|Why is it called a miracle?","famous"],["What animals live in the Klamath Mountains?","The mountains hold black bears and mountain lions. They also hold nine native species in the salmon family.","Are there animals in there?|Are there bears in those mountains?|What lives there?","nature"]]},"ca786-randolph-collier-rest-area":{"n":"Randolph Collier Rest Area","k":"p","q":[["Who was Randolph Collier?","He was a California State Senator who lived in Siskiyou County nearly all his life. This rest area by the Klamath River honors him.","Who was he?|Who is this rest area named after, anyway?|Who's Collier?","people"],["What is Randolph Collier famous for?","He co-wrote the 1947 law that laid the groundwork for California's freeway system. So in a way, you're driving on his idea.","What did he do that was so important?|Why did they name a rest area after him?|What's he known for?","famous"],["How long was Randolph Collier a state senator?","He served in the California state Senate from 1939 to 1976, and he chaired its Transportation Committee.","How long was he in the Senate?|How many years did he work for the state?|How long did he serve?","history"],["What did Randolph Collier have to do with Highway 99?","In 1963, his bill to simplify California's highway numbers began the end of U.S. 99.","Did he have something to do with it ending?|Why did old Highway 99 go away?|Who ended 99?","history"],["What is the Randolph Collier Rest Area exit number?","It's at exit 786, and the rest area is just 0.1 miles off the freeway.","Which exit is it?|What exit do we take for it?|Exit number?","exit"],["What else is named after Randolph Collier?","A tunnel on U.S. 199, the Redwood Highway, also carries his name.","Is anything else named after him?|Is there anything else with his name on it?|Other Collier stuff?","other"]]},"ca786-the-klamath-river":{"n":"The Klamath River","k":"p","q":[["What happened to the dams on the Klamath River?","Four hydroelectric dams upstream were torn down in 2023 and 2024. It was the largest dam removal in American history.","What happened to the dams on it?|Did they really knock down dams here?|Where did the dams go?","history"],["Why does the Klamath River matter for salmon?","The Klamath is the third-largest salmon and steelhead river on the West Coast of the lower 48 states.","Why does it matter for fish?|Are there lots of fish in this river?|Is this a fish river?","famous"],["Have salmon come back to the Klamath River since the dams came down?","Yes. On October 17, 2024, a fall-run Chinook salmon was spotted in Oregon's Upper Klamath Basin, the first to reach it in more than 100 years.","Did the fish come back after that?|Did any salmon swim up the river?|Are the salmon back?","nature"],["How much river did removing the Klamath dams open up?","Removing the dams reopened about 400 miles of river to spawning salmon. Copco No. 2 came down first, in October 2023, and the last dam was gone in 2024.","How much river did that open up?|How far can the fish go now?|Is it a lot of river?","size"],["How do I spot the Klamath River from I-5?","Look for the river below the freeway bridge. It's near Exit 786, where the river turns sharply south to meet I-5.","How do we spot it from the freeway?|Where do we look to see the river?|What exit is the river?","exit"]]},"ca787-into-karuk-country":{"n":"Into Karuk country","k":"p","q":[["What does the word Karuk mean?","Karuk means upriver. Traditionally the Karuk also called themselves the middle of the world people.","What does that word mean?|Why are they called the Karuk, Mom?|Karuk meaning?","name"],["How many Karuk people are there?","The Karuk Tribe is one of the largest tribes in California. The 2010 census counted about 6,100 Karuk people.","How many people are in that tribe?|Are there a lot of Karuk people?|Karuk population?","people"],["How much land is Karuk ancestral territory?","Karuk ancestral territory covers more than a million acres along the Klamath. It's land the tribe has never ceded.","How big is it?|Is that a lot of land, Dad?|How big is Karuk land?","size"],["What was the Klamath River like for the Karuk long ago?","The river was once lined with more than 120 Karuk villages. Highway 96 follows the Klamath River west into Karuk country.","What used to be along that river?|Did people live along the river, Mom?|Old Karuk villages?","history"],["What does the Karuk Tribe do today?","The tribe serves its people from Happy Camp, Orleans and Yreka. It runs everything from health clinics to salmon restoration, cultural burning and dam removal work.","What do they do now?|Is the tribe still around, Dad?|Karuk tribe today?","today"],["Is the Karuk language still spoken?","The Karuk language is not related to any other known language. Fewer than five people spoke it fluently full-time as of 2025, and the tribe has an active program to bring it back.","Do people still speak it?|Can they still talk Karuk, Mom?|Karuk language?","other"]]},"ca789-where-siskiyou-got-its-name":{"n":"Where Siskiyou got its name","k":"p","q":[["Where did the name Siskiyou come from?","Nobody is sure. One story says it was a Cree word for a bob-tailed horse. Another says it comes from the French name Six Cailloux, meaning six stones.","Where did it get that name?|Why is it called Siskiyou, anyway?|Siskiyou name?","name"],["What does Siskiyou mean?","It might mean bob-tailed horse, from a Cree word, or six stones, from the French Six Cailloux. Nobody knows for sure which story is right.","What does that word mean?|Does Siskiyou mean something silly, like a horse?|Meaning of Siskiyou?","name"],["What is the bob-tailed horse story behind Siskiyou?","Historian Richard Mackie says a bob-tailed horse died in 1829 on Alexander McLeod's Hudson's Bay Company trip over a pass. The pass was later named for it.","What happened with that horse?|Was there really a horse named Siskiyou?|Tell me the horse story.","history"],["What is the six stones story about the Siskiyou name?","The ford was on Oregon's Umpqua River, where six large rocks lay in the water. Trapper Stephen Meek is said to have carried the name to the Klamath.","Where were the six stones?|What are the six rocks the name is about?|Six stones story?","history"]]},"ca790-agricultural-inspection-at-hornbrook":{"n":"Agricultural inspection at Hornbrook","k":"p","q":[["Why does Hornbrook have an agricultural inspection station?","Inspectors here look for pests in plants or cargo that could harm California's farms. It's one of 16 stations the state runs where major highways cross into California.","Why do we have to stop at that station?|Why are we stopping here, is somebody checking us?|What's this stop for?","other"],["What will the inspectors ask us at the Hornbrook station?","Passenger cars with California or neighboring-state plates usually get just a few questions about what they're carrying. RVs, trucks, and vehicles hauling boats or livestock are higher risk, and their loads may be looked over.","Do they check passenger cars like ours?|Are they going to search our car, Mom?|What do they ask?","visit"],["How old are the agricultural inspection stations?","The first stations went up in the early 1920s on highways from Nevada near Reno. Destructive alfalfa weevils had turned up in campgrounds along those roads.","When did they start doing this at the border?|How long have they been checking cars, like a hundred years?|How old is it?","age"],["What happened that led to the first inspection stations?","Destructive alfalfa weevils turned up in campgrounds along highways from Nevada. In the first year, inspectors found live weevils inside camping gear.","What was the first bug they found?|Did they really find bugs in people's camping stuff?|What started it?","history"],["What happens with bees at the Hornbrook inspection station?","Loads of beehives are logged, and the driver must name where in California the bees are going. That way the county's agriculture office can inspect them on arrival.","Do they check trucks carrying beehives?|Do they check for bees, too?|Can bees get through here?","nature"]]},"ca796-hilt-the-border-town":{"n":"Hilt, the border town","k":"p","q":[["Why is the town called Hilt?","Hilt is named for an early settler, John Hilt.","Who is it named after?|Why's it got such a weird name, Mom?|Hilt name origin?","name"],["What kind of town is Hilt?","Hilt grew up as a company town, first for the Northern California Lumber Company and then for the Fruit Growers Supply Company, an affiliate of Sunkist.","What was that town built for?|Why did people even live there?|What's Hilt's story?","history"],["What exit is Hilt?","Hilt is exit 796, the northernmost I-5 exit in California.","Which exit do we take for it?|Is that the last exit in California?|Hilt exit number?","exit"],["What happened to Hilt in the Klamathon Fire?","In July 2018 the Klamathon Fire forced Hilt to evacuate. It destroyed 82 structures and killed one person before it was contained on July 21.","Did that town burn down?|Was there a big fire there?|Hilt fire story?","history"],["What started the Klamathon Fire near Hilt?","The fire started near Hornbrook, and investigators blamed reckless burning of debris.","How did it start?|Who started the fire, Dad?|Fire cause?","history"],["What mountains are just north of Hilt?","The Siskiyou Mountains rise just north of Hilt. They are the divide between the Klamath and Rogue river basins.","What are those mountains past it?|What's up the road from the town?|Mountains ahead?","nature"]]},"ca797-the-42nd-parallel":{"n":"The 42nd parallel","k":"p","q":[["What is the 42nd parallel?","It's the line the California and Oregon border follows. It was set in an 1819 treaty between the United States and Spain.","What is that line we're crossing?|Why is there a line on the map here?|What's the 42nd?","history"],["Why is the California border at the 42nd parallel?","California kept the line from the 1819 treaty. When it became a state in 1850, it kept that line as its northern border.","Why did California use that line?|Why is the border here, Mom?|Who picked this border?","history"],["Is there a border dispute at the 42nd parallel?","Yes. A survey around 1870 marked the line with errors, creating a dispute that continues to this day.","Is it still argued about?|Did people fight over the border?|Is the line wrong?","history"],["How high does I-5 get near the 42nd parallel?","A few miles north, in Oregon, I-5 crosses Siskiyou Summit at 4,310 feet, its highest point anywhere.","How high is it up there?|Are we going up a mountain?|Highest point?","size"],["What is Pilot Rock near the 42nd parallel?","Pilot Rock is a volcanic plug about 25 million years old, just east of the summit. It guided pioneers coming north from California on the Applegate Trail.","What's that rock up there?|What's that big rock, Dad?|Pilot Rock?","nature"],["Does the Pacific Crest Trail cross I-5 near the 42nd parallel?","Yes. A few miles north at Siskiyou Summit, the Pacific Crest Trail crosses the freeway. Snow generally falls up there from November through April.","Can hikers cross the freeway up there?|Is there a hiking trail up there?|Pacific Crest Trail?","see"]]},"z:0":{"n":"Border and South Bay","k":"z","q":[["Whose land is the Border and South Bay stretch on?","This stretch crosses Kumeyaay land, between the San Ysidro border crossing and Chula Vista.","Whose land is it here?|Who lived here first, mom?|Who's land is this?","people"],["What kind of land does I-5 cross near the South Bay?","The freeway crosses river valleys, salt ponds and marsh at the south end of San Diego Bay.","What kind of land is that out there?|What's out the window, dad?|What's the land like?","nature"]]},"z:9":{"n":"San Diego Bay and downtown","k":"z","q":[["What do we pass along San Diego Bay and downtown?","This stretch follows the east side of the bay through National City, the Navy's piers, Barrio Logan, downtown, the airport and Old Town.","What all do we go through on this stretch?|What's along here, like what places?|What's on this part?","see"],["Why is San Diego often cool and gray in summer mornings?","Marine layer clouds, most common from May to August, often keep mornings near the coast cool and gray. Locals call it May gray and June gloom.","Why is it so gray here in the morning?|Why are there so many clouds near the coast?|Why the gray mornings?","nature"],["What is May gray and June gloom in San Diego?","It's the local name for marine layer clouds that often keep mornings near the coast cool and gray. They're most common from May to August.","What do locals mean by June gloom?|What's May gray, can we ask about it?|June gloom?","name"],["When does San Diego get most of its rain?","Most of the year's rain falls from December through March, and it totals less than 12 inches on average.","Does it rain much around here?|When does it rain here, kids want to know?|Rainy season?","nature"]]},"z:20":{"n":"Mission Bay to La Jolla","k":"z","q":[["What is special about Mission Bay's islands?","Mission Bay's islands are man-made. They are one of the things that line this stretch of the road.","What's special about those islands?|Why are the islands in Mission Bay weird?|Are the Mission Bay islands real?","nature"],["What is Rose Canyon along this stretch?","Rose Canyon is one of the landmarks along this stretch, and it comes with a fault. The fault is named right along with the canyon.","What's that canyon we're passing?|Is Rose Canyon a big deal?|Rose Canyon, what is it?","nature"],["Is there a fault near Rose Canyon?","Yes, the stretch is lined by Rose Canyon and its fault. That fault is one of the features of this part of the drive.","Is that fault dangerous?|Mom, is there an earthquake line here?|A fault on the road?","nature"],["What is Mount Soledad on this drive?","Mount Soledad's slopes line this stretch of road between Mission Bay and La Jolla. They are one of the landmarks you pass.","What's that hill beside the road?|What's the big hill we're passing?|Mount Soledad, what's that?","see"],["What can we see between Mission Bay and La Jolla?","You pass Mission Bay's man-made islands, Rose Canyon and its fault, and the slopes of Mount Soledad. UC San Diego and University City are along this stretch too.","What's along this part of the drive?|What are we passing right now?|What's to see here?","see"]]},"z:31":{"n":"Torrey Pines to Encinitas","k":"z","q":[["What are the lagoons we pass between Torrey Pines and Encinitas?","They're coastal lagoons where creeks and rivers meet the sea. The three named here are Los Peñasquitos, San Dieguito and San Elijo.","What are those lagoons out there?|Mom, what's that watery stuff by the ocean?|What lagoons are these?","nature"],["Which beach towns are on this stretch of I-5?","This stretch alternates between the lagoons and the beach towns of Del Mar, Solana Beach and Encinitas.","What towns are along here?|What towns are we passing, Dad?|Which beach towns are next?","people"],["Is there another Del Mar race season besides summer?","Yes, there's a second Del Mar race meet that runs in November.","Do they race again later in the year?|Do the horses come back in the fall, Dad?|Any fall racing?","visit"]]},"z:44":{"n":"Carlsbad and Oceanside","k":"z","q":[["What kind of town is Oceanside?","Oceanside is a beach town. It fills part of this stretch of road along with Carlsbad's fields and lagoons.","What is it like there?|Is Oceanside a beach, Mom?|What's Oceanside?","see"],["Whose land were Carlsbad and Oceanside originally?","This stretch of road runs through Luiseño and Kumeyaay country, so those are the peoples tied to this land.","Who lived here first?|Whose country is this, Dad?|Who was here before?","people"],["Is there anything to do with the strawberry farm in fall?","Yes, the strawberry farm's pumpkin season picks up in late September, so fall brings pumpkins along with the berry fields.","What happens there in autumn?|Are there pumpkins, Mom?|Anything fun in fall?","kids"]]},"z:54":{"n":"Camp Pendleton coast","k":"z","q":[["What is Camp Pendleton?","It's Marine Corps Base Camp Pendleton. The land along this stretch of the coast is kept for Marine training.","What is that big base the freeway runs through?|Is this where the Marines live, Buddy?|What's Camp Pendleton?","other"],["How long does I-5 follow the ocean through Camp Pendleton?","The freeway follows the ocean for about 18 miles through the base.","How far does it stay along the water here?|Are we gonna see the ocean for a long time?|How long is this coastal part?","size"],["What can we see along the Camp Pendleton coast?","You'll pass open hills, river valleys and the Surf Line railroad, all with the ocean beside you.","What is there to look at on this stretch?|What's out the window, Buddy? I'm bored!|What's the scenery?","see"]]},"z:70":{"n":"San Onofre and San Clemente","k":"z","q":[["What is Trestles near San Onofre?","Trestles is a set of surf breaks at the north edge of Camp Pendleton. The freeway runs above San Onofre's beaches and the Trestles surf breaks.","What is that surf spot by the freeway?|What are Trestles, Dad?|Trestles?","see"],["Where does Orange County start on this stretch?","The Orange County line is at Cristianitos Road, beside the hillside streets of Spanish-style San Clemente.","Where is it that we leave San Diego County?|Are we in Orange County yet?|Where's the county line?","other"],["What happens at San Mateo Creek in winter?","Winter storms can push San Mateo Creek through the sandbar at its mouth. That lets steelhead trout swim upstream to spawn.","What does it do when storms come?|Why do fish swim up the creek?|Creek and fish?","nature"],["Is there a stream at Trestles?","Yes, usually in winter and spring. After heavy rains, a small seasonal stream usually runs through Trestles.","Does it have water running through it?|Is there a little river at the surf spot?|Trestles stream?","nature"]]},"z:76":{"n":"Dana Point and Capistrano Valley","k":"z","q":[["Whose homeland is the Capistrano Valley?","The valley of San Juan and Trabuco creeks is Acjachemen homeland. The freeway runs inland through it between Dana Point and Mission Viejo.","Whose homeland is it?|Who lived here first, like before everybody?|Who were the first people here?","people"],["What town are we passing near in Capistrano Valley?","We pass the mission town of San Juan Capistrano. The freeway runs by it as it heads inland through the valley.","What's that town we're passing?|What town is this, is it a real mission?|Which town is that?","see"],["What is the ranch land out east of the freeway here?","To the east lie the ranch lands of Rancho Mission Viejo. They sit along the freeway as it runs through the valley.","What's that land to the east?|Is that a farm out there?|What's out there?","see"],["When do the Capistrano swallows leave?","Tradition says the swallows leave around October 23. That is the old feast day of Saint John of Capistrano.","When do they leave?|When do the birds fly away?|Do the swallows go?","nature"],["How much rain does the Capistrano Valley area get?","The area gets roughly 12 inches of rain a year. Almost all of it falls between November and March.","Does it rain much here?|Is it a rainy place?|How rainy is it?","nature"]]},"z:86":{"n":"Saddleback Valley","k":"z","q":[["What towns are in Saddleback Valley?","Suburbs of Mission Viejo, Laguna Hills and Lake Forest fill the Saddleback Valley, below the mountain's two peaks.","What towns are in it?|What cities are down in this valley, Dad?|Which towns here?","people"],["What is the El Toro Y near Saddleback Valley?","It's where Interstate 405 meets this freeway, in the Saddleback Valley area.","What is that Y thing?|What's the El Toro Y, Mom?|El Toro Y?","other"],["Does it ever snow on Saddleback?","Yes, a little. During most winters, snow dusts Santiago Peak, the higher summit of Saddleback, at least once.","Does it snow on that mountain?|Is there ever snow up on those peaks?|Snow on Saddleback?","nature"],["Which peak is the highest on Saddleback?","Santiago Peak is the higher summit of Saddleback. The mountain has two peaks in all.","Which one is taller?|What's the tallest part of that mountain?|Highest peak?","size"],["What are the Santa Ana winds around Saddleback Valley?","Autumn is the season known for hot, dry Santa Ana winds. They create critical fire weather in the hills.","What are those winds?|Why are the hills so dangerous in fall?|Santa Ana winds?","nature"]]},"z:95":{"n":"Irvine Ranch and Tustin","k":"z","q":[["What is the Irvine Ranch?","It's the old ranch land the freeway crosses here. The land is flat, and the stretch is named for it, the old Irvine Ranch.","What was that ranch we're crossing?|Is this land a ranch, Dad?|Irvine Ranch?","history"],["What is special about the land around Irvine Ranch?","The land here is flat. The freeway crosses the flat land of the old Irvine Ranch.","What's the land like around it?|Why is everything so flat here?|Flat land?","nature"],["What will we see driving past Tustin?","You'll cross the flat land of the old Irvine Ranch and pass Tustin's old Marine base. It's a stretch to watch the landscape and spot that old base.","What is there to see along here?|What's out the window, Mom?|Anything cool to see?","see"],["Why is the morning so gray in Tustin and Irvine Ranch?","In late spring and early summer it's June Gloom. Mornings are overcast, with occasional drizzle.","Why is it so cloudy in the morning?|Why is the sky gray, Dad?|Gray skies?","nature"]]},"z:103":{"n":"Santa Ana and Anaheim","k":"z","q":[["What famous places does I-5 pass in Santa Ana and Anaheim?","The freeway passes Christ Cathedral and Disneyland. It also crosses the Santa Ana River and the Orange Crush interchange.","What famous stuff is near it?|Are we going to see anything cool, like Disneyland?|What can we see here?","famous"],["What is the Santa Ana River like?","It carries only a small flow most of the year. In the brief winter rainy season it can get flash floods.","Does it always have a lot of water in it?|Is that a real river, it looks dry?|What's the river like?","nature"],["What are the Santa Ana winds?","They bring hot, dry weather in the fall. Their wind damage is most common along the Santa Ana River basin.","What are those winds about?|Why is it so hot and windy in the fall?|What's with the wind?","nature"]]},"z:112":{"n":"Buena Park to Norwalk","k":"z","q":[["What counties does I-5 cross between Buena Park and Norwalk?","Between Buena Park and Downey, the freeway crosses the line between Orange and Los Angeles counties.","Does it go from one county to another here?|Are we leaving Orange County yet?|Which counties are these?","other"],["What are the Gateway Cities along this part of I-5?","The freeway runs through the Gateway Cities of La Mirada, Santa Fe Springs and Norwalk.","Which cities is it running through here?|What towns are these, kid-style?|Gateway Cities?","people"],["What did people grow or raise on this land in the past?","The land later held olive groves and dairies, and it also had oil fields.","Were there farms along here?|Did they have cows or something here?|What grew here?","nature"]]},"z:124":{"n":"Downey, Commerce and East LA","k":"z","q":[["What rivers does I-5 cross around Downey and Commerce?","Through this stretch the freeway crosses the San Gabriel River and the Rio Hondo, on its way through Downey, Commerce and East Los Angeles.","What rivers does it cross here?|Are we going over any rivers, like real ones?|What rivers are here?","nature"],["What is Downey known for along I-5?","Downey is described as an aerospace town. The freeway runs right through it, so this is aerospace country.","What's it known for?|Why do they call Downey the aerospace place, what's that mean?|Downey, what's there?","famous"],["What is Commerce like along the freeway?","Commerce is a place of rail-side factories. The freeway passes right through that industrial area on the way to East Los Angeles.","What's it like there?|Are there lots of factories, like in a big factory city?|Commerce, what's there?","see"],["What places does I-5 run through between Downey and East LA?","The freeway runs through aerospace Downey, then the rail-side factories of Commerce, and then East Los Angeles.","What places does it go through?|What towns are we driving through next?|What's on this stretch?","other"]]},"z:134":{"n":"Boyle Heights to Elysian Park","k":"z","q":[["What is Lincoln Heights along this stretch of I-5?","Lincoln Heights is described as the city's first suburb. The freeway runs right through it between Boyle Heights and Elysian Park.","What's that neighborhood the freeway runs through?|What's Lincoln Heights, is it a big deal?|Lincoln Heights, what's that?","history"],["What river does I-5 cross near Elysian Park?","I-5 crosses the Los Angeles River here. It's the spot where the Arroyo Seco joins the river, right beside the wooded hills of Elysian Park.","What river is it that we cross there?|Are we going over a river, what's it called?|Which river is that?","nature"],["What is Elysian Park like?","Elysian Park has wooded hills. You'll see them beside the freeway where the road crosses the Los Angeles River near the Arroyo Seco.","What's it like around that park?|Is there anything green up ahead, like a park?|What's in Elysian Park?","nature"],["Where does the Arroyo Seco meet the Los Angeles River?","It joins the Los Angeles River right where I-5 crosses it, next to the wooded hills of Elysian Park.","Where does that creek join the river?|What's the Arroyo Seco, kid question: is it a river?|Arroyo Seco, where's that?","nature"]]},"z:140.2":{"n":"LA River and Griffith Park","k":"z","q":[["What river is the I-5 following here?","It's the Los Angeles River. The freeway follows it through a section called the Glendale Narrows.","What river is that next to us?|Is that a real river, or just a ditch?|What's that river?","nature"],["What are the Glendale Narrows?","The Glendale Narrows is the stretch of the Los Angeles River that the freeway follows here.","What is that part of the river called?|What are the Narrows, and why do they have a name?|Glendale Narrows?","name"],["What are the hills by Griffith Park like?","The hills of Griffith Park are brushy. They sit on the west side of the freeway along this stretch.","What are those hills over there?|Why are those hills all bushy and brown?|What's on the hills?","nature"]]},"z:145":{"n":"Burbank and Sun Valley","k":"z","q":[["What is there to see around Burbank and Sun Valley?","This flat stretch has studios, the airport and the neighborhoods of Glendale, Burbank and Sun Valley, all spread across the east end of the San Fernando Valley.","What's out there along this part of the road?|What are all those buildings out the window?|What's around here?","see"],["What are the mountains next to Burbank and Sun Valley?","Those are the Verdugo Mountains, described as rocky, rising above the flat east end of the San Fernando Valley.","What are those mountains over there?|Are those big rocky hills called something?|Which mountains are those?","nature"],["What is Tujunga Wash near Burbank and Sun Valley?","Tujunga Wash is a mostly dry riverbed in this part of the valley. It usually carries much water only during storms between November and April.","What's that dry riverbed?|Is there a river near here?|What's a wash?","nature"],["Is there water in Tujunga Wash?","Usually not much. It's mostly dry, and it usually carries much water only during storms between November and April.","Does it ever have water in it?|Why is that river dry?|When does the wash fill up?","nature"]]},"z:153":{"n":"North San Fernando Valley","k":"z","q":[["What towns are in the north San Fernando Valley?","Pacoima, Mission Hills and Sylmar fill the north end of the valley.","What towns are in it?|What places are we driving through here?|Which towns are these?","people"],["What was the north San Fernando Valley like long ago?","It was once a land of mission vineyards, wheat and olive groves.","What was it like before the houses?|What was here a long time ago?|What used to be here?","history"],["What are the mountains next to the north San Fernando Valley?","They're the San Gabriel Mountains, rising close by the north end of the valley.","What are those mountains?|What are those big mountains called?|Which mountains are those?","nature"],["Does it snow in the north San Fernando Valley?","Snow almost never falls on the valley floor, but the mountains above Sylmar in the Angeles National Forest are capped with snow every winter.","Does it ever snow there?|Is there snow on the mountains?|Does it snow here, ever?","nature"]]},"z:162":{"n":"Newhall Pass and Newhall","k":"z","q":[["What is Newhall Pass?","Newhall Pass sits between the San Gabriel and Santa Susana Mountains. It links the San Fernando Valley with the Santa Clarita Valley.","What is that pass we're going through?|What's this pass thing, Dad?|Newhall Pass?","other"],["What kind of town is Newhall?","Newhall grew up as a railroad and oil town in the Santa Clarita Valley.","What is it known for?|What did people do in Newhall, like way back?|What's Newhall about?","history"],["Which valleys does Newhall Pass connect?","It connects the San Fernando Valley with the Santa Clarita Valley, running between the San Gabriel and Santa Susana Mountains.","What does it connect?|What's on the other side of the mountains?|Where does the pass go?","nature"],["How hot does Newhall Pass get in summer?","Summer heat in the pass can top 100 degrees.","Does it get hot in the pass?|Is it super hot here in summer?|Is it hot there?","nature"],["Does it ever snow at Newhall Pass?","Snow is possible from December to February, but it is quite uncommon.","Can it snow there?|Can we see snow here, Mom?|Snow in the pass?","nature"]]},"z:168":{"n":"Santa Clarita Valley","k":"z","q":[["What are the neighborhoods in the Santa Clarita Valley like?","The Valencia neighborhoods are planned communities. They spread out along the Santa Clara River in the Santa Clarita Valley.","What are those neighborhoods like?|Why are all the houses so neat here?|What's the town by the river?","people"],["What river runs through the Santa Clarita Valley?","It's the Santa Clara River. The planned neighborhoods of Valencia spread out along it.","What's that river called?|Is there a river here, what's its name?|Which river is that?","nature"],["What can we see in the Santa Clarita Valley?","Look for the Valencia neighborhoods along the Santa Clara River and Magic Mountain's roller coasters on the hills west of the freeway.","What is there to see around here?|What's cool here for us kids?|What should I look for?","see"],["What plants bloom in the Santa Clarita Valley in spring?","In April and May, chaparral yucca send up flower stalks 3 to 10 feet tall.","What are those tall flower stalks?|What are the big flowers growing here?|What blooms here in April?","nature"],["How tall are the yucca flower stalks in the Santa Clarita Valley?","Chaparral yucca flower stalks grow 3 to 10 feet tall. They rise in April and May.","How big do those flower stalks get?|How tall do the stalks grow?|Are the yucca stalks tall?","size"]]},"z:176.9":{"n":"Castaic and the Five Mile Grade","k":"z","q":[["What mountains does I-5 cross after Castaic?","The freeway crosses the Sierra Pelona Mountains, inside the Angeles National Forest, north of Castaic.","What mountains are those up ahead?|What are we driving over, kids ask?|Which mountains?","nature"],["What forest is north of Castaic?","North of Castaic the Five Mile Grade leads you into the Angeles National Forest.","What forest is it that we're entering?|Are we in a forest now?|What forest?","nature"],["What lakes are near the Five Mile Grade?","The freeway crosses the mountains between Castaic Lake and Pyramid Lake.","Are there lakes along this stretch?|Is there water near here, can we see a lake?|Any lakes?","see"],["Does I-5 ever close between Castaic and Grapevine?","Yes. In winter, ice and snow sometimes lead the Highway Patrol to close the 40 miles of I-5 between Castaic and Grapevine.","Can it close up here in winter?|Why would the road shut down, is it snow?|Does it ever close?","other"]]},"z:191":{"n":"Pyramid Lake to Gorman","k":"z","q":[["What is Pyramid Lake and where does it sit?","Pyramid Lake fills the canyon of Piru Creek in the Sierra Pelona Mountains, just west of the freeway.","What's that lake we're passing?|Is that a real lake in the mountains?|What lake is this?","nature"],["What mountains are around Pyramid Lake?","Pyramid Lake sits in the Sierra Pelona Mountains, filling the canyon of Piru Creek.","What mountains is it in?|Are we in the mountains right now?|What are these mountains called?","nature"],["What can we do near Gorman in spring?","From mid-April through May, Hungry Valley near Gorman offers guided wildflower tours.","Is there anything to do there in spring?|Are there wildflowers near it?|Can we see flowers there?","visit"],["Where is Hungry Valley?","Hungry Valley is near Gorman, and it offers guided wildflower tours from mid-April through May.","Where is that wildflower place?|What's Hungry Valley near?|Where do the flower tours happen?","see"]]},"z:203":{"n":"Tejon Pass and Lebec","k":"z","q":[["What is Tejon Pass like to drive through?","Tejon Pass sits among the open hills of Tejon Ranch around Lebec, so expect wide, open hill country around you.","What's it like going over that pass?|Are we in the mountains now?|What's around us here?","nature"],["Does Tejon Pass really get snow?","Yes. When snow and ice close the freeway over the pass, the shutdown usually lasts four to six hours, though some closures run longer than a day.","Does it snow up there?|Can snow stop us on the freeway?|Snow on the road?","other"],["How long do Tejon Pass snow closures last?","Usually four to six hours, but some closures run longer than a day.","How long does it stay closed when it snows?|If it's closed, are we stuck for long?|How long is the shutdown?","other"],["What can you see at Tejon Ranch in spring?","Spring brings showy wildflower blooms to Tejon Ranch, where several of California's natural regions meet.","Are there flowers there in spring?|Why is it so colorful in spring?|Any wildflowers?","nature"]]},"z:210":{"n":"Grapevine Canyon","k":"z","q":[["What is Grapevine Canyon known for?","Grapevine Canyon carries the freeway's long, steep grade. It leads past Fort Tejon's centuries-old valley oaks and down to the southern edge of the San Joaquin Valley.","What's it known for?|Why is this canyon a big deal, mom?|What's special here?","famous"],["What trees can we see in Grapevine Canyon?","The canyon passes Fort Tejon's valley oaks, and they're centuries old.","What are those trees by it?|Are there old trees, dad?|What trees?","nature"],["Where does Grapevine Canyon lead to?","It leads to the southern edge of the San Joaquin Valley, after passing Fort Tejon's old valley oaks.","Where does it come out?|What's at the end of the canyon, mom?|Where does it go?","other"],["Who lived in the mountains around Grapevine Canyon?","The Kitanemuk people, whose homeland includes these mountains. In autumn it was acorn harvest time for them.","Who lived around it first?|Did people live here long ago, dad?|Who's from here?","people"]]},"z:215":{"n":"Wheeler Ridge and the valley's edge","k":"z","q":[["What is Wheeler Ridge?","It's the spot below the Grapevine where the land flattens out. The Tehachapi and San Emigdio mountains meet the floor of the San Joaquin Valley there.","What is that place we're coming to?|What's Wheeler Ridge, a mountain?|Wheeler Ridge, what's that?","other"],["Which mountains meet at Wheeler Ridge?","The Tehachapi and San Emigdio mountains meet the San Joaquin Valley floor at Wheeler Ridge.","Which mountains are those around it?|What mountains are we looking at, kids ask?|Which mountains are there?","nature"],["What is there to do near Wheeler Ridge in spring?","In spring, Wind Wolves Preserve in the hills to the southwest holds a nature festival with guided hikes and wildflower viewing.","Is there anything to see near it in spring?|Anything fun for kids there in spring?|Any wildflowers around?","visit"]]},"z:221":{"n":"The vanished southern lakes","k":"z","q":[["What are the vanished southern lakes?","They are Kern Lake and Buena Vista Lake. Their old beds are now covered by flat farmland.","What were those lakes that disappeared?|Where did the lakes go?|Vanished lakes?","history"],["What is on the old Kern Lake and Buena Vista Lake beds now?","Flat farmland covers the old beds of both Kern Lake and Buena Vista Lake.","What is out there where the lakes used to be?|Why is it so flat here, were there lakes?|What covers the old lakes?","today"],["What mountains are on the south side of the vanished southern lakes?","The San Emigdio Mountains form the valley's southern wall along this stretch.","What are those mountains on the horizon?|What mountains is that, kids ask?|Which mountains are those?","see"],["What is out to the west of the vanished southern lakes?","Off to the west is the oil country of Maricopa and Taft.","What is over that way past the lakes?|What is out west of here, a kid might ask?|What's to the west?","see"],["What is the landscape like around the vanished southern lakes?","It is flat farmland on the old lake beds, with mountains forming a wall to the south and oil country off to the west.","What is it like around here?|Why is everything so flat out here?|What's the terrain?","nature"]]},"z:234":{"n":"Kern County orchards","k":"z","q":[["What crops grow in the Kern County orchards?","This part of Kern County's valley floor is farm country. Almonds and pistachios are among the most valuable crops grown here.","What are they growing in those orchards?|What kind of trees are out there, Dad?|What's growing here?","nature"],["Are almonds and pistachios important in Kern County?","Yes. Almonds and pistachios are among the most valuable crops on Kern County's valley floor, which is farm country here.","Are those nuts a big deal around here?|Are those nut trees worth a lot?|Big crops here?","famous"],["What's out west of the Kern County orchards?","Highways 119 and 43 lead west to the oil towns of Taft and Maricopa.","What towns are off that way from here?|Where do those side roads go?|Anything west of here?","see"],["Which highways go to Taft and Maricopa from here?","Highways 119 and 43 lead west to the oil towns of Taft and Maricopa.","How do you get to those oil towns from it?|Which roads go to the oil towns?|Roads to Taft?","other"]]},"z:246":{"n":"Kern River and the Elk Hills","k":"z","q":[["What river does I-5 cross on this stretch?","The freeway crosses the Kern River here, near the Kern Water Bank's recharge basins.","What river is that we're crossing?|Is there a river here, Buddy?|Which river is it?","nature"],["What are the Elk Hills?","The Elk Hills are the low, bare hills the freeway runs past on this stretch.","What are those hills we're driving past?|Why are those hills so bare?|What hills are those?","see"],["What is the Kern Water Bank?","The source only says the freeway crosses the Kern River near the Kern Water Bank's recharge basins. It doesn't say more about what the bank does.","What's that water bank thing near the river?|What are those basins by the river?|Water bank?","other"],["When is the best time to see the tule elk?","Late summer is when the tule elk at the state reserve become most active, and they stay most active into early autumn.","When are the elk most active?|When should we look for elk, Buddy?|Elk season?","visit"]]},"z:262":{"n":"Buttonwillow cotton country","k":"z","q":[["What is Buttonwillow known for around here?","Around Buttonwillow, the area is known locally as cotton country, with farm fields stretching across the valley floor.","What is it known for?|Why do they call this cotton country?|What's Buttonwillow famous for?","famous"],["What can we see out the window in Buttonwillow cotton country?","You'll see farm fields and big power lines spread across the valley floor, with the Temblor Range on the far western skyline.","What are we looking at out there?|What's all that stuff in the fields?|What's there to see?","see"],["What are those mountains on the horizon near Buttonwillow?","Those are the Temblor Range, sitting on the far western skyline past the farm fields of the valley floor.","What are those mountains out there?|What are those far-away mountains called?|What mountains are those?","nature"]]},"z:278":{"n":"Lost Hills oil and pistachios","k":"z","q":[["What are those oil pumps near Lost Hills?","Oil pumps dot the low Lost Hills on the west side of the freeway. That is the oil part of this stretch of road, Lost Hills oil and pistachios.","What are those things pumping oil out there?|Why are there so many oil pumps, Dad?|Oil pumps?","see"],["What is that water channel running along Lost Hills?","That is the aqueduct. It runs past the town of Lost Hills.","What is that thing running past it?|Is that a river next to the road, Mom?|What's that canal?","see"],["Is there a wildlife refuge near Lost Hills?","Yes, Kern National Wildlife Refuge lies out on the flats to the east of the freeway.","Is there a nature place out there?|Are there animals out on those flats?|Any wildlife around?","nature"],["What is the land like around Lost Hills?","There are low hills to the west with oil pumps on them, and open flats to the east where Kern National Wildlife Refuge sits.","What's the land like around it?|Why is it so flat out here?|Hills or flat?","nature"]]},"z:292":{"n":"Into Kings County","k":"z","q":[["What is the old lakebed east of the freeway?","It's the old bed of Tulare Lake. It's now farmland, and it spreads out east of the freeway north of the Kern County line.","What is that out there?|Kids: Was there really a lake here?|What's that flat land?","nature"],["Is Tulare Lake still here?","Mostly it's farmland now. But in very wet winters, storms can bring Tulare Lake back, flooding the old lakebed east of the freeway.","Is it still a lake?|Kids: Where did the lake go?|Any water out there?","nature"],["Has Tulare Lake ever come back?","Yes. In very wet winters, like 2023, storms can bring Tulare Lake back and flood the old lakebed east of the freeway.","Did it ever flood back?|Kids: Did the lake come back?|Did the lake return?","history"],["What's the land around here used for today?","The old lakebed is farmland now. It spreads out east of the freeway, north of the Kern County line.","What do they do with that land?|Kids: Why is it so flat?|What's out there now?","today"]]},"z:309":{"n":"Kettleman Hills","k":"z","q":[["What are the Kettleman Hills?","They are hills that rise beside the freeway, and they hold an oil field.","What is that over there?|What are those hills, mom?|Kettleman Hills?","other"],["Is there an oil field in the Kettleman Hills?","Yes, the Kettleman Hills have an oil field, and the hills rise beside the freeway.","Is there oil in it?|Is there really oil in those hills?|Oil field here?","see"]]},"z:319":{"n":"Fresno County's West Side","k":"z","q":[["Is Fresno County a big farming county?","Yes. In 2022, Fresno County led California counties in the value of its almonds, pistachios, garlic and processing tomatoes.","Is it a big deal for farming out here?|Are these farms important, dad?|Farming country?","famous"]]},"z:330":{"n":"Coalinga plains","k":"z","q":[["What is the land like around the Coalinga plains?","It's wide-open farmland stretching east of the freeway, with oil field ridges around Coalinga and the Diablo Range off to the west.","What is all that out there?|What are we looking at, Buddy?|What's the scenery here?","see"],["What is that big feedlot beside the freeway near Coalinga?","That's the big Harris Ranch feedlot, and it sits right beside the freeway on this stretch of the Coalinga plains.","What is that place next to the road?|What's that big farm thing, Buddy?|Big feedlot?","famous"],["How hot does it get around Coalinga in summer?","Summers around Coalinga are very hot and dry.","Is it hot out there?|Is it super hot there, Buddy?|Summer weather?","nature"],["How much rain does Coalinga get?","Coalinga gets about 8 inches of rain a year, and most of it falls from October to May.","Does it ever rain there?|Is it a desert, Buddy?|Is it dry there?","nature"]]},"z:341":{"n":"Westlands farm country","k":"z","q":[["What do they grow in the Westlands farm country?","The big farms here grow crops such as almonds, tomatoes, pistachios and wheat across the valley floor.","What are they growing in those fields?|What's all that stuff growing out there?|What crops are here?","nature"],["What are the Joaquin Rocks near Westlands farm country?","They're three rocks, called the Joaquin Rocks, sitting on the ridge to the west of the farm country.","What are those rocks on the ridge?|Why are there three rocks on the hill?|Joaquin Rocks?","see"],["What can we see driving through Westlands farm country?","You'll see big farms across the valley floor, and the three Joaquin Rocks standing on the ridge to the west.","What is there to look at out here?|Is there anything to see besides farms?|What's out the window?","see"]]},"z:357":{"n":"Panoche Hills","k":"z","q":[["What are the Panoche Hills?","They're low hills that rise close to the road on the west side. Beyond them stands the higher Diablo Range.","What are those hills we're driving past?|What's that range right next to the road, Mom?|Panoche Hills?","nature"],["What mountains are behind the Panoche Hills?","The higher Diablo Range sits beyond the low Panoche Hills, further to the west.","What's behind those hills?|Are there bigger mountains past the little hills?|What's farther back?","see"],["What kind of animals live around the Panoche Hills?","Golden eagles, hawks and mountain plovers rely on the grasslands of Panoche Valley, just beyond the hills. It's especially important to them in winter.","What animals live out there?|Are there any big birds out there?|Any wildlife?","nature"],["What is Panoche Valley known for?","Its grasslands are critical habitat for golden eagles, hawks and mountain plovers, especially in winter.","What's special about the valley past the hills?|Why do the birds like it out there?|What's in that valley?","famous"]]},"z:372":{"n":"Firebaugh and the canals","k":"z","q":[["When is the cantaloupe harvest around Firebaugh?","The melon harvest peaks in late July, and the town celebrates it every summer with its Cantaloupe Round-Up.","When is it harvest time there?|When do they pick the melons?|Melon season when?","nature"],["What is the landscape like along the Firebaugh canals?","The aqueduct runs through flat farmland. The Panoche Hills are off to the west.","What does it look like out there?|What's all that flat land?|What are we driving through?","see"],["What are the hills to the west near Firebaugh?","Those are the Panoche Hills, off to the west of the aqueduct and the flat farmland.","What are those hills over there?|What are the mountains called?|Name of those hills?","nature"]]},"z:391":{"n":"Los Banos and the Grasslands","k":"z","q":[["What birds can we see at Merced National Wildlife Refuge?","Each autumn, more than 20,000 cranes and 60,000 geese arrive at Merced National Wildlife Refuge.","What animals does it have in the fall?|Are there real cranes and geese there?|Which birds come there?","nature"],["How long do the cranes and geese stay at the refuge?","They stay about six months. They arrive each autumn at Merced National Wildlife Refuge, more than 20,000 cranes and 60,000 geese.","Do they stay there all year?|Do the birds leave, or do they live there?|How long are the birds there?","nature"],["What happens at Merced National Wildlife Refuge in spring?","In spring, wildflowers bloom in rings around the drying vernal pools of Merced National Wildlife Refuge.","What blooms there when it warms up?|Are there flowers there, Dad?|Anything to see in spring?","see"]]},"z:403":{"n":"San Luis water crossroads","k":"z","q":[["What is the San Luis water crossroads?","It's the spot where the state and federal water projects meet. They share San Luis Reservoir, its dam and its forebay.","What is it?|Why do they call it a crossroads, Dad?|What's here?","other"],["What do the state and federal water projects share at San Luis?","They share San Luis Reservoir, its dam and its forebay. It's where the two water systems come together.","What do they share there?|Who owns the lake, kids wonder?|Shared what?","people"],["What happens at San Luis Reservoir in winter?","In the wet season, from about November to April, extra water is pumped from O'Neill Forebay up into San Luis Reservoir.","What does it do in winter?|Why do they move water in the wet season?|Winter water?","nature"],["Where does the water pumped into San Luis Reservoir come from?","It's pumped up from O'Neill Forebay. That happens in the wet season, from about November to April, when there's water to spare.","Where does it get its water?|Where does the water come from, mom?|Water source?","nature"]]},"z:410":{"n":"Gustine and Newman","k":"z","q":[["What kind of land is around Gustine and Newman?","It's farm country. Farms spread east across Merced and Stanislaus counties toward the two towns and the San Joaquin River.","What's out there around it?|What are all those fields out there?|What's that out the window?","nature"],["What river is near Gustine and Newman?","The San Joaquin River. The farms, and the old railroad towns of Gustine and Newman, spread east toward it.","What river is near it?|Is there a river out there?|Which river?","nature"],["Does Newman have any festivals?","Yes. Newman holds its yearly Fall Festival on Labor Day weekend.","Is there anything fun going on there?|Do they have a party or fair there?|Any events in Newman?","visit"]]},"z:428":{"n":"Patterson and Westley","k":"z","q":[["What are the mountains west of Patterson and Westley?","Those are the Diablo Range, which rises close to the freeway on the west side. It's cut by Del Puerto Canyon.","What mountains are those?|Mom, what are the big hills on that side called?|What range is that?","nature"],["What is Patterson known for?","Patterson is known for its distribution centers. The town sits east of the freeway.","What's it known for?|What do they make in Patterson?|Why is Patterson famous?","famous"],["What is the canyon cutting through the mountains here?","It's Del Puerto Canyon, which cuts through the Diablo Range on the west side of the freeway.","What is it called?|What's that gap in the hills?|Is there a canyon?","nature"],["Can you see Mount Diablo from Patterson and Westley?","Yes, on clear days Mount Diablo shows on the northwest skyline.","Can we see it from here?|Is there a big mountain far away?|See any peaks?","see"],["What happens in Patterson in the summer?","Downtown Patterson hosts its Apricot Fiesta on the first weekend of June.","Is there anything to do there then?|What's the Apricot thing?|Is there a festival?","visit"]]},"z:446":{"n":"Tracy and the river country","k":"z","q":[["What kind of land are we driving through near Tracy?","It's flat San Joaquin County farmland near the edge of the Delta. The freeway runs between the San Joaquin River and the old railroad towns of Banta and Tracy.","What is that land around us?|What are all those fields out there?|What's this land?","nature"],["What towns are around the river country here?","Banta and Tracy are the two old railroad towns here. The freeway passes between them and the San Joaquin River.","What are those towns near the road?|Are there any towns out here, Mom?|Which towns are close?","people"],["What birds can we see near the San Joaquin River refuge?","From autumn through spring, flocks of geese and sandhill cranes gather at the San Joaquin River refuge. It's east of the freeway.","Are there birds out there?|Can we see any big birds, Dad?|What birds are those?","nature"]]},"z:460":{"n":"San Joaquin River and Lathrop","k":"z","q":[["Where does I-5 cross the San Joaquin River?","The freeway crosses the San Joaquin River at Mossdale.","Where is it that the freeway crosses the river?|Where do we go over the water?|Where's the river crossing?","other"],["What kind of town is Lathrop?","Lathrop is a railroad town. The freeway runs right through it.","What's that town all about?|What kind of place is this, Dad?|What is Lathrop?","history"]]},"z:467":{"n":"Stockton","k":"z","q":[["What body of water does I-5 cross in Stockton?","In Stockton, I-5 crosses the Calaveras River.","Does it go over a river here?|What river are we crossing, are we on a bridge?|Which river is that?","nature"],["Is Stockton really a port city?","Yes. I-5 runs past Stockton's deepwater port, which has a channel connecting it to San Francisco Bay.","Does it have a port, that deepwater thing?|Are there ships here, like real boats?|Port city?","famous"],["How is Stockton connected to San Francisco Bay?","Stockton's deepwater port has a channel that leads to San Francisco Bay, and I-5 runs right past it.","How does it reach the bay from here?|Can boats from here get to the ocean, like, the bay?|Stockton to the bay?","see"],["When does it rain in Stockton?","Nearly all of Stockton's rain falls from October through April, so the winter months are the wet ones.","Does it rain a lot here?|Is it rainy where we are, like in winter?|Rainy season?","nature"],["What are summers like in Stockton?","Summers in Stockton are very hot and dry.","Is it hot there in summer?|Is it gonna be super hot, like melting hot?|Summer weather?","nature"]]},"z:481":{"n":"Lodi vineyards and the Delta's edge","k":"z","q":[["What are the Delta islands like near Lodi?","They're flat, reclaimed islands, held in by levees, and they stretch off to the west of the vineyards around Lodi.","What are those islands like?|Why is the land out there so flat?|Delta islands?","nature"],["What can we see along the Lodi vineyards stretch?","Vineyards spread east toward Lodi, while the flat, reclaimed islands of the Delta stretch off to the west, held in by levees.","What is there to see there?|What are we looking at out the window?|What's out there?","see"],["What wildlife can you see near Lodi in winter?","In winter, cranes, geese and swans use the Woodbridge reserve as their winter home.","Are there animals there in winter?|Are there any birds out there?|Birds in winter?","nature"],["Where do the cranes and swans spend winter near Lodi?","They use the Woodbridge reserve as their winter home. Cranes, geese and swans all gather there in the cold months.","Where do they stay in winter?|Where do the big birds go?|Winter birds spot?","nature"]]},"z:498":{"n":"Cosumnes wetlands and Elk Grove","k":"z","q":[["What are the Cosumnes wetlands near the freeway?","They're the wetlands of the Cosumnes River Preserve, and they lie near the freeway along this stretch.","What is that wetland out there?|What's all that marsh stuff by the road?|Wetlands?","nature"],["What is the Cosumnes River Preserve?","It's a preserve whose wetlands lie near the freeway on this stretch. The source doesn't say more than that about it.","What is that preserve we're passing?|Is the preserve a park, kind of?|Cosumnes Preserve?","other"],["What is the Stone Lakes refuge near Elk Grove?","It's a refuge on this stretch of I-5, and the freeway runs along its eastern edge as it passes through Elk Grove.","What is that refuge next to the road?|What's the lake place by the highway?|Stone Lakes?","nature"],["Where does the freeway run in relation to Stone Lakes refuge?","I-5 runs along the eastern edge of the Stone Lakes refuge as it passes through Elk Grove.","Is the highway right next to that refuge?|Are we driving beside the lakes?|Does the road touch Stone Lakes?","other"]]},"z:510":{"n":"Along the river to Land Park","k":"z","q":[["What river is the freeway running next to here?","That's the Sacramento River. The freeway runs close to it through the south side of Sacramento.","What river is that by the road?|Is that a real river next to us, or what?|Which river?","nature"],["What part of Sacramento are we driving through along the river?","We're on the south side of Sacramento. The freeway runs close to the Sacramento River through that part of the city.","What part of town is that?|Are we in the middle of the city or what?|Which side of town?","other"],["Is there a zoo near the freeway here in Sacramento?","Yes, the zoo is close by. The source says the freeway runs near William Land Park and the zoo.","Is that near the zoo?|Are there animals close by, like a zoo?|Any zoo around here?","kids"]]},"z:518":{"n":"Downtown Sacramento","k":"z","q":[["What is there to see in Downtown Sacramento along I-5?","As you pass through, you'll go between downtown and the river, with Old Sacramento and the Tower Bridge along the way.","What can we see there from the highway?|What's cool around here, mom?|What's near the road here?","see"],["Which landmarks does I-5 pass in Downtown Sacramento?","I-5 goes past Old Sacramento and the Tower Bridge as it runs between downtown and the river.","What does it go past?|What are we driving by, dad?|Any landmarks here?","famous"],["Where does I-5 run through Downtown Sacramento?","I-5 runs between downtown and the river here, so the river is on one side and downtown is on the other.","Where does it go through town?|Are we near the river?|What's it run between?","other"]]},"z:524":{"n":"The airport and the Sacramento River","k":"z","q":[["What do we pass north of downtown Sacramento?","North of downtown the freeway passes the old ARCO Arena site and the airport. It then crosses the Sacramento River.","What is that we're passing up here?|What's coming up after downtown, Dad?|What's along here?","see"]]},"z:531":{"n":"Yolo Bypass","k":"z","q":[["What is the Yolo Bypass?","It's a huge area that floods in wet winters and springs. When it floods, it becomes a big wetland for birds and young salmon.","What is that stretch of land we're driving through?|What's this big flat place, Dad?|What's the bypass?","other"],["What do they grow in the Yolo Bypass?","Much of the Yolo Bypass is farmed between floods, mostly in rice.","What are they growing in those fields?|What's growing in the fields, Mom?|What are the crops?","nature"],["What animals live in the Yolo Bypass?","When it floods in wet winters and springs, it becomes a huge wetland for birds and young salmon.","What kind of animals are there?|Are there any animals in there, Mom?|Any wildlife?","nature"],["Is the Yolo Bypass still used for farming today?","Yes. Between floods, much of the Yolo Bypass is farmed, mostly in rice.","Do people still farm it?|Do farmers use this land, Dad?|Is it a farm?","today"]]},"z:537":{"n":"Woodland and Cache Creek","k":"z","q":[["What is Woodland known for?","Woodland is known as a farm town with a big tomato cannery.","What is it known for?|Why do they have a cannery, Mom?|Woodland famous for what?","famous"],["What do they make at the Woodland cannery?","It is a tomato cannery, a big one, in the farm town of Woodland.","What does that cannery do?|Do they make tomatoes, Dad?|What is canned there?","see"],["What creek does I-5 cross near Yolo?","The freeway crosses Cache Creek. It crosses beside the little town of Yolo.","What creek is it that we cross?|What river thing are we driving over?|Which creek is this?","name"],["How big is the town of Yolo?","Yolo is a little town. The freeway crosses Cache Creek right beside it.","Is it a big town?|Is Yolo small, Mom?|Yolo, big or small?","size"]]},"z:548":{"n":"Zamora and the Dunnigan Hills","k":"z","q":[["What kind of land is around Zamora and the Dunnigan Hills?","It's farm country. The farmland keeps going past Zamora and Dunnigan, near the Dunnigan Hills.","What is out here around it?|What's all this land, is it just farms?|What's around here?","nature"],["When can we see the Sutter Buttes near Zamora?","You can see them on clear days. They rise from the valley floor to the north.","When can you see them from here?|Why can't I see the Buttes?|Where are the Buttes?","see"],["Which way should we look to spot the Sutter Buttes?","Look north. On clear days the Sutter Buttes rise from the valley floor in that direction.","Which direction are they from here?|Where do I look to see them?|Where are the Buttes?","see"]]},"z:560":{"n":"Almonds, rice and the Sutter Buttes","k":"z","q":[["What are all those trees around Arbuckle?","Those are almond orchards. They ring Arbuckle, so you see them all around the town.","What are those trees around there?|Mom, what's growing on all those trees?|Which trees are those?","nature"],["Why are the rice fields flooded in fall?","Many rice fields are flooded in fall to give ducks and geese food and shelter through the winter.","Why is it flooded?|Why are the fields full of water?|Why flood the fields?","nature"],["When do the ducks and geese show up here?","Ducks and geese arrive in fall. Many flooded rice fields give them food and shelter through the winter.","Do the birds come here too?|Can we see ducks and geese?|When do the birds come?","nature"]]},"z:578":{"n":"Rice fields and wildlife refuges","k":"z","q":[["What are those fields of water and green plants along the freeway?","Those are rice fields. They spread across the valley here, and the marshes of the Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge sit just east of the freeway.","What are they growing in those fields?|Why are the fields all wet and flat, Mom?|What's that crop?","nature"],["What is the Sacramento National Wildlife Refuge?","It's a wildlife refuge with marshes, sitting just east of the freeway among the rice fields of the valley.","What's that refuge over there?|Is that a place with animals, Dad?|What's the refuge?","see"],["When is the best time to see lots of birds at the refuge?","Winter is the big time. Waterfowl numbers at the refuge peak in late November and early December.","When are the most birds there?|Are there more birds in winter, Mom?|Best time for birds?","nature"],["Do birds stop at the Sacramento refuge in spring?","Yes. In April, shorebirds and songbirds stop at the refuge on their way north.","Do they come through in spring?|Do little birds visit the refuge, Dad?|Any birds in April?","nature"]]},"z:597":{"n":"Willows and Glenn County","k":"z","q":[["What grows around Artois north of Willows?","Almond orchards grow around Artois, which is just north of Willows. So the trees along this stretch are almonds.","What do they grow around there?|What are those trees we keep passing, Dad?|What's growing out here?","nature"]]},"z:619":{"n":"Orland and Corning orchards","k":"z","q":[["What grows in the orchards around Orland and Corning?","Almonds, walnuts, prunes and olives all grow in the orchards around Orland and Corning.","What are those trees growing around there?|What are all those trees out the window?|What's growing there?","nature"],["Which creeks does the freeway cross near Orland and Corning?","The freeway crosses Stony Creek and Thomes Creek in this stretch.","Does it cross any creeks around there?|Are there any creeks we go over, Dad?|What creeks are here?","nature"],["When does Thomes Creek run highest?","Thomes Creek runs highest in February, on average, during the winter.","When does it have the most water?|When does the creek have lots of water, mom?|Best month for water?","nature"],["Is there a festival in Corning?","Yes, the Corning Olive Festival is held on the second Saturday in October, in the fall.","Is there anything fun they celebrate there?|Is there a party in Corning for olives?|Any festivals?","famous"]]},"z:636":{"n":"Tehama County to Red Bluff","k":"z","q":[["What is the farmland around Tehama County to Red Bluff?","This is the northern end of a band of farmland that runs almost unbroken all the way down to Bakersfield.","What's all that farmland out there?|Why is it all farms here, Dad?|Farms for how long?","nature"],["What mountain can we see near Red Bluff?","On clear days, Lassen Peak stands on the eastern skyline. If the air is clear, look for it on the horizon.","What's that peak on the horizon?|Is that a big mountain way over there?|What mountain is that?","see"],["What happens in Red Bluff in the spring?","The Red Bluff Round-Up rodeo comes to town in late April.","Is there anything going on in town in spring?|Is there a rodeo here?|What's the Round-Up?","visit"],["How hot does Red Bluff get in the summer?","Red Bluff averages about 100 days a year with highs of 90 degrees or more, so summers there run hot.","Is it hot there in summer?|Does it get super hot in Red Bluff?|How many hot days does it get?","nature"]]},"z:649":{"n":"Red Bluff rangeland","k":"z","q":[["What is the land like around Red Bluff rangeland?","It's mildly rugged rangeland. It sits between Red Bluff and Cottonwood.","What's that land out there like?|Why does it look so bumpy and empty out here?|What kind of land is this?","nature"],["Which towns does the Red Bluff rangeland run between?","It runs between Red Bluff and Cottonwood.","Where does that rangeland start and end?|Which towns are on each end of it?|What's it between?","other"]]},"z:662":{"n":"Cottonwood and Anderson","k":"z","q":[["What is the land like around Cottonwood and Anderson?","It's ranch land, with Cottonwood Creek running through it, near the northern tip of the Central Valley.","What's out there around it?|What are we looking at here, are those ranches?|What's the scenery?","nature"],["Where are Cottonwood and Anderson in the Central Valley?","They sit near the northern tip of the Central Valley, where the freeway passes both towns.","Where is it in the valley?|Are we almost out of the valley yet?|Where in the valley is this?","other"],["What is Cottonwood Creek like in winter?","In winter and spring, Cottonwood Creek often runs like a large river.","Does it get big in winter?|Is that creek ever a real river?|Is the creek full in winter?","nature"],["What happens to Cottonwood Creek in summer?","By summer and fall, Cottonwood Creek dwindles to a trickle.","Does it dry up in summer?|Why is the creek so tiny now?|Is the creek still running?","nature"]]},"z:675":{"n":"Redding","k":"z","q":[["Where does Redding sit in the Central Valley?","Redding sits at the northwestern end of the Central Valley. There are mountains to the north, east and west, and farmland to the south.","Where is it in the valley?|Are we in the middle of the valley, Dad?|Where in the valley?","nature"],["What is the land like around Redding?","Mountains surround Redding on the north, east and west, and there is farmland to the south.","What surrounds it?|Are there mountains around here?|What's around us?","nature"],["How hot does Redding get in summer?","It gets hot. The average July high in Redding is near 100 degrees.","Is it hot there in July?|Is it gonna be super hot, Mom?|How hot in summer?","nature"],["What is winter like in Redding?","Winter brings most of Redding's rain, often with fog, and at times snow.","Does it rain there in winter?|Does it snow there?|Winter weather?","nature"]]},"z:680":{"n":"North Redding and the dam towns","k":"z","q":[["What's the city of Shasta Lake up here?","It's a city right where Redding's northern edge ends. Redding meets the city of Shasta Lake along this stretch of the road.","What is that town right there?|Is that a real city or just a lake, Buddy?|What's Shasta Lake?","name"],["Where is Shasta Dam from here?","Shasta Dam sits a few miles west of this stretch of I-5, near where Redding meets the city of Shasta Lake.","How close is it to the road?|Is the dam near us, Buddy?|Where's the dam?","see"],["What is the summer like around North Redding?","Summers are hot and dry, with big swings between daytime highs and nighttime lows.","Is it hot up here in summer?|Will it be super hot in the summer there, Buddy?|How hot is summer?","nature"],["What is winter like around the dam towns?","Winters here are cool and wet.","Does it get cold there in winter?|Is it rainy there in the winter, Buddy?|How's winter there?","nature"]]},"z:688":{"n":"Shasta Lake","k":"z","q":[["What grows along the shores of Shasta Lake?","The steep shores are covered with tall evergreens and manzanita.","What kind of trees are around it?|What are all those plants by the water, mom?|What's growing there?","nature"],["What does the shoreline of Shasta Lake look like?","The shores are steep, and they're covered with tall evergreens and manzanita.","Is it flat around it?|Are there mountains by the water, dad?|What's the land like there?","see"],["What is Bridge Bay on Shasta Lake?","Bridge Bay is the lake's largest marina.","What is it on that lake?|Is there a boat place, mom?|What's Bridge Bay?","visit"]]},"z:704":{"n":"Sacramento River canyon","k":"z","q":[["What history is along the Sacramento River canyon?","The freeway passes old stage stops and Gold Rush camps, so this canyon has a lot of history from the Gold Rush era.","What happened along it back in the day?|Was there anything cool here, like cowboys or gold?|What's the history here?","history"],["Were there Gold Rush camps in the Sacramento River canyon?","Yes, the freeway runs past old Gold Rush camps in the canyon, along with old stage stops.","Did people dig for gold along it?|Were there real gold miners here, like in the movies?|Gold Rush camps here?","history"],["Does it snow in the Sacramento River canyon north of Redding?","Yes, snow can fall at higher elevations north of Redding from fall to spring, and Caltrans sometimes requires chains.","Could we get snow up there?|Will there be snow on the road up ahead?|Snow or chains needed?","other"]]},"z:722":{"n":"Castle Crags and Dunsmuir","k":"z","q":[["What kind of town is Dunsmuir?","Dunsmuir is an old railroad town. It fills the bottom of the canyon, with granite spires rising above it.","What's it like down in that canyon?|Is Dunsmuir an old town, Mom?|Dunsmuir?","history"],["How much rain and snow does Dunsmuir get?","Dunsmuir gets about 63 inches of rain and snow a year. That's about three and a half times as much as Yreka or Medford.","Does it get a lot of snow there?|Is it super snowy in Dunsmuir?|How wet is it?","nature"],["When does Dunsmuir get most of its rain and snow?","More than half of Dunsmuir's yearly rain and snow falls in just three months: December, January and February.","When is it wettest there?|When does it snow in Dunsmuir?|Is winter the wet season?","nature"],["Are there fish to catch in Dunsmuir?","Yes. In summer, Dunsmuir stocks the river in town with trophy rainbow trout up to 14 pounds.","Can people fish in that river?|Are there big fish in the river?|Any fish there?","see"],["When does it start freezing in Dunsmuir?","The first freeze usually arrives around October 20. In an average year, the last freeze comes around April 24.","When is the first frost there?|When does the freezing stop in spring?|Does it freeze there?","nature"]]},"z:735":{"n":"Mount Shasta and Black Butte","k":"z","q":[["What is Black Butte near Mount Shasta?","Black Butte is the dark cone you see near Mount Shasta. It stands close to the foot of the volcano, along with the city of Mount Shasta and Black Butte Summit.","What is that dark cone over there?|What's that black hill, is it a volcano too?|What's Black Butte?","see"],["What record did Mount Shasta set for snow?","In February 1959 the old Ski Bowl on Mount Shasta got 15.75 feet of snow in one storm. That is a U.S. record.","What happened with the snow on it in 1959?|How much snow did it get, was it a lot?|Mount Shasta snow record?","famous"],["Can people climb Mount Shasta?","Yes, climbers head for Mount Shasta's summit from late April into October.","Do people climb it to the top?|Can kids climb the big volcano?|Anyone climb Shasta?","visit"],["What will we see around Mount Shasta?","The canyon opens onto the foot of the volcano. You'll see the city of Mount Shasta, the dark cone of Black Butte and Black Butte Summit.","What can we see around it?|What's at the bottom of the big volcano?|What's there to see?","see"]]},"z:746":{"n":"Weed, lumber and wind","k":"z","q":[["Why did the town of Weed grow up here?","Weed grew up around lumber mills. The lumber industry is what gave this town its start.","Why did people build up Weed here?|Why is there even a town here, anyway?|How'd Weed get started?","history"],["What happened here when Shastina erupted?","Hot flows from Shastina's eruptions once covered the ground here. So the land around Weed was buried by those volcanic flows.","What did it do to the ground here?|What did the volcano do to this place, Mom?|What did Shastina do?","history"],["Has Weed had any wildfires?","Yes. Recent wildfires have left their marks on the area around Weed, so you may notice signs of them along this stretch.","Did that burn here recently?|Did stuff burn down here, Dad?|Any fires around here?","nature"],["What is Weed's Carnevale?","It's Weed's summer festival, with bocce ball tournaments. It's held each June or July.","What happens at it in the summer?|Is there a party in Weed, Mom?|What's the Carnevale thing?","visit"]]},"z:756":{"n":"Shasta Valley","k":"z","q":[["What kind of valley is the Shasta Valley?","It's a wide, dry valley sitting in the rain shadow of the Klamath Mountains. You'll see hay fields, cattle and juniper dotted across it.","What is it like out here?|What kind of place is this, Buddy?|Describe the valley","nature"],["Does it rain in the Shasta Valley in summer?","Almost never. Summer rain hardly ever falls here, except for an occasional thunderstorm.","Does it rain here in summer?|Is it always sunny here, Buddy?|Summer rain?","nature"],["Does it snow in the Shasta Valley in winter?","Yes, fairly often. Winters here are cool to cold, with fairly frequent snow.","Does it snow here?|Is it cold here in winter, Buddy?|Snow in winter?","nature"]]},"z:770":{"n":"Yreka gold country","k":"z","q":[["What is Yreka gold country known for?","This stretch is Gold Rush country, built around the county seat of Yreka. The Klamath Mountains rise to the west and the Shasta River cuts north toward the Klamath.","What is it known for?|Why is this place famous, mom?|What's special here?","famous"],["What mountains are near Yreka gold country?","The Klamath Mountains rise to the west of this Gold Rush country around Yreka.","What mountains are those over there?|Are there big mountains here, dad?|What's that range?","nature"],["What river runs through Yreka gold country?","The Shasta River cuts north through this country toward the Klamath.","What river is it near here?|Is there a river here, mom?|Which river?","nature"],["Is there a Gold Rush event in Yreka?","Yes, in summer Yreka holds Gold Rush Days every June.","Does it have a festival?|Is there a fun gold thing to do, dad?|Any events there?","visit"],["Does it snow much in Yreka?","Not much. Yreka averages only about 12 inches of snow a year, mostly from November through February.","Does it get snow there?|Does it snow a lot, mom?|Snowy there?","nature"]]},"z:785":{"n":"Klamath River to the Oregon line","k":"z","q":[["What are the Siskiyou Mountains north of the Klamath River?","The Siskiyou Mountains rise to the north. They're the divide between the Klamath and the Rogue.","What are those mountains up ahead?|What are those big mountains, are they important?|What mountains are those?","nature"],["Does Siskiyou Summit get snow near the Oregon line?","Yes. Just over the Oregon line, Siskiyou Summit usually gets snow from November through April.","Does it snow up there?|Will there be snow on the pass, can we play in it?|Snow on the pass?","nature"],["What do people do with the land along the Klamath River here?","The valley is farm and ranch country. Below the old Iron Gate Dam site, the Klamath runs through an arid valley of farms and ranches.","What do they do with all this land?|Is somebody farming out here, what's growing?|What's the land used for?","today"]]},"mission-san-diego-de-alcala":{"n":"Mission San Diego de Alcalá","k":"m","q":[["When was Mission San Diego de Alcalá founded?","It was founded on July 16, 1769. It's the first of California's 21 missions.","How old is it?|When did they start this mission, like how long ago?|When was it built?","age"],["Who founded Mission San Diego de Alcalá?","Father Junípero Serra founded the mission in 1769, beside a new Spanish fort.","Who started it?|Who made this place, like who was the boss?|Who built it?","people"],["What happened to Mission San Diego de Alcalá in 1775?","On November 4 and 5, 1775, people from several Kumeyaay villages surrounded the mission and burned it. Father Luis Jayme was killed.","What happened to it back then?|Did something bad happen here, like a fight?|What went wrong?","history"],["Who are the Kumeyaay people connected to Mission San Diego de Alcalá?","The Kumeyaay have lived in this valley for thousands of years, and they live in San Diego County today.","Who are the Kumeyaay, and what do they have to do with it?|Who lived here before the mission, like way back?|Kumeyaay who?","people"],["What can we see at Mission San Diego de Alcalá?","Look for Father Jayme's tomb near the altar, a tall bell wall with five bells outside, and a small museum in the visitor center. The gardens are quiet.","What's there to see at it?|Is there anything cool for kids to look at there?|What's inside?","see"],["Is the Mission San Diego de Alcalá church the original building?","Not quite. It's the fifth church on this site, largely rebuilt in 1931. Only one room, the Casa del Padre Serra, dates to 1774 and is believed to be the only original room.","Is that the original church?|Is it really old or did they rebuild it?|Original?","history"]]},"mission-carmel":{"n":"Carmel Mission","k":"m","q":[["Who founded Carmel Mission?","Father Junípero Serra founded it at Monterey in 1770 and moved it here in 1771. It was his headquarters until he died in 1784.","Who started it?|Who built this old mission, anyway?|Who's the founder?","people"],["Why did Carmel Mission move from Monterey?","Serra moved the mission here from Monterey, partly to keep Native people away from the soldiers.","Why did it move here?|Why did they pick up the mission and move it, Dad?|Why'd it move?","history"],["Is Father Serra buried at Carmel Mission?","Yes. He died on August 28, 1784, and he is buried beneath the sanctuary floor of the church.","Is he buried in that church?|Is there a real grave in there?|Where's Serra buried?","history"],["What is there to see at Carmel Mission?","The stone church, four small museums, a chapel gallery, the cemetery and the gardens. Plan one to two hours. Easy to miss is Serra's simple cell in the convento museum.","What can we see at it?|What cool stuff is in there?|Is it worth a stop?","see"],["Is Carmel Mission still used today?","Yes. It's an active Catholic parish and a minor basilica, so some areas close during Mass.","Is it still a working church?|Do people still go to church there?|Still open for Mass?","today"]]},"mission-san-antonio-de-padua":{"n":"Mission San Antonio de Padua","k":"m","q":[["What is Mission San Antonio de Padua known for?","It was among the first places in Alta California to make fired red clay roof tiles, which protected buildings from fire.","What's it famous for?|What's so special about this mission?|Why is it a big deal?","famous"],["Why is Mission San Antonio de Padua inside a military base?","In World War Two the Army took over the surrounding ranch land, so the mission now sits inside a military base. The base has its own gate rules, so read the mission's directions page first.","Why is it on an Army base?|Why is the mission inside a military base, Mom?|Army base?","history"],["What is there to see at Mission San Antonio de Padua?","There's the rebuilt church, a museum with Native American and mission-era archaeology, and the original water system ruins beyond the church. There's also a gift shop and a retreat center.","What can we see there?|What's there to do at the mission?|Anything cool to see?","see"],["Whose homeland is Mission San Antonio de Padua on?","It sits in the homeland of the Salinan people, and the Salinan Tribe helps care for the cemetery here today. Salinan people did the farming and building at the mission.","Which Native people lived here?|Who lived here before the mission?|Whose land is it?","people"]]},"mission-san-gabriel":{"n":"Mission San Gabriel","k":"m","q":[["When was Mission San Gabriel founded?","Mission San Gabriel was founded on September 8, 1771. It was later moved to its current spot a few years after, because of floods.","How old is it?|When did they start this place, mom?|When was it built?","age"],["Who founded Mission San Gabriel?","Fathers Pedro Benito Cambón and Ángel Fernández de la Somera founded it, under the authority of Junípero Serra. It was mission number 4 of 21.","Who started it?|Who made this place, dad?|Who built that?","people"],["Who lived on this land before Mission San Gabriel?","The mission stands in the homeland of the Tongva people. Some of their descendants use the name Kizh.","Whose land was it before that?|Who lived here first, mom?|Were there people here before?","people"],["What makes the church at Mission San Gabriel different from other missions?","Its architect, Father Antonio Cruzado, gave it tall narrow windows and capped buttresses. The California Missions Foundation ties that style to the cathedral of Córdoba in Spain.","What's special about it?|Why does that church look weird, dad?|What's cool to see?","famous"],["What happened to Mission San Gabriel in 2020?","An arson fire destroyed the church roof on July 11, 2020. The roof was rebuilt, and the church walls are original from when it was finished in 1805.","What happened to it?|Did the church burn down, mom?|Was there a fire?","history"]]},"mission-san-luis-obispo":{"n":"Mission San Luis Obispo","k":"m","q":[["Why was Mission San Luis Obispo built here?","The soldiers and priests at Monterey were short of food in 1772. Serra sent hunters to this valley, and they brought back so much bear meat that he chose it for a mission.","Why did they put it in this valley?|Why'd they pick this spot, anyway?|Why here?","history"],["What is special about the church at Mission San Luis Obispo?","Its church is the only L-shaped church among the California missions. A long second nave sits beside the main one, and that is what makes the L.","What makes it different from the other missions?|Why is the church shaped funny?|What's unusual about it?","famous"],["Who built Mission San Luis Obispo?","Serra founded it on September 1, 1772, and Father José Cavaller built it with Chumash labor. It was the fifth mission.","Who started it?|Who made it, kids?|Who founded it?","people"],["Why does Mission San Luis Obispo have clay roof tiles?","In 1776 a flaming arrow set the mission's thatched roofs on fire. The padres then began firing clay roof tiles here.","How did it start using tiles?|Why did they make tiles back then?|Tiles, why?","history"],["Is Mission San Luis Obispo still used today?","Yes, it is still a working parish, Old Mission Parish. There is also a small museum and gift shop beside the church.","Is it still a working church?|Do people still go to church there?|Is it just a museum now?","today"]]},"mission-dolores":{"n":"Mission Dolores","k":"m","q":[["How old is Mission Dolores?","The first Mass here was said on June 29, 1776. The little adobe chapel was finished in 1791 and is the oldest intact building in San Francisco.","How old is it?|How many years has that been standing?|When did it start?","age"],["Who founded Mission Dolores?","Father Francisco Palóu founded it with Father Pedro Benito Cambón. Serra was not there, though it was under his authority.","Who started it?|Who built this place, like, way back?|Who founded it?","people"],["Whose land is Mission Dolores on?","This is the homeland of the Ramaytush Ohlone, who are still here. People from the Yelamu group joined the mission, and Coast Miwok and others were brought from farther away.","Who lived here before it was built?|Who were the first people here?|Whose homeland is it?","people"],["Did Mission Dolores survive the 1906 earthquake?","The original adobe chapel did survive. The brick church built next door in 1876 did not make it through 1906.","Did it survive the earthquake?|Did the big shaking knock it down?|Was it wrecked in 1906?","history"],["What is there to see at Mission Dolores?","You can see the original adobe chapel, whose ceiling still shows Ohlone designs in plant dyes, plus a small museum and the cemetery. Plan 45 minutes to an hour.","What can we see at it?|Is there cool stuff to look at?|Worth stopping at?","see"]]},"mission-san-juan-capistrano":{"n":"Mission San Juan Capistrano","k":"m","q":[["What is Mission San Juan Capistrano famous for?","It is famous for its swallows. The Serra Chapel there is also regarded as the oldest building in California still in use.","What is it famous for?|Why is this place so famous, mom?|What's it known for?","famous"],["When was Mission San Juan Capistrano founded?","It was founded on November 1, 1776, after a first try in 1775 was abandoned within days.","When was it founded?|How old is this place, dad?|How old is it?","age"],["Why did the priests abandon the first attempt at the mission?","Word came that Kumeyaay people had burned Mission San Diego, so the priests buried the bells and left. Serra later returned and founded the mission on All Saints' Day, 1776.","Why did they leave it at first?|Why did the priests run away and bury the bells?|Why'd they quit?","history"],["What happened to the Great Stone Church?","It collapsed in the earthquake of December 8, 1812, during Mass, and about 40 people died. The ruins still stand today, kept as ruins.","What happened to it?|Why is the big church just ruins?|Did it fall down?","history"],["Who lived here before the mission was built?","This is Acjachemen homeland. They lived in villages along the creeks here, and Acjachemen families still live in this region and keep their language and traditions alive.","Who lived on this land first?|Were there Native people here before the mission?|Who's homeland is this?","people"]]},"mission-santa-clara":{"n":"Mission Santa Clara","k":"m","q":[["When was Mission Santa Clara founded?","Mission Santa Clara was founded on January 12, 1777. It was the eighth of the 21 California missions, and the first one named for a woman.","How old is it?|How long ago did they start this place, mom?|When'd it start?","age"],["Is the Mission Santa Clara church the original building?","No, it's a replica. A fire in October 1926 destroyed the church, and it was rebuilt in concrete in 1928 and 1929 with one bell tower like the 1825 church.","Is that the real old church?|Did they build this a really long time ago, dad?|Is it a fake?","history"],["What is special about Mission Santa Clara compared to other missions?","It's the only mission on a university campus. It stands on the grounds of Santa Clara University, which the Jesuits opened as a college in 1851.","What makes it different?|Why is this mission cool, dad?|What's unique?","famous"],["Who lived here before Mission Santa Clara was built?","This is the homeland of the Tamien Ohlone. The first Mass was said at their village of So-co-is-u-ka on the Guadalupe River.","Whose land was it before that?|Did people live here first, mom?|Who was here first?","people"],["What can we see at Mission Santa Clara?","You can visit the church, with a bell tower holding bells cast in different years. You can also walk the quiet mission gardens, easy to miss among the campus paths.","What's there to do at it?|Is there anything fun to see, mom?|What's worth seeing?","see"],["Is Mission Santa Clara still used today?","Yes, it's a working chapel on the Santa Clara University campus. Some hours are set aside for services, so check before you go.","Do people still use it?|Is it a real church now, dad?|Still open?","today"]]},"mission-san-buenaventura":{"n":"Mission San Buenaventura","k":"m","q":[["Who founded Mission San Buenaventura?","Junípero Serra founded it. It was the last mission he founded, back in 1782.","Who started it?|Who made this place, Dad?|Who built it?","people"],["When was Mission San Buenaventura founded?","It was founded on Easter Sunday, March 31, 1782. It was the ninth of the 21 California missions.","How old is it?|How many years ago was this built, Mom?|What year?","age"],["What can we see at Mission San Buenaventura?","The museum has two wooden bells, the only wooden bells known from any California mission. There's also a thirty-minute history video and a garden with a grotto.","What is there to see at it?|What's cool inside, Dad?|What's in there?","see"],["What are the wooden bells at Mission San Buenaventura for?","They were rung during Holy Week, when the metal bells were silent. They're the only wooden bells known from any California mission.","Why does it have wooden bells?|Why are the bells made of wood, Mom?|Wooden bells?","famous"],["Is Mission San Buenaventura still used today?","Yes, it's a living parish and a minor basilica, with a museum too. Be quiet if there's a Mass going on.","Is it still a church?|Do people still go there, Dad?|Is it open for real?","today"],["What happened at Mission San Buenaventura in 1812?","Earthquakes in 1812 and a wave from the sea sent the friars and Chumash people inland for a time.","What happened to it back then?|Did something bad happen here, Mom?|Earthquake?","history"]]},"mission-santa-barbara":{"n":"Old Mission Santa Barbara","k":"m","q":[["Who founded Old Mission Santa Barbara?","Father Fermín Lasuén founded it on December 4, 1786, on the feast of Saint Barbara. It was the tenth of the 21 missions.","Who started it?|Who built the mission, kids want to know?|Who founded it?","people"],["What can we see and do at Old Mission Santa Barbara?","There's a nine-room museum, fifteen acres of gardens, the original fountain, and the lavadero, a stone basin where women washed clothes. An audio tour comes in six languages.","What's there to see at it?|What's cool to look at there for kids?|Worth stopping?","see"],["Is the church at Old Mission Santa Barbara the original building?","No. An 1812 earthquake destroyed the third church. The stone church you see was built from about 1815 to 1820, and a 1925 earthquake caused extensive damage, with repairs finished in 1927.","Is that church the original one?|Did the mission fall down in an earthquake?|How old is the church?","history"],["Who are the Chumash people connected to Old Mission Santa Barbara?","This is Chumash homeland, and Barbareño Chumash people are still here. Today the mission offers space for classes in the Barbareño Chumash language.","Who were the Chumash at that mission?|Who lived there before the mission?|Are Chumash people still around?","people"]]},"mission-la-purisima":{"n":"La Purísima Mission","k":"m","q":[["When was La Purísima Mission founded?","La Purísima Mission was founded on December 8, 1787. It was the 11th of the 21 California missions in founding order.","How old is it?|When did they build this place, mom?|What year was it founded?","age"],["Who founded La Purísima Mission?","Father Fermín Lasuén founded it, at a Chumash place called Algsacpi, near today's downtown Lompoc.","Who started it?|Who made this place, dad?|Who founded that?","people"],["Why was La Purísima Mission moved to a new site?","An earthquake and heavy rains on December 21, 1812 destroyed the buildings. The mission was re-founded at this new site on April 23, 1813.","What happened to the first mission?|Did something bad happen to it, mom?|Why a second site?","history"],["Are the buildings at La Purísima Mission original?","No, it's a reconstruction. Civilian Conservation Corps crews built it from the ruins between 1934 and 1941, so the walls are not original.","Is it real or rebuilt?|Did they fake the old stuff, dad?|Is it old?","history"],["What can we see and do at La Purísima Mission?","You can see the church, the padre's chapel and ten restored buildings, plus a five-acre garden. The corrals hold burros, horses, longhorn cattle, sheep and goats, and there are trails and horseback rides.","Is it worth stopping at?|Are there animals there, mom?|What's there to do?","see"],["Is La Purísima Mission still a church today?","No, it is not a parish church. Today it is a state historic park run by California State Parks, so you can wander at your own pace.","Is it still used?|Can people pray there, dad?|What is it now?","today"]]},"mission-santa-cruz":{"n":"Mission Santa Cruz","k":"m","q":[["How old is Mission Santa Cruz?","Mission Santa Cruz was founded on August 28, 1791, when Father Fermín Lasuén raised the cross. It was the twelfth of the 21 California missions.","How old is it?|How many years ago did they build this thing?|When was it started?","age"],["Why is it called Mission Santa Cruz?","It is named for the Holy Cross, and it is the only mission with that name.","Why is it called that?|Why's it called Santa Cruz, like what's it mean?|Where'd the name come from?","name"],["Is anything original left at Mission Santa Cruz?","Just one original building survives, an adobe built from 1822 to 1824 for Native Yokuts and Ohlone families. The chapel on High Street is a replica finished in 1931.","Is any of it still original?|Is any of the real old stuff still here?|Anything old left?","history"],["Who lived here before Mission Santa Cruz was built?","This is the homeland of the Awaswas-speaking Ohlone, the Uypi people. Yokuts families also lived at the mission.","Whose land was it before that?|Who were the Native people here, before the mission?|Who lived here first?","people"],["Why is it called the hard-luck mission?","Floods soon forced the mission up to Mission Hill, and the 1857 earthquake knocked down the front wall of the old church. That's how it earned the nickname.","Why was it so unlucky?|What bad stuff happened to it, like floods and earthquakes?|Why hard luck?","history"]]},"mission-soledad":{"n":"Mission Soledad","k":"m","q":[["Why is it called Mission Soledad?","Soledad means solitude. The full Spanish name is Misión Nuestra Señora de la Soledad.","Why is it called that?|Why does it have that weird name, Soledad?|What does Soledad mean?","name"],["When was Mission Soledad founded, and by whom?","It was founded on October 9, 1791 in the Salinas Valley by Father Fermín Lasuén. It was the 13th of the 21 missions.","How old is it and who started it?|Who built this old mission, and when?|When did it start?","age"],["Is the chapel at Mission Soledad the original one?","No, the small chapel was rebuilt in the 1950s. The long adobe ruins beside it are original, the unrestored walls of the old quadrangle.","Is that chapel the real old one?|Is the old building really old, or is it fake?|Is it original?","see"],["What happened to Mission Soledad after the mission era?","After Mexico ended the mission system, it was sold for a reported 800 dollars, stripped for building materials and left in ruins. The chapel was rebuilt and dedicated in 1955.","What happened to it after the missions ended?|Why did the mission fall apart?|Why is it in ruins?","history"],["Who lived here before Mission Soledad was built?","Esselen and Chalon Ohlone people lived in this valley, and the mission was founded near an Esselen village. Yokuts and Salinan people were brought here later.","Who were the Native people of this land?|Were there Native Americans here before it?|Who lived here first?","people"]]},"mission-san-jose":{"n":"Mission San José","k":"m","q":[["Is Mission San José in the city of San Jose?","No. Mission San José is in Fremont, not in the city of San Jose.","Is it in San Jose?|Wait, is this the San Jose from the map?|San Jose or not?","name"],["Is the church at Mission San José the original building?","No, the church you see is a replica, built from 1982 to 1985. It copies the 1809 adobe church, which the 1868 Hayward Fault earthquake shattered.","Is it the real old church?|Is that church really old, or new?|Original or fake?","history"],["Who founded Mission San José and when?","Father Fermín Lasuén founded it on June 11, 1797, at the Ohlone village of Oroysom. Native people from Mission Santa Clara did the first building.","Who started it and when was it founded?|Who built this place, and how long ago?|When did it start?","people"],["What is there to see at Mission San José?","Look for the original hammered copper baptismal font and the bell wheel the Ohlone used during the sacred parts of Mass. The museum is in an original adobe wing, one of the few old buildings left.","What can we see inside it?|What's cool to look at there?|What should we look for?","see"]]},"mission-san-juan-bautista":{"n":"Mission San Juan Bautista","k":"m","q":[["How old is Mission San Juan Bautista?","It was founded on June 24, 1797, so it has stood for a very long time. It was the fifteenth of the 21 California missions founded.","How old is it?|Mom, how long ago was this place built?|When was it founded?","age"],["Why is Mission San Juan Bautista called the Mission of Music?","Father Estevan Tapis taught a Native boys' choir using color-coded music notation. That choir earned the mission its nickname, the Mission of Music.","Why do they call it that?|Why is it the music mission?|Why that nickname?","name"],["What is special about the church at Mission San Juan Bautista?","Its church is the only one among the California missions with three aisles. The side aisles were walled in after earthquake damage and reopened in 1975.","What's special about it?|Why is the church different from the other missions?|Anything cool inside?","famous"],["Is Mission San Juan Bautista really on the San Andreas Fault?","Yes. The fault runs just below the cemetery wall, and the 1906 earthquake ruined the outer walls. Earthquake damage in 1812 also led to the side aisles being walled in.","Is it really on top of that earthquake fault?|Can we see where the earthquake fault is?|Did an earthquake hurt it?","nature"],["What movie was filmed at Mission San Juan Bautista?","It appeared in Alfred Hitchcock's 1958 film Vertigo. The studio added the bell tower you see in the movie.","Was it in a movie?|Mom, did a famous movie use this place?|Any movies here?","famous"]]},"mission-san-miguel":{"n":"Mission San Miguel","k":"m","q":[["When was Mission San Miguel founded?","Mission San Miguel was founded on July 25, 1797, in Salinan homeland. The church you see today was finished in 1818.","How old is it?|When did they start this place, like how long ago?|How old?","age"],["Why was Mission San Miguel built where it is?","Father Fermín Lasuén founded it to close the gap between San Antonio and San Luis Obispo. The site was chosen because many Salinan people lived nearby, close to a village called Vahca.","Why did they put it there?|Why did they build it here, who picked this spot?|Why here?","history"],["What is Mission San Miguel famous for?","Its church still has the original painted walls, which have never been repainted. It's the place to see what a mission church really looked like inside.","What makes it special?|What's the big deal about it, why do people stop?|Why is it famous?","famous"],["Who painted the walls inside Mission San Miguel?","The bright interior was painted by Native people under the supervision of Esteban Munras, a Spanish artist. The painted patterns are the work of Native artists.","Who made the pictures on the walls in it?|Who did the colorful painting, was it kids or grownups?|Who painted it?","people"],["What happened to Mission San Miguel in the 2003 earthquake?","The San Simeon earthquake of December 22, 2003 severely damaged the church and closed it for six years. It was repaired and reopened in 2009.","Did the earthquake hurt it?|Did the whole church fall down in the earthquake?|Earthquake damage?","history"],["Is Mission San Miguel still used as a church today?","Yes, it's an active Catholic parish of the Diocese of Monterey, with a museum. It's a working parish, so be quiet during Mass.","Do people still go to church there?|Is anybody still in it, like does anyone still pray there?|Still active?","today"]]},"mission-san-fernando":{"n":"Mission San Fernando","k":"m","q":[["How old is Mission San Fernando?","Mission San Fernando was founded on September 8, 1797. It was the 17th of the 21 California missions, in founding order.","How old is it?|How long ago did they start this mission, Dad?|When was it built?","age"],["Who founded Mission San Fernando?","Father Fermín Lasuén founded it, on a farming settlement called Achooykomenga. The land was worked by Ventureño Chumash, Tongva and Tataviam laborers.","Who started it?|Who made this mission, Mom?|Who built it?","people"],["Why is it called San Fernando, like the valley?","It works the other way around. The mission gave its name to the San Fernando Valley.","Is that where the valley's name comes from?|Why does the valley have the same name, Mom?|Name of the valley?","name"],["Is the church at Mission San Fernando original?","No. The 1971 San Fernando earthquake destroyed the church, and it was rebuilt by 1974. The long convento next to it is original and was finished in 1822.","Is it the real old church?|Did the church fall down, Dad?|Was there an earthquake?","history"],["What can we see at Mission San Fernando?","You can see the long convento with its nineteen-arch colonnade, the church, and a courtyard fountain described as original. Bob Hope and his wife Dolores are buried in the memorial garden.","What is there to do at it?|Is there anything cool to see, Mom?|Worth a stop?","see"]]},"mission-san-luis-rey":{"n":"Mission San Luis Rey","k":"m","q":[["When was Mission San Luis Rey founded and by whom?","It was founded on June 13, 1798, by Father Fermín Lasuén. It was mission number 18 of the 21 missions, in founding order.","When did they build it and who started it?|Who made this mission, and how long ago?|Who founded it?","history"],["What is special about the church at Mission San Luis Rey?","It was finished in 1815 and is the only surviving mission church built in the shape of a cross. It has a wooden dome and cupola.","What makes that church different from the others?|Is there anything cool about the church?|Why is the church famous?","famous"],["Who was Pablo Tac at Mission San Luis Rey?","He was born at the mission around 1822. A priest took him to Rome as a boy, and there he wrote down the Luiseño language and the story of his people.","Who was that boy who went to Rome?|Was there a famous kid from here?|Pablo Tac?","people"],["What is there to see at Mission San Luis Rey?","Besides the church, the grounds have soldiers' barracks, the lavandería where clothes were washed, a kiln, a museum, the Madonna Chapel, and the first pepper tree planted in California, in 1830.","What can we see when we stop at it?|What is there to look at, is it fun?|What's on the grounds?","see"]]},"mission-santa-ines":{"n":"Mission Santa Inés","k":"m","q":[["When was Mission Santa Inés founded?","Mission Santa Inés was founded on September 17, 1804, by Father Estevan Tapis. It is mission number 19 of the 21 California missions.","How old is it?|When did they build this place, mom?|What year was it founded?","age"],["What happened at Mission Santa Inés in 1824?","On February 21, 1824, a soldier beat a young Chumash man here, and people rose up. The revolt spread to La Purísima and Santa Barbara, and Chumash people burned parts of this mission.","What happened here with the revolt?|Did something bad happen here, dad?|What was the Chumash revolt?","history"],["What can we see at Mission Santa Inés?","The museum holds vestments, art, old manuscripts and bells. The mission garden and the rose garden are good places to sit, and there is a gift shop on site.","What is there to do at it?|Is there anything cool inside, dad?|What's inside?","see"],["Is the church at Mission Santa Inés the original one?","The first church was mostly destroyed by the earthquake of December 21, 1812. The present church was dedicated on July 4, 1817, and its walls are original.","Is it the same church from way back?|Is this the real old church, mom?|Original church?","history"],["Is Mission Santa Inés still used today?","Yes. It is an active Catholic parish of the Archdiocese of Los Angeles, served by Capuchin Franciscan friars since 1924, and it also has a museum.","Do people still go to it?|Does anyone still live there, mom?|Still a church?","today"]]},"mission-san-rafael":{"n":"Mission San Rafael","k":"m","q":[["Why was Mission San Rafael built?","It was founded in 1817 as a hospital outpost of Mission Dolores for sick Native people. It was meant to heal people who were sick in foggy San Francisco.","Why did they build it?|Why'd they make a mission here, Dad?|What was it for?","history"],["Who founded Mission San Rafael?","Father Vicente Francisco de Sarría founded it on December 14, 1817, as an asistencia of Mission Dolores.","Who started it?|Who made this mission, Mom?|Who built it?","people"],["Why is it called Mission San Rafael?","It was named for the archangel of healing, which fits its purpose as a place to heal sick people.","Why is it named that?|Why's it got that name, Dad?|Name origin?","name"],["Is the Mission San Rafael building original?","No. The ruins were cleared away by 1870. The chapel you see is a replica built in 1949 near the site of the original hospital.","Is it the real old building?|Is this the real one, Mom?|Original or fake?","age"],["What can we see at Mission San Rafael?","There's the replica chapel with its star-shaped window, plus a small museum showing three of the four original mission bells. Plan 30 to 45 minutes.","What's there to see at it?|Is there anything cool to look at, Dad?|What's inside?","see"]]},"mission-sonoma":{"n":"Sonoma Mission","k":"m","q":[["When was Sonoma Mission founded?","Sonoma Mission was founded on July 4, 1823. It was the last of the 21 missions, and the northernmost one.","How old is it?|How long ago did they build this mission?|When did it start?","age"],["Who founded Sonoma Mission?","Father José Altimira founded it, with soldiers and Native people who were moved over from Mission Dolores.","Who started it?|Who was the guy who built this place?|Who made it?","people"],["What makes Sonoma Mission different from the other missions?","It is the only mission founded after Mexico won independence from Spain. It was also the last and northernmost of the 21.","Why is it special?|Why is it the last one?|What's the deal with that mission?","famous"],["What happened at Sonoma Mission in 1826?","Native people who lived outside the mission were unhappy about their share of the harvest and burned some of the wooden buildings. Altimira fled.","Did something bad happen to it?|Why did the buildings burn?|Was there a fire?","history"],["What can we see at Sonoma Mission?","The museum shows paintings by Chris Jorgensen, and outside is the Sonoma Mission Indian Memorial. The same park has the Sonoma Barracks and Vallejo's home.","Is it worth stopping at it?|Is there anything cool to look at?|What's inside?","see"],["Is Sonoma Mission still a church today?","No, it is a museum, not a church. It is part of Sonoma State Historic Park.","Is it still used?|Can people pray there now?|Is it a church or a museum?","today"]]}}};
